    {
      "id": "academic-writing-rules",
      "kind": "rule",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Academic writing rules",
      "description": "Standing rules for academic writing that hedge claims to the evidence, define terms, forbid invented citations, keep tense and terminology consistent, and report numbers precisely.",
      "category": "scientific-writing",
      "tags": [
        "hedging",
        "academic-integrity",
        "citations",
        "scholarly-style",
        "precision"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "review"
      ],
      "body": "When you write or edit academic text (papers, theses, proposals, reports, reviews):\n\nClaims and evidence\n- Match the strength of every claim to the strength of the evidence. Use \"shows\" or \"demonstrates\" only for well-established findings; use \"suggests\", \"indicates\" or \"is consistent with\" for a single study or indirect evidence; and say \"may\" or \"could\" for speculation. Do not stack hedges (\"may possibly suggest\").\n- Use causal language (\"causes\", \"leads to\", \"effect of\", \"improves\") only when the design supports causal inference. For observational findings write \"is associated with\" or \"predicts\".\n- Never write \"proves\" about empirical findings. Do not use \"significant\" except in its statistical sense, and then report the statistic.\n- Separate what the data show, what the author infers, and what is speculation, and signal each.\n\nCitations\n- Never invent a citation, author, year, title, journal, DOI, page number or quotation. Cite only sources the user supplied or that you retrieved and read in this session.\n- When a claim needs a source you do not have, insert a visible placeholder such as [CITE: evidence that X] and leave it for the author.\n- Cite the original study for a finding, not a review or news article about it, unless the user asks otherwise; say when a citation is secondary.\n- Follow the citation style the user names, consistently; do not mix styles.\n\nTerms and consistency\n- Define every technical term and abbreviation at first use, and use the abbreviation consistently afterwards; avoid abbreviations used fewer than three times.\n- Use one term for one concept throughout. Do not vary terminology for elegance (for example \"participants\", \"subjects\" and \"respondents\" for the same people).\n- Keep tense consistent with discipline conventions: present tense for established knowledge and for what the paper itself shows in its figures; past tense for what was done and found in this and earlier studies.\n- Use the first person when the field and venue accept it (\"we measured\") rather than contorted passives; follow the style guide the user names (for example APA, AMA, Chicago or the journal's).\n\nNumbers and precision\n- Give exact numbers with units and the appropriate precision; keep decimal places consistent within a measure, and do not report more precision than the measurement supports.\n- Report effect sizes with confidence intervals alongside p values; give exact p values (p < .001 below that) in the format the style guide requires.\n- Use SI units and the number formatting conventions of the named style guide.\n- Never change, round differently or \"tidy\" the author's data, statistics or quotations when editing prose. Flag apparent errors instead.\n\nStyle\n- Put the main point of each paragraph in its first sentence and keep each paragraph to one idea.\n- Prefer concrete, specific wording to vague intensifiers (\"very\", \"highly\", \"novel\", \"crucial\") and remove promotional language.\n- Keep the author's voice and argument when editing; explain substantive changes rather than silently rewriting meaning.\n- Remind the author to follow their venue's policy on disclosing AI assistance when you have drafted substantial text.",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-introduction-section",
          "write-results-section",
          "write-discussion-section",
          "verify-citations"
        ],
        "rules": [
          "source-citation-rules"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:9aa8522650a7654889483bfaa4be61d189f8bcba49dce078554cbe37e1b3db46"
