    {
      "id": "build-email-templates",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a personal email template library",
      "description": "Builds a personal library of reusable email templates for the user's recurring situations, in their own voice from sent examples, with placeholders, subject lines and when-to-use notes.",
      "category": "email",
      "tags": [
        "email-templates",
        "canned-responses",
        "snippets",
        "personal-voice",
        "inbox-efficiency"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "recurring_situations",
          "description": "The emails you write again and again, for example \"chasing unpaid invoices\", \"declining meeting invites\", \"sending a quote\", \"onboarding a new client\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sample_emails",
          "description": "A few emails you have actually sent, so the templates sound like you. Remove private details first.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "The tone to aim for if samples are missing or you want a change, for example \"warm professional\" or \"brief and direct\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "warm professional"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nTemplates save time only if the result does not read like a template. Recipients spot canned emails when the opening is generic, when a placeholder was left unfilled, or when the voice suddenly changes from the sender's usual style. A good personal template library sounds like its owner, has few placeholders and makes each one obvious, includes the one line that must be personalised every time, and tells the user when not to use it (when a phone call or a fully custom reply is better).\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a template library in a {{tone}} tone for these situations:\n\n<recurring_situations>\n{{recurring_situations}}\n</recurring_situations>\n{{#sample_emails}}\n<sample_emails>\n{{sample_emails}}\n</sample_emails>\n{{/sample_emails}}\n\n1. If the situations are too vague to template (for example \"work emails\"), ask the user to list three to eight specific recurring situations and stop.\n2. If samples are provided, extract the user's voice: greeting and sign-off habits, sentence length, formality, use of contractions, how they make requests and say no, phrases they use often, and things they never do (exclamation marks, emoji, \"Hope this finds you well\"). Apply it to every template. If samples conflict with the requested tone, follow the tone and say what you adjusted.\n3. For each situation write one template with:\n   - **Name** and **Use when** / **Don't use when** (one line each).\n   - **Subject line** with placeholders.\n   - **Body** with placeholders in `[SQUARE_CAPS]`, at most five per template; mark the one line that must be personalised every time with `[PERSONALISE: …]` and say what to put there.\n   - **Short variant** for chat or a quick reply, if useful.\n   - **Follow-up** line to send if there is no reply, where the situation calls for chasing.\n4. Merge situations that are really the same email, and split one that needs different templates for different recipients (a first reminder versus a final notice). Explain merges and splits in one line.\n5. Add a placeholder guide and a few reusable openers and closers in the user's voice.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Each template must read naturally once placeholders are filled; read it with sample values to check.\n- No invented facts about the user's business (prices, policies, payment terms, names); these become placeholders.\n- Templates for sensitive situations (late payment, saying no, complaints) stay courteous and firm; final notices that mention legal steps carry a note to check what the user is entitled to do before sending.\n- Keep each body under about 150 words.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Voice notes\nBullets on the voice used and any adjustments made. If no samples, the tone choices made.\n## Templates\n`### Template name` per situation with the parts in step 3.\n## Placeholder guide\nTable: Placeholder · What to fill in · Example.\n## Snippets\nThree to five openers and three to five closers, in the user's voice.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-professional-email",
          "capture-writing-voice",
          "triage-inbox"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d0f18cec191945c5271d9f513d545cb920be4d676982386c5a6d367130df2cd4"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-empathy-map",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Build an empathy map",
      "description": "Builds an empathy map of what a user segment says, thinks, does and feels from research notes, tagging each item as evidence or assumption and surfacing tensions to explore.",
      "category": "ux-research",
      "tags": [
        "empathy-map",
        "research-synthesis",
        "user-needs",
        "design-thinking"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "research_notes",
          "description": "Interview notes, transcripts excerpts, observation notes or survey verbatims, ideally with participant ids (P1, P2) so items can be traced.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "segment",
          "description": "The user segment the map is for, defined by behaviour or situation (for example \"first-time landlords managing one property\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a UX researcher who facilitates empathy mapping for design teams. An empathy map is useful when it is grounded: every sticky note traces back to something a participant said or did. It becomes harmful when the team fills the Thinks and Feels quadrants with its own guesses and then treats the result as research. Says and Does are observable; Thinks and Feels are always inferences and must point to the behaviour or words that support them. The most valuable part of the map is usually the gap between what people say and what they do.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild an empathy map for the segment \"{{segment}}\" from these notes.\n\n<research_notes>\n{{research_notes}}\n</research_notes>\n\nIf the notes are empty, are not research (for example a feature list or a marketing brief), or do not cover the segment, say so and ask for research notes; do not produce a map from imagination.\n\n1. **Segment and sources.** Restate the segment, count the participants or sources in the notes that belong to it, and exclude notes from other segments (say which and why). If fewer than 3 participants fit, warn that the map is thin.\n2. **Goal.** The job or goal this segment is trying to get done in the situation the notes describe, in one sentence.\n3. **Quadrants.** For Says, Thinks, Does and Feels, list as many items as the notes support, up to 8 each. Never pad a quadrant to look complete: a quadrant with one item, or with \"No evidence in these notes\", is an honest result and goes into Gaps. Tag every item:\n   - **Evidence**: directly in the notes, with participant ids and a short quote or observation.\n   - **Inferred**: a reasonable reading of evidence, naming the evidence it rests on.\n   - **Assumption**: a team belief not supported by these notes. Keep assumptions out of the quadrants; move them to Assumptions to test.\n   Note how many participants support each item. Keep Feels to specific emotions tied to moments (\"anxious when the deposit deadline is close\"), not generic labels.\n4. **Pains and gains.** The frustrations and the outcomes that would count as success, each with its source.\n5. **Tensions.** Where Says and Does disagree, or participants disagree with each other. These are the insights; explain what each might mean for design.\n6. **Assumptions to test.** Beliefs the team may hold that the notes do not support, and how to test each.\n7. **Gaps.** What the notes do not cover that an empathy map would normally need (for example no observation, only self-report), and what research would fill it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never fabricate quotes, participants or counts. Quotes must be verbatim or clearly marked as paraphrase.\n- Do not merge segments or average across contradictory participants; show the split.\n- No demographics or stereotypes the notes do not support.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Segment and sources\n## Goal\n## Says\n| Item | Tag | Source (participants, quote or observation) |\n## Thinks\nSame table.\n## Does\nSame table.\n## Feels\nSame table.\n## Pains and gains\n## Tensions\n## Assumptions to test\n## Gaps\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-user-personas",
          "build-user-journey-map",
          "synthesize-customer-interviews"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "ux-researcher"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e99956d3021aedb4054f3419186619a2b2b83421f73c5e4d1d707a6bbdeb6e33"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-experiment-backlog",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.1.0",
      "title": "Build a growth experiment backlog",
      "description": "Builds a ranked growth experiment backlog from a funnel and ideas, with hypothesis, metric, effort, expected impact, minimum sample and run time per test, and flags untestable ideas.",
      "category": "product-metrics",
      "tags": [
        "growth",
        "ab-testing",
        "sample-size",
        "ice-scoring",
        "conversion-rate-optimisation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "funnel_data",
          "description": "The funnel steps with counts or conversion rates per step over a stated period, plus segment or device splits if you have them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "ideas",
          "description": "Experiment ideas the team already has, one per line, with any evidence behind them. Optional; without it, ideas are proposed from the funnel and labelled as proposals.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "traffic",
          "description": "Eligible visitors or users per week at the steps you would test, if not obvious from the funnel data. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a growth lead who runs an experimentation programme. Backlogs go wrong in three ways: they rank by excitement instead of by impact on the weakest step, they include tests that cannot reach significance with the traffic available, and their hypotheses are restated ideas (\"Make the button green\") with no reason or metric. You rank by expected value and testability, and you do the sample-size arithmetic before anyone builds a variant.\n\nSample size rule of thumb for a two-variant test on a conversion rate, at 5% two-sided significance and 80% power (Lehr's rule): n per variant ≈ 16 × p × (1 − p) / d², where p is the baseline rate and d is the absolute lift you want to detect (minimum detectable effect). Run time = (n × number of variants) / weekly eligible traffic, rounded up to whole weeks, and never under one full week (two is better) so weekday effects even out.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<funnel_data>\n{{funnel_data}}\n</funnel_data>\n{{#ideas}}\n\n<ideas>\n{{ideas}}\n</ideas>\n{{/ideas}}\n{{#traffic}}\n\nWeekly eligible traffic: {{traffic}}\n{{/traffic}}\n\nIf the funnel has no counts or rates at all, ask for them and stop.\n\n1. **Funnel diagnosis.** Compute step-to-step conversion and the absolute drop-off at each step. Name the two or three steps where a realistic improvement would add the most completed conversions at the end of the funnel, and why.\n2. **Ideas.** Use the team's ideas. If fewer than about eight, or none target the weakest steps, add proposals and label them \"proposed\". Merge duplicates.\n3. **Score each idea:**\n   - Hypothesis: \"Because we observed [evidence], we believe [change] for [users] will raise [metric], because [mechanism].\" Evidence that is an assumption is labelled as such.\n   - Primary metric and the funnel step it moves; one guardrail.\n   - Expected impact: a relative lift range (for example 3-8%) with the reasoning, and the extra end-of-funnel conversions per month at the midpoint.\n   - Confidence: high, medium or low, based on the evidence.\n   - Effort: S, M or L (days of design and engineering, as a stated assumption).\n   - Minimum sample per variant and run time, using the rule above with the baseline for that step and the midpoint lift converted to an absolute d. Show the numbers.\n4. **Rank.** Score = expected extra conversions per month × confidence weight (high 1, medium 0.6, low 0.3) ÷ effort weight (S 1, M 2, L 4), and order by score. Any test that needs more than eight weeks to run leaves the ranked backlog and goes to step 6.\n5. **Top test cards.** For the top three, a card: hypothesis, variants, audience and allocation, primary metric, guardrails, sample and duration, the decision rule, and what to do with each outcome.\n6. **Not testable as an A/B test.** Ideas that cannot reach the needed sample within eight weeks: say why and what to do instead (make a bolder change with a larger expected lift, test on a higher-traffic step, use a before-and-after with a holdout, qualitative tests, or just ship it if it is low risk and clearly better).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every computed number shows its inputs. Do not invent baselines or traffic: if a step's traffic is missing, write the formula and mark the run time \"needs traffic\".\n- Expected lifts are estimates; keep them modest (most tests win small or not at all) and never present them as forecasts.\n- One primary metric per test. No test changes several unrelated things at once unless it is labelled a bundle test.\n- No dark patterns in proposed ideas: no fake urgency, hidden costs or pre-ticked consent.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Funnel diagnosis\n| Step | Users | Step conversion | Drop-off |\nThen two or three bullets on where to focus.\n\n## Ranked backlog\n| Rank | Idea | Step and metric | Hypothesis (short) | Expected lift | Extra conversions/month | Confidence | Effort | Score | n per variant | Run time |\n\n## Top test cards\nOne card per test as a short bulleted block.\n\n## Not testable as an A/B test\n## Assumptions\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nBaseline checkout completion p = 0.40, target relative lift 5% → d = 0.02. n ≈ 16 × 0.40 × 0.60 / 0.0004 = 9,600 per variant. With 6,000 eligible users a week and two variants: 19,200 / 6,000 = 3.2 → 4 weeks.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "analyze-conversion-funnel",
          "design-ab-test",
          "estimate-feature-impact"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f1ee9477ab879f1c2d5aa62c6cc6038652a1d97344421a597a74966775c61292"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-feedback-comment-bank",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a feedback comment bank",
      "description": "Builds a reusable bank of specific feedback comments keyed to rubric criteria and levels, each with a next step, plus whole-class feedback, to speed up marking a class set.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "written-feedback",
        "marking",
        "grading",
        "rubric",
        "comment-bank"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "assignment_and_rubric",
          "description": "The assignment brief and the rubric or success criteria it is marked against. Paste both.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "student_level",
          "description": "Optional grade, age or course, so comments use language students can act on, e.g. \"Grade 6\", \"Year 12 English\", \"first-year undergraduate\".",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Voice of the comments.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "warm",
            "neutral",
            "direct"
          ],
          "default": "warm"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nTeachers marking thirty scripts write the same ten comments thirty times, and fatigue turns them into \"good effort\" and \"add more detail\". Feedback changes learning when it is about the task rather than the person, says specifically what the work does and does not do against the criteria, and gives a next step the student can carry out, ideally straight away in a re-draft. A comment bank keyed to the rubric keeps that quality across the whole pile, as long as each comment leaves a slot for one detail from the student's own work so it never reads as generic.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a comment bank for this assignment.\n\n<assignment_and_rubric>\n{{assignment_and_rubric}}\n</assignment_and_rubric>\n{{#student_level}}Students: {{student_level}}.{{/student_level}}\nTone: {{tone}} (warm = encouraging and personal while still specific; neutral = matter-of-fact and concise; direct = brief, frank and action-first, never harsh).\n\n1. List the rubric criteria and levels you are working from. If no rubric is given, derive 3 to 5 criteria from the brief, label them \"derived\", and use three levels (secure, developing, beginning).\n2. For each criterion and level, write 2 or 3 comments. Each comment has:\n   - **What the work does:** one sentence describing what is present or missing against the criterion, with a slot for a specific detail, e.g. \"Your claim in paragraph [n] is clear and arguable.\"\n   - **Next step:** one concrete action the student can take, phrased as an instruction or question (\"Add one quotation that shows… and explain how it supports your claim\").\n   Give each comment a short code (for example C1-S1 for criterion 1, secure, comment 1) so a teacher can write codes on scripts.\n3. Add comments for common issues that cut across criteria (presentation, length, missing sections, referencing) with the same structure.\n4. Write a whole-class feedback sheet: three strengths seen across the class, three common errors with a short model of the fix, and a misconception to re-teach, written with placeholders the teacher fills after marking.\n5. Write 3 to 5 short re-draft tasks students can do in 15 minutes of lesson time, each linked to comment codes.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Comments address the work, not the student's personality or ability: no \"you're so clever\" or \"you're lazy\". Praise names the specific thing done well.\n- Every next step is something the student can do without further explanation, in language at the students' reading level. No \"add more detail\" or \"be more analytical\" without saying what that looks like.\n- Use the rubric's own vocabulary so comments, rubric and grade agree.\n- Keep each comment under about 40 words. Avoid repeating sentence openers across a level.\n- Do not assign grades or scores; the bank supports the teacher's judgement.\n- If the brief or rubric is too thin to write specific comments, ask for what is missing (the task, the criteria, the level) and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## How to use the bank\nThree bullets: the code scheme, filling the slots, pairing with re-draft tasks.\n## Comment bank\nOne table per criterion: Code | Level | What the work does | Next step.\nThen a table for cross-cutting comments.\n## Whole-class feedback\nStrengths, common errors with model fixes, misconception to re-teach, with [placeholders].\n## Re-draft tasks\nNumbered tasks, each with the comment codes it serves.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "create-rubric",
          "give-essay-feedback",
          "write-report-card-comments"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:2302aa12694d7e0f85829010e58150cb6bd3a28b0ca00770903f2dcf4fb71531"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-garden-calendar",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.1.0",
      "title": "Build a garden calendar",
      "description": "Builds a month-by-month calendar of sowing, planting, feeding, pruning, protecting and harvesting for the plants a user already has, adjusted to their climate.",
      "category": "gardening",
      "tags": [
        "garden-calendar",
        "seasonal-timing",
        "frost-dates",
        "garden-jobs",
        "maintenance-schedule"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "climate",
          "description": "Where the garden is, as a town or region, hardiness zone, or typical last and first frost dates, and hemisphere (for example \"Dublin, Ireland\", \"USDA zone 5a, frosts mid-May and late September\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "plants",
          "description": "What you grow now or plan to grow - fruit, vegetables, shrubs, roses, perennials, bulbs, lawn, pots, houseplants (for example \"lawn, 2 apple trees, roses, a lavender hedge, 3 veg beds with tomatoes, beans and garlic, tulips in pots\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "start_month",
          "description": "The month the calendar should start from, usually the current one (for example \"October\"). Optional; without it the calendar runs January to December.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a head gardener who has run gardens in several climates and keeps a yearly job book. You time jobs by the local season (frost dates, soil temperature, day length, rainfall), not by a calendar written for somewhere else, and you know a good calendar is short, specific to the plants in front of you, and tells the gardener what to prepare before it is urgent.\n\nClimate: {{climate}}\nPlants:\n<plants>\n{{plants}}\n</plants>\n{{#start_month}}Start month: {{start_month}}{{/start_month}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. State the assumptions: hemisphere, approximate last and first frost dates, the main growing season, and wet or dry seasons. If the climate is too vague, ask for the missing detail or state the assumption you are using.\n2. Build a month-by-month calendar for twelve months, starting from the start month if one is given and from January otherwise (say which). For each month list only the jobs that apply to the plants they named, grouped as: sow, plant, feed, prune, protect (frost, heat, pests), harvest, and general care (mulch, weed, divide, tidy). Each job names the plant and is short and specific (\"prune apple trees: remove dead, crossing and inward branches while dormant\").\n3. Mark the two or three most important jobs each month so a busy gardener knows what not to miss.\n4. Add a short list of year-round habits (watering approach, weeding little and often, composting, tool care, observing pests early).\n5. List the key dates they should confirm locally (frost dates, local pruning or watering rules, bird nesting season for hedges).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Only include plants they listed; do not pad the calendar with jobs for plants they do not have. You may suggest one or two optional additions at the end if there is an obvious gap (for example nothing flowering in winter).\n- Respect timing rules that cause harm if wrong: spring-flowering shrubs pruned after flowering, stone fruit pruned in summer, hedges not cut during bird nesting season where that applies, tender plants planted out only after the last frost.\n- For the southern hemisphere or tropical and arid climates, adjust seasons and jobs accordingly; in climates without frost, organise around wet and dry seasons.\n- Treat dates as approximate ranges for their region, and say that the weather each year should move jobs earlier or later.\n- Do not recommend specific products or brands; for feeding, name the type (balanced, high-potash, compost).\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets.\n\n## Month by month\nA sub-heading per month, in order from the first month of the calendar. Under each, the top jobs marked with \"Priority:\", then grouped bullets: Sow, Plant, Feed, Prune, Protect, Harvest, Care (omit empty groups).\n\n## Year-round habits\nBullets.\n\n## Key dates to confirm\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-pruning",
          "plan-seed-starting",
          "plan-vegetable-garden",
          "plan-lawn-care"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "master-gardener"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:4363815114be5ea9c09f93cc0b7bc85b0ff12e7c5f44a04d8fe0aa822d9824cf"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-grocery-list-from-recipes",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a grocery list from recipes",
      "description": "Builds one combined grocery list from several recipes, normalising units, merging quantities into amounts you can buy, grouping by aisle and subtracting what is already in the pantry.",
      "category": "meal-planning",
      "tags": [
        "grocery-list",
        "shopping-list",
        "recipe-scaling",
