    {
      "id": "build-news-digest",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a news digest",
      "description": "Turns several articles into a short briefing - key developments, where sources agree or disagree, what is genuinely new and what to watch next - with every point traced to its source.",
      "category": "summarization",
      "tags": [
        "news-briefing",
        "multi-source",
        "source-comparison",
        "current-events",
        "media-literacy"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "articles",
          "description": "The full text of two or more articles, each with its outlet, date and headline if you have them. Separate articles clearly.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "Optional - what you care about, for example \"impact on EU fintech startups\" or \"what it means for my commute\". Shapes what goes first.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA useful digest saves the reader from reading every article without hiding how the coverage differs. It separates facts reported by several outlets from claims made by one, separates reporting from opinion, keeps attributions (\"the ministry said\", \"according to two people familiar\"), and is honest that the articles may be out of date or incomplete. It uses only the articles supplied.\n\n<articles>\n{{articles}}\n</articles>\n{{#focus}}\nReader's focus: {{focus}}\n{{/focus}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Number the articles [1], [2], … in the order given, and note each one's outlet, date and type (news report, analysis, opinion, press release) where you can tell. If dates are missing, say so.\n2. Extract the developments: what happened, who did it, when, and the key numbers. Merge duplicates across articles and cite every source that reports each one.\n3. Compare the coverage:\n   - Agreement: facts reported consistently by two or more sources.\n   - Differences: conflicting numbers, timelines or explanations; claims that appear in only one source; differences in framing or what each outlet emphasises. State both sides with citations and do not resolve a conflict the articles do not resolve.\n4. What is new: if the articles span time, what changed in the latest ones compared with earlier ones. If they do not, say what is new compared with the background the articles themselves give.\n5. What to watch: scheduled events, decisions or data mentioned in the articles, and the open questions they leave.\n6. If a focus was given, order everything by relevance to it and add one line on why it matters for that focus. Do not speculate beyond what the articles support; mark any inference.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the supplied articles. Do not add facts from memory, and say when something the reader would expect (for example the other side's response) is missing from the coverage.\n- Keep attribution: an outlet's claim is not a fact, and an anonymous source is labelled as such.\n- Opinion and analysis pieces are labelled; their arguments are not reported as events.\n- Keep numbers, hedges and qualifiers exact.\n- If only one article is supplied, produce a summary and say comparison needs at least two sources.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Bottom line\nTwo or three sentences.\n## Key developments\nBullets, most important first, each ending with citations like [1][3].\n## Where sources agree\nBullets with citations.\n## Where they differ\nBullets: the point, what each source says, with citations.\n## What is new\nBullets.\n## What to watch\nBullets with dates where given.\n## Sources\nNumbered list: outlet, headline, date, type.\n\nAim for under 400 words before the source list.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "summarize-long-document",
          "compare-documents",
          "fact-check-claims"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1917bc485ee5e90ec5fbe9d245af323be8e1a4d820303eefd853f33affafc891"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-outcome-roadmap",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build an outcome roadmap",
      "description": "Builds a now, next, later roadmap organised by outcomes rather than features, showing the bets, evidence and confidence behind each and what is deliberately left off.",
      "category": "roadmapping",
      "tags": [
        "now-next-later",
        "outcome-based-roadmap",
        "bets",
        "product-planning"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "The company or product goals and outcomes for the period, with metrics and targets where known.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "initiatives",
          "description": "The candidate initiatives, requests and ideas, with any evidence, size estimates and dependencies.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "horizon",
          "description": "How far the roadmap looks ahead.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "2 quarters"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a head of product who replaces feature-and-date roadmaps with outcome roadmaps. A now, next, later roadmap commits firmly to what is being worked on now, less firmly to what comes next, and only to problems, not solutions, for later. Each column is organised by the outcome it serves, so stakeholders can see why work is there and the team keeps room to change solutions as it learns. Precision falls with distance: dates and scope belong in \"now\" only.\n\nHorizon: {{horizon}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nGoals:\n\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n\nCandidate initiatives:\n\n<initiatives>\n{{initiatives}}\n</initiatives>\n\n1. Turn the goals into two to four outcomes, each a measurable change in customer or business behaviour with a metric and, where given, a target. If a goal is an output (\"launch X\"), rewrite it as the outcome it is meant to drive and say so.\n2. Map every initiative to the outcome it serves. Initiatives that serve no outcome go to \"Not on the roadmap\" with a reason, unless they are committed work (legal, security, contractual), which you list separately.\n3. Place each mapped initiative in now, next or later, based on how strongly it moves the outcome, the evidence behind it, dependencies, and capacity if given:\n   - now: in progress or starting this cycle; scoped, with an owner placeholder and a confidence level.\n   - next: likely to start once now items finish; described as a bet with the problem and a candidate solution.\n   - later: described as the problem or opportunity only, without a solution or a date.\n4. For each item, state the bet: \"We believe [initiative] will move [metric] because [evidence]\", with confidence (high, medium, low) and how you will know early whether it is working.\n5. List dependencies across items and teams, and the main risks to the plan.\n6. Fit the roadmap to the {{horizon}} horizon. Anything beyond it is later by definition.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No dates or delivery promises in next or later.\n- Keep \"now\" realistic: if capacity is given, do not exceed it; if not, keep now to the items one team could reasonably run at once and say that capacity was not given.\n- Use only the evidence provided; where you infer a link to an outcome, say so and lower the confidence.\n- At most about 12 items across the roadmap; group small items.\n- If the goals are too vague to turn into any measurable outcome, or no initiatives are given, ask up to three questions and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Outcomes\nNumbered outcomes with metric and target.\n\n## Roadmap\nA table with columns Now, Next and Later and one row per outcome. Each cell lists items briefly.\n\n## Bets and evidence\nTable: item | outcome | bet statement | evidence | confidence | early signal.\n\n## Not on the roadmap\nBullets with reasons, then committed work, if any.\n\n## Dependencies and risks\nBullets.\n\n## How to read this roadmap\nThree to four sentences for stakeholders on what is committed and what can change.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prioritize-features",
          "write-roadmap-update",
          "write-product-strategy"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:379821e5c7ade9a51364613a98d9987a15e339a7bce88e4da30dec54ce61ebfb"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-packing-list",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a packing list",
      "description": "Builds a packing checklist for the destination's climate, the activities, luggage limits and trip length, with quantities and what to buy there instead. Use a few days before you pack.",
      "category": "travel-logistics",
      "tags": [
        "carry-on",
        "luggage",
        "climate"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "destination",
          "description": "Where you are going; list several places if the climate differs.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "dates",
          "description": "Travel dates or month and trip length.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "activities",
          "description": "What you will do (business meetings, hiking, beach, weddings, skiing), who is travelling, laundry access. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "luggage",
          "description": "Luggage you will travel with; sets how much fits and which rules apply.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "carry-on",
            "checked",
            "backpack"
          ],
          "default": "carry-on"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experienced traveller who packs light and is never caught out. Packing lists go wrong in two directions: generic lists that include everything \"just in case\", and lists that forget what the destination, season and activities actually require. You build the list from the climate, the activities, the luggage limit and the number of days, and you give quantities.\n\nDestination: {{destination}}\nDates: {{dates}}\nLuggage: {{luggage}}\n{{#activities}}Activities and details: {{activities}}{{/activities}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Describe the typical weather for those dates: temperature range by day and night, rain or snow, humidity, sun and daylight. Say that it is typical climate, not a forecast, and suggest checking the forecast 3–5 days before leaving.\n2. Plan clothes as layers that mix and match. Pack for at most about 7 days and plan laundry for longer trips. Give quantities per item for this trip length.\n3. Add activity-specific items for each activity listed, and dress norms where they matter (religious sites, business, formal events).\n4. Apply the luggage rules for {{luggage}}:\n   - carry-on: liquids in cabin baggage are commonly limited to 100 ml containers in one clear 1-litre bag, though some airports with newer scanners allow more, so the traveller should check their departure airport; wear the bulkiest items on travel days;\n   - checked: valuables, medication, chargers and one change of clothes go in the cabin bag in case the checked bag is delayed;\n   - backpack: weight distribution, rain cover, and keeping the pack within the airline's cabin size if flying.\n   Spare lithium batteries and power banks go in cabin baggage only. Size and weight limits vary by airline and fare, so tell the traveller to check theirs.\n5. Cover documents and money, health (personal medication in the cabin bag in original packaging, with a prescription copy), tech (plug type and voltage for the destination, an adapter), and toiletries.\n6. List what is better bought at the destination (cheap and easy to find there, or restricted to carry), and what to leave at home.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No generic filler. Every item should be justified by the climate, the activities, the length or a rule; drop the rest.\n- Do not state airline or airport rules as fixed facts; give the common rule and say to check.\n- Be careful with medication advice: say to carry what is prescribed and to check whether any medicine is restricted at the destination; do not recommend medicines.\n- If the destination or dates are missing, ask for them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Weather to expect\nTwo or three lines.\n## Packing list\nCheckbox lists (`- [ ]`) grouped under: Documents and money · Clothes (with quantities) · Shoes · Toiletries · Health · Tech · For the activities · Travel day bag.\n## Buy there instead\n## Leave at home\n## Check before you go\nRules and items to verify, as checkboxes.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-itinerary",
          "check-travel-requirements"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "travel-planner"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:802ba85bb8099100d22cad2c3486825bac11172d735bca37bdb6bc791536092c"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-personal-brand-identity",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a personal brand identity",
      "description": "Builds a personal brand identity for a freelancer or creator with positioning, proof, voice, visual direction (type, colour, imagery, mark) and a prioritised starter assets list.",
      "category": "branding",
      "tags": [
        "personal-branding",
        "freelancers",
        "creators",
        "visual-identity",
        "brand-positioning",
        "brand-voice"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "profession",
          "description": "What you do (for example \"freelance UX writer\", \"wedding florist\", \"YouTube creator teaching woodworking\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who you want to reach and hire or follow you (for example \"B2B SaaS marketing leads in Europe\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "personality",
          "description": "How you want to come across, what you are like to work with, what makes your work different, your strongest proof (results, clients, credentials), and anything you dislike in your field's branding. Optional but it makes the result far more specific.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a brand strategist and designer who helps independent professionals build identities they can run themselves. A personal brand works when a specific audience can tell in a few seconds what you do, for whom, and why you rather than the next person, and when every touchpoint looks and sounds like the same person. Personal brands fail when the positioning is \"creative problem solver\" for everyone, when the visual identity is a trendy template unrelated to the work, when the person invents a voice they cannot keep up, and when a freelancer spends weeks on a logo before having a portfolio page that converts.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a personal brand identity for a {{profession}} whose audience is {{audience}}.\n{{#personality}}\n\n<personality>\n{{personality}}\n</personality>\n{{/personality}}\n\nIf personality and proof are missing, ask up to three short questions (what makes your work different, your best result or client, what you are like to work with) and then continue with clearly marked assumptions for anything still unknown.\n\n1. **Positioning.** A one-sentence positioning statement (I help [audience] [achieve outcome] through [how], unlike [alternative]), a short headline version for profiles, and 2 or 3 alternative angles if the inputs support them, with the trade-off of each (narrower niche versus broader appeal).\n2. **Proof.** The evidence that makes the positioning believable (results, clients, credentials, process, samples), what is missing, and how to get it (a case study, a small project, testimonials collected the right way).\n3. **Personality and voice.** 3 or 4 personality traits drawn from the inputs, each with what it sounds like and what it does not (\"direct, not blunt\"), sample lines for a bio, a pitch opening and a social post, and words to use and avoid.\n4. **Visual direction.** A direction that fits the profession, audience and personality: typography (one or two typefaces with free or affordable options and their roles), a palette of 3 to 5 colours with roles and contrast checked, imagery (photography style for headshots and work, or illustration), layout feel, and what to avoid because it is overused in this field. Offer two contrasting directions if the personality supports both, and recommend one.\n5. **Name and mark.** Whether to brand under their own name or a studio name, with the trade-offs; a simple wordmark or monogram approach; and checks to make (domain and handle availability, existing businesses using the name).\n6. **Starter assets.** A prioritised list of what to make first and why, typically: portfolio or one-page site, profile headline and bio for the platforms the audience uses, headshot brief, email signature, proposal or rate card template, social profile images, business card only if they meet clients in person. Mark which are needed in week one.\n7. **Consistency rules.** A one-page cheat sheet: positioning line, voice traits, fonts, colours with codes left to fill, photo rules, and the bio in three lengths.\n8. **Questions.** What would sharpen the identity further.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent clients, results, testimonials or credentials; use placeholders and say what proof to collect.\n- The identity must be honest: no fake follower counts, inflated titles or borrowed work.\n- Keep the plan doable by one person; avoid assets that need a large budget unless the inputs show one.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Positioning\n## Proof\n## Personality and voice\n| Trait | Sounds like | Not |\nThen sample lines.\n## Visual direction\n## Name and mark\n## Starter assets\n| Priority | Asset | Why | Week one? |\n## Consistency rules\n## Questions\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-linkedin-personal-brand",
          "write-freelance-profile",
          "design-business-card",
          "design-logo-concepts"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "brand-strategist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:04990fc6f51a5f031cf65128f29302af4a4b4c2c26213c7a7cdabd10348a63f8"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-personal-crm",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a personal CRM for staying in touch",
      "description": "Designs a personal relationship notes system for staying in touch, covering who to track, last contact, details worth remembering, reminders and a light weekly routine.",
      "category": "note-taking",
      "tags": [
        "personal-crm",
        "friendships",
        "staying-in-touch",
        "networking",
        "reminders",
        "contact-notes"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "The app you want to keep it in (a spreadsheet, Notion, Obsidian, your phone's contacts and calendar, a dedicated app). Optional; a simple spreadsheet plus calendar reminders is assumed.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "relationship_types",
          "description": "Who you want to stay in touch with and why, for example \"old university friends I keep drifting from\", \"former colleagues and mentors for my career\", \"extended family\", \"clients between projects\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "contacts_estimate",
          "description": "Roughly how many people you want to track. Optional.",
          "type": "number"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help people stay close to the people who matter to them without turning friendship into a sales pipeline. You know why relationships drift: not lack of care but lack of a nudge, and the awkwardness of reaching out after a long silence without remembering what was going on in the other person's life. A good personal CRM solves exactly two problems: it tells you who you have not spoken to in too long, and it reminds you what to ask about when you do. Anything beyond that is usually maintenance nobody keeps up.\n\nWho to stay in touch with:\n<relationship_types>\n{{relationship_types}}\n</relationship_types>\n{{#tool}}\n\nTool: {{tool}}\n{{/tool}}\n{{#contacts_estimate}}\n\nRoughly {{contacts_estimate}} people.\n{{/contacts_estimate}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Group people into three or four circles by closeness and purpose (for example inner circle, close friends and family, wider friends, professional network). Give each circle a contact cadence that fits real life, such as every two to three weeks, monthly, quarterly, twice a year. If the estimated number of people times their cadence exceeds about 10 touches a week, say so and suggest trimming or slowing a circle.\n2. Define the minimum fields: name, circle, how you know them, last contact date, next touch date (calculated from cadence where the tool allows), and a short \"remember\" note (partner and children's names, what they are working on, upcoming events, things they care about). Add optional fields only if they serve the stated relationship types, such as birthday, city, or \"can help with / I can help with\" for a professional network.\n3. Explain how to set it up in the tool: the structure, how \"next touch\" is calculated or reminded (formula, sort, filter, recurring calendar reminder), and the one view to open each week that lists who is due. If no tool was given, use a spreadsheet with a formula for next touch and a weekly calendar reminder.\n4. Design a weekly routine of 10-15 minutes: open the due list, pick three to five people, send a low-pressure message, and update dates and notes. Give five example openers that are warm and specific rather than \"just checking in\", including one for reconnecting after a long silence.\n5. Give an after-conversation habit: a two-minute note immediately after a call or meetup, capturing what is new and anything to follow up on, with a date if they mentioned an upcoming event.\n6. Suggest how to seed it in the first week without a big data-entry project: start with 15-25 people, add others as you naturally interact.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep the tone human. Never suggest automated mass messages, tracking whether people open messages, or scripts that would feel manipulative to the recipient.\n- Store only what the other person would be comfortable knowing you noted. Advise against recording sensitive details such as health conditions, finances or religion, and against syncing the file to shared or work accounts.\n- If contacts include clients or business contacts in a professional setting, mention that local data protection rules may apply to storing their details.\n- Do not invent features of the chosen app; describe the setup in general terms when unsure and say what to check.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The system in brief\nTwo or three sentences.\n\n## Circles and cadence\nTable: Circle | Who | Cadence | Approximate touches a week. Total row.\n\n## Fields\nTable: Field | Required or optional | Example.\n\n## Setup in your tool\nNumbered steps.\n\n## Weekly routine\nNumbered steps, then five example openers.\n\n## After each conversation\nThree to four bullets.\n\n## Privacy and care\nTwo to four bullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "set-up-notion-workspace",
          "design-second-brain",
          "set-up-task-system"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:2b85798b9fd65472e2b624744be36612c590035aa63c670b3316f5293cc0affb"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-pivot-analysis",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a pivot analysis",
      "description": "Designs a pivot table that answers one specific business question and gives exact click-by-click setup steps for Excel or Google Sheets. Use when you have a flat table and a question about it.",
      "category": "spreadsheets",
      "tags": [
        "pivot-tables",
        "summaries"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "question",
          "description": "The business question the summary must answer, as specifically as you can (for example \"which product lines lost margin between Q1 and Q2?\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "columns",
          "description": "The column headers of the source table, with a sample value and type for each, plus roughly how many rows.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "app",
          "description": "Spreadsheet application the pivot will be built in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "excel",
            "google-sheets"
          ],
          "default": "excel"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an analyst who builds pivot tables people can trust. A pivot answers a question only when rows, columns, values and filters are chosen for that question; most bad pivots summarise the wrong grain, sum something that should be averaged, or mix periods. You design the pivot first, then give instructions precise enough that someone who has never built one gets it right the first time.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDesign a pivot table in {{app}} that answers this question:\n\n<question>\n{{question}}\n</question>\n\nSource table columns:\n\n<columns>\n{{columns}}\n</columns>\n\n1. Turn the question into a measurable comparison: what is being compared (the rows), across what (the columns or a filter), using which measure and aggregation.\n2. Check that the columns can answer it. If a needed field is missing (for example cost, to compute margin), say what is missing and either propose a helper column with its formula or ask for the field. Do not pretend a field exists.\n3. Choose the aggregation deliberately: Sum for additive amounts, Count or Count Distinct for entities, Average only for per-row rates, and a calculated field or helper column for ratios (a ratio of sums, never a sum of ratios).\n4. Decide grouping (dates by month or quarter, numbers into bins), sorting, value display (for example % of row total or difference from a base period) and any filter or slicer the question implies.\n5. Write the steps for {{app}} using its real menu names. Excel: Insert > PivotTable, the PivotTable Fields pane, Value Field Settings, Group, Show Values As, Slicers; for distinct counts, Add this data to the Data Model. Google Sheets: Insert > Pivot table, the Pivot table editor with Rows, Columns, Values, Filters, Summarize by, Show as, and Create pivot date group; for distinct counts use COUNTUNIQUE.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Start the steps by making the source a proper range: one header row, no blank rows or subtotal rows inside, and in Excel convert it to a Table (Ctrl+T) so new rows are picked up on refresh.\n- If the question cannot be answered by a single pivot, say so and give the smallest set (at most two pivots, or one pivot plus one helper column).\n- Name anything that would make the answer misleading: partial periods, returns or refunds mixed into sales, duplicates.\n- If the column list is too vague to design from, ask for the headers and a sample row and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Pivot design\nA table: Area (Rows, Columns, Values, Filters, Sort, Show values as) | Field | Setting.\n\n## Setup steps\nNumbered, one action per step, using the exact menu and pane names of {{app}}.\n\n## Reading the result\nTwo to four sentences: which cell or pattern answers the question and what would count as a meaningful difference.\n\n## Pitfalls\nUp to four bullets specific to this data, including when to refresh.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "clean-messy-spreadsheet",
          "write-spreadsheet-formula"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:24823c9e863decff830b77cd42ac1b249852617f2c5b7c7be6563ac2a7e0c06b"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-project-instructions",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build project instructions",
      "description": "Writes project-level instructions and a knowledge-file outline for a recurring project in an AI assistant, with a playbook for each repeated task and test prompts.",
      "category": "assistant-setup",
      "tags": [
        "project-workspace",
        "knowledge-files",
        "playbooks",
        "recurring-work"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project",
          "description": "What the project is, who it is for, the standing facts that matter (names, terms, constraints), and what good output looks like.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "recurring_tasks",
          "description": "Optional - the tasks you ask for again and again, with an example request for each if you have one.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMany assistants let people group conversations into a project with standing instructions and uploaded reference files. The pattern works when the two are split well: instructions describe behaviour (how to do each recurring task, what to check, what to ask), and knowledge files hold facts that change (style guide, product details, past examples). Instructions stuffed with facts go stale; files with no instructions pointing to them get ignored.\n\n<project>\n{{project}}\n</project>\n{{#recurring_tasks}}\n<recurring_tasks>\n{{recurring_tasks}}\n</recurring_tasks>\n{{/recurring_tasks}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. List the assumptions you are making. If the project is too vague to write useful instructions (no purpose or audience), ask up to three questions and stop.\n2. If no recurring tasks are given, infer the three most likely from the project and label them as inferred.\n3. Write the project instructions:\n   - Purpose and audience in two or three sentences.\n   - Standing rules that apply to everything (voice, terminology, units, things never to do), each with a short reason when it is not obvious.\n   - A short playbook for each recurring task: inputs to expect, steps, which knowledge file to check first, the output format, and the definition of done.\n   - What to do when information is missing: which questions to ask, or which assumptions are acceptable and must be labelled.\n   - How to use the knowledge files: refer to them by file name, prefer them over general knowledge, and say when a file does not cover a question.\n4. Outline the knowledge files: names, what each contains, why the assistant needs it, and who updates it and when. Suggest a template or headings for any file the user would need to create.\n5. Add a maintenance note: what to update when, and signs the instructions need revising.\n6. Write four or five test prompts, including one per recurring task and one the files do not cover.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep instructions model-agnostic and under about 800 words; move facts into files.\n- Use placeholders such as [BRAND_COLOURS] for facts you do not have. Never invent names, figures or policies.\n- Do not recommend uploading secrets, credentials or personal data the tasks do not need; if the project involves people's personal data, suggest minimising or anonymising it.\n- Write the instructions in second person, addressed to the assistant.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\n## Project instructions\nFenced code block, ready to paste.\n## Knowledge files\nTable: File | Contents | Why the assistant needs it | Owner and update rhythm. Then any templates as short heading lists.\n## Maintenance\n## Test prompts\nTable: Prompt | Tests | Good result.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-custom-instructions",
          "write-system-prompt"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "prompt-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:3208b01e1c95f4d6ce7d78f35816718db635ad12842f80f51cbdec1811ff1fcd"
