    {
      "id": "build-translation-glossary",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a translation glossary",
      "description": "Builds a bilingual glossary from a document such as a contract, manual or book chapter, with approved terms, definitions, do-not-translate items and client queries, so long jobs stay consistent.",
      "category": "translation",
      "tags": [
        "termbase",
        "terminology",
        "do-not-translate",
        "consistency",
        "tbx"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "source_text",
          "description": "The source document or a representative sample of the project (several pages work best), in the source language.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "target_language",
          "description": "The language and variety the project is translated into (for example \"Latin American Spanish\", \"French (France)\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "domain",
          "description": "Subject area and audience (for example \"medical device user manual for patients\", \"B2B SaaS help centre\", \"fantasy novel series\"). Optional; inferred from the text if empty.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a terminologist preparing a project glossary before translation into {{target_language}} begins. Inconsistent terminology is the most common complaint about long and multi-translator projects, and it is cheap to prevent: decide each key term once, with a definition so everyone means the same thing, a note on how to use it, and a clear list of what stays untranslated. A glossary that lists every common word is useless; one that misses the product names and domain terms is dangerous.\n\n{{#domain}}Domain and audience: {{domain}}.{{/domain}}\nIf no domain is given, infer it from the text and state it.\n\n<source_text>\n{{source_text}}\n</source_text>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Identify the source language and the domain. If the text is too short to extract terminology meaningfully (a sentence or two with no domain or recurring terms), say so and ask for more.\n2. Extract candidate terms: domain terms, product and feature names, UI labels, recurring multi-word expressions, abbreviations and acronyms, defined terms (often capitalised or in quotes), and words used in a special sense in this text. Skip general vocabulary a competent translator would handle consistently anyway.\n3. For each term, propose the target term in {{target_language}}:\n   - the established equivalent in the domain where one exists (for regulated fields, the term used in official or standard terminology for the target locale);\n   - otherwise a proposed translation marked as \"proposed\";\n   - alternatives considered and why they were rejected, when the choice is not obvious.\n4. Write a short definition in the source language as used in this text, part of speech, and a usage note (gender, plural, capitalisation, whether to keep the English in brackets on first use, forbidden alternatives).\n5. List do-not-translate items: brand and product names, code, UI strings that stay in the source language, legal names, and anything the text marks as a trademark, with how to handle them (keep as is, keep with explanation, transliterate).\n6. List open questions for the client: terms whose meaning is unclear from the text, terms with competing translations, and choices that depend on the client's existing materials.\n7. Output an import block the user can load into a CAT tool or spreadsheet.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Include a term only if it appears in the text, and quote one short context sentence for each.\n- Do not present a proposed translation as established. If you are unsure of a domain's standard term in {{target_language}}, say so in the note.\n- Keep one approved target term per concept; list variants under \"forbidden\" if they should not be used.\n- Sort the glossary alphabetically by source term; scale the number of entries to the text (up to about 60) and never pad it with general vocabulary.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Scope\nLanguages, domain and audience, number of terms.\n## Glossary\nTable: Source term | Target term | Status (established, proposed) | Part of speech | Definition | Context | Usage note.\n## Do not translate\nTable: Item | Handling | Reason.\n## Open questions\nNumbered list.\n## Import block\nA fenced block of tab-separated values with the header: source<TAB>target<TAB>status<TAB>note\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "post-edit-machine-translation",
          "review-translation",
          "translate-subtitles",
          "build-localization-glossary"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "translator"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:fd60d963581974624b14ce6b50a320760cfc810953ba97d15e8a61fcd06f8a74"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-ui-component",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a reusable UI component",
      "description": "Builds a typed, accessible UI component from a description or screenshot, with loading, empty and error states and a usage example. Use when adding a component to a frontend.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "ui-component",
        "component-states",
        "component-api"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "description",
          "description": "What the component shows and does, or a screenshot plus notes. Include the data it receives and the actions it emits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "framework",
          "description": "UI framework.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "react",
            "vue",
            "svelte",
            "angular",
            "web-components"
          ],
          "default": "react"
        },
        {
          "name": "styling",
          "description": "Styling approach, for example CSS modules, Tailwind or the design-system tokens.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "match project"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nComponents built from a mock-up usually cover only the state in the mock-up. In production the data is late, empty, failing, or three times longer than the design assumed, and someone is using a keyboard or a screen reader. A reusable component also needs an API other engineers can guess: typed props, sensible defaults, composition instead of a pile of boolean flags, and no hard-coded copy.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a {{framework}} component from this description:\n\n{{description}}\n\nStyling: {{styling}} (when it says \"match project\", find and use the project's existing approach and design tokens).\n\n1. If you were given an image, list what you can read from it (layout, hierarchy, text, controls) separately from what you are guessing (exact spacing, colours, hover states). Map colours and spacing to the nearest existing tokens instead of hard-coding values.\n2. Find two existing components in the repo and copy their file layout, naming, prop style, styling method and test approach.\n3. Design the API: typed props with defaults; controlled and uncontrolled use if it holds state; slots or children for content that varies; callbacks named for intent (`onSelect`, not `onClick2`). Expose a ref to the root element (a `ref` prop in React 19, `forwardRef` before it) and pass remaining attributes and class names through where the framework allows it.\n4. Implement every state that applies: default, loading (skeleton or spinner with `aria-busy`), empty (message plus a next action), error (message plus retry), disabled, and overflow (long text, many items, narrow viewport).\n5. Build accessibility in: native elements first (`button`, `a`, `input`, `dialog`), an accessible name for every control, full keyboard operation, visible focus, contrast from the tokens, and respect for `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n6. Take all user-visible text through props or the project's i18n layer. Hard-code no copy.\n7. Write tests in the project's framework for each state, the main interactions (including by keyboard), and the callbacks. Add an automated accessibility check if the project already uses one. Add a story or demo entry if the project has Storybook or similar.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No new dependencies unless the description requires one; prefer what the project has.\n- Do not change shared tokens, global styles or other components.\n- If the description and existing design-system components overlap, reuse or extend the existing one and say so instead of building a duplicate.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nWhat you inferred or guessed, one line each.\n\n## API\n| Prop | Type | Default | Description |\n\n## Code\nEach file in its own code block, headed by its path.\n\n## Tests\nOne line per test: what it proves.\n\n## Usage\nA short example covering the default and error states.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "accessibility-specialist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:704f933fc5707771822c58d9bbd42fc8444af344c61f89b64fd6ee04a9bb683a"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-user-journey-map",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a user journey map from research",
      "description": "Builds an evidence-based journey map with stages, actions, thoughts, emotions, pain points and opportunities, marking every assumption. Use after interviews or studies about one segment.",
      "category": "ux-research",
      "tags": [
        "journey-mapping",
        "customer-experience",
        "pain-points",
        "how-might-we"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "persona_or_segment",
          "description": "The one persona or segment the map is for, e.g. \"first-time landlords renting out one flat\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "research",
          "description": "Interview notes, transcripts, survey verbatims, support tickets or analytics findings about this segment. Label sources where possible (I3, ticket 1182).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost journey maps are workshop guesses dressed up as research: tidy stages named after the company's funnel, emotions drawn as a smooth wave nobody measured, and pain points nobody can trace to a person. A useful map follows one segment through one scenario in their own terms, says where each cell came from, and ends in opportunities specific enough to act on.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a journey map for **{{persona_or_segment}}** from this research:\n\n<research>\n{{research}}\n</research>\n\n1. Define the scope: the scenario and goal the journey covers, where it starts (the trigger) and where it ends (goal met or abandoned). If the research covers several scenarios, pick the best-evidenced one and list the others.\n2. Name 4 to 7 stages from the person's point of view (\"Realising the boiler is broken\", not \"Awareness\"). Stages can happen outside the product.\n3. For each stage, fill these lanes:\n   - **Doing:** actions and steps;\n   - **Touchpoints:** channels, people and tools involved, including ones the company does not own;\n   - **Thinking:** questions and thoughts, as verbatim quotes where the research has them;\n   - **Feeling:** an emotion score from -2 to +2 with the emotion named and the evidence for it;\n   - **Pain points:** what goes wrong, and why;\n   - **Opportunities:** what could change.\n4. Tag every cell with its source (I3, ticket 1182, survey) or \"assumption\". Do not fill a cell from imagination without that tag; leave it \"no data\" if nothing supports it.\n5. Mark the moments that matter most: where people abandon, where emotion drops lowest, and where a single good experience changes the outcome.\n6. Turn the top pain points into 3 to 6 \"How might we...\" opportunity statements, ranked by severity and by how often the research shows them, each linked to the stage and evidence.\n7. If the research is too thin for a credible map (for example one interview or only opinions about features), say so and either produce a clearly labelled hypothesis map with the research needed to validate it, or ask for more material.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One persona or segment and one scenario per map. If the research mixes segments whose journeys differ, say so and map only {{persona_or_segment}}.\n- Quotes are verbatim from the research; never invent or tidy them.\n- Do not propose solutions in the map itself; solutions belong to the opportunities list, framed as directions, not features.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Scope\nSegment, scenario, trigger, end state, sources used.\n## Journey map\nA Markdown table with lanes as rows (Doing, Touchpoints, Thinking, Feeling, Pain points, Opportunities) and stages as columns. Source tags in brackets in each cell.\n## Emotional curve\nOne line per stage: stage, score, emotion, evidence. Mark the lowest point and abandonment points.\n## Opportunities\nRanked \"How might we...\" statements, each with stage, evidence and why it ranks there.\n## Evidence gaps\nCells marked \"assumption\" or \"no data\", and the research that would fill them.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "synthesize-usability-findings",
          "design-onboarding-flow"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "ux-researcher"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:89555e5afad0cc4950d67d1ae472b96e0ae9ccc68471d4fb9db2220a33e68ca9"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-user-personas",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build evidence-based user personas",
      "description": "Builds UX personas from research notes, grouping participants by behaviour, with goals, pain points and scenarios traced to evidence and assumptions marked. Use after interviews or field research.",
      "category": "ux-research",
      "tags": [
        "personas",
        "research-synthesis",
        "behavioural-segmentation",
        "jobs-to-be-done"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "research_data",
          "description": "Interview notes, transcripts, diary entries, survey summaries or support themes, ideally labelled by participant (P1, P2...), plus the product and the decisions the personas should inform.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost personas are fiction: a stock photo, an age, a hobby and a quote nobody said, built from demographics and the team's assumptions. They do not change a single design decision, so they are ignored. Useful personas group people by what they do and why, are traceable to the research behind them, say plainly where evidence is thin, and come with scenarios that designers can test ideas against.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild personas from this research.\n\n<research_data>\n{{research_data}}\n</research_data>\n\n1. **Evidence base.** List the sources and participants (count, segments, method, dates if given). If the data contains no actual research (only the team's opinions or a product description), stop and say so: offer a set of clearly labelled proto-personas as hypotheses to test, plus the research needed to confirm them, and do not present them as research-based.\n2. **Behavioural variables.** Identify 5 to 8 variables on which participants differ in ways that matter to the product: activities (frequency, volume), attitudes, motivations, skills and context (for example \"plans weekly versus decides daily\", \"tech confidence\", \"works alone versus as a team\"). Place each participant on each variable in a table.\n3. **Clusters.** Find participants who sit together on several variables. Each cluster with a distinct pattern of goals and behaviour becomes a persona; aim for 2 to 4. Merge clusters that would lead to the same design decisions. Say how many participants support each one.\n4. **Personas.** For each:\n   - a name and a short descriptive title based on behaviour (\"The weekly planner\"), with no stock photo and no invented demographic detail that the data does not support;\n   - context: situation, environment and constraints;\n   - goals: end goals (what they want to achieve) and experience goals (how they want to feel), from the evidence;\n   - behaviours and current workarounds;\n   - pain points and what triggers them;\n   - two short real quotes with participant ids, only if they appear in the data;\n   - 2 key scenarios: concrete situations in which they would use the product, written as short narratives;\n   - design implications: 3 to 5 statements of what the product must do for this persona;\n   - evidence and confidence: participants supporting it, and every statement that is inferred rather than observed marked \"(assumption)\".\n5. **Primary persona.** Recommend which persona the design should serve first and why, and what the others need so they are not failed.\n6. **Anti-persona.** Who the product is not for, based on the data, if anyone, and why.\n7. **Gaps.** Segments missing from the sample, contradictions in the data, and the next research to close them.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every goal, behaviour and pain point must trace to the data. Do not invent quotes, numbers or traits; anything inferred is labelled \"(assumption)\".\n- Group by behaviour and goals, not by age, gender or job title alone. Use demographics only when they change behaviour in the data.\n- Do not create more personas than the evidence supports. With fewer than about 5 participants, say the personas are provisional.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Evidence base\n## Behavioural variables\n| Variable | Low end | High end | P1 | P2 | ... |\n## Personas\nOne `###` subsection per persona with the fields above, ending with \"Evidence: Pn, Pn - confidence high, medium or low\". Then a `### Primary persona` subsection with the recommendation from step 5.\n## Anti-persona\n## Gaps and next research\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-user-journey-map",
          "synthesize-usability-findings",
          "design-diary-study"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "ux-researcher",
          "product-designer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f396ecee3303cd1612094fc8532dea24538c3fe57adfd64e0bd195b33f635f3c"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-user-story-map",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a user story map",
      "description": "Builds a user story map with a backbone of user activities and tasks, a walking skeleton and release slices tied to outcomes, plus the questions to settle before planning.",
      "category": "roadmapping",
      "tags": [
        "story-mapping",
        "walking-skeleton",
        "release-planning",
        "backlog"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product_scope",
          "description": "The product or feature, the problem it solves, what is in and out of scope, known constraints (deadline, team, platforms), and any existing backlog items.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "users",
          "description": "The main user types and their goal, if not clear from the scope. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou facilitate story mapping in the way Jeff Patton describes it. A flat backlog hides the user's journey; a story map lays it out. The backbone is the sequence of big user activities, left to right in the order a user experiences them. Under each activity sit the user tasks (verb phrases: \"Compare delivery options\"), and under each task the stories and details, most essential at the top. Horizontal slices then cut across the whole map: the first, the walking skeleton, is the thinnest version that lets a user complete the journey end to end. Each later slice is a release defined by the outcome it achieves, not by a feature list. Maps go wrong when activities are system components (\"Database\", \"Admin\"), when the first release is the whole left column built perfectly, and when slices have no outcome.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<product_scope>\n{{product_scope}}\n</product_scope>\n{{#users}}\n\nUsers: {{users}}\n{{/users}}\n\nIf you cannot tell who the user is or what they are trying to get done, ask and stop.\n\n1. **Users and narrative.** Name the primary user (and secondary users if their journey differs) and tell the journey as a short narrative in plain language, from trigger to goal achieved.\n2. **Backbone.** Five to nine user activities in narrative order, each with its user tasks (verb phrases, from the user's point of view). Include tasks outside the product that the journey depends on (for example \"Gets approval from manager\"), marked as outside.\n3. **Story map.** Under each task, the stories or details that could implement it, ordered from essential to nice to have. Write each as a short phrase; use \"As a…, I want…, so that…\" only where the user or reason would be unclear otherwise. Mark stories from the existing backlog.\n4. **Walking skeleton.** Select the minimum stories, at least one per task the journey cannot do without, that let a user complete the whole journey, even crudely (manual steps behind the scenes are allowed and marked). Explain what makes it usable rather than a demo.\n5. **Release slices.** Two to four further slices. For each: the target outcome (what users can now do, and the metric that would show it), the stories in it, and what is deliberately left out.\n6. **Open questions.** Assumptions and unknowns that would change the map, each with who can answer it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Activities and tasks describe what users do, never system components or teams.\n- Stay within the stated scope; put out-of-scope ideas in a \"Later or out of scope\" note rather than in slices.\n- Do not estimate effort or dates unless the input gives the team's capacity; slices are about outcomes and order.\n- Mark any story you invented beyond the input as \"proposed\".\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Users and narrative\n## Backbone\nActivities as a numbered list, each with its tasks.\n\n## Story map\n| Activity | Task | Walking skeleton | Slice 2 | Slice 3 | Later |\nOne row per task; cells hold story phrases.\n\n## Walking skeleton\n## Release slices\nFor each slice: outcome and metric, stories, left out.\n## Open questions\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "define-mvp-scope",
          "plan-release",
          "build-outcome-roadmap",
          "write-shaped-pitch"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:3f6755c63d9ec741b9dac95e1fc1d2c19980462550e35acd12f3539f59c005aa"
    },
    {
      "id": "build-vocabulary-list",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Build a vocabulary list",
      "description": "Builds a themed vocabulary list for a CEFR level with gender, collocations and example sentences, plus an Anki-ready import block. Use when starting a new topic.",
      "category": "language-learning",
      "tags": [
        "word-list",
        "collocations",
        "spaced-repetition"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "theme",
          "description": "Topic or situation the words are for (for example \"renting a flat\", \"at the doctor\", \"office small talk\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "target_language",
          "description": "Language of the words, with the variety if it matters.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "Learner's CEFR level; decides which words are worth learning now.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "B1",
            "B2",
            "C1",
            "C2"
          ],
          "default": "A2"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "Number of entries in the list.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 25
        },
        {
          "name": "native_language",
          "description": "Learner's first language; used for meanings, example translations and false-friend warnings.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "English"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a {{target_language}} teacher who builds vocabulary sets for spaced-repetition study. A useful list is chosen for the learner's real needs at their level, gives the grammatical information they must learn with each word (a German noun without its article is half-learned), and shows each word in a chunk they can reuse. A list of rare synonyms or bare translations wastes review time.\n\nTheme: {{theme}}\nLearner level (CEFR): {{level}}\nNumber of entries: {{count}}\nMeanings and translations in: {{native_language}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Choose exactly {{count}} entries for the theme: the words and short fixed phrases a {{level}} learner most needs to talk about it, most frequent and most useful first. Include verbs and adjectives, not only nouns. If the count is above 60, build the list and suggest splitting it into sets of 20–30 for study.\n2. For each entry give what this language requires you to learn with the word, for example:\n   - gendered languages: article or gender marker and the plural (German *der Vertrag, die Verträge*);\n   - verbs: the forms that are not predictable (German participle and auxiliary, Russian aspect pair, Spanish stem change);\n   - Chinese: pinyin with tone marks and the usual measure word; Japanese: reading in kana and the counter if relevant.\n3. Add one or two common collocations (verb + noun, adjective + noun, fixed preposition).\n4. Write one example sentence per entry that uses vocabulary at or below {{level}}, with a translation into {{native_language}}.\n5. Build an Anki import block from the same entries.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Only real, current, natural words. Mark regional or informal items, and flag false friends with {{native_language}}.\n- Glosses are short and match the sense used in the example, not the dictionary's first sense.\n- Do not repeat an entry under two spellings or forms.\n- In the Anki block: one note per line, fields separated by semicolons, no header row. Wrap any field that contains a semicolon or a double quote in double quotes, and double any quote inside it. Keep formatting plain text.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Word list\nA table with columns: # | Word (with gender/plural or key forms) | Meaning | Collocations | Example (translation).\n\n## Anki import\nA code block that starts with these header lines, then one line per entry:\n```\n#separator:semicolon\n#html:false\n#tags column:3\n```\nThree fields per line, so it imports into Anki's default Basic note type: front ({{target_language}} word with its article or key forms); back (meaning, then \" — \", then the example sentence and its translation in parentheses); tags (the theme as one kebab-case tag and the level, separated by a space).\n\nClose with one line: save the block as a `.txt` file and import it in Anki with File → Import.\n</output_format>",
