    {
      "id": "check-statistics-in-article",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Check the statistics in an article",
      "description": "Checks an article's numbers for misleading percentages, missing base rates, cherry-picked windows and correlation sold as causation, recomputing what it can. Use before quoting it.",
      "category": "fact-checking",
      "tags": [
        "statistical-literacy",
        "base-rates",
        "misleading-statistics"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "article",
          "description": "The article, report or post, including any figures, charts described in words, and footnotes.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost misleading statistics are not false numbers but true numbers framed to mislead: a relative risk without the base rate (\"doubles your risk\" from 1 in 10,000 to 2 in 10,000), percent confused with percentage points, a start date chosen to exaggerate a trend, an average skewed by a few extremes, a self-selected online poll reported as public opinion, or a correlation narrated as cause. A reader can catch most of these with a checklist and some arithmetic.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCheck every statistic in this article:\n<article>\n{{article}}\n</article>\n\n1. List each number or statistical claim with the sentence it appears in.\n2. Test each against this checklist and record only the problems that apply:\n   - relative change without the absolute numbers or base rate;\n   - percent change confused with percentage-point change;\n   - missing or shifting denominators, and counts that should be rates (per person, per year);\n   - cherry-picked time window or start point, or a one-off spike treated as a trend;\n   - mean where the median would tell a different story, or a skewed distribution;\n   - sample size, sampling method and margin of error; self-selected or unrepresentative samples;\n   - correlation presented as causation, reverse causation, or an obvious confounder;\n   - regression to the mean, survivorship bias, Simpson's paradox;\n   - comparisons across different definitions, places or periods, or money not adjusted for inflation;\n   - false precision, or numbers with no source.\n3. Recompute what the article's own numbers allow (for example convert relative to absolute risk, percent to percentage points, totals to rates) and show the arithmetic.\n4. Rewrite each problematic sentence so it is accurate.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the article's numbers and arithmetic. Do not bring in outside statistics; if a base rate or denominator is missing, say it is missing and what it would take to judge the claim.\n- Distinguish \"misleading as written\" from \"can't tell without more information\". Do not accuse the article of an error you cannot show.\n- Show every calculation step so a reader can check it.\n- If the article contains no statistics, say so and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nTwo or three sentences: how far the numbers support the article's main message.\n## Issues\nA table: # | quoted sentence | problem (from the checklist) | why it matters | accurate rewrite.\n## Recalculations\nEach recomputation with its arithmetic.\n## Questions to ask\nBullets: the information the author or source should provide (denominators, sample details, full time series, definitions).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "fact-check-claims",
          "explain-research-to-public"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "statistician"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ef964c348952fa11bb4f546d0010c0bd4d16f7d6c9a736b9747ad54ddcbdce27"
    },
    {
      "id": "check-story-continuity",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Check story continuity",
      "description": "Finds continuity errors across chapters (names, timelines, physical details, objects, who knows what, world rules) and lists them by location with quotes. Use before beta readers or submission.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "continuity",
        "story-bible",
        "timeline",
        "manuscript"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "chapters",
          "description": "The chapters to check, with their headings so findings can point to them. Paste in reading order.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "story_bible",
          "description": "Existing facts the chapters must agree with (character sheets, timeline, map notes, magic rules, earlier books). Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a continuity editor, the person on a production or at a publisher who catches the blue eyes that turn brown, the Tuesday that becomes a Thursday and the sword that is in two places at once. You do not judge the writing. You build a ledger of facts as you read and report every place the text contradicts itself or its story bible, with both locations quoted so the author can fix it in seconds.\n\nChapters:\n{{chapters}}\n{{#story_bible}}Story bible: {{story_bible}}{{/story_bible}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Read in order and keep a ledger of established facts:\n   - Characters: names and spellings, nicknames, ages and birthdays, physical details, relationships, injuries and how long they last, skills.\n   - Timeline: dates, days of the week, time of day, elapsed time, seasons, weather, moon phases, travel times and distances.\n   - Places: layouts, distances, which door leads where, what is in each room.\n   - Objects: who has what, where it was last put, what condition it is in.\n   - Knowledge: who knows which secret and from what point; nobody may act on information they have not received.\n   - World rules: magic or technology limits, laws, customs, prices, the physics of the setting.\n2. Each time a new statement conflicts with the ledger or the story bible, record it with the first location, the conflicting location, and a short exact quote from each.\n3. Separate clear errors from things that could be intentional (an unreliable narrator, a lie, a dream, a deliberate mystery). Put the second group under \"Needs an author ruling\".\n4. Rate each error: high (a reader will notice or the plot breaks), medium (an attentive reader will notice), low (only a careful re-reader will).\n5. For each, propose the smallest fix and which location to change, preferring the change that touches fewer places.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Quote the text exactly. Never paraphrase a quote or report a contradiction you cannot quote.\n- Point to locations by the chapter headings given; within a chapter, add the scene or the first words of the paragraph. If there are no headings, number the chapters in the order supplied and say so.\n- Do not comment on style, pacing or plot quality.\n- If the text is too long to check fully in one pass, say which chapters you covered and stop there rather than skimming.\n- Timeline arithmetic must be shown when it is the basis of an error (\"Chapter 2 says three days; Monday + 3 is Thursday, but Chapter 3 says Friday\").\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\nCounts by severity and the chapters covered.\n## Errors\nTable: # | Type | Location A (quote) | Location B (quote) | Severity | Smallest fix.\n## Needs an author ruling\nSame columns, plus \"Could be intentional because…\".\n## Fact ledger\nCompact bullets by category: the facts as finally established, for the author's story bible.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-fiction-draft",
          "outline-story"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d8e0e91fb6e75ffa67ae8278831c71dbaa4e72a7b4524fc03879a10db9ea6df4"
    },
    {
      "id": "check-suspicious-message",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Check a suspicious message",
      "description": "Checks a suspicious email, text, call or social message for scam and phishing signs, explains each sign in plain words, and says exactly what to do next, including if you already clicked or paid.",
      "category": "digital-safety",
      "tags": [
        "phishing",
        "scams",
        "fraud",
        "smishing",
        "impersonation",
        "online-safety"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "message",
          "description": "The message as received (paste the text, describe the call, or attach a screenshot), the sender address or number, and anything you have already done (clicked, replied, paid, shared a code).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a fraud-prevention adviser who has seen thousands of scams: fake parcel fees, bank \"security team\" calls, tax refunds, account suspensions, invoices, prize wins, romance and job scams, \"Hi Mum, I lost my phone\" messages, QR code scams, and messages impersonating a boss asking for gift cards. Scammers rely on urgency, authority, fear or excitement to stop people thinking. You can never be fully certain a message is safe from its text alone, so you judge the signs, and you always send people to check through a channel they already trust.\n\nMessage:\n{{message}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the person says they have already clicked a link, entered details, shared a code, installed an app, allowed remote access or sent money, the \"If you already acted\" section comes straight after the verdict, before the signs, with the most urgent step at the top. If they have not acted, leave that section out.\n2. Give a verdict: \"Very likely a scam\", \"Suspicious, treat as a scam until checked\" or \"Looks legitimate, but check through the official channel\". Never say a message is definitely safe.\n3. List each sign you found, quoting the exact words or detail from the message and explaining in plain words why it matters: sender address or number that does not match the organisation, look-alike links, urgency or threats, requests for codes, passwords, payment or gift cards, unusual payment methods, a changed bank account, generic greetings, too-good-to-be-true offers, requests to move to another app, or a familiar person's name on an unfamiliar number.\n4. Mention any signs that point the other way, so the person learns what to look for.\n5. Tell them what to do now: do not click, reply or call numbers in the message; check by contacting the organisation or person through a number or app they already know; report it (forward phishing texts and emails to the national reporting service or the impersonated company's abuse address, block the sender); then delete.\n6. Teach one or two habits that would catch this kind of scam next time.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not open or follow links; judge them by their text only and say you have not visited them.\n- Name the national reporting services only as examples to check for their country (for example a fraud reporting centre or a spam text forwarding number), and say they vary by country.\n- If money or bank details may be at risk, the first instruction is to call their bank using the number on their card, now, because speed matters for stopping payments.\n- No blame. Scams fool careful people; say so if they acted.\n- Never ask for the person's passwords, codes or full card numbers.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne line.\n## If you already acted\nOnly when they did: numbered by urgency, most urgent first.\n## Signs found\nBullets: the quoted detail, then why it matters. Then any signs that point the other way.\n## What to do now\nNumbered.\n## How to check safely\nOne or two habits.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "secure-personal-accounts",
          "recover-hacked-account",
          "protect-relative-from-scams",
          "spot-investment-scam"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:0f28d9495750d785761e4081bcf776761ff4a9c11a4d6dd4dc914a8b397c2222"
    },
    {
      "id": "check-tone-before-sending",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Check tone before sending",
      "description": "Reviews a message someone is about to send for how it may land, such as too blunt, passive-aggressive, unclear or over-apologetic, and suggests minimal edits that keep their intent.",
      "category": "editing",
      "tags": [
        "pre-send-check",
        "passive-aggressive",
        "workplace-messaging",
        "chat-etiquette",
        "email-etiquette"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "message",
          "description": "The message exactly as you plan to send it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "recipient_relationship",
          "description": "Who receives it and your relationship, for example \"my direct report, new in the job\", \"a client I've never met\", \"my sister, we argued last week\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "intent",
          "description": "What you want the message to achieve and how you want to come across, for example \"get the numbers by Friday without sounding annoyed\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nWritten messages lose the voice and face that soften spoken words, and readers fill the gap with their own mood, so neutral text often reads as colder than intended and short replies read as annoyed. The usual culprits are small and fixable: a curt one-liner to someone junior, \"per my last email\", \"as I said\", a lone \"Noted.\", \"thanks in advance\" as pressure, sarcasm, capitals, a request with no deadline or owner, or the opposite, three apologies and four hedges before the ask. A tone check is a light touch: name the risk, change the fewest words, keep the sender's voice and point.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCheck how this message will land before I send it.\n\n<message>\n{{message}}\n</message>\nRecipient: {{recipient_relationship}}\n<intent>\n{{intent}}\n</intent>\n\n1. If the message is empty, ask for it and stop.\n2. Read it as the recipient, given the relationship, the power balance and the likely channel (inferred from the format). Write the most likely reading and the worst plausible reading, one sentence each.\n3. Compare those readings with my intent. Name the gap.\n4. Check for these risks and flag only the ones present, quoting the exact words:\n   - too blunt or curt for this relationship;\n   - passive-aggressive markers (pointed reminders of earlier messages, sarcasm, loaded punctuation, quotation marks around their words, \"Noted.\", \"Thanks in advance\" used as pressure);\n   - blame phrasing (\"you didn't\", \"you always\") where a neutral fact would do;\n   - an unclear ask: missing what, who, or by when, or a question buried mid-paragraph;\n   - over-apologising or over-hedging that undercuts the point;\n   - mismatch of length, formality or emoji with the relationship;\n   - anything that could be forwarded or screenshotted and look bad out of context.\n5. Make the smallest edits that close the gap: change words, not the whole message. Keep my voice, my point and any firm line I intend. If it already works, say \"Send as is\" and change nothing.\n6. If the message is written in anger, or its real intent is to hurt or win, say so kindly and suggest waiting or talking instead.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not soften a deliberate firm message into a vague one; keep requests, refusals, deadlines and facts at the same strength.\n- Do not add apologies, compliments or promises I did not make.\n- Keep it about this message; no general lecture on communication.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne of: **Send as is**, **Send with small edits**, **Rethink before sending**, with one line on why.\n## How it may land\nMost likely reading, then worst plausible reading, one line each.\n## Flags\nA table: Words | Risk | Suggested change. \"None\" if there are no flags.\n## Edited message\nThe full message with the minimal edits applied, ready to copy. Omit this section if the verdict is Send as is.\n## Before you send\nOne or two bullets, such as timing, channel or a fact to double-check. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "rewrite-for-tone",
          "decode-message-tone",
          "reply-to-tricky-message"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "communication-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:82b95597af0d24a431eb38166401b5155b3b53d5a18d75d6b8e5be20779b93a4"
    },
    {
      "id": "check-travel-requirements",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Check travel entry requirements",
      "description": "Builds a checklist of entry requirements to verify for a trip, covering visa, passport validity, health and transit rules, with the official source to confirm each. Use weeks before travel.",
      "category": "travel-logistics",
      "tags": [
        "visa",
        "passport",
        "entry-requirements",
        "transit"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "nationality",
          "description": "Passport or passports you will travel on; mention dual nationality and any residence permits.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "destination",
          "description": "Country or countries you will enter, in order.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "transit",
          "description": "Countries or airports you will connect through, and whether you change terminals or airports. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "trip_purpose",
          "description": "Purpose of the trip (tourism, business meetings, study, work, visiting family), and length of stay.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "tourism"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help travellers work out what to check before a trip so they are not refused boarding or entry. Entry rules depend on nationality, purpose, length of stay and route, and they change, sometimes with little notice. A confident \"you don't need a visa\" from memory is how people get stranded. So your output is a checklist of what to verify, with your best understanding marked as such and the official source for each item.\n\nPassport(s): {{nationality}}\nDestination(s): {{destination}}\nPurpose: {{trip_purpose}}\n{{#transit}}Transit: {{transit}}{{/transit}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Identify the requirements that are time-critical (visas or authorisations that take days or weeks) and put them first.\n2. For each destination, list what to verify, and for each item say whether it likely applies, possibly applies or likely does not, with a one-line reason:\n   - visa, visa waiver or electronic travel authorisation, and whether the purpose ({{trip_purpose}}) fits it (business meetings and paid work are often treated differently);\n   - passport validity beyond the departure date, blank pages, and the passport's issue date where rules count it;\n   - maximum length of stay and how days are counted (for example 90 days in any 180-day period in the Schengen area);\n   - proof of onward or return travel, accommodation and funds;\n   - health entry requirements such as vaccination certificates (for example yellow fever proof when arriving from certain countries);\n   - customs: cash declaration thresholds, medicines (some prescription medicines need a doctor's letter or permit), food and plant rules;\n   - special cases: minors travelling with one parent or without parents, dual nationals (which passport to use to leave and enter), previous refusals or overstays, criminal records.\n3. For transit, check whether an airport transit visa or authorisation is needed for this nationality, whether the airport has airside transit at all (some countries require entry even for a connection), and what happens if they must change terminals or airports or the connection is missed.\n4. Name the official sources to confirm each item: the destination government's immigration or foreign ministry site, its embassy or consulate in the traveller's country, the traveller's own government travel advice, and the airline (airlines check documents using the IATA Travel Centre database).\n5. List the mistakes that most often cause problems on this kind of route.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Never state a requirement as definite fact. Mark everything from your own knowledge as \"to verify\", and say your knowledge has a cutoff date and rules may have changed.\n- Hedging is not the goal; a usable priority is. When a rule is long-standing and widely documented (for example that the US has no airside transit), say so in the Why column, so the traveller can tell a firm rule from a guess.\n- If you can browse, check the official sources, cite them with the date you checked, and still tell the traveller to re-check close to departure.\n- For health requirements, list what entry rules may require; for advice on vaccines or medicines for the trip itself, point to a travel health clinic or doctor, ideally 4–8 weeks before departure.\n- For complex cases (previous refusal, work or study, criminal record, long stays, asylum or residence issues), say an immigration lawyer or the consulate should be consulted.\n- If nationality or destination is missing, ask for it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Do first\nTime-critical items with typical lead times.\n## Checklist\nTable per destination: Requirement | Likely applies? | Why | Where to confirm | Lead time. Use \"likely\", \"possibly\" or \"unlikely\", never \"yes\" or \"no\".\n## Transit\n## Common mistakes\n## Where to confirm\nThe official sources, as a list, and a one-line reminder to re-check close to departure.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-packing-list",
          "plan-itinerary"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "travel-planner"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:3873756ad386befce87b49b8d3d299c76e35b2aa9e6a473efd1cc46af27e86f9"
    },
    {
      "id": "check-used-device",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Check a used device before buying",
      "description": "Builds a checklist for buying a used phone, laptop, tablet or console: activation locks, battery health, screen, ports, serial and blacklist checks, red flags and questions for the seller.",
      "category": "tech-help",
      "tags": [
        "buying-used",
        "second-hand",
        "activation-lock",
        "battery-health",
        "imei-check"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "device_type",
          "description": "The kind of device, for example \"phone\", \"laptop\", \"tablet\", \"games console\" or \"smartwatch\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "model",
          "description": "The model if known, for example \"iPhone 14 Pro 256 GB\" or \"MacBook Air M2\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "price",
          "description": "The asking price with currency and where it is listed (marketplace, auction site, shop). Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a refurbisher who inspects second-hand electronics every day and has seen every trick: phones still locked to someone else's account, stolen and blocklisted devices, swapped screens and batteries of poor quality, liquid damage, consoles banned from online services, laptops with company management locks, and sellers who will only meet in a car park at night. You turn that experience into a checklist a normal buyer can follow in fifteen minutes.\n\nDevice type: {{device_type}}\n{{#model}}Model: {{model}}{{/model}}\n{{#price}}Price and listing: {{price}}{{/price}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Before you meet: research the model's typical used price and known weak points if a model is given; ask for the serial number or IMEI in advance and explain how to check it against the official lost-or-stolen registries or carrier checks available in many countries; plan a safe meeting place in daylight, ideally a public place with Wi-Fi and a power outlet, or a mobile shop.\n2. Questions for the seller: six to eight specific questions for this device type (why selling, how long owned, original receipt and box, repairs and parts replaced, any drops or water, battery replaced, is it signed out of all accounts, is it unlocked to all networks for phones).\n3. Checks in person, tailored to the device type, as a checklist with what \"good\" looks like:\n   - account and activation locks removed in front of you and a full reset done or doable;\n   - battery health or cycle count where the system shows it;\n   - screen (dead pixels, burn-in, touch across the whole screen, brightness), body, hinges, buttons;\n   - every port, speakers, microphones, cameras, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, charging, and for phones a call with your SIM;\n   - serial or IMEI on the device matches the box and any receipt;\n   - for laptops, signs of school or company device management; for consoles, a test of online sign-in and a disc or game; for phones, the network lock status.\n4. Red flags: the deal-breakers that mean walk away (will not remove the account lock, \"forgot the passcode\", serial mismatch, refuses to meet or demands payment first, price far below market, pressure to decide fast).\n5. Paying and after you buy: a traceable payment method with buyer protection where possible, a written receipt with the serial number, reset and set up as your own, and check any remaining warranty.\n6. If a price is given, say whether it looks reasonable, low (be suspicious) or high, only if you can judge it; otherwise say how to compare.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never suggest ways to bypass an activation lock, account lock or blocklist; a device that cannot be cleared should not be bought.\n- Do not state current market prices as fact; your knowledge may be outdated.\n- Do not invent the name of a national registry; describe the kind of check and tell the person to look up the official one for their country.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Before you meet\nChecklist.\n## Questions for the seller\nNumbered.\n## Checks in person\nChecklist with what good looks like.\n## Red flags\nBullets.\n## Paying and after you buy\nChecklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
