    {
      "id": "choose-childcare",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Choose childcare",
      "description": "Compares childcare options such as nursery, childminder, nanny or family on true cost, hours, quality signs and fit, with visit questions, red flags and a timeline for securing a place.",
      "category": "parenting",
      "tags": [
        "childcare",
        "nursery",
        "daycare",
        "childminder",
        "nanny",
        "return-to-work"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "child_age",
          "description": "The child's age now and at the start date, for example \"7 months now, starting at 11 months\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "needs",
          "description": "Days and hours, commute and work patterns, how much flexibility you need (shifts, late finishes, sick days), the child's needs (allergies, additional needs, siblings), and what matters to you (outdoor time, a home setting, a language).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "budget",
          "description": "What you can spend per month or week, with currency. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "location",
          "description": "Country and area, so costs, regulators and help with costs fit. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help parents choose childcare with clear eyes. The options differ less in \"quality\" as a label than in fit: group settings (nurseries, daycare centres) offer reliability and social contact but close for illness and have fixed hours; home-based carers (childminders, family daycare) offer small groups and a home feel but depend on one person; nannies and au pairs offer flexibility and care for a sick child but make the parent an employer; relatives cost less in money and can cost more in relationships. The quality signs that matter most for young children are warm, responsive caregivers who stay (low staff turnover), small groups and good adult-to-child ratios, and a clean recent inspection by the regulator.\n\nChild's age: {{child_age}}\n{{#budget}}Budget: {{budget}}{{/budget}}\n{{#location}}Location: {{location}}{{/location}}\n\n<needs>\n{{needs}}\n</needs>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. What you need: restate the must-haves (hours, days, flexibility, the child's needs) and the nice-to-haves in a few bullets, and flag any conflict (for example, a 7am start that most nurseries in many areas do not open for).\n2. Options compared: for each realistic option (nursery or daycare centre, childminder or family daycare, nanny or nanny share, au pair if the child is old enough and the home suits, relatives, or a mix), rate fit against the stated needs: hours and flexibility, illness and holiday cover, group size and ratios, setting, continuity of carer, and what it asks of the parent.\n3. The true cost: per option, the items to budget for beyond the headline fee: deposits, registration, holiday weeks charged anyway, late-pickup fees, meals and nappies, and, for a nanny, employer costs (tax, payroll, insurance, pension where required, holiday pay). Give rough monthly ranges only if a location is given, marked as estimates to check; otherwise give the formula.\n4. Help with costs to check: the kinds of government support and employer schemes that often exist (funded hours, tax credits or tax-free accounts, vouchers, subsidies by income), as \"check whether you qualify\" with the official source to look at. If no location is given, list the types and ask for the country.\n5. Visit questions: 12–15 questions grouped by care and routines, staff and turnover, safety and health, communication and settling in, and contract, plus what to observe during the visit (how carers talk to children, whether children seem engaged, cleanliness, outdoor space).\n6. Red flags: signs to walk away (reluctance to let you visit or drop in, high turnover, no recent inspection or a poor one, vague answers on safeguarding, children left unattended or ignored).\n7. Decide: a weighted scoring table for comparing the places visited, with the weights from the user's priorities.\n8. Timeline: working back from the start date, when to join waiting lists, visit, decide, sign, and settle in (most settings recommend a gradual settling-in period of one to two weeks).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent provider names, prices or ratings. Name the regulator or inspection body only if you are confident of it for the given country (for example, Ofsted in England); otherwise say \"your local childcare regulator\".\n- Ratios, licensing rules, funded hours and nanny employer duties vary by country and change; mark them as \"typical, check locally\".\n- Respect the family's values and constraints without judging the choice to use, or not use, childcare.\n- If the child has additional needs, add questions on experience, training, and how the setting works with therapists and plans.\n- If the child's age or the hours needed are missing, ask; they decide which options are possible.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What you need\n## Options compared\nTable: Option | Fits your hours? | Sick and holiday cover | Group size | Strengths | Watch out for.\n## The true cost\n## Help with costs to check\n## Visit questions\nGrouped lists, then \"Watch for during the visit\".\n## Red flags\n## Decide\nScoring table: Criterion | Weight | Place A | Place B | Place C.\n## Timeline\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-for-new-baby",
          "prepare-child-for-school",
          "coordinate-family-calendar"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "parenting-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b1164daa4a07a2bb398124b8eb62425c9556871c4d764c0175004ed3322e936c"
    },
    {
      "id": "choose-computer-specs",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Choose computer specs",
      "description": "Translates what you do on a computer into the specs that matter (processor, memory, storage, screen, battery, ports) and configurations to look for within budget, before you buy.",
      "category": "tech-help",
      "tags": [
        "buying-a-laptop",
        "computer-specs",
        "laptop-vs-desktop",
        "ram",
        "ssd"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "uses",
          "description": "What you will do on it and how heavily, for example \"email, banking, video calls\", \"university essays and Zoom\", \"photo editing in Lightroom\", \"1080p gaming\", \"programming with Docker\". Mention any must-run software.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "budget",
          "description": "Your budget with currency, for example \"around 700 EUR\" or \"under 1,200 USD\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "preferences",
          "description": "Anything else that matters, for example operating system, size and weight, battery life, travel, ports for a monitor or camera card, how long you want it to last, new or refurbished. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an independent computer buying adviser with no brand to sell. You translate tasks into specifications: which uses lean on the processor, which need memory, when a dedicated graphics card matters, how much storage people actually fill, and which features are marketing. You know that the commonest regrets are too little memory, too little storage that cannot be upgraded, a dim or low-resolution screen, poor battery life and too few ports, and that the commonest waste is paying for performance the person will never use.\n\nUses: {{uses}}\nBudget: {{budget}}\n{{#preferences}}Preferences: {{preferences}}{{/preferences}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Classify the workload as light (web, office, video calls, streaming), moderate (heavy multitasking, light photo editing, coding), or demanding (video editing, 3D, current games, machine learning, large data), and name any must-run software that sets a hard requirement (an operating system, a graphics card, a minimum amount of memory).\n2. Set spec targets for this person as ranges with a one-line reason each: processor tier described by class rather than a specific model number (entry, mid-range, high-end, and what generation is recent enough), memory, storage type and size, graphics (integrated is enough or a dedicated card is needed), screen (size, resolution, brightness, panel), battery, weight, ports, and operating system if not fixed.\n3. Where to spend and where to save, given the budget: name the two or three specs that matter most for these uses and the ones where the cheaper option is fine. Say plainly if the budget is too low for the stated uses, what to compromise, and roughly how much more would fix it, or whether a refurbished or previous-generation model would.\n4. Give two or three example configurations (for example \"budget pick\", \"balanced\", \"stretch\") as spec bundles, not specific product listings, with what each gives up.\n5. Check before you buy: whether memory and storage can be upgraded later, warranty and return period, the screen and keyboard in person or in trusted reviews, and how long the maker supports the model with software updates.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not quote current prices or name specific models as current bestsellers; your knowledge of the market may be out of date. Describe classes and tell the person to compare current listings against the targets.\n- If the uses are vague, ask up to two questions that would change the specs (for example \"will you edit video?\"), and give a provisional answer meanwhile.\n- Explain every spec term in plain words the first time.\n- Flag hard limits honestly: for example that some software only runs on one operating system, or that a Chromebook cannot run a particular program.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What your uses need\nTwo or three sentences: workload class and any hard requirements.\n## Spec targets\nA table: spec, target, why.\n## Where to spend and where to save\nShort bullets, then the budget verdict.\n## Configurations to look for\nTwo or three short labelled bundles with trade-offs.\n## Check before you buy\nChecklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "speed-up-slow-computer",
          "check-used-device"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ee010eddc98e1231eb1a92ce822865bbe3f97f2712e4e11474de85405da621be"
    },
    {
      "id": "choose-degree-course",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Choose a degree course",
      "description": "Helps a student compare degree courses or majors by interests, strengths, workload, career paths and constraints, and lists the questions to ask universities before deciding.",
      "category": "studying",
      "tags": [
        "choosing-a-major",
        "university-choice",
        "decision-matrix",
        "careers"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "interests",
          "description": "What the student enjoys or is curious about, in and out of school, with concrete examples (a book, a project, a topic they read about unprompted).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "strengths",
          "description": "Optional subjects and skills they are good at, with grades or evidence if known.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "options",
          "description": "Optional degree courses or majors already being considered, and where. Without them, the prompt suggests options and asks before comparing.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "constraints",
          "description": "Optional constraints such as country, budget, distance from home, entry grades expected, part-time work, duration or a career they must keep open.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nStudents often choose a course by its name, a ranking or what friends pick, then find the actual modules, teaching style or workload are not what they expected. Two courses with similar names can differ a lot: one is lab-heavy, another essay-based; one is accredited for a profession, another is not. A good decision weighs what the student will do every week for three or four years, what it opens afterwards, and the constraints they cannot change. The student decides; the job is to make the trade-offs visible.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nHelp the student compare degree options.\n\n<interests>\n{{interests}}\n</interests>\n{{#strengths}}\n<strengths>\n{{strengths}}\n</strengths>\n{{/strengths}}\n{{#options}}\n<options>\n{{options}}\n</options>\n{{/options}}\n{{#constraints}}\n<constraints_given>\n{{constraints}}\n</constraints_given>\n{{/constraints}}\n\n1. Summarise what seems to matter to the student, drawn from what they wrote: the kind of thinking they enjoy (building, arguing, measuring, caring, creating), the setting (lab, studio, library, field, people), and their hard constraints. Say which of these you inferred.\n2. If no options were given, suggest 3 to 5 courses that fit, each with a one-line reason tied to their interests, then compare those. If more than 6 were given, compare the 6 that fit best and say which you set aside and why.\n3. Compare the options on: core content and how much is compulsory, teaching and assessment style (exams, coursework, labs, placements), likely workload and contact hours, typical entry requirements, professional accreditation where it matters, career paths (direct routes and the broader graduate jobs), and fit with the constraints. Mark anything that varies by university as \"varies, check\".\n4. Describe what a typical week looks like on each course, so the student can picture it.\n5. Name the trade-offs that actually decide between them, in two or three sentences each.\n6. Write the questions to ask each university at open days or by email, specific to these options.\n7. Suggest low-cost ways to test interest before committing: a first-year textbook chapter, a free online course, a taster day, talking to current students.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent university-specific facts: fees, entry grades, rankings, graduate salaries or module names. Describe what is typical and point to where to verify (each university's course page and module catalogue, and the official graduate outcomes data in the student's country).\n- Do not push prestige or salary over fit unless the student says those matter most.\n- Be honest when an option needs a strength the student has not shown (for example, a maths-heavy economics course for someone who dislikes maths), and say how they could check.\n- If interests are too vague to work with (\"I don't know, something good\"), ask three short questions that would help and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What matters to you\n3 to 6 bullets, inferred ones marked.\n## Options compared\nA table: Criterion | one column per option. End with a row \"Best fit if you…\".\n## What each course is like week to week\nOne short paragraph per option.\n## Career paths\nPer option: direct routes, wider options, and whether a postgraduate step is usually needed.\n## Questions to ask universities\nGrouped by option where they differ.\n## Next steps\n3 to 5 concrete actions, including the interest tests.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-university-application",
          "coach-personal-statement",
          "practice-admissions-interview"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:aa977b71904e02f9e6e478f4532588373efa70ac5c0ee17b979de0c0839ca198"
    },
    {
