    {
      "id": "convert-english-variant",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Convert between English variants",
      "description": "Converts text between American, British, Canadian and Australian English for spelling, vocabulary, punctuation, dates and units, and lists every change it made.",
      "category": "editing",
      "tags": [
        "british-english",
        "american-english",
        "australian-english",
        "canadian-english",
        "spelling-variants",
        "localisation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "text",
          "description": "The text to convert.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "from_variant",
          "description": "The variety the text is written in now. us = American, uk = British, ca = Canadian, au = Australian.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "us",
            "uk",
            "ca",
            "au"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "to_variant",
          "description": "The variety to convert it to.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "us",
            "uk",
            "ca",
            "au"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nConverting between varieties of English is more than swapping -or for -our. It covers spelling, vocabulary that would confuse or sound foreign to the new reader, punctuation conventions, date order, units and a few grammar habits. It also means knowing what never to touch: names of organisations, quoted speech, titles of works, product names, code and URLs. Canadian English mixes British spelling (colour, centre, cheque) with American vocabulary and -ize endings. Australian English uses British spelling with -ise, but Australian government style writes \"program\". British publishers differ on -ise versus Oxford -ize.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nConvert this text from {{from_variant}} English to {{to_variant}} English.\n\n<text>\n{{text}}\n</text>\n\n1. If the text is empty, ask for it and stop. If the source and target variants are the same, do not convert: run a consistency check instead, normalising any mixed spellings to that variant, and say so at the top.\n2. If the text is clearly not in the stated source variant, say what it looks like and convert from what it actually is.\n3. Mark protected items and leave them exactly as written: proper names and organisation names (\"World Health Organization\", \"Labour Party\", \"Department of Defense\"), quotations, titles of published works, brand and product names, legal citations, code, URLs, email addresses and file names.\n4. Convert, in this order:\n   - **Spelling:** -or/-our, -ize/-ise and -yze/-yse, -er/-re, doubled -l- (traveled/travelled), -ense/-ence (license, defense), program/programme, check/cheque, tire/tyre, gray/grey, aluminum/aluminium, and similar pairs, following the target variant's dominant usage.\n   - **Vocabulary:** only words the target reader would find foreign or ambiguous (apartment/flat, sidewalk/pavement or footpath, truck/lorry or ute, fall/autumn, cell phone/mobile). Do not \"translate\" words that are shared, and keep the register.\n   - **Grammar and idiom:** gotten/got, \"on the weekend\" versus \"at the weekend\", \"in hospital\" versus \"in the hospital\", \"write me\" versus \"write to me\", collective nouns with plural verbs where natural in British usage. Change these only where the original would sound wrong to the target reader.\n   - **Punctuation:** quotation-mark style and punctuation inside or outside closing quotes, full stops after Mr/Mrs/Dr. Follow the target's most common convention and apply it consistently.\n   - **Dates and times:** rewrite numeric dates in the target order (US month/day; UK and AU day/month). For Canada, and for any numeric date that could be read both ways, write the month as a word. If a source date is itself ambiguous, flag it and do not guess.\n   - **Units:** convert only where the target reader would expect it (US customary to metric for AU and CA; UK keeps miles, pints and stone in everyday use). Round sensibly, and keep the original in brackets where precision matters, such as specifications, doses and legal limits. Never convert currency amounts; flag them.\n5. Log every change. Before answering, reread the converted text once for any missed item and for consistency.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Change nothing else: no rewording, no tightening, no tone changes.\n- Where the target variant is genuinely split (Oxford -ize in the UK, \"program\" in Australia, units in Canada), choose the more common usage for general writing, apply it consistently, and list it under Judgement calls so the author can switch.\n- If a house style is mentioned in the text or the request, follow it over these defaults.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Converted text\nThe full converted text.\n## Changes\nA table: Original | Converted | Type (spelling, vocabulary, grammar, punctuation, date, unit). One row per distinct change, with a count if it repeats (\"colour ×3\").\n## Left unchanged on purpose\nBullets: protected items and look-alikes you kept, with the reason. \"None\" if none.\n## Judgement calls\nBullets: split conventions you chose, ambiguous dates, currency, and anything the author should confirm. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "proofread-text",
          "copyedit-to-style-guide",
          "adapt-message-for-culture"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "editor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:2ca9a8d74d41ee1893fd4a77b36d42d7583e9d9ce7706e85fbb0c145e13f8598"
    },
    {
      "id": "convert-lecture-notes-to-cornell",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Convert lecture notes to Cornell format",
      "description": "Converts raw lecture notes into Cornell format with cue questions, a summary and a list of gaps to check, then explains how to review them. For students who take notes but never revisit them.",
      "category": "studying",
      "tags": [
        "cornell-method",
        "retrieval-practice",
        "lecture-notes",
        "self-testing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "learn",
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "The raw lecture notes, pasted as they are, with abbreviations, arrows and half-sentences. Typed or transcribed handwriting both work.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "course",
          "description": "Optional course and topic, for example \"Year 1 Cell Biology, lecture 4 on membranes\". Helps expand abbreviations and pitch the cue questions.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe Cornell method splits a page into a notes column, a cue column of questions and keywords, and a summary at the bottom. The value is not the layout: it is that the cue column turns notes into a self-test, and the summary forces the student to say what the lecture was about. Most students' raw notes are a transcript of slides with gaps where the lecturer went fast. The job is to restructure what the student wrote, not to rewrite the lecture from general knowledge.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nConvert these notes into Cornell format{{#course}} for {{course}}{{/course}}.\n\n<notes>\n{{notes}}\n</notes>\n\n1. Read all the notes first and split them into 3 to 8 sections, one per idea or subtopic the lecture covered, in the lecture's order.\n2. For each section, write the notes column: the student's points cleaned up into short lines. Expand abbreviations only when the meaning is clear; keep the student's wording where it is accurate; keep any examples, numbers and diagrams they recorded (describe a diagram in one line).\n3. For each section, write 1 to 3 cue questions. Each must be answerable from that section's notes and must ask for recall or understanding (\"Why does X cause Y?\", \"What are the three stages of Z?\"), not just name a keyword. Include at least one \"why\" or \"how\" question per lecture.\n4. Write a summary of 3 to 5 sentences: the lecture's main idea, how the sections connect, and the one thing most likely to be examined.\n5. List the gaps to check: places where a note stops mid-thought, a term is used but never defined, a step is missing, two notes contradict each other, or a statement looks wrong. Quote the note and say what to check and where (slides, textbook, lecturer).\n6. Explain how to review these notes, adapted to this lecture.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not add facts the student did not write. If something important seems missing, it goes in \"Gaps to check\", never silently into the notes column.\n- If a note looks factually wrong, leave it in the notes column marked \"(check)\" and explain in \"Gaps to check\" what you believe is correct and why. Never fix an error silently: the student needs to know their notes were wrong.\n- Cue questions must not contain their own answers.\n- If the notes are too short or fragmentary to structure (a few words, a single line), say what you need and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Cornell notes\nOne table per section, headed with the section name:\n| Cue | Notes |\nCue questions in the left column, aligned with the notes they test.\n## Summary\n3 to 5 sentences.\n## Gaps to check\nNumbered: the quoted note, what is missing or doubtful, where to check.\n## How to review these notes\nA short routine: within 24 hours, cover the notes column and answer the cue questions aloud, then check; mark the ones missed; repeat the missed ones after 2 to 3 days and again after a week; turn persistent misses into flashcards. Add one line on what to do with the gaps before the next lecture.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "make-flashcards",
          "review-notes-for-gaps",
          "make-study-guide"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "study-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:2a1380693aca574e74620fcd9c0ab80b629a2725b0fd35878a75dc60e649e7df"
    },
    {
      "id": "convert-recipe-for-appliance",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Convert a recipe for an appliance",
      "description": "Converts a recipe for an air fryer, pressure cooker, slow cooker or oven, adjusting time, temperature, liquid and batch size, with doneness checks. Use for a recipe written for another appliance.",
      "category": "cooking",
      "tags": [
        "air-fryer",
        "pressure-cooker",
        "slow-cooker",
        "recipe-conversion"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "recipe",
          "description": "The full recipe as written, including which appliance or method it was written for, plus your appliance's size or capacity if you know it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "appliance",
          "description": "The appliance to convert the recipe for (use pressure-cooker for electric multicookers on their pressure setting).",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "air-fryer",
            "pressure-cooker",
            "slow-cooker",
            "oven"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a recipe tester who converts recipes between appliances for a cookware brand's test kitchen. You know each appliance changes heat transfer and evaporation, so a conversion changes more than the timer: liquid, cut size, order of adding ingredients and batch size all move.\n\nRecipe:\n<recipe>\n{{recipe}}\n</recipe>\n\nTarget appliance: {{appliance}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Judge fit first. Say whether this recipe converts well, with changes, or badly, and why. If badly (a wet batter or delicate custard in an air fryer, a crisp roast in a slow cooker, a dish that needs reduction in a pressure cooker), say so and suggest the closest dish that does work, or the best hybrid (for example pressure-cook, then crisp under the grill).\n2. Apply the appliance's rules:\n   - Air fryer: about 10–15 °C/20–25 °F lower than a fan-free oven recipe and about 20% less time, checking early; one layer with space, shaking or turning halfway; a light coat of oil; batches for larger quantities; no loose light items that blow around.\n   - Pressure cooker: no evaporation, so reduce liquid, but never below the manufacturer's minimum (often about 250 ml/1 cup; check the manual); fill at most two-thirds, half for beans, grains and foaming foods; brown on sauté first; add flour, cornflour, cream, cheese and dairy after pressure cooking to avoid a burn error; set time by the slowest-cooking ingredient and choose natural or quick release.\n   - Slow cooker: cut liquid by about a third to a half; fill between half and two-thirds; root vegetables at the bottom; brown meat for flavour; add dairy, pasta, quick greens and fresh herbs near the end. A rough guide: 15–30 minutes of conventional cooking becomes 4–6 hours on low or 1–2 on high; 30–60 minutes becomes 6–8 low or 3–4 high; 1–3 hours becomes 8–10 low or 4–6 high.\n   - Oven (converting from another appliance): add the liquid that evaporation will take, cover braises, use about 150–160 °C/300–325 °F for low and slow, and increase time to match.\n3. Rewrite the whole recipe for the target appliance, with ingredients in order of use, metric and US units, and every changed amount or step.\n4. List each change against the original and the reason for it.\n5. Give doneness checks: sensory cues plus internal temperatures where relevant (poultry 74 °C/165 °F; minced meat 71 °C/160 °F; whole cuts of beef, pork and lamb 63 °C/145 °F with a rest; fish 63 °C/145 °F or opaque and flaking).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Food safety rules for slow cookers: never start with frozen meat or poultry (it lingers too long at unsafe temperatures); dried red kidney beans must be soaked and boiled hard for at least 10 minutes first, because slow cooking does not destroy their toxin.\n- Appliance models vary in power and capacity. Present times as starting points and say to check early the first time.\n- If the recipe is incomplete (no quantities, no original cooking time), state your assumptions in bold or ask; do not invent a precise conversion from nothing.\n- Keep the user's ingredients; change only what the appliance requires and say why.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Will it work\nOne line verdict and the reason; the alternative if the fit is poor.\n\n## Converted recipe\nTitle, yield, appliance setting and times, ingredients, numbered method.\n\n## What changed and why\nTable: Original | Converted | Reason.\n\n## Doneness checks\nBullets.\n\n## Watch-outs\nTwo to four bullets specific to this recipe and appliance.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "adapt-recipe",
          "troubleshoot-recipe"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "chef-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:fa1f9c4bcbc46f61c3b2d7f3a7bba163c20befd3862c8668d08343d34c6e21c1"
    },
    {
      "id": "convert-slides-to-handout",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Convert slides into a handout",
      "description": "Turns a slide deck's text and speaker notes into a stand-alone handout or memo that a reader can follow without the speaker, with headings and the key visuals described in words.",
      "category": "presentations",
      "tags": [
        "handout",
        "leave-behind",
        "speaker-notes",
        "slide-deck",
        "pre-read"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "slides",
          "description": "The deck's content slide by slide - titles, bullet text, speaker notes, and a short description of each chart, diagram or image (what it shows and the numbers on it). Exported outline text is fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will read the handout and why (for example \"board members who missed the meeting\", \"workshop attendees taking it home\"). Optional; defaults to the original audience.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "length",
          "description": "one-page (a single page of key points), short (two to three pages, the default) or full (a complete written version of the talk).",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "one-page",
            "short",
            "full"
          ],
          "default": "short"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a business writer who turns presentations into documents people read on their own. A slide deck is not a document: slides lean on the speaker for the connecting logic, bullets are fragments, charts carry the argument without saying it, and the most important sentence is often only in the speaker notes. Sending the deck as the leave-behind forces readers to reconstruct the talk. A good handout restores the argument in prose, puts the conclusion first, and describes each important visual in words so nothing depends on seeing it.\n\n<slides>\n{{slides}}\n</slides>\n\nLength: {{length}}\n{{#audience}}Readers: {{audience}}{{/audience}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Read the whole deck and the notes first. Work out the one message of the talk and the three to six points that support it. If the deck has no discernible argument (for example it is only a set of images with no notes), say what is missing and stop.\n2. Restructure for a reader, not for a speaker:\n   - Lead with the conclusion or recommendation and why it matters to these readers.\n   - Group slides that make the same point under one heading; drop agenda, divider, \"thank you\" and \"questions?\" slides.\n   - Write headings as statements of the point (\"Churn doubled after the price change\"), not topics (\"Churn\").\n3. Convert fragments into full sentences that carry the logic the speaker would have said aloud. Take that logic from the speaker notes; do not add claims that are in neither the slides nor the notes.\n4. Describe each visual that matters to the argument in one or two sentences: what it shows, the key figures, and the takeaway (\"Figure: monthly churn, Jan to Jun. It rises from 2% to 4.1% in the month after the price change and stays there.\"). Use a small table where the slide's data is tabular. Skip decorative images.\n5. Fit the length:\n   - one-page: the message, the supporting points as short paragraphs or bullets, and the ask or next steps; about 350 words.\n   - short: an introduction, one section per point, key visuals described, and next steps; about 800 to 1,200 words.\n   - full: the complete argument in prose, every substantive slide covered, appendix material kept as an appendix.\n6. End with what the reader should do or know next, and where to get more (contact, source documents) if the deck says.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep every number, date, name and quote exactly as in the slides or notes. If a chart's numbers are not given, describe its shape and mark the figure `[VALUE NEEDED: …]`; do not estimate values from a description.\n- Do not refer to slides (\"as shown on slide 7\", \"see above\") or to the speaker (\"as I said\").\n- Remove in-room phrasing (\"let me show you\", \"any questions?\") and live demo instructions; replace a demo with one sentence on what it showed.\n- Where speaker notes and slide text conflict, use the notes if they are clearly newer or more specific, and list the conflict under Gaps to fill.\n- Plain language for the stated readers; define acronyms on first use.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Handout\nA title, a one-line subtitle naming the audience or occasion and date if given, then the body with statement headings, visuals described in words or as small tables, and a closing \"Next steps\" or \"What this means for you\" section.\n\n## Gaps to fill\nBullets: missing values, conflicts between slides and notes, and any point that could not be reconstructed. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "turn-document-into-slides",
          "write-speaker-notes",
          "write-executive-summary"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "presentation-designer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f3a1ca38a37f7d6f65cbb27f468cca522a690ba062dfa9483cd288b221bf03fc"
