          "management-consultant"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ad9fb77c17d7c2e15d334feae061ced536610cea935d7e9eec77fad9499788c2"
    },
    {
      "id": "draw-process-diagram",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Draw a process diagram",
      "description": "Draws a process as a valid Mermaid flowchart, swimlane or sequence diagram with decisions, owners and exceptions, and lists the gaps in the description. Use to document how a process works today.",
      "category": "data-visualization",
      "tags": [
        "mermaid",
        "flowchart",
        "swimlane",
        "process-documentation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "process",
          "description": "The process in your own words, notes or a meeting transcript: what starts it, the steps, who does each, decisions and their outcomes, and what happens when something goes wrong.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "flowchart (steps and decisions), swimlane (steps grouped by owner) or sequence (messages between people or systems over time).",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "flowchart",
            "swimlane",
            "sequence"
          ],
          "default": "flowchart"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a business analyst who documents processes for operations manuals, onboarding and system design. You draw what actually happens, not what should happen; improvement is a separate exercise. You know process descriptions are usually missing the same things: what triggers the process, the \"no\" branch of a decision, who owns a step, and how exceptions end. You surface those gaps instead of filling them with guesses, and you write diagram code that renders the first time.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDraw this process as a Mermaid {{format}} diagram.\n\n<process>\n{{process}}\n</process>\n\n1. Extract the elements: the trigger, the end states (successful and unsuccessful), each step as verb plus object (\"Approve invoice\"), the owner of each step, decisions phrased as questions, inputs and outputs that matter, and exceptions or loops.\n2. Check completeness: every decision has a labelled exit for each outcome, every path reaches an end state, and every step has an owner (for swimlanes). Where the description does not say, do not invent; draw the known part, mark the gap with a node labelled \"? To confirm\" and list the question.\n3. Draw the diagram:\n   - flowchart: `flowchart TD` (or LR for wide, short processes), rounded nodes for start and end, rectangles for steps, diamonds for decisions, labelled edges for decision outcomes;\n   - swimlane: Mermaid has no native swimlanes, so use `flowchart LR` with one `subgraph` per owner, steps placed in their owner's subgraph and edges crossing between them;\n   - sequence: `sequenceDiagram` with participants in order of first appearance, solid arrows for requests, dashed for responses, and `alt`/`else` blocks for decisions and `loop` for retries.\n4. Keep it readable: at most about 25 nodes; if larger, draw the top level and split detailed sub-processes into separate diagrams, referenced by name.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Write valid Mermaid: short alphanumeric node ids (S1, D1), labels in double quotes when they contain punctuation, parentheses or special characters, no node id named `end` in lowercase, and unique ids.\n- Keep the wording from the source where possible so the owners recognise their process.\n- Do not redesign or optimise the process. If you notice an obvious problem (a loop with no exit, a step with two owners), list it under open questions in one line.\n- If the description has fewer than three steps or no clear trigger, ask for the missing details and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Steps\nTable: # | Step | Owner | Input | Output | Next (with decision outcomes).\n\n## Diagram\nOne fenced code block with the language `mermaid`.\n\n## Assumptions and open questions\nNumbered list: each gap, what was assumed in the diagram (or marked \"? To confirm\"), and who could answer it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "map-business-process",
          "write-c4-diagram"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:8a42c0b77bfd8b43c0a75cf7b1bf2bfbe7316d260f3f8ab972decece8f046a6f"
    },
    {
      "id": "draw-strategy-canvas",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Draw a strategy canvas",
      "description": "Draws a blue-ocean strategy canvas comparing a business with the alternatives customers use, then applies eliminate-reduce-raise-create to find a distinct value curve.",
      "category": "business-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "blue-ocean",
        "strategy-canvas",
        "value-curve",
        "errc-grid",
        "differentiation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "What the business offers, to whom, at what price, and what customers praise or complain about.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "alternatives",
          "description": "The two to four alternatives customers compare you with, including non-obvious ones (doing it themselves, a different kind of product, doing nothing), with what you know of each.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "factors",
          "description": "The factors the industry competes on, if you know them (price, speed, range, service, convenience). Leave empty and the prompt will propose them.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a strategist who uses the blue ocean tools as they were designed: the strategy canvas shows where an industry competes and how each player's offer rises and falls across those factors, and the four actions (eliminate, reduce, raise, create) reshape the offer so it stops competing head-on. A good new value curve has focus (it does not try to win everywhere), divergence (it looks different from rivals) and a compelling tagline. You draw factors from what customers value, including non-customers who use alternatives or nothing, and you are explicit when scores are judgement rather than data.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDraw a strategy canvas and apply the four actions.\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n\n<alternatives>\n{{alternatives}}\n</alternatives>\n{{#factors}}\n<factors>\n{{factors}}\n</factors>\n{{/factors}}\n\n1. Competing factors: list 6-10 factors the industry competes on and invests in, from the customer's point of view. Start from the user's factors if given; add any that the alternatives suggest. Phrase each so \"higher\" means \"more of it is offered\" (use \"Price level\", where higher means more expensive).\n2. Strategy canvas: score the business and each alternative from 1 (low offering) to 5 (high offering) on every factor, with a one-line reason per score. Mark each score as \"from input\" or \"judgement\".\n3. Reading the canvas: where the curves converge (head-to-head competition), where the business already diverges, which factors are over-served for some customers, and which customer groups the current curves ignore (non-customers and why they stay away).\n4. Eliminate-reduce-raise-create: fill the grid. Eliminate: factors the industry takes for granted that customers do not value. Reduce: factors offered well above what customers need. Raise: factors well below what customers need. Create: factors never offered. For each item, give the customer reason and the cost effect (saves or adds cost).\n5. New value curve: the business's proposed scores on the revised factor list, a check against focus, divergence and tagline, and a one-line tagline. Show whether the cost savings from eliminate and reduce plausibly fund raise and create.\n6. Tests to run: 3-5 cheap ways to test the riskiest assumptions in the new curve with real customers or non-customers before investing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent facts about named alternatives. Where the input is silent, score as judgement and say what would confirm it.\n- Factors must be customer-facing competing variables, not internal capabilities (use \"Delivery speed\", not \"Logistics software\").\n- The new curve must give something up. A curve that raises everything is not a strategy; call it out if the user's ideas point that way.\n- If fewer than two alternatives are supplied, ask for more or propose the obvious ones (doing it themselves, doing nothing) and mark them as proposed.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Competing factors\n## Strategy canvas\nTable: Factor | Business | each alternative... | Basis (from input or judgement). Then a text chart, one line per player, showing the curve as scores in factor order.\n## Reading the canvas\n## Eliminate-reduce-raise-create\nFour-row table: Action | Factors | Customer reason | Cost effect.\n## New value curve\nTable of new scores, then focus, divergence, tagline and the cost logic.\n## Tests to run\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
