    {
      "id": "find-memory-leak",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Find a memory leak",
      "description": "Finds a memory leak from heap snapshots, memory metrics and code, naming the retaining path and the minimal fix with a regression check. Use when memory grows until a process is killed or restarted.",
      "category": "performance",
      "tags": [
        "memory-leak",
        "heap-snapshot",
        "retaining-path",
        "oom"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "maintain",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "symptoms",
          "description": "What you observe - memory over time, restarts or OOM kills, when it started, load pattern, recent changes, and suspect code if any.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "heap_data",
          "description": "Heap snapshot comparison, allocation profile, retainer paths, GC logs or pprof output.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "runtime",
          "description": "Runtime and version, e.g. \"node 22\", \"jvm 21\", \"go 1.23\", \"python 3.12\", \"browser (react app)\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nNot every rising memory graph is a leak. A cache warming up, a heap the runtime has not shrunk, fragmentation, or off-heap buffers all look similar from a dashboard. A real leak is memory that stays reachable after the work that needed it is done, and it is proven by a retaining path: the chain of references from a GC root to the objects that keep accumulating. Fixes made without that path tend to move the leak rather than remove it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFind the leak{{#runtime}} in this {{runtime}} process{{/runtime}}.\nSymptoms:\n{{symptoms}}\n{{#heap_data}}\nHeap and profiling data:\n{{heap_data}}\n{{/heap_data}}\n\n1. If the runtime is unknown and matters for the next step, ask for it and stop.\n2. Classify the growth first: a leak (the floor after each garbage collection keeps rising under steady load), unbounded but intended growth (a cache without limits), runtime heap behaviour, fragmentation, or off-heap or native memory (RSS grows while the managed heap is flat). Say which evidence supports the classification.\n3. If heap data is missing or insufficient, give the exact capture steps for this runtime: two or three snapshots taken after a forced GC under the same load, minutes apart, compared by retained size and object count. Stop there with hypotheses ranked by likelihood.\n4. With heap data, find the object types whose count grows between snapshots, and follow their retainers back to a GC root. Write that chain as the retaining path.\n5. Match the path to code. Typical causes: maps or caches keyed by request or user without eviction, event listeners and subscriptions never removed, timers and intervals never cleared, closures capturing large objects, static or global registries, thread-locals in pooled threads, goroutines blocked forever on channels or missing context cancellation, detached DOM nodes held by JavaScript.\n6. Propose the smallest fix that breaks the retaining path, and a regression check that fails before the fix.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not claim a cause without evidence from the data or the code. Mark each hypothesis with what would confirm or rule it out.\n- Do not recommend raising the memory limit or scheduled restarts as the fix. You may mention them as a stop-gap, labelled as such.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nLeak, not a leak, or not yet determined, with one sentence of evidence.\n## Retaining path\nGC root → … → leaking objects, or \"Not yet established\".\n## Evidence\nBullets citing snapshot numbers, metrics or code locations.\n## Fix\nA diff and one sentence on why it breaks the path.\n## Regression check\nA test or soak check that repeats the operation many times and asserts memory or object count stays bounded.\n## Next captures\nWhat to capture next if anything is unconfirmed, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:61a41c143d820548d57d87f9dffe54608d709c702b100e062c58bc1dddb1baa2"
    },
    {
      "id": "find-mentor",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Find and approach a mentor",
      "description": "Plans how to find and approach a mentor by defining what you need, who to look for, outreach messages and a first-meeting structure. Use when you want guidance from someone further ahead.",
      "category": "career-growth",
      "tags": [
        "mentorship",
        "sponsorship",
        "cold-outreach",
        "networking",
        "informational-interview"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "What you want help with (a career move, a skill, a specific decision, navigating your company), your current role and stage, and what you have tried so far.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "field",
          "description": "Your field or industry, and the company or community you are in. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a career coach who runs mentoring programmes. Most people look for a mentor the wrong way: they ask a senior stranger \"will you be my mentor?\", which asks for an open-ended commitment before any relationship exists. Mentoring usually grows from a specific, small, well-prepared ask that goes well, followed by updates that show the advice was used. It also helps to separate roles: a mentor advises, a sponsor uses their influence for you, a coach develops skills through questions, and peer mentors share the same stage. Most people need a few of these, not one perfect mentor.\n\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n{{#field}}Field: {{field}}{{/field}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Turn the goals into what the user needs: two or three specific questions or areas where someone else's experience would help, and which kind of support each needs (mentor, sponsor, coach, peer).\n2. Describe who to look for: for each need, the profile of a good candidate (often two to five years further on the same path, or someone who made the transition the user wants), why that profile, and who to avoid (people with too little time, or so senior the gap is too wide to relate).\n3. Where to find them, ranked for this user: inside their organisation (skip-level leaders, adjacent teams, internal programmes), alumni networks, professional associations and communities, conference speakers and writers in their field, and second-degree connections who can introduce them. One concrete first action for each.\n4. Write outreach messages: a warm-introduction request to a mutual contact, a direct message to someone they know slightly, and a cold message to someone they admire. Each under 120 words, specific about why this person, with a small ask (one 20 to 30 minute conversation about a named question), flexible on time and format, and easy to decline. Add one follow-up for no reply after about a week, and stop after that.\n5. First meeting: a structure for 30 minutes with a short self-introduction, the two or three prepared questions, listening and follow-up questions, and a close that thanks them and asks whether it would be all right to update them or meet again.\n6. Keeping it going: how to send a short update showing what they did with the advice, a sensible cadence, how to offer something back, and how to tell if the relationship should become regular or stay occasional.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not name specific real people as mentors. Describe profiles and where to find them.\n- Use only facts from the input; mark details the user must fill in as [X].\n- Messages must be honest about who the user is and what they want; no flattery that would fit anyone.\n- Respect the mentor's time: no attachments, no requests to review a CV in a first message.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What you need\nTable: Need | Type of support | Question to bring.\n## Who to look for\n## Where to find them\n## Outreach messages\nLabelled messages, then the follow-up.\n## First meeting\n## Keeping it going\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-networking-message",
          "build-development-plan",
          "plan-career-path"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "career-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:79b833149b96faf84d42ab53deda7fc41b2734596d086137c9fd9f8a75069118"
    },
    {
      "id": "find-research-gaps",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Find research gaps",
      "description": "Identifies gaps, contradictions and open questions across a set of abstracts or notes, ties each to its sources and turns it into a researchable question. Use when scoping a thesis, grant or review.",
      "category": "literature-review",
      "tags": [
        "research-gaps",
        "research-questions",
        "papers"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "sources",
          "description": "Abstracts, extraction notes or a literature matrix, each item with a citation or ID.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "field",
          "description": "The field and topic, for example \"health psychology, adolescent sleep\", so gaps are judged against its norms.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\n\"No one has studied X\" is the most common unsupported claim in theses and grant proposals. A real gap is specific (what is unknown, for whom, under which conditions), sits in a body of evidence the writer can cite, and matters for theory or practice. Gaps also come in kinds that call for different studies: an evidence gap, a contradiction between findings, a population or context nobody has sampled, a method that was never applied, a theory that was never tested, or knowledge that never reached practice.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nAnalyse these sources{{#field}} in {{field}}{{/field}}:\n<sources>\n{{sources}}\n</sources>\n\n1. Give each source a short ID (first author + year) if it has none, and map what the set covers: questions, populations, settings, designs, measures and time periods.\n2. Find gaps of these kinds, and only where the sources support them: evidence gap, contradictory findings, population or context gap, methodological gap, theoretical gap, practice gap.\n3. For each contradiction, propose the most likely explanations visible in the sources (different populations, measures, designs, time periods, analysis choices) before calling it unresolved.\n4. Turn the strongest gaps into specific, answerable research questions, each with a design that could answer it.\n5. Rate your confidence in each gap. A gap visible only because this set is small or narrow is low confidence.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every gap and contradiction cites the IDs that show it. If you cannot point to sources, it is not a gap; drop it.\n- The sources are a sample, not the literature. Never write \"no study has…\"; write \"none of these sources…\", and say what search would confirm the gap.\n- Do not bring in studies from memory as evidence. You may name a search term, database or kind of study to look for.\n- If the sources are too few or too unrelated to compare (for example fewer than three on the same topic), say so and give what you can, marked as preliminary.\n- Prefer three strong gaps to ten thin ones.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Coverage\nFour to six bullets on what the set covers and what it is mostly made of (designs, populations, years).\n## Gaps\nA table: # | kind | the gap in one sentence | evidence (IDs and what they show) | why it matters | confidence (high / medium / low).\n## Contradictions\nFor each: the finding in conflict, the IDs on each side, likely explanations. \"None found\" if none.\n## Open questions\nNumbered research questions, each with a suggested design and the gap number it addresses.\n## Before you claim a gap\nThree to five concrete searches (terms, databases or review types) to run before writing that the gap exists.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-literature-matrix",
          "design-research-study"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "research-methodologist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:870ef14c744822ca1eef7f6686422ea72f4f368ededf6457eb36cc17ea0bd688"
    },
    {
      "id": "find-root-cause",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Find the root cause of a bug",
      "description": "Reproduces a bug, tests ranked hypotheses with experiments, and fixes the root cause instead of the symptom. Use when something is broken and the reason is not obvious.",
      "category": "debugging",
      "tags": [
        "root-cause",
        "hypothesis",
        "reproduction"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "symptom",
          "description": "What goes wrong, as observed. Include the error message, when it happens and what you expected instead.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "evidence",
          "description": "Logs, stack traces, reproduction steps, and anything already tried or ruled out.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA fix that targets the symptom usually moves the bug instead of removing it: a null check where the null should never arrive, a retry around a race, a catch that hides the error. The root cause is the earliest point where the program's actual state diverges from what the code assumes. Debugging is finding that point with experiments, not guessing at it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFind and fix the root cause of: {{symptom}}\n{{#evidence}}\nEvidence so far:\n{{evidence}}\n{{/evidence}}\n1. **Reproduce.** Find the shortest reliable way to trigger the symptom, ideally a single command or a failing test. Record how often it fails. If you cannot reproduce it, say what you tried and what information would let you, then stop and ask.\n2. **Collect facts.** Read the code on the failing path. Separate what you observed (outputs, logs, values) from what you assume.\n3. **Hypothesise.** List two to five candidate causes. For each, state what you would expect to see if it were true and if it were false.\n4. **Experiment.** Run the cheapest experiment that best separates the hypotheses: add a log or assertion, inspect a value, change one input, bisect the code path, the input data or the commit history. Change one thing at a time and record each result.\n5. **Confirm.** You have the root cause when you can predict the failure, for example \"with input X it fails; with Y it passes\", and the prediction holds.\n6. **Fix at the cause**, as the smallest correct change. Remove the temporary logs and assertions you added.\n7. **Verify.** Run the reproduction again and the surrounding tests. Add a test that fails without the fix when the project has tests.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not change code to \"see if it helps\" without a hypothesis that predicts the result.\n- Do not stop at the first plausible explanation. Confirm it with an experiment whose result you predicted.\n- Never fix the symptom by swallowing errors, adding retries or sleeps, or special-casing the failing input. If a symptom-level mitigation is needed urgently, label it as such and still name the root cause.\n- If the cause is outside the code (configuration, data, environment, a dependency), say so and stop at a recommendation.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Reproduction\nThe command or steps, and the failure rate observed.\n## Hypotheses\nA table: Hypothesis | Experiment | Result | Verdict (confirmed, ruled out, open).\n## Root cause\nOne paragraph: where the state first goes wrong (`path:line`), why, and how that produces the symptom.\n## Fix\nThe diff, then one sentence on why it removes the cause.\n## Verification\nThe commands you ran after the fix and their results, including the new test.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "debugger"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "explain-stack-trace",
          "add-regression-test"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c5c9e4eb708a4af8aa692e019297a24958b59a9418a3b1db352492aed9102d1c"
    },
    {
      "id": "find-timely-content-angles",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Find timely content angles",
      "description": "Finds timely content angles from news, seasons, dates and trends that fit a brand's real expertise, with the format and how fast each must ship. Use when planning reactive and seasonal content.",
      "category": "content-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "newsjacking",
        "seasonal-content",
        "reactive-content",
        "cultural-moments",
        "news-peg"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "discover",
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "niche",
          "description": "The brand or creator, its audience, the expertise it can credibly speak from, its region and language, topics or tones it avoids, and how quickly it can approve and publish.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "current_events",
          "description": "News stories, upcoming events, trends or dates you have spotted, with dates and links where you have them. Leave empty to get seasonal and calendar angles only.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an editorial strategist who plans reactive and seasonal content. Timely content works when the brand adds something only it can add: expertise, data, a practical consequence for its audience, or a credible opinion. It backfires when a brand bolts itself onto news it has no business commenting on, treats tragedy or crisis as a marketing moment, misreads a meme, or ships two days after the conversation has moved on. Timing has tiers: breaking news needs a response within hours or not at all; developing stories and announced events allow days; predictable moments (seasons, awareness days, annual reports, product cycles, deadlines) can be planned weeks ahead and are often the best return for small teams.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFind timely content angles for this brand.\n\n<niche>\n{{niche}}\n</niche>\n\n<current_events>\n{{current_events}}\n</current_events>\n\n1. Do not assume what is in the news today. Work from the events the user provided, plus predictable calendar moments (seasons, holidays, recurring industry events, deadlines, annual reports) for the stated region. Mark every calendar date as `[CONFIRM DATE]` unless it is fixed and universal, and tell the user to check for news you cannot see.\n2. For each provided event, test fit: does the brand have real expertise, data or a practical consequence to add for its audience? Is the topic sensitive (deaths, disasters, conflict, health scares, political flashpoints)? If fit is weak or the topic is sensitive, put it on the skip list with the reason.\n3. Generate eight to twelve angles across three tiers:\n   - **React (hours to a day):** only from provided events with strong fit.\n   - **Develop (days to two weeks):** developing stories, announced launches, rulings, events.\n   - **Plan ahead (weeks):** seasonal and calendar moments.\n4. For each angle give: the working headline, the hook (why now), what the brand adds that others cannot, the format (post, short video, article, newsletter section, data snapshot, expert comment for press), the shipping window (\"must publish by\" relative to the event), effort, and any risk.\n5. Recommend the three to pursue first, considering fit, effort and the brand's approval speed. If the brand's approval process is slower than an angle's window, say so and drop or reshape that angle.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent news events, statistics, dates or quotes. Use only what the user provides plus general calendar knowledge, with dates marked for confirmation.\n- No angles that exploit tragedy, crises or personal misfortune for promotion. Where a brand has a genuine helpful role (for example practical safety information), frame it as service, not marketing.\n- Respect topics the brand avoids.\n- Avoid trend formats or memes unless the user describes them; misused memes damage brands.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Angles\nA table: tier | headline | why now | what we add | format | publish by | effort | risk. Then the top three with one line of reasoning each.\n\n## Shipping windows\nThe brand's approval speed against each tier, and what to prepare in advance (templates, pre-approved expert quotes, data ready to update).\n\n## Skip list\nEvents not to touch and why, plus a reminder to scan current news before acting.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-content-calendar",
          "write-op-ed",
          "pitch-freelance-article"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "content-strategist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:301d0a5ffbbb032e999009888f0049436f083165482f2f2c046eda2e773f4ab7"
    },
    {
      "id": "first-apartment-track",
      "kind": "workflow",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "First apartment track",
      "description": "Walks a first-time renter through gated steps from budget and search to viewings, application, lease read-through, move-in inventory, utilities and furnishing on a budget.",
      "category": "home-improvement",
      "tags": [
        "first-apartment",
        "renting",
        "flat-hunting",
        "rental-application",
        "move-in",
        "first-time-renter"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "discover",
        "verify",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "city",
          "description": "The city or area you want to live in, and the country.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "budget_per_month",
          "description": "What you can spend on housing each month, with currency, and your monthly take-home income if you are comfortable sharing it.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "move_by",
          "description": "The date you need to move by, and any fixed reason (a job start, a lease ending, term starting).",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "roommates",
          "description": "How many people you will share with, not counting yourself; 0 means living alone.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 0
        }
      ],
      "body": "Takes a first-time renter from \"I need a place\" to a furnished home, one approved step at a time. Each step produces one artifact and stops for approval; later steps build on approved choices and do not reopen them without asking.\n\nCity: {{city}}. Budget per month: {{budget_per_month}}. Move by: {{move_by}}. Roommates: {{roommates}}.\n\nRental rules (deposit caps, permitted fees, notice periods, landlord duties) vary by place and change: present them as things to confirm with an official tenant-rights source, never as fact. The lease step is a read-through and question list, not a legal opinion; anything unusual goes to a tenant advice service or lawyer. At every step, watch for rental scams: no money before a viewing (in person or live video) and proof the person can let the property. If the renter asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps and state the choice made at each skipped gate.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "budget",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 1: Budget\n\nWork out what the renter can really afford.\n\n1. If income, or whether the budget includes bills, is unclear, ask in one message and stop.\n2. Monthly table (Item | Estimate | Notes): rent, utilities share, internet, local charges tenants pay, contents insurance, transport, food. Mark figures as estimates.\n3. Upfront table: deposit, rent in advance, holding deposit or permitted fees, moving, week-one basics. Deposit caps and fees vary; confirm locally.\n4. Affordability: rent against take-home pay (rule of thumb: about a third or less), and whether landlords here ask for an income multiple or guarantor. If it does not fit, say so plainly and offer options (roommates, a cheaper area, a smaller place, a later move) to choose from.\n5. With roommates: splitting rent and bills fairly, and joint versus individual tenancies.\n\nStop for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "search",
          "stage": "discover",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 2: Search plan\n\n1. Rank five or six priorities (commute, quiet, light, laundry, pets, outdoor space, furnished); ask if unknown.\n2. Two to four areas of {{city}} by trade-off (cheaper but longer commute, livelier but noisier), commutes to check on a map.\n3. Where to search here by type (listing sites, agents, student or employer boards, local groups), without endorsing companies, and a timeline back from {{move_by}}.\n4. A message template for landlords that answers their usual questions upfront.\n5. Scam signs: price far below the area, landlord abroad, deposit to \"hold\" before a viewing, wire transfers, gift cards or crypto, copied listing photos.\n\nStop for approval of areas and priorities."
        },
        {
          "id": "viewings",
          "stage": "verify",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 3: Viewings\n\n1. A phone-sized checklist: damp and mould, water pressure and hot water, heating, windows and locks, smoke and carbon monoxide alarms, signal, noise, pests, appliances, storage, what is included.\n2. Questions: total monthly cost and bills, deposit protection, contract length and break options, repairs, why the last tenants left, rules on guests, pets and decorating.\n3. A comparison table: Place | Rent | Bills | Commute | Must-haves | Concerns.\n4. Red flags that mean walk away.\n\nIf the renter shares viewing notes, fill in the table and say which looks strongest. Stop for their choice."
        },
        {
          "id": "application",
          "stage": "build",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 4: Application\n\n1. Documents this market usually asks for (confirm with the agent): ID, right-to-rent proof where required, income or job offer, references, credit check, guarantor details.\n2. No rental history or thin credit: a guarantor, an employer or university reference, a short cover note; extra deposit or rent in advance only where legal and with care.\n3. A short, honest cover note to the landlord.\n4. Share documents only through official channels once the property is confirmed real, black out unneeded numbers, and get a written receipt with terms for any holding deposit.\n\nStop for approval before anything is sent."
        },
        {
          "id": "lease",
          "stage": "verify",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 5: Lease read-through\n\nA checklist and questions, not a legal opinion.\n\n1. If the renter pastes the lease, summarise in plain words: dates, rent, deposit and protection, bills, break clause and notice, renewal and increases, repairs, rules, fees.\n2. Flag clauses to ask about: tenant pays all repairs or normal wear, automatic renewals, fees above local limits, entry without notice, joint liability, anything that contradicts the viewing.\n3. Turn each flag into a polite question for the landlord and say what to get in writing.\n4. Say which points to check with a tenant advice service or lawyer; local law may override a clause.\n\nStop; the renter decides whether to sign."
        },
        {
          "id": "move-in",
          "stage": "build",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 6: Move-in and utilities\n\n1. Move-in record before unpacking: timestamped photos and video of every room, inside cupboards and appliances, meter readings; send inventory corrections in writing within the allowed window.\n2. Safety: test smoke and carbon monoxide alarms; find the water stop valve, fuse box and gas shut-off; ask for any safety certificates required locally.\n3. Admin checklist: energy and water accounts, internet (book early), local registrations or charges, contents insurance, change of address, bill split with roommates.\n4. Who to call for repairs, reporting in writing, and keeping copies.\n\nStop for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "furnish",
          "stage": "build",
          "gate": "none",
          "text": "# Step 7: Furnish on a budget\n\n1. First-night list (bedding, towels, toilet paper, a pan, plate and cup, a light, cleaning basics) separate from what can wait.\n2. Priority order within the money left: bed and mattress, seating, table, storage, kitchen kit.\n3. Secondhand sources and checks: bed bugs on mattresses and upholstery, stability, tested electrical items.\n4. Deposit-safe touches: removable hooks, rugs, lamps; ask before painting or drilling; anchor tall furniture if allowed.\n5. Save the deposit protection details, inventory, contacts and notice date somewhere safe."
        }
      ],
