      "id": "home-renovation-track",
      "kind": "workflow",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Home renovation track",
      "description": "Runs a home renovation in gated steps from scope and budget to permits to check, contractor bid comparison, schedule and a final punch list. Use to manage a renovation from idea to handover.",
      "category": "home-improvement",
      "tags": [
        "renovation",
        "contractor-bids",
        "building-permits",
        "punch-list",
        "project-schedule"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project",
          "description": "What you want done, room by room, with sizes, the age and condition of the home, the finish level, your location (country and region) and what you will do yourself.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "budget",
          "description": "The total you can spend, with currency, and whether it includes a buffer. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "Manages a home renovation one approved step at a time: scope, budget, permits to check, contractor bids, schedule, and the punch list at handover. Each step produces one artifact and stops for the homeowner's approval; later steps build on approved versions and do not reopen settled decisions without asking. Costs are ranges to verify with local quotes, and permit and legal rules are questions to confirm with the local building authority, never stated as fact. If the homeowner asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps and state the choice made at each skipped gate.\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "scope",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 1: Scope\n\nPin down exactly what work is in and out before any money is discussed.\n\nProject: {{project}}\n\n1. If the location, the age of the home, room sizes or the finish level are missing and matter, ask for them in one message and stop.\n2. Write the scope room by room: work items in the order trades would do them, finish level, who supplies materials, what the homeowner does, and what is out of scope.\n3. List hidden-risk items for this home: asbestos or lead paint in older homes, old wiring or pipes behind walls, damp, structural changes. Mark any that need a surveyor, structural engineer or licensed trade.\n4. List open decisions as `[DECIDE: …]`.\n\nStop for approval or edits. Do not price anything yet."
        },
        {
          "id": "budget",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 2: Budget\n\nPrice the approved scope as ranges.\n{{#budget}}Budget: {{budget}}{{/budget}}\n\n1. Table: Line item | Low | High | Who prices it. Follow the scope order and include often-missed costs: waste removal, making good, permits and fees, design or engineering, temporary living costs, delivery, tax.\n2. Add contingency: 10–15% for simple work, 20% or more for older homes and structural work, with the reason.\n3. Compare with the budget if given. If short, show where to save and what never to cut (structure, waterproofing, electrics).\n\nAll figures are estimates to verify with local quotes. Stop for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "permits",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 3: Permits and approvals to check\n\nList what to confirm before work starts. Do not state rules as fact.\n\n1. For each scope item, say whether it commonly needs approval: building permits or building regulations sign-off, planning or zoning (extensions, windows in protected areas, listed or historic homes), electrical, gas and plumbing work by certified trades, structural changes, and condominium, landlord or homeowners' association consent.\n2. Table: Item | Approval to check | Who to ask (building authority, planning office, association) | Typical lead time | Who applies (homeowner or contractor).\n3. Note insurance: tell the home insurer about major works.\n\nStop for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "bids",
          "stage": "build",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 4: Contractor bids\n\nGet comparable bids and choose well.\n\n1. Turn the approved scope into a bid request so every contractor prices the same work.\n2. Give the checks: licence or registration where required, insurance verified with the insurer, recent references, a written itemised quote.\n3. If the homeowner pastes bids, compare them in a table: Item | Contractor A | B | C, covering exclusions, allowances, tax, start date, duration, payment terms and warranty. Flag gaps, low allowances and red flags (cash only, large upfront payment, offers to skip permits).\n4. Propose a payment schedule tied to finished stages, with a final payment held until the punch list is done.\n\nStop for the homeowner's choice."
