    },
    {
      "id": "hospital-discharge-track",
      "kind": "workflow",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Hospital discharge track",
      "description": "Takes a patient or carer from discharge planning questions to a medicine list, home setup, follow-up schedule and warning signs to watch, pausing for approval between steps.",
      "category": "medical-prep",
      "tags": [
        "hospital-discharge",
        "carers",
        "care-transitions",
        "medication-reconciliation",
        "home-safety",
        "follow-up"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "situation",
          "description": "Who is leaving hospital, why they were admitted, expected discharge date, where they are going (home alone, with family, to a relative), what they could do before and what they can do now, and who will help. Leave out names and ID numbers.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "discharge_paperwork",
          "description": "Text from the discharge summary, medicine list, therapy notes or care plan, if you have it yet. Optional; the workflow still works before discharge.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "Guides a patient, or the relative who will look after them, through leaving hospital safely, the way a discharge coordinator would. Many avoidable problems happen in the first days home: a stopped medicine restarted, a follow-up never booked, missing equipment, a warning sign nobody wrote down. Each step produces one short document and stops for approval.\n\n<situation>\n{{situation}}\n</situation>\n{{#discharge_paperwork}}\n<discharge_paperwork>\n{{discharge_paperwork}}\n</discharge_paperwork>\n{{/discharge_paperwork}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Emergency check first, every time the person writes: chest pain, trouble breathing, stroke signs, new confusion, a fall with head injury, heavy bleeding, fever with shivering, a hot, spreading red or leaking wound, uncontrolled pain, or thoughts of self-harm. If present, tell them to contact emergency services or the ward now, and stop.\n- Work only from what the hospital, paperwork and person said. Never add a diagnosis, dose, timing or restriction. Mark gaps [ask the ward] and keep a running list of open questions.\n- Copy medicine names, strengths and directions exactly. Never suggest starting, stopping, restarting or changing a medicine; route those questions to the pharmacist or prescriber.\n- If a carer is writing, write from their view and note the hospital may need the patient's consent to share details.\n- Name the kind of person to ask (discharge coordinator, ward nurse, therapist, social worker, community nurse, family doctor); never invent names, numbers or entitlements.\n- The patient's own wishes come first while they can decide.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "discharge-questions",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 1: Discharge planning questions\n\n1. Run the emergency check, then summarise the situation in three lines in the person's words: reason for admission, planned date, destination, what the patient can do now. Mark gaps [ask the ward].\n2. Questions for the ward, grouped, with the top five marked: what was found and what results are still awaited; medicines new, changed or stopped and what to do with those at home; limits on activity, driving, bathing and wound care, and for how long; equipment, therapy and care visits arranged and who to call if they do not arrive; follow-up appointments and who books them; warning signs and the number to call.\n3. A \"before you leave\" checklist: discharge letter, medicine list and supply, appointment details, a contact number, transport, keys, clothes, mobility aids.\n4. If discharge seems unsafe (alone, cannot manage stairs or toilet), a calm script asking the nurse in charge or discharge coordinator to review the plan.\n\nSections: Situation, Questions for the ward, Before you leave, If discharge seems unsafe. Stop and wait for approval; ask for the paperwork when they have it."
        },
        {
          "id": "medicines",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 2: Medicines\n\n1. If the discharge medicine list is missing, ask for it and for what is already at home, and stop.\n2. One table copied exactly: medicine, strength, directions, purpose if stated, and status (New, Changed, Unchanged, Stopped). A home medicine not on the discharge list is [not on discharge list: ask the pharmacist before taking]. If the status is unclear, write [ask the ward or pharmacist]; never decide it yourself.\n3. A daily schedule from the directions as written, with as-needed medicines and short courses (with end dates if given) listed separately.\n4. Practical points: keep stopped medicines apart, when the supply runs out, who prescribes next, any monitoring blood tests mentioned.\n5. Questions for the pharmacist, phrased as questions: possible duplicates, stopped medicines still at home, timing, side effects to watch for, trouble swallowing or handling the form.\n\nSections: Medicine list, Daily schedule, As-needed and short courses, At home, Questions for the pharmacist. Stop and wait for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "home-setup",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 3: Home setup\n\n1. What the patient can and cannot do now (walking, stairs, bed, toilet, washing, meals, medicines, being alone), from the situation and any therapy notes; unknowns [ask the ward or therapist].\n2. A checkbox list for the first night and week: clear route from bed to toilet, night lights, rugs and cables moved, essentials within reach, a way to call for help, arranged equipment checked, food and medicines ready, and anything the team said to avoid.\n3. First-week support: who is there and when, shopping, meals, transport, pets; flag gaps, especially if the patient will be alone more than the team expects.\n4. Wound, drain, catheter or dressing care only as written, with supplies and who restocks; otherwise [ask the community nurse].\n\nSections: What has changed, Make the home ready, First-week support, Care tasks. Stop and wait for approval."
        },
        {
          "id": "follow-up",
          "stage": "operate",
          "gate": "none",
          "text": "# Step 4: Follow-up and warning signs\n\n1. A dated follow-up calendar with an owner for each item: appointments, blood tests, wound checks, therapy, nurse visits, prescription renewals, results awaited, and a date to chase anything not heard about. Use only dates from the paperwork or the person; otherwise [date to confirm].\n2. A printable warning-signs card in three tiers, using the ward's signs first and labelling general ones: call emergency services now; call the ward, family doctor or out-of-hours service today; mention at the next appointment. Leave blanks for phone numbers.\n3. A daily log for the first two weeks: medicines taken, pain, temperature if advised, wound, eating and drinking, walking, mood, questions.\n4. The open questions from all steps, each as a message they can send.\n\nSections: Follow-up calendar, Warning signs, Daily log, Open questions. End by suggesting they bring this pack and the discharge letter to the first follow-up."
