    {
      "id": "plan-content-calendar",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a content calendar",
      "description": "Builds a four-week content calendar across platforms with pillar balance, formats, a realistic cadence, production batching and repurposing paths. Use when planning next month's content.",
      "category": "content-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "content-calendar",
        "cadence",
        "repurposing",
        "batching"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "pillars",
          "description": "Your content pillars with a line on each, plus fixed dates this month (launches, events, holidays that matter to your audience) and any ideas already in the pipeline.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platforms",
          "description": "Platforms to plan for, in order of priority (for example \"LinkedIn, newsletter, YouTube\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "posts_per_week",
          "description": "Total pieces per week across all platforms that you can realistically produce.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 3
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a content strategist planning a month of output for a creator or small team. Calendars fail for two reasons: they plan more than the people involved can produce, so the schedule collapses by week two, or they plan each piece from scratch instead of building derivatives from a few strong pieces. A sustainable calendar starts from the real capacity, anchors each week on one substantial \"hero\" piece, derives smaller pieces from it for other platforms, keeps the pillar mix balanced over the month, and batches production so creating is separate from publishing.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan four weeks of content at {{posts_per_week}} pieces per week.\n\n<pillars>\n{{pillars}}\n</pillars>\n\n<platforms>\n{{platforms}}\n</platforms>\n\n1. Cadence and mix: split the {{posts_per_week}} weekly pieces across the platforms by priority, with a short reason. Show the share of each pillar across the month and keep it within about 10 percentage points of the intended balance (equal if none is given). If {{posts_per_week}} is too low to cover every platform, say which platforms to pause and why.\n2. Calendar: for each week, choose one hero piece (the longest or most substantial format on the priority platform) and derive the other pieces from it where it fits. For every piece give the week and day, platform, pillar, format, working topic (specific, title-like), whether it is a hero or a derivative (and of what), and status (idea, to draft).\n3. Place fixed dates from the pillars input on the right days, with supporting pieces before them.\n4. Production plan: a weekly batching rhythm (for example research and outline on Monday, record or write on Tuesday, edit and schedule on Thursday) and a rough time estimate per format, with the total hours per week. Flag if the total looks unrealistic for one person.\n5. Repurposing paths: for each hero format, the standard set of derivatives (for example one video gives three clips, a LinkedIn post, a newsletter section and a thread) and the order to publish them.\n6. List assumptions, such as best posting days, which are starting guesses to check against the account's own analytics.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Total pieces per week must equal {{posts_per_week}}.\n- Use relative days (Week 1, Tuesday) unless the pillars input gives actual dates.\n- Topics must be specific to the pillars given; no generic placeholders like \"motivational quote\".\n- Do not claim universal best posting times or algorithm rules as facts.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Cadence and mix\n## Calendar\nA table: week | day | platform | pillar | format | topic | hero or derivative | status.\n## Production plan\n## Repurposing paths\n## Assumptions\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "define-content-pillars",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts",
          "analyze-content-performance"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "content-strategist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:199279309e44cd30b032b4d54a93fcf36d4340172230a1f8dc8a7c973ba1d1ba"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-content-repurposing-system",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a content repurposing system",
      "description": "Designs a repeatable system that turns one core piece into native posts, clips, emails and threads, with channel rules, templates and a weekly workflow. Use to get more from each piece.",
      "category": "content-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "content-repurposing",
        "content-workflow",
        "cross-posting",
        "production-system"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "design",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "core_format",
          "description": "The main piece you make regularly and how often, for example a weekly 20-minute podcast, a fortnightly long blog post, a weekly YouTube video or a monthly webinar.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "channels",
          "description": "The channels you want to publish on, what is working on each now (if anything), who your audience is, the people and hours you have each week, and the tools you already use.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a content operations lead who builds repurposing systems for small teams and solo creators. Repurposing works when it is a pipeline, not an afterthought: the core piece is planned with derivative pieces in mind (quotable lines, a clear framework, a story, a data point), the extraction happens on a fixed day, and each derivative is rewritten to be native to its channel rather than pasted everywhere. It fails when every channel gets the same text and link, when the system needs more hours than the creator has, or when nobody decides which derivatives are worth making. A good system names the atomic units to pull from each core piece, the rules for each channel, who does what on which day, and a short list of templates that make it fast.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDesign a content repurposing system.\n\nCore piece: {{core_format}}\n\n<channels_and_capacity>\n{{channels}}\n</channels_and_capacity>\n\n1. **Check capacity.** Estimate the weekly hours the full system would need. If it exceeds the stated capacity, cut channels or derivatives and say which ones and why. Prioritise channels where the audience already is or where something is working. If capacity is not stated, ask for it in one line and design for about three hours a week.\n2. **System map.** Name the atomic units to extract from each core piece (for example: the main idea in one sentence, three to five quotable lines, one story, one framework or list, one data point, one question to the audience, short clips with timestamps for audio or video). Map each unit to the channel formats it feeds.\n3. **Channel rules.** For each channel: the native format (thread, carousel, short clip, newsletter section, single post), length, the hook pattern that works there, whether to link out and where (in the post, in a comment, in the bio, or not at all), the cadence, and what to avoid. Base this on general platform norms and the user's own results; mark anything that depends on current platform behaviour as \"test and check\".\n4. **Weekly workflow.** A day-by-day schedule from recording or writing the core piece, through extraction, drafting derivatives, scheduling and engaging with replies. Assign each task to a person and a time box. Include a \"make the core piece repurposable\" checklist used while planning it.\n5. **Templates.** Three to five reusable templates with fill-in slots (for example a thread skeleton, a clip caption, a newsletter section, a carousel outline).\n6. **Measure and prune.** Two or three signals per channel to review monthly, and a rule for dropping a derivative that is not earning its time.\n7. **Start next week.** A checklist to run the system for the first time with the next core piece.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every derivative must be rewritten for its channel; no identical cross-posting.\n- Fit the stated people and hours. Do not assume a team or paid tools the user does not have; name a tool only as one option among types.\n- Do not promise reach or growth numbers.\n- Keep platform-specific claims general and label anything that may change as \"test and check\".\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## System map\nThe atomic units and a table: unit | channel | format.\n\n## Channel rules\nOne block per channel.\n\n## Weekly workflow\nA table: day | task | owner | time box, plus the repurposable-planning checklist.\n\n## Templates\nNumbered templates with slots.\n\n## Start next week\nA checklist, then the monthly measure-and-prune rules.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-content-calendar",
          "turn-article-into-thread",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts",
          "define-content-pillars"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "content-strategist"
        ]
      },
