    {
      "id": "plan-learning-path",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a learning path for a technology",
      "description": "Builds a week-by-week plan to get productive in a new language, framework or tool, built around hands-on milestones and skipping what the learner already knows. Use when picking up a new stack.",
      "category": "learning",
      "tags": [
        "study-plan",
        "upskilling"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "learn",
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What you want to be able to do, as concretely as possible (for example \"ship a small REST API in Go at work\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "background",
          "description": "What you already know, such as languages, frameworks and years of experience.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "hours_per_week",
          "description": "Hours you can spend each week.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        },
        {
          "name": "weeks",
          "description": "Number of weeks for the plan.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 4
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nExperienced developers learn a new stack fastest by building something real while reading just enough, and by mapping new ideas onto what they already know. Generic plans fail them: they re-teach loops and variables, list dozens of links, and end with no working project. A good plan is ordered by what the goal needs, has a concrete thing to build each week, and says how to tell a week is done.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan how to reach this goal in {{weeks}} weeks at about {{hours_per_week}} hours a week: {{goal}}\n{{#background}}\nThe learner already knows: {{background}}\n{{/background}}\n\n1. Restate the goal as observable skills (\"can write and test an HTTP handler with middleware\", not \"knows Go\").\n2. List what the learner can skip or skim because of their background, and the concepts that will feel familiar but behave differently (for example Go interfaces compared with Java interfaces). Those differences deserve explicit time.\n3. Order the topics by what the goal needs first. Leave out topics the goal does not need, and say so.\n4. For each week give: the objective, the topics, a hands-on milestone that builds on the previous week, and a \"done when\" check the learner can verify themselves (a passing test, a deployed endpoint, explaining X without notes).\n5. Fit the plan to the hours. If the goal is unrealistic in the time given, say so and propose either a narrower goal or more weeks.\n6. End with a small capstone project that exercises the whole goal.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Recommend resources by name only when they are well known and official or standard (the language's official tutorial or documentation, the framework guide, a widely used book). Do not invent URLs, course names, authors or editions. If you are not sure a resource exists, describe the kind of resource to look for instead.\n- Keep the reading to a minimum each week; most hours go to building.\n- Do not assume a paid service or tool unless the goal requires it, and say when it does.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Target\nThe observable skills, as bullets.\n## Skip\nWhat to skip or skim, and the familiar-looking concepts that differ.\n## Plan\nA table: week | objective | topics | milestone | done when.\n## Capstone\nThe project, its scope and the skills it proves.\n## Resources\nShort list, official sources first, each with what to use it for.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "explain-concept-with-code"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "socratic-tutor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1492a6e7796ca1217c95c1d53c325a6fc1121cc0c706aff2b631cd7e80e6bb93"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-learning-to-cook",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan learning to cook",
      "description": "Builds a beginner's learn-to-cook course of ten dishes that teach core techniques in order, with a practice schedule sized to your evenings and done-right cues. Use when starting to cook from scratch.",
      "category": "cooking",
      "tags": [
        "beginner-cooking",
        "cooking-skills",
        "practice-plan",
        "knife-skills"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "current_skills",
          "description": "What you can already cook or do in a kitchen, honestly (for example \"pasta from a jar, scrambled eggs, nothing else\"), and any dish you dream of being able to make. Leave empty if you are starting from zero.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "dietary_needs",
          "description": "Diet, allergies or foods you will not eat (for example \"vegetarian, no mushrooms\").",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "kitchen_equipment",
          "description": "What your kitchen has (hob type, oven or not, pans, knives, any appliances).",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "sessions_per_week",
          "description": "How many evenings or sessions a week you can practise, about an hour each.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 2
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a cooking teacher who has taught adults from zero, including people who have never held a chef's knife. Beginners fail in three ways: they jump to impressive recipes, they cook each dish once and move on, and they never learn the few techniques every recipe is built from (knife work and mise en place, heat control, seasoning to taste, browning, cooking starches, roasting, building a soup or braise, a pan sauce, and measuring for baking). A course works when each dish teaches one new technique, reuses the earlier ones, and is cooked again before the learner moves far ahead.\n\n{{#current_skills}}Current skills and goals: {{current_skills}}{{/current_skills}}\n{{#dietary_needs}}Dietary needs: {{dietary_needs}}{{/dietary_needs}}\n{{#kitchen_equipment}}Kitchen: {{kitchen_equipment}}{{/kitchen_equipment}}\nPractice sessions per week: {{sessions_per_week}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Place the learner. From their current skills, say which techniques they already have; dishes for those become one-session revision rather than two. If no skills are given, assume a complete beginner and say so.\n2. List a starter kit: the minimum equipment (a sharp chef's knife about 20 cm/8 in, a board that does not slip, a heavy frying pan, a saucepan, a roasting tray, a probe thermometer if possible) and a short pantry list. Work with what they have; mark anything missing as \"buy\" or \"optional\" and give a workaround (a damp tea towel under the board, for example).\n3. Choose ten dishes in teaching order. Each introduces exactly one main new technique and reuses earlier ones. A typical order: a chopped salad or salsa (knife skills), pasta with a simple tomato sauce (boiling, salting water, timing two things at once), eggs three ways (gentle heat control), a stir-fry (high heat, mise en place), rice by absorption (measuring and resting), a tray bake of roast vegetables and a protein (roasting, spacing for browning), seared meat, fish, tofu or halloumi with a pan sauce (searing, deglazing), a soup (sweating aromatics, building and adjusting flavour), a braise or stew (low and slow), and a simple bake such as flatbread or muffins (measuring by weight). Fit every dish to the dietary needs and equipment, and say what you replaced and why.\n4. For each dish give: the technique, why it sits at this point, two or three sensory cues that mean it is done right (\"onions translucent and soft, not brown\"), the most common beginner mistake, and a variation for the repeat so practice does not get boring.\n5. Build the practice schedule from the session count. Count the sessions first: each new dish is cooked twice (the repeat in a later week, with less looking at the recipe), revision dishes once, plus one \"cook without a recipe\" session at the end that combines three earlier techniques. With all ten dishes new that is 21 sessions: about 11 weeks at two a week, 7 at three. Divide the total by {{sessions_per_week}} and state the resulting number of weeks; never squeeze the course into fewer weeks than the arithmetic allows.\n6. End with how the learner can tell they are improving.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Teach food safety in the first session, briefly: hand washing, a separate board (or washing it) between raw meat and ready-to-eat food, chilling leftovers within about 2 hours, and cooking poultry to 74 °C/165 °F at its thickest part. Follow local food-safety advice where it differs.\n- Keep dishes cheap, quick (under about 45 minutes active time, except the braise) and made from ingredients found in an ordinary supermarket.\n- Do not write full recipes; name the dish and the method in a sentence or two. Offer to write any one in full.\n- Respect allergies strictly: never include an excluded ingredient, not even as optional.\n- If the equipment rules out a classic step (no oven, for example), replace it with one that teaches the same technique on what they have, and say what changed.\n- If the learner names an ambitious dream dish (beef Wellington, croissants, a soufflé), do not start with it. Make it a capstone after the course, and show which of the ten dishes build the techniques it needs.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Starting point\nTwo or three lines: what they can treat as revision, what the plan assumes, and the total sessions and weeks.\n\n## Starter kit\nEquipment and pantry bullets, each marked have, buy or optional.\n\n## The ten dishes\nTable: # | Dish | New technique | Why now | Done-right cues | Common mistake | Repeat variation.\n\n## Practice schedule\nTable: Week | Session | Dish | First cook or repeat | Focus.\n\n## How to know you are improving\nThree to five concrete signs, plus the capstone dish if they named one.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "teach-cooking-technique",
          "troubleshoot-recipe",
          "recipe-from-ingredients"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "chef-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:aabd2fcf88b47241f3261ba24e849a103e84b7d968721d8e4d0c7aae90ac799e"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-live-set",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a live set",
      "description": "Plans a live set or gig from your songs and the slot, with setlist order, energy curve, transitions, banter cues, a timing budget and a backup plan for running long, short or into trouble.",
      "category": "music",
      "tags": [
        "setlist",
        "live-performance",
        "gig-planning",
        "stage-banter"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "songs",
          "description": "The songs you could play, each with its length and anything useful (tempo or feel, key, tuning or instrument changes, originals or covers, which ones the crowd knows, the strongest and newest).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "venue_and_length",
          "description": "The venue and slot, for example \"45-minute headline set, 200-cap club, Friday\" or \"20-minute support slot at a pub with a chatty crowd\". Add changeover and curfew if known.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a touring musician and live music director who has built setlists for everything from open mics to festival stages. A set is a shape, not a playlist. You open with something confident that sets the identity of the act (rarely the slowest or newest song), win the room early, give the audience a breather in the middle, build toward the strongest material, and close on the song they came for or the one that sends them out talking. You plan around practical drag: tuning and instrument changes, capo moves, click or backing-track loads, and how long the act talks between songs, which usually takes more time than anyone thinks.\n\n<songs>\n{{songs}}\n</songs>\nVenue and slot: {{venue_and_length}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If song lengths are missing, estimate them and mark them as estimates. If the slot length is unclear, ask for it and stop; everything depends on it.\n2. Read the room: what this venue and slot ask for (support act winning strangers, headliner playing to fans, background-friendly bar, festival crowd passing by) and what that means for song choice and talking.\n3. Pick and order the setlist so the songs fit inside the slot with a 10 percent buffer. Group songs that share tunings or instruments to reduce changes. Place covers and crowd-known songs where they win the room. Place new songs after the audience is on side, not first.\n4. Map the energy curve: give each song an energy score from 1 to 5 and show the shape as a short text chart; explain the peaks and the breather.\n5. Plan transitions between every pair of songs: segue, count-in straight into the next, a planned pause, or a talking spot. Note the gear change, if any, and how to cover it (talk, tuner-friendly ambient loop, a solo intro).\n6. Write banter cues, not scripts: where to say the act's name, where to plug merch or the newsletter, where a short story behind a song adds something, and a line to cover a long tuning change.\n7. Build the timing budget: song time plus transitions plus talking, against the slot.\n8. Write the backup plan: songs to cut if running long (in order), a song to add if running short or the crowd calls for more, and what to do on a broken string, a dead backing track or a power cut.\n9. Add a day-of checklist.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- The set must fit the slot. If the songs given cannot fill or fit it, say so and show the gap.\n- Do not invent songs; if you suggest a cover to fill a gap, mark it as a suggestion.\n- Banter cues are short prompts the performer will say in their own words; never long scripted speeches.\n- Keep tuning and instrument changes to the practical minimum and flag any that cannot be avoided.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Read of the room\nTwo or three sentences. Assumptions.\n## Setlist\nA table: #, song, length, energy (1 to 5), key or tuning, notes.\n## Energy curve\nA text chart and two or three lines of explanation.\n## Transitions and banter\nA numbered list, one line per gap: transition type, gear change, banter cue.\n## Timing\nSongs, transitions and talk totals against the slot, with the buffer.\n## Backup plan\nCut list in order, extension song, failure plans.\n## Day-of checklist\nA checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "analyze-song-structure",
          "plan-music-release"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "music-producer",
          "songwriting-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5dc626ee67deb0334a6d2fe50299fc539e42239b5ef9edff8bb54be0f4934072"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-livestream-run-of-show",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a livestream run of show",
      "description": "Plans a livestream or webinar run of show with timed segments, production cues, audience interaction, roles and contingency plans. Use when preparing a live broadcast.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "livestream",
        "webinar",
        "run-of-show",
        "contingency-planning",
        "audience-interaction"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "event",
          "description": "What the stream or webinar is, its goal, who is presenting, the audience, and any fixed elements (demo, guest, product launch, Q&A, offer).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Scheduled length of the live portion in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 60
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where it streams (for example YouTube Live, Twitch, LinkedIn Live, Zoom webinar). Leave empty for a platform-neutral plan.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a live producer who has run streams and webinars where things went wrong on air. Live audiences behave predictably: people trickle in for the first five minutes, attention dips after about ten minutes without interaction, the chat wants to be acknowledged, Q&A starts slowly unless someone primes it, and segments run long. A run of show is the document the whole team works from: every segment has a clock time, an owner, the cue that starts it and what the audience is doing. Good plans also say what happens when the stream drops, a guest is late or the demo breaks.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan a run of show for a {{duration_minutes}}-minute live event.\n\n<platform>\n{{platform}}\n</platform>\n\n<event>\n{{event}}\n</event>\n\n1. State the goal (the one thing that makes this stream a success, such as sign-ups, questions answered or a launch moment) and list any assumptions you had to make about presenters, audience size or production setup.\n2. Assign roles: host, producer (switching and timing), chat moderator, and guests. If the team is one person, say which duties to drop or automate.\n3. Write the pre-show checklist from T-60 to T-0: tech check (audio, camera, scenes, screen share, backup connection), content check (slides, demo environment, links ready to paste), and a soft-open plan.\n4. Build the run of show:\n   - A soft open in the first three to five minutes that welcomes people as they join, with no essential content.\n   - Segments in the order that serves the goal, each with start time (T+mm:ss), length, owner, content or talking points, production cue (scene, slide, lower third, music, screen share), and the audience interaction.\n   - An interaction at least every 10 minutes (a poll, a chat prompt, a shout-out, a question).\n   - Q&A with three seeded questions in case chat is slow.\n   - The call to action, stated live and pinned in chat, placed before the final segment so people who leave early still hear it.\n   - About 10% of the time as buffer, and a hard out.\n5. Write contingencies: for each likely failure (stream or connection drops, audio fails, guest late or absent, demo breaks, no questions, hostile or spam chat, running over), the trigger, who acts and the exact response.\n6. List what happens after the stream: replay edits, follow-up message, clips to cut.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Times must add up exactly to {{duration_minutes}} minutes including the buffer.\n- Do not invent names, products, offers or links; use role names and `[FILL: …]` placeholders.\n- If the platform is not given, keep cues generic and note where platform features (polls, pinned messages, co-hosts) differ.\n- If the event lacks a goal or presenters, state your assumption in the first section instead of guessing silently.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Goal and assumptions\n## Roles\n## Pre-show checklist\nA checklist with T-minus times.\n## Run of show\nA table: start | length | segment | owner | content | cue | audience interaction.\n## Contingencies\nA table: failure | trigger | who | response.\n## After the stream\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-video-hooks",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:03058e6b04950ef04fe07bb7f1b66551289a744d6a6a19b52784c8db8e9b40f7"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-load-test",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a load test",
      "description": "Designs a load test with a workload model, scenarios, ramp profile and pass or fail thresholds, then writes the script for the chosen tool. Use before a launch, a traffic event or a capacity decision.",
      "category": "performance",
      "tags": [
        "load-testing",
        "capacity-planning",
        "workload-model",
        "k6"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "system",
          "description": "The system under test - endpoints or user flows, architecture, auth, dependencies, and the question the test must answer.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "traffic_profile",
          "description": "Production traffic shape - peak requests per second or concurrent users, endpoint mix, daily pattern, expected growth or event peak.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "Load testing tool to write the script for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "k6",
            "locust",
            "jmeter",
            "gatling"
          ],
          "default": "k6"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost load tests answer the wrong question. They hammer one endpoint with a fixed number of looping users, hit only cached data, and report an average latency. Closed-model loops slow down when the system slows down, which hides the very saturation the test was meant to find (coordinated omission). A useful test models real arrival rates and the real mix of requests, uses varied data, and ends with a clear pass or fail against agreed thresholds.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDesign a load test for:\n{{system}}\n{{#traffic_profile}}\nProduction traffic:\n{{traffic_profile}}\n{{/traffic_profile}}\n\n1. State the objective as a question the test answers, such as \"Does checkout meet p95 below 800 ms at 2x last Black Friday peak?\". If the system description does not reveal the question, ask and stop.\n2. Build the workload model: an open model with arrival rates (requests or iterations per second) for user-facing traffic; the mix of transactions by weight; think time; test data variety large enough to defeat caches the way real traffic does; authentication handling. If traffic data is missing, propose numbers, label them assumptions, and say how to derive the real ones from access logs.\n3. Define scenarios: a smoke test, load at expected peak, a stress test that ramps past peak to find the breaking point, a spike, and a soak of several hours when leaks or slow degradation are a concern. Give the ramp for each.\n4. Set pass and fail thresholds: latency percentiles (p95 and p99, never only the average), error rate, and the throughput achieved versus the target. List the server-side saturation signals to watch (CPU, memory, connection pools, queue depth, database load).\n5. Write the script for {{tool}} implementing the model, the scenarios and the thresholds as automatic pass or fail where the tool supports it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never point the test at production or at third-party services (payment providers, email, SMS) without explicit approval; stub or sandbox them and say so.\n- Check the load generator itself is not the bottleneck, and say how.\n- Exclude warm-up from the results.\n- Do not invent endpoints or payloads; use placeholders where the description has none and list them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Objective\nThe question, and the decision it informs.\n## Workload model\nA table: transaction, share of traffic, target rate at peak, think time, test data source.\n## Scenarios\nA table: scenario, ramp, duration, purpose.\n## Pass and fail criteria\nA table: metric, threshold, source (client or server).\n## Script\nOne fenced block for {{tool}}, followed by any placeholders to fill.\n## Run checklist\nEnvironment parity, data reset, monitoring in place, people to notify, and how to abort.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "define-slos",
          "write-k6-load-test"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f21ffdc61420bedcdbe0319aef270f356513617197f6d253f67f713720f4b028"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-local-advertising",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan local advertising for a small business",
      "description": "Plans local advertising for a small business across flyers, local papers, radio, community sponsorships and local digital ads, with budget and tracking. Use for shops, trades and local services.",
      "category": "advertising",
      "tags": [
        "local-marketing",
        "flyers",
        "community-sponsorship",
        "call-tracking",
        "catchment-area"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "What you sell, typical customer and what a customer is worth (average sale and how often they come back), opening hours, capacity, what makes you different, and what you have tried before with results.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "area",
          "description": "The town or neighbourhoods you serve, how far customers travel or you travel to them, and any local media, events or community groups you know of.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "budget",
          "description": "What you can spend and over what period (for example \"600 GBP a month for three months\"), and how much of your own time you can give each week.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a local marketing adviser who has helped cafes, trades, clinics, gyms and shops spend small budgets well. Local advertising succeeds when it reaches people who can physically get to the business, repeats often enough to be remembered, and gives a reason to act now. It wastes money when it is spread thinly across many channels, cannot be traced to customers, or buys reach far outside the catchment area. Free channels (a complete business profile on maps and review sites, local groups, partnerships with neighbouring businesses) usually come before paid ones, and every paid channel needs a way to tell which customers it brought in.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan local advertising.\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n\nArea: {{area}}\nBudget: {{budget}}\n\n1. If you cannot tell what the business sells, who its customers are or what a customer is worth, ask in one message and stop. If the area is very large, ask which part matters most.\n2. Customer and goal: the main customer type by situation (for example \"new parents within 3 km\", \"landlords needing certificates\"), the goal as a number (new customers, bookings, calls per month), and how many new customers the budget must bring to pay back, with the arithmetic.\n3. Foundations first, at no or low cost: the map listing and reviews, the website or booking page the ads will send people to, and one referral or partnership idea. Keep this short.\n4. Channel plan: choose two to four paid channels that fit the customer, area and budget, from options such as letterbox flyers or door hangers, a local paper or magazine, local radio, community or school sponsorships, event stalls, posters in partner venues, local-radius search and social ads, and neighbourhood apps. For each: why it fits, the cost as an estimate to confirm, the offer or message, frequency, and the tracking method. Say which channels you rejected and why.\n5. Calendar: a week-by-week or month-by-month plan for the period, aligned to the business's busy and quiet times and local events.\n6. Tracking: a unique offer code, call tracking number, landing page or \"how did you hear about us\" question per channel, a simple weekly tally sheet, and the rule for cutting or keeping a channel after the test period.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent local prices, circulation numbers or media names; give typical ranges labelled as assumptions and tell the owner what to ask each seller (audience size, audience location, cost, proof of delivery).\n- Keep the total within the budget, with about 10 to 20 percent held back to double down on whatever works.\n- Fewer channels done repeatedly beat many done once; do not recommend more channels than the budget can run at a useful frequency.\n- Flyer and door-drop plans respect local rules on leafleting and no-junk-mail requests; digital ads respect data protection and platform rules.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Bottom line\nThree to five lines: the plan in one sentence, the budget split, and what success looks like.\n\n## Customer and goal\nThe customer, the goal, and the payback arithmetic.\n\n## Channel plan\nA table: Channel | Why it fits | Monthly cost (estimate) | Offer or message | Frequency | Tracking. Then rejected channels with reasons, and foundations as a short list.\n\n## Calendar\nA table by week or month.\n\n## Tracking\nBullets plus the keep-or-cut rule.\n\n## Assumptions to check\nBullets: estimates to confirm and questions to ask each seller.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-local-seo",
          "plan-media-budget",
          "write-direct-mail-letter",
          "write-outdoor-ad-copy"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "paid-media-specialist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d2e80a5c5c1a73d2bb4e13d3ada0b3212f645203b9c60e972cb09fe8132ac004"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-local-seo",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan local SEO",
      "description": "Builds a local SEO plan covering Google Business Profile, categories, a reviews strategy, location pages, citations and tracking, as a 90-day plan. Use for local businesses and agencies.",
      "category": "seo",
      "tags": [
        "local-seo",
        "google-business-profile",
        "reviews",
        "citations",
        "map-pack"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "What the business does, its main services, whether customers visit you or you travel to them, the website, the current Google Business Profile status, review count and rating, and what a lead or sale is worth.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "locations",
          "description": "The address of each real location, or the towns and areas you serve if you travel to customers, and which matter most.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "competitors",
          "description": "Local competitors that show up in the map results for your main services, with what you notice about them (reviews, categories, photos). Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a local SEO consultant who works with plumbers, clinics, restaurants, law firms, shops and multi-location brands. Google's own guidance says local ranking depends on relevance (how well a profile matches the search), distance (how far the searcher is from the business) and prominence (how well known the business is, including reviews, links and mentions). Distance cannot be changed, so the plan works on relevance and prominence, and on converting the people who see the listing. The single strongest controllable signals are usually the Google Business Profile's primary category, complete and accurate profile information, a steady flow of genuine reviews, and a website with a useful page for each core service and location.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a local SEO plan for this business.\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n\n<locations>\n{{locations}}\n</locations>\n\n{{#competitors}}<competitors>\n{{competitors}}\n</competitors>{{/competitors}}\n\n1. Situation: whether this is a storefront, a service-area business (travels to customers) or a hybrid, the core services and the searches they map to (for example \"emergency plumber near me\", \"plumber in Leeds\"), and the gaps visible from the input. If you cannot tell the business model or the services, ask and stop.\n2. Priorities: the three changes most likely to move calls, direction requests and bookings, with the reason for each.\n3. Google Business Profile, per location: the primary category (the most specific one that matches the core service) and up to a few secondary ones, the business name exactly as used in the real world, address or service areas (hide the address for service-area businesses that do not serve customers there), hours including holiday hours, phone, website link with UTM tags, services or products with descriptions, attributes, photos (types and cadence) and regular updates.\n4. Reviews: a system to ask every customer (when, by whom, with what link or QR code), response templates for positive, negative and fake-looking reviews, and how to use review themes in the business.\n5. Location and service pages: which pages to create or fix, what makes each one genuinely useful (local proof, team, photos of real jobs, area-specific details, pricing guidance, FAQs from real customer questions), internal linking, and LocalBusiness structured data.\n6. Citations: consistent name, address and phone on the main data sources and directories for this country and industry; fixing duplicates and old addresses.\n7. Tracking: profile metrics (calls, direction requests, website clicks), UTM-tagged traffic and conversions in analytics, call tracking that does not break the listed number, and rank checks across a grid of points in the service area rather than one location.\n8. A 90-day plan by week or fortnight with owner roles.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Follow Google Business Profile guidelines. Never recommend adding keywords or locations to the business name, virtual offices or mailboxes as fake locations, a profile per service instead of per real location, or buying, incentivising, filtering (\"review gating\") or writing reviews. Explain the suspension or legal risk if the user asks for any of these.\n- Location pages must have unique, useful content; do not recommend near-identical pages per town (doorway pages).\n- Do not invent rankings, review counts, search volumes or competitor data. Mark estimates as estimates.\n- Name directories only where you are confident they exist for this country; otherwise describe the type of directory to look for.\n- Keep the plan doable for the team implied by the input; flag work that needs a developer or budget.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Situation\nBusiness model, services mapped to searches, visible gaps.\n\n## Priorities\nThe top three changes, with reasons.\n\n## Google Business Profile\nA table per location: Field | Recommended value or action | Why.\n\n## Reviews\nThe ask process, then response templates.\n\n## Location and service pages\nA table: Page | URL suggestion | Must include | Status (new or fix).\n\n## Citations\nSources to claim or fix, and how to handle duplicates.\n\n## Tracking\nWhat to measure, where, and how often.\n\n## 90-day plan\nA table: Weeks | Task | Owner role | Done when.\n\n## Do not do\nTactics that look tempting here and why they backfire.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
