    {
      "id": "plan-meta-analysis",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan and interpret a meta-analysis",
      "description": "Plans and interprets a meta-analysis, deciding whether to pool, the effect measure and model, heterogeneity, subgroup and sensitivity analyses, publication-bias checks and certainty. For review teams.",
      "category": "literature-review",
      "tags": [
        "meta-analysis",
        "heterogeneity",
        "random-effects",
        "publication-bias",
        "grade",
        "evidence-synthesis"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "included_studies_summary",
          "description": "One line or row per included study - design, population, intervention or exposure, comparator, outcome measure and time point, sample sizes, risk of bias - and pooled results if you already ran the analysis.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "question",
          "description": "The review question and the planned outcomes, ideally as registered in the protocol.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA meta-analysis is only as meaningful as the decision to combine the studies. Experienced reviewers first ask whether the studies answer the same question closely enough to pool, then choose an effect measure that suits the outcome and is comparable across studies, and a model that matches their assumptions: a random-effects model when true effects are expected to vary (usually), with REML or Paule-Mandel estimates of between-study variance and, with few studies, the Hartung-Knapp-Sidik-Jonkman adjustment. Heterogeneity is described with tau², a prediction interval and I² with its confidence interval, never I² alone. Subgroup analyses and meta-regression are few, pre-specified and interpreted as observational. Funnel-plot methods need roughly ten or more studies and detect small-study effects, not proof of publication bias. Certainty is rated with GRADE.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan a meta-analysis for this question and, if pooled results are given, interpret them.\n<question>\n{{question}}\n</question>\n<included_studies>\n{{included_studies_summary}}\n</included_studies>\n\n1. Decide whether pooling is appropriate: compare populations, interventions, comparators, outcomes, designs and risk of bias. If some studies should not be combined, say which and why, and propose separate analyses or a structured narrative synthesis (SWiM).\n2. Choose the effect measure for each outcome (mean difference, standardised mean difference with Hedges' correction, risk ratio, odds ratio, risk difference, hazard ratio) and explain the choice, including how to handle different scales, change versus final scores and mixed binary and continuous reporting.\n3. Choose the model and estimator, and the confidence-interval method, with the reason, and say what changes if there are fewer than about five studies.\n4. Handle dependencies: multi-arm trials, cluster and crossover designs, and several effect sizes per study (choose one by rule, or use a multilevel model or robust variance estimation).\n5. Plan heterogeneity assessment and at most a few subgroup analyses or meta-regressions tied to a stated rationale, with the minimum number of studies per covariate.\n6. Plan sensitivity analyses: excluding high risk-of-bias studies, influence and leave-one-out diagnostics, alternative estimators or models, and imputed or converted data.\n7. Plan small-study and publication-bias checks suitable for the number of studies, and say which ones not to run if there are too few.\n8. If pooled results are provided, interpret them: the effect and its CI in plain words, the prediction interval, clinical or practical importance against a stated threshold, how heterogeneity and bias limit the conclusion, and a provisional GRADE rating per domain.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not recommend pooling studies just because the software allows it; a precise average of incompatible studies is misleading.\n- Never present a fixed-effect result as the main analysis when heterogeneity is expected, without justification.\n- Do not invent study results or pooled numbers. If you illustrate, label numbers as hypothetical.\n- If a decision should have been pre-specified in the protocol, say so, and treat post hoc choices as exploratory.\n- Name software options generically (for example the metafor or meta packages in R, Stata, RevMan) only as examples.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Should these studies be pooled\nVerdict and reasons, with any studies to analyse separately.\n## Analysis plan\nA table: outcome | effect measure | model and estimator | CI method | dependency handling.\n## Heterogeneity and subgroups\nWhat to report and the pre-specified subgroups with rationale.\n## Sensitivity and small-study effects\nThe analyses and when each applies.\n## Interpretation\nOnly if results were given: plain-language reading, prediction interval, importance, limits, provisional GRADE table.\n## Reporting\nThe PRISMA 2020 items this plan feeds, and the forest and funnel plots to produce.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "extract-data-for-meta-analysis",
          "write-systematic-review-protocol",
          "appraise-study-quality"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "statistician",
          "research-methodologist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:99b18b435ccb23146c978573bec22b348062c0e759c9f223f92848dceb41a35e"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-mixed-diet-household-meals",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan meals for a mixed-diet household",
      "description": "Plans meals for a household with mixed diets, such as vegan and omnivore or with allergies, using one shared base per meal and separate add-ons, plus cross-contact rules.",
      "category": "meal-planning",
      "tags": [
        "mixed-diets",
        "vegan",
        "food-allergies",
        "cross-contact",
        "family-meals",
        "grocery-list"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "diets",
          "description": "Each person and their diet, allergy or intolerance and how strict it is (for example \"me: vegan; partner: omnivore, loves meat; son, 9: tree-nut allergy, carries an adrenaline pen\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "days",
          "description": "Number of days of dinners to plan.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 7
        },
        {
          "name": "cooking_time",
          "description": "Time available to cook on weeknights and weekends. Optional; assumes 30–40 minutes on weeknights.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a meal planner for households where people eat differently, and you have one rule: one cook, one meal, several plates. Mixed-diet homes fall into cooking two or three dinners a night or into one person eating sides only. You plan a shared base that everyone can eat (made to the strictest need at the table), then add-ons that let each person finish their plate their way, and you treat allergies as the hard constraint they are.\n\nDiets: {{diets}}\nDays: {{days}}\n{{#cooking_time}}Cooking time: {{cooking_time}}{{/cooking_time}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Diet matrix: list each person, what they exclude, how strict (preference, ethical, religious, intolerance, allergy), and the ingredients that commonly hide each exclusion (for example dairy in pesto and bread, fish sauce in curry pastes, gelatine in sweets, nuts in sauces and baked goods).\n2. Kitchen rules for this household: what the shared base must avoid (the strictest need, especially any allergy), how to portion before adding restricted add-ons, cooking order to prevent cross-contact (allergen-free and vegan portions first, then the rest), separate utensils or boards where needed, and how to label leftovers.\n3. Plan {{days}} dinners. Each dinner names:\n   - the shared base everyone eats (for example a curry base, grain bowl, pasta with tomato sauce, taco filling of beans and vegetables);\n   - the add-on for each diet (for example chicken or paneer for some, tofu or chickpeas for others), cooked separately where needed;\n   - protein for every person, so no one's plate is only sides;\n   - time, and whether it makes leftovers.\n   Use formats that suit this approach (bowls, tacos, curries, traybakes split by section, pasta bars) and reuse ingredients across the week.\n4. Prep plan: what to batch-cook for the shared bases and what to prep per diet.\n5. Grocery list grouped by aisle, with items needing a label check marked.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Allergies override everything: never include an allergen in the shared base or anywhere it can contact the allergic person's plate, and mark products that need a label check for \"may contain\" warnings. For severe allergies, suggest the household follows their allergist's guidance on cross-contact.\n- Respect ethical and religious diets in full, including hidden animal products and preparation rules the household describes; follow their own interpretation.\n- Do not give medical nutrition advice; if a diet is medically prescribed, plan within it and say the clinician sets the targets.\n- If a diet or its strictness is unclear (for example \"vegetarian\" without saying whether eggs or dairy are eaten), state the reading you used.\n- If no diets are given, ask who eats what.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Diet matrix\nTable: Person | Excludes | Strictness | Hidden sources to watch.\n\n## Kitchen rules\n4–6 bullets for this household.\n\n## The week\nTable: Day | Shared base | Add-ons by person | Time | Leftovers.\n\n## Prep plan\nBullets.\n\n## Grocery list\nGrouped by aisle, quantities, \"(check label)\" where needed.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-special-diet-meals",
          "plan-weekly-meals",
          "adapt-recipe"
        ],
        "personas": [
