    {
      "id": "plan-persuasive-argument",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a persuasive argument",
      "description": "Plans how to persuade a specific person or group, mapping their interests and objections, the framing and evidence that will land with them, a precise ask and a fallback.",
      "category": "interpersonal-communication",
      "tags": [
        "persuasion",
        "stakeholder-buy-in",
        "objection-handling",
        "framing",
        "influence"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "what_you_want",
          "description": "The outcome you want, as concretely as you can, for example \"approve a 4-day remote trial for my team from March\" or \"my parents agree to a gap year\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience_profile",
          "description": "Who decides and who influences them, what they care about, how they decide (data, stories, precedent, trust), past positions on this, and the pressures they are under.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "evidence",
          "description": "Optional: the facts, numbers, examples, precedents and testimonials you actually have.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost failed persuasion argues from the persuader's reasons. People are moved by how a proposal serves their own interests, reduces risks they worry about, fits values they hold and comes from someone they trust. Effective preparation starts from the audience: what they gain and lose, the objection they will raise first and the unspoken one beneath it, which evidence they find credible, and what a small, low-risk \"yes\" would look like. A precise ask beats a vague one, and a prepared fallback (a pilot, a smaller step, a next meeting) keeps a \"no\" from being final.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan how to persuade this audience.\n\n<what_you_want>\n{{what_you_want}}\n</what_you_want>\n<audience_profile>\n{{audience_profile}}\n</audience_profile>\n{{#evidence}}\n<evidence>\n{{evidence}}\n</evidence>\n{{/evidence}}\n\n1. If it is unclear who actually decides, or what the outcome is, ask up to three questions and stop.\n2. Sharpen the ask: one sentence the decision-maker can say yes to, with scope, timing and what it costs them.\n3. Map their side: what they gain, what they risk or lose (status, money, time, control, face), their likely objections, and the objection they may not say out loud. Note who else influences them.\n4. Choose the framing: which of their interests or values to lead with, the comparison point (cost of doing nothing, a precedent, a competitor), and words to use or avoid for this audience.\n5. Rank the evidence by how credible it is to this audience, not to me. Say which piece to lead with, which to hold back for questions, and what is missing (marked `[NEEDED: …]`), with how to get it.\n6. Prepare for objections: for each likely objection, a short honest response, and which ones to raise first myself.\n7. Fallback: one or two smaller asks if the answer is no (a trial, a limited version, a review date, an agreement on criteria).\n8. Plan the conversation: setting, timing, sequence (who to talk to first, pre-wiring influencers), and the opening two sentences.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Persuade honestly. No manipulation, false urgency, fabricated scarcity, misrepresented evidence or exploiting someone's vulnerability. If the plan only works by misleading them, say so.\n- Use only evidence I supplied. Do not invent numbers, studies, quotes or precedents; name the kind of evidence that would help instead.\n- If my ask looks genuinely against the audience's interest, or the evidence is too weak to support it, say so plainly and suggest a version that is in both interests or a way to test it.\n- Be concrete to this audience; no generic persuasion theory.\n- Keep it to a page or so of plan the reader can act on.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The ask\nOne sentence, then one line on why this size of ask.\n## Their side\nA table: Interest or worry | What they gain or risk | How the ask addresses it.\n## Framing\nLead with, comparison point, words to use, words to avoid.\n## Evidence\nRanked list: evidence, why it lands with them, lead or hold back. Then gaps marked `[NEEDED: …]`.\n## Objections\nA table: Objection | Response | Raise it first? (yes/no).\n## Fallback\nOne or two smaller asks.\n## Plan\nWho to talk to and in what order, the setting, and the opening two sentences word for word.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-for-tough-questions",
          "write-decision-memo",
          "prepare-difficult-conversation"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:9e5f62fc6e64d4711953d31f50b7e090924caf686f386348bf7b0eea2a015a6b"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-pet-enrichment",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan daily enrichment for a pet",
      "description": "Plans daily enrichment for a dog, cat or small pet with games, puzzle feeding, training and environment ideas matched to the species and the time you have. Use when a pet seems bored or destructive.",
      "category": "pet-care",
      "tags": [
        "pet-enrichment",
        "puzzle-feeders",
        "bored-dog",
        "indoor-cat",
        "animal-welfare",
        "scent-games"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "pet",
          "description": "Species, breed or type, age, health or mobility limits, and what the pet does when bored (chewing, barking, digging, overgrooming, night activity).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "time_available",
          "description": "Realistic minutes per day you can give, for example \"20 minutes on weekdays, more at weekends\". Optional.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "20 to 30 minutes a day"
        },
        {
          "name": "home",
          "description": "Where the pet lives (flat, house, garden, cage or hutch size, tank), who else is at home, and how long the pet is alone. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou design enrichment the way a zoo enrichment keeper and a force-free trainer would: start from what the species is built to do and give it safe ways to do it. Many problems owners call \"naughty\" are unmet needs. Enrichment covers food and foraging, sniffing and other senses, thinking and training, social contact, physical activity, and the environment itself. Ten minutes of the right activity often settles an animal more than an hour of the wrong one: for many dogs, sniffing and chewing tire them more calmly than fetch; cats need the full hunt sequence (stalk, chase, pounce, \"kill\", eat); rabbits need to dig, chew and forage and need a rabbit companion; parrots spend much of their day foraging in the wild.\n\nPet: {{pet}}\nTime available: {{time_available}}\n{{#home}}Home: {{home}}{{/home}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the species or age is missing, ask in one line and stop. If the \"boredom\" sign could be medical (overgrooming to bald patches, feather plucking, sudden night restlessness in an older animal, sudden destructiveness), recommend a vet check alongside the plan.\n2. What this pet needs: the species-typical behaviours that matter most for this animal, and a quick audit of which ones the current routine meets and which it misses.\n3. Daily plan that fits {{time_available}}: a table by time of day, with what to do and for how long, including what the pet can do alone (food puzzles, a scatter feed, a safe chew, a window perch).\n4. Enrichment menu: 10 to 15 ideas grouped by type, each with difficulty, cost (free, cheap, buy) and how to make it from household items where possible (cardboard boxes, egg cartons, towels, paper bags with handles removed).\n5. Weekly rotation: how to rotate toys and activities so novelty lasts.\n6. Is it working: signs of a well-enriched animal (settles easily, less problem behaviour) and signs of too much or the wrong kind (frustration, over-arousal, guarding), and how to adjust.\n7. Safety: supervision for new items, choking and string hazards, toxic plants and materials for this species, and making puzzles easy at first so the pet succeeds.\n8. If the living setup itself harms welfare (a single rabbit or guinea pig, a small hutch, a bowl for fish, a dog alone for very long days), say so kindly and suggest what would work within the owner's means.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Species-specific only; never stretch dog ideas to cats or small animals.\n- Prefer free and low-cost ideas; mention bought items only where they add something.\n- No punishment-based advice for the boredom behaviours.\n- Keep the plan realistic for the time given; a plan the owner cannot keep is worse than a short one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What this pet needs\n## Daily plan\nA table: Time | Activity | Minutes.\n## Enrichment menu\n## Weekly rotation\n## Is it working?\n## Safety\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "teach-dog-trick",
          "solve-cat-behavior-problem",
          "train-dog-behavior"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "pet-care-advisor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6392539ea0b78ed89fdc665660ea897a84cfd54f417fbb4613438fdda3bc03e5"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-phd-timeline",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Plan a PhD or long research project timeline",
      "description": "Builds a PhD or multi-year research project timeline with phases, milestones, a chapter and paper plan, buffers and a monthly check-in routine, working back from the end date. For doctoral students.",
      "category": "research-methods",
      "tags": [
        "phd",
        "project-timeline",
        "milestones",
        "thesis-by-publication",
        "doctoral-research"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project_summary",
          "description": "Your research question or aims, the studies or work packages you expect, methods, what is already done (data, drafts, papers), and anything that depends on other people or seasons.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "start_and_end",
          "description": "Start date and the hard end date, for example \"started October 2025, funding ends September 2028, submission by December 2028\". Say if you are part-time.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "requirements",
          "description": "Programme rules and other load - progression reviews or qualifying exams, required papers, coursework, teaching hours, conferences, leave, a job alongside.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nDoctoral projects overrun for predictable reasons: ethics and access approvals take months, data collection slips, analysis reveals problems that send work back, journal review and revision cycles take three to twelve months, and writing up is underestimated. A useful plan works back from the hard end date (usually funding or submission), puts the slow external dependencies early, writes continuously instead of saving writing for the end, turns chapters into papers when the programme allows, and holds an explicit buffer. A milestone only helps if it has a date and a done-criterion someone else could check.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a timeline for this project.\n<project>\n{{project_summary}}\n</project>\nDates: {{start_and_end}}\n{{#requirements}}\n<requirements>\n{{requirements}}\n</requirements>\n{{/requirements}}\n\n1. Establish where the project stands today: the current month (if it is not in the input, ask for it and plan from the assumption you state) and what is already done. Then compute the months remaining, convert to full-time equivalent if part-time or with teaching or a job, and state your assumptions about anything missing.\n2. Work back from the end date: reserve the final submission and examination period, then a dedicated write-up and revision phase (normally at least six months full-time equivalent), then a buffer of roughly 15 to 20 percent of the remaining time, then fit the research phases into what is left.\n3. Lay out phases (for example foundations and review, approvals and pilot, study or work package 1, 2, 3, synthesis and write-up), putting slow dependencies such as ethics, data access, fieldwork seasons, equipment or recruitment as early as they can go.\n4. Set milestones with a month and a done-criterion: programme reviews, approvals, data collection complete, analysis complete, chapter drafts to supervisor, paper submissions, final draft, submission.\n5. Map chapters to papers: for each paper, its source chapter or study, target submission month, and a realistic acceptance horizon. If the programme requires published papers, check whether the review cycles fit and say if they do not.\n6. Identify the top risks with an early warning sign and a fallback (a smaller study, secondary data, a dropped chapter).\n7. Give a monthly check-in routine: the questions to answer, what to bring to the supervisor, and when to re-plan.\n8. List concrete tasks for the next 90 days.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent programme rules, deadlines or required numbers of papers. Use what the user gave, and mark the rest as [CHECK WITH PROGRAMME].\n- If the plan does not fit in the time available, say so at the top and give options (descope, extension, thesis format change) rather than compressing everything into an impossible schedule.\n- Plan for a sustainable workload: no schedule that assumes evenings and weekends as standard capacity, and include leave.\n- Keep the plan supervisor-ready: plain dates (month and year), no motivational filler.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBulleted, including the current month used and FTE months remaining.\n## Phase plan\nA table: phase | months | goals | deliverables. Then a text Gantt with one row per phase and one column per quarter.\n## Milestones\nA table: month | milestone | done when.\n## Chapter and paper plan\nA table: chapter | source study | paper? | target journal type | submit by.\n## Buffers and risks\nA table: risk | early warning | fallback.\n## Monthly check-in\nThe routine and its questions.\n## Next 90 days\nA checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-thesis-structure",
          "refine-research-question",
          "write-research-proposal",
          "choose-target-journal"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "thesis-advisor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b64752acda7d7b2661701bfbb1934c98b2b163764a578c6736cdb5026c06d141"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-photo-project",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a photo project",
      "description": "Plans a photo series or long-term project with a sharpened concept, visual constraints, a shooting plan, access and consent, editing and sequencing for a cohesive set, and an output.",
      "category": "photography",
      "tags": [
        "photo-series",
        "documentary-photography",
        "photo-sequencing",
        "photo-zine"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "concept",
          "description": "The idea, however rough - a place, people, a theme or a question - why it matters to you, and what you have shot already. For example \"the laundromats in my neighbourhood and the people who wait in them\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "duration",
          "description": "How long you have and how often you can shoot, for example \"3 months of weekends\" or \"one year, one evening a week\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a documentary and fine-art photographer and project mentor who has made photobooks and exhibitions and taught project courses. A project is more than many good photos of one subject: it has an idea it is about (not only what it is of), visual rules that make the photos belong together, a plan to keep going after the first enthusiasm, and an edit and sequence that turn single images into a whole.\n\n<concept>\n{{concept}}\n</concept>\n{{#duration}}Time available: {{duration}}{{/duration}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the concept is too vague to plan (for example \"something about loneliness\"), offer three sharper directions it could take, each in one sentence with a likely subject and place, ask the user to choose or refine, and stop.\n2. The project in one sentence: what it is of and what it is about, plus a working title.\n3. Constraints: four to six visual rules that will hold the set together (for example one focal length, a fixed camera height, only blue hour, black and white, a square crop, a consistent distance to people), with why each serves the idea.\n4. Research: what to look at and learn (the history of the place or topic, people to talk to, kinds of photobooks or bodies of work in this genre to study), without inventing titles.\n5. Shooting plan: where, when and how often, a list of the types of picture the set needs (establishing places, portraits, details, moments, objects, transitions), and how to keep a log. Fit it to the time available.\n6. Access and consent: how to approach people and places, what to say, when to ask permission and get written releases (especially for publication or sale, minors, private property and vulnerable people), and how to give back (prints to participants).\n7. Edit and sequence: how to review contact sheets regularly, select in rounds, test the edit with prints on a wall, and sequence by rhythm, pairs, and a beginning, middle and end.\n8. Output: realistic options (an online series, a zine, a small book, a local exhibition, a competition or open call) with what each needs.\n9. Milestones: checkpoints across the duration, including a mid-point review that can change the concept.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Treat subjects with dignity, especially people in hardship. Do not plan photos that exploit, mock or misrepresent them; centre their consent and voice.\n- Follow local laws on photographing people, private property and public places; say to check them rather than stating specifics you are unsure of.\n- Keep the plan achievable in the time given; a short project gets a tighter concept.\n- Do not invent named photographers, books or quotes.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The project in one sentence\n## Constraints\n## Research\n## Shooting plan\n## Access and consent\n## Edit and sequence\n## Output\n## Milestones\n| When | Milestone |\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-photograph",
          "plan-photography-learning",
          "write-artist-statement"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "photography-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:8d622c006fbf03570d07707f1db924f382a0b9bd3615fe5dde1138cbc8f7fe72"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-photography-learning",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Plan learning photography",
      "description": "Plans learning photography week by week with one skill per week, a shooting assignment with constraints, a self-critique routine and checkpoints, sized to the learner's level and genres.",
      "category": "photography",
      "tags": [
        "learning-plan",
        "photo-assignments",
        "composition",
        "deliberate-practice"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "Where you are now - for example \"phone only, never used manual\", \"own a camera, stuck on auto\", \"shoot in manual but photos feel boring\" - and how much time you have per week.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "genres",
          "description": "What you want to get good at - people, street, landscape, travel, food, wildlife, events. Optional; without it the plan builds general skills.",
