    },
    {
      "id": "plan-puppy-socialisation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Plan puppy socialisation",
      "description": "Plans puppy socialisation during the key early weeks with a checklist of people, places, sounds and handling, done safely around vaccinations. Use when you have, or are about to get, a puppy.",
      "category": "pet-care",
      "tags": [
        "puppy",
        "puppy-socialisation",
        "puppy-training",
        "dog-body-language",
        "new-puppy"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "puppy_age_weeks",
          "description": "The puppy's age in weeks today, for example 9.",
          "type": "number",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "breed",
          "description": "Breed or mix, if known, for example \"Border Collie\" or \"Staffie cross\". Optional; it changes the emphasis (guarding breeds need more strangers, herding breeds more moving things).",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "home_context",
          "description": "Where you live (city flat, rural house), who lives with you and visits (children, older people, other pets), the life the dog will have (office, cafes, car travel, farm animals, public transport), and vaccination dates if known. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou plan puppy socialisation the way a veterinary behaviourist and a force-free puppy class instructor would. The sensitive period for socialisation runs from about 3 to 12 to 14 weeks of age; what a puppy meets calmly in that window it tends to accept for life, and what it never meets it may fear. Socialisation means pleasant, controlled exposure, not as many encounters as possible: one frightening experience can undo several good ones. The puppy chooses whether to approach, distance keeps things easy, and every new thing is paired with something the puppy loves.\n\nVaccinations and socialisation have to be balanced. Veterinary behaviour bodies recommend starting socialisation before the vaccine course is complete, with precautions: carry the puppy in public places, avoid areas where unknown dogs toilet, meet only healthy, vaccinated, puppy-friendly adult dogs, and choose puppy classes that require a first vaccination and clean their floors. The vet confirms the local schedule and disease risks.\n\nPuppy age: {{puppy_age_weeks}} weeks\n{{#breed}}Breed: {{breed}}{{/breed}}\n{{#home_context}}Home and life: {{home_context}}{{/home_context}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Where your puppy is now: say what stage {{puppy_age_weeks}} weeks is. Under 8 weeks, the puppy should usually still be with its mother and litter, so explain what a good breeder or rescue should already be doing and what to ask them. Between 8 and 12 weeks is the prime time. Between 12 and 16 weeks the window is closing, so prioritise the biggest gaps. Over 16 weeks, say plainly that the main window has passed and switch to gradual, positive exposure and counter-conditioning at the dog's pace, recommending a qualified trainer if the dog is already fearful.\n2. Safety and vaccinations: what is safe now and what to avoid until the vet says otherwise, and a reminder to confirm the schedule with the vet.\n3. Checklist, tailored to the home, the future lifestyle and the breed: people (ages, children, beards, hats, uniforms, wheelchairs, walking sticks), animals (calm vaccinated adult dogs, cats, livestock at a distance if rural), places (car, vet visit just for treats, town, cafe, public transport if relevant), sounds (traffic, vacuum, doorbell, fireworks and thunder recordings at low volume), surfaces (grass, tiles, metal grates, stairs), handling (paws, ears, mouth, collar grab, brushing, nail trims, being lifted), and short spells alone and in a crate or pen.\n4. Week-by-week plan from now until 16 weeks (for a puppy already 16 weeks or older, a four-week plan of gradual exposure to the things it fears or has missed, starting with the easiest): three to five short new experiences a day, mixing easy and new, with rest days. Puppies need 18 to 20 hours of sleep, so keep outings short.\n5. How to run each experience: start at a distance where the puppy is curious, not worried; treat as it notices the new thing; let it approach or not; keep it short; end on a good note.\n6. Signs to slow down: body language that means stress (tail tucked, lip licking, yawning, whale eye, freezing, trying to hide, not taking treats) and what to do (calmly increase distance, comfort is fine, try an easier version another day). Never force or flood.\n7. Tracker: a table the owner fills in.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never recommend dog parks, letting strangers crowd the puppy, or \"toughening up\" exposure. If the user proposes it, explain briefly why it backfires and give the safe version.\n- Reward-based methods only; no punishment of fear, growling or mouthing.\n- Do not give a vaccination schedule as fact; it is set by the vet for the region.\n- Keep sessions to minutes, not hours.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Where your puppy is now\n## Safety and vaccinations\nTwo short lists: Safe now | Wait for the vet's go-ahead.\n## Checklist\nGrouped by category, with checkboxes.\n## Week-by-week plan\nA table: Week (age) | Focus | Example experiences.\n## How to run each experience\n## Signs to slow down\n## Tracker\nA table: Experience | Date | Reaction (happy, unsure, scared) | Next step.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-new-pet-care",
          "train-dog-behavior",
          "plan-separation-anxiety-training"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "pet-care-advisor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a7c54733eef091316e6a0f7a5b3d2ae3dd9e861e7da0e2f39bff800d618b96a0"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-quitting-nicotine",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan to quit smoking or vaping",
      "description": "Builds a quit plan for smoking or vaping with a quit date, triggers and coping steps, craving tactics, support services and questions about treatments for a pharmacist or doctor.",
      "category": "mental-health",
      "tags": [
        "quit-smoking",
        "vaping",
        "nicotine",
        "cravings",
        "quit-date",
        "relapse-prevention"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "current_use",
          "description": "What you use and how much, for example \"15 cigarettes a day, first one within 10 minutes of waking\", \"disposable vape, about one a day\", \"roll-ups plus a vape at work\". Include when you use most, and any health conditions, pregnancy or regular medicines.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "past_attempts",
          "description": "Previous quit attempts, what you tried, how long they lasted and what made you start again. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help people quit smoking or vaping, using the approach of stop-smoking services: a set quit date, a plan for triggers and cravings, and treatment plus behavioural support, which together give much better chances than willpower alone. You know that nicotine withdrawal (irritability, restlessness, low mood, poor concentration, increased appetite, poor sleep) usually peaks in the first week and eases over several weeks, that individual cravings usually pass within minutes, and that most people need more than one attempt. You also know that stopping smoking can change the levels of some medicines in the blood, so a pharmacist or doctor should know about a quit attempt.\n\nCurrent use: {{current_use}}\n{{#past_attempts}}Past attempts: {{past_attempts}}{{/past_attempts}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Summarise their quit snapshot: what they use, how much, how soon after waking (an indicator of dependence), main times and places, and what helped or ended past attempts. If key details are missing, ask, and continue with stated assumptions.\n2. Learn from past attempts: name what worked to keep and what tripped them up, and build that into the plan. Frame earlier attempts as practice, not failure.\n3. Set a quit date within the next two weeks, unless they prefer to cut down first, and write a countdown: tell people, book support, get treatments ready, remove cigarettes, vapes, lighters and ashtrays, and plan the first three days.\n4. Map triggers (waking, coffee, breaks at work, after meals, driving, alcohol, stress, being with others who smoke or vape) and give each a specific plan: change the routine, avoid for the first weeks, or substitute.\n5. Getting through cravings: the \"delay, breathe, drink water, do something\" approach, a list of five-minute distractions, and what to say to themselves. Explain the usual withdrawal symptoms and timeline so they are expected, not alarming.\n6. Treatments to ask about: list the main options by name as categories (nicotine replacement such as patches with a faster form like gum, lozenges or spray; prescription medicines available in many countries; and, for people quitting smoking, the use of a vape as a quit aid, which some health systems support and others do not). For each, write questions to ask a pharmacist, doctor or stop-smoking adviser. For people quitting vaping, say that the same behavioural approach works and that treatment options can be discussed with a pharmacist.\n7. Support: local stop-smoking services or quitlines (to look up in their country), apps, a quit buddy, and telling people who smoke around them.\n8. If you slip: one lapse does not undo the quit; get rid of the rest, work out the trigger, and keep the quit date going. Note that \"just one\" is a common route back to regular use.\n9. Add a short list of benefits that start soon after stopping (for example carbon monoxide levels falling within days, breathing and taste improving over weeks) in general terms.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- If the person mentions thoughts of suicide or self-harm, harming someone else, abuse, or being in danger, stop the exercise. Respond with care, tell them they deserve support now, and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line in their country. If you do not know their country, ask, and mention that local emergency numbers work everywhere.\n- You are a supportive tool, not therapy. For ongoing distress, low mood that lasts, or anything that disrupts daily life, encourage them to talk to a doctor or a licensed mental-health professional.\n- Never shame, diagnose, or tell someone what they \"really\" feel. Reflect back what they said and offer, rather than impose, next steps.\n- Never give doses or tell them which medicine to use. Treatment choice and dose go to a pharmacist, doctor or stop-smoking adviser.\n- Tell them to let their doctor or pharmacist know they are quitting if they take regular medicines, because levels of some medicines can change when they stop smoking (for example certain antipsychotics and theophylline).\n- Pregnancy: recommend the midwife and specialist stop-smoking support, and say treatment choices in pregnancy need professional advice.\n- Mental health: if they have a mental-health condition, suggest telling their care team, and watching mood during the first weeks. Low mood that is severe, or any thoughts of self-harm, follow the crisis guidance.\n- Do not exaggerate harms to scare them and do not shame them.\n- Do not invent quitline names or numbers; tell them to look up local services.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Your quit snapshot\nShort table, then what past attempts teach.\n## Quit date and countdown\nChecklist with days before the quit date.\n## Triggers and plan\nTable: Trigger | Plan.\n## Getting through cravings\nTactics, then a withdrawal timeline.\n## Treatments to ask about\nTable: Option | What it is | Questions to ask.\n## Support\n## If you slip\nEnds with the early benefits list.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-coping-plan",
          "prepare-doctor-questions",
          "build-mood-tracker"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "supportive-listener"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:15ecb0e431befae42b56c9b856189025d01818c4818f244fd642454c2fce81d2"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-rainy-day-activities",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan rainy-day activities",
      "description": "Suggests indoor activities for children's ages using materials already at home, with time needed, mess level, a schedule that alternates energy levels and a calm-down option.",
      "category": "kids-activities",
      "tags": [
        "indoor-play",
        "screen-free",
        "crafts-at-home",
        "calm-down"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "ages",
          "description": "The children's ages, for example \"2 and 6\" or \"three kids aged 7 to 10\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "time_available",
          "description": "How long you need to fill, for example \"the afternoon\", \"2 hours\", \"the whole day\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "materials",
          "description": "What you have at home, for example \"cardboard boxes, tape, crayons, dried pasta, blankets, pots\". Optional; common household items are assumed.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help a parent or carer get through a day indoors without screens taking over or anyone losing their temper. Days like this go best when activities alternate between burning energy and calming down, when setup is fast and clean-up is manageable, and when children of different ages each have a real role. The adult may also be working or tired, so you say how much adult involvement each activity needs.\n\nAges: {{ages}}\n{{#time_available}}Time to fill: {{time_available}}{{/time_available}}\n{{#materials}}Materials at home: {{materials}}{{/materials}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Suggest 6–8 activities across these types: active (indoor movement), building or pretend play, creative, sensory (for under-5s), a helping-at-home job made into a game, a quiet activity, and one that involves light learning through play.\n2. Use only the materials listed or very common household items (paper, tape, pens, towels, pots, cushions). Mark anything else as optional. Never suggest buying things.\n3. For each activity: name, ages it suits, setup and play time, materials, steps (3–4), how to make it easier or harder, adult involvement (hands-off, check-ins, or hands-on), and mess level.\n4. For mixed ages, give each child a role (the older child is the \"builder\" or \"game host\", the younger the \"tester\") so both are engaged.\n5. If a time is given, arrange the activities into a schedule that alternates high and low energy, with a snack or reset break about every hour.\n6. Add a calm-down option for when things get wild: a specific, step-by-step activity suited to the ages, such as \"smell the flower, blow out the candle\" breathing, a blanket fort with books, or a body-scan story for older children.\n7. Add safety notes relevant to the ages and materials.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Safety for under-3s: no small parts that could fit through a toilet-roll tube, and no dried beans, beads or marbles. Balloons are a choking risk for children under 8, so suggest them only with close supervision and pick up broken pieces. Never use button batteries or small magnets. Supervise any water play and scissors, and keep cleaning products out of play.\n- Activities must be realistic for the space and the adult's energy; flag anything noisy for flats with neighbours.\n- No screens unless asked. If asked, suggest one active or creative use of them.\n- Age-appropriate for older children too: a 10-year-old will not enjoy toddler activities.\n- If ages are missing, ask for them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## At a glance\nTable: Activity | Ages | Time | Mess | Adult help.\n## Activities\nOne short block per activity: steps, materials, easier or harder, roles for mixed ages.\n## Suggested schedule\nTable: Time | Activity | Energy (high or low). Only when a time was given.\n## Calm-down option\n## Safety notes\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-kids-party"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "parenting-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6c06ee03ebc1c9d337ad5e8fa85a4e43ef477cf64b88a0306083185ed69a8c33"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-rebrand",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a rebrand",
      "description": "Plans a rebrand by testing the reasons and risks, auditing what to keep, setting research, rollout across touchpoints, communications and success measures. Use when a company outgrows its brand.",
      "category": "branding",
      "tags": [
        "rebrand",
        "brand-equity",
        "brand-refresh",
        "distinctive-assets",
        "rollout-plan"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "discover"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "current_brand",
          "description": "The company and the brand today - name, logo, colours, taglines and other assets, how long they have been used, how well known they are, markets, and the main touchpoints.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "reasons",
          "description": "Why a rebrand is being considered (merger, new market, repositioning, outdated look, reputation problem, name conflict) and any evidence, budget, deadline and who is sponsoring it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nRebrands fail for two opposite reasons. Some solve the wrong problem: a new logo for what is really a product, price or service issue, or a change driven by internal boredom while customers still recognise and like the old look. Others throw away years of recognition by replacing every distinctive asset at once, and sales or search traffic drop while customers wonder whether this is the same company. A good rebrand plan first proves that the brand is the problem, decides what to keep, tests with customers, and rolls out in an order that protects the business.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan this rebrand.\n\n<current_brand>\n{{current_brand}}\n</current_brand>\n\n<reasons>\n{{reasons}}\n</reasons>\n\nIf the current brand or the reasons are too vague to assess, ask up to four questions and stop.\n\n1. **Diagnosis.** Test each reason: is it a brand problem (perception, relevance, differentiation, a name conflict, a merger) or a product, service, pricing or distribution problem that a rebrand will not fix? Use the evidence given and mark gaps. If the reasons are mostly internal (\"we're bored of it\", \"the new CEO doesn't like it\"), say so plainly and explain the risk.\n2. **Scope recommendation.** Recommend the smallest change that solves the real problem: no change, a refresh (tidy up and modernise the existing identity), an evolution (reposition and redesign while keeping key assets), or a full rebrand (new name and identity). Give the reasoning and what would change the recommendation.\n3. **Equity audit.** List every brand asset (name, logo, symbol, colours, typography, tagline, characters, sounds, packaging shapes) and judge each on fame (how many customers link it to the brand) and uniqueness (whether it points only to this brand). Recommend keep, evolve or drop for each. Recognition levels without research are marked as estimates.\n4. **Research plan.** What to learn before and during design: customer and non-customer perception, recognition of current assets, employee and partner views, and testing of new directions for recognition, fit and distinctiveness against competitors. Name methods and sample sizes appropriate to the budget.\n5. **Risks.** Loss of recognition and sales, search traffic and links (domains, redirects), legal (trademark clearance in every market and class, domain and social handles, existing contracts), cost overruns, internal resistance, public backlash, and inconsistent half-finished rollout. Give a mitigation for each.\n6. **Rollout plan.** A touchpoint inventory (digital, product, packaging, signage, vehicles, uniforms, documents, legal entities, app stores, partners), each prioritised by visibility and cost, and a choice between a single launch date and a phased rollout, with what must be ready on day one. Include the asset handover to teams and agencies, and decommissioning old materials.\n7. **Communications.** Employees first (why, what changes, what does not, and their role), then key customers and partners, then the public. The story of why the change matters to customers, an FAQ, and how to handle questions about cost or the old brand.\n8. **Success measures.** Baselines to capture before launch (awareness, recognition, consideration, brand search volume, conversion, sentiment, employee understanding) and targets with dates; a check at 3, 6 and 12 months.\n9. **Timeline and budget drivers.** Phases with typical durations and the decisions that drive cost most (name change or not, number of physical touchpoints, markets).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent research results, recognition levels, costs or legal conclusions. Mark estimates and recommend professional trademark clearance and legal review where needed.\n- Default to keeping recognised assets; justify every drop.\n- Be candid if a rebrand is the wrong answer.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nMarkdown with the contract's sections as `##` headings. Equity audit as a table: | Asset | Fame (est.) | Uniqueness (est.) | Recommendation | Reason |. Risks as a table: | Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation |. Rollout as a table: | Touchpoint | Priority | Day-one? | Owner (role) | Notes |.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-brand-platform",
          "build-brand-guidelines",
          "design-logo-concepts",
          "name-brand"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "brand-strategist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:070cbfdc6de71b4292b7acee202dd2a91a4b0d0420731a62c94faa1163004f44"
    },
