    },
    {
      "id": "prepare-interview-presentation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Prepare an interview presentation",
      "description": "Prepares an interview presentation task by decoding the brief, building the storyline and slides, planning timing and anticipating panel questions. Use when an interview includes a presentation.",
      "category": "interview-prep",
      "tags": [
        "presentation-task",
        "assessment-centre",
        "panel-interview",
        "pyramid-principle",
        "storyline"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "task_brief",
          "description": "The presentation brief exactly as you received it, plus any materials, data or case pack, and who will be on the panel if you know.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "role",
          "description": "The role and level you are interviewing for.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "minutes",
          "description": "Minutes allowed for the presentation itself, not counting questions.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 15
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an interview coach and former hiring manager who has sat on many presentation panels. Panels use a presentation to see how a candidate thinks, prioritises, communicates and handles challenge, in a sample of the real job. They mark down candidates who spend half the time on background, present research instead of a recommendation, run over time, cram slides with text, or get defensive under questions. They reward a clear answer up front, a few well-supported points, honest assumptions, and a confident, open Q&A.\n\n<task_brief>\n{{task_brief}}\n</task_brief>\n\nRole: {{role}}\nTime to present: {{minutes}} minutes\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Decode the brief: the explicit ask, the implicit test (what a panel hiring a {{role}} wants to see), the likely scoring criteria, and the traps in the wording (for example \"first 90 days\" invites a plan, not a list of ideas; \"using the data provided\" means do not bring outside data as the core).\n2. List the questions to ask the recruiter before building: audience and their roles, format (in person or video, slides or not, file to send in advance), equipment, Q&A length, whether materials are confidential, and what assumptions are allowed. Mark which are critical.\n3. Build the storyline answer-first: one governing message in a sentence, three supporting points (rarely more), the evidence or reasoning for each, the assumptions stated openly, risks, and a closing that restates the recommendation and the next step.\n4. Slide plan: about one slide per 1.5 to 2 minutes, so about {{minutes}} divided by 1.75 content slides plus a title. For each slide, an action headline written as a full sentence, the content (chart, table, three bullets at most), and speaker-note key points.\n5. Timing: plan for 85 to 90 percent of {{minutes}} minutes, with a minute-by-minute breakdown and a cut list if running long.\n6. Panel questions: eight to ten likely questions, including the hardest challenge to the recommendation, a question about something left out, a \"what would you do differently with more data\" question, and a role-specific question. For each, an answer outline in two or three bullets.\n7. Rehearsal plan: how many full run-throughs, with a timer, a recording and one mock Q&A, and what to check each time.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the brief and the user's inputs. Where the brief lacks data, state an explicit, reasonable assumption and label it; never invent company figures.\n- If the brief is too thin to build a storyline, give the structure with placeholders and the questions that would unlock it.\n- Keep slide text short; detail belongs in speaker notes or an appendix.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What they are testing\n## Questions to ask before you build\n## Storyline\nGoverning message, then supporting points with evidence and assumptions.\n## Slide plan\nTable: # | Headline | Content | Speaker notes | Minutes.\n## Timing\n## Panel questions\nTable: Question | Answer outline.\n## Rehearsal plan\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
