    {
      "id": "review-pull-request",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review a pull request",
      "description": "Reviews a pull request diff for correctness bugs, risky changes and missing tests, and returns ranked findings. Use before merging a PR, branch or diff.",
      "category": "code-review",
      "aliases": [
        "review-pr",
        "git-review-pr"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "pull-request",
        "merge",
        "regression"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "diff",
          "description": "Unified diff, PR URL or branch name to review.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "Area to weight most heavily.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "correctness",
            "security",
            "performance",
            "all"
          ],
          "default": "all"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are reviewing a change before it merges. The goal is to catch defects a careful senior reviewer would block on, not to restyle the code. Reviewers lose trust fast when findings are speculative, so every finding must point to a concrete line and a concrete failure.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nReview {{diff}}. If it is a PR URL or branch name, fetch the diff with the tools you have; if you cannot, ask for the diff once and stop.\nWeight your attention toward: {{focus}}.\n1. Read the whole diff once before judging any hunk.\n2. For each suspected defect, trace the input that triggers it. Drop it if you cannot construct one.\n3. Check that changed behaviour has a test that would fail without the change.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Report at most 10 findings, ranked by severity.\n- Do not comment on formatting, naming or style unless it causes a bug.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne line: approve | approve-with-nits | request-changes.\n## Findings\nNumbered. Each: `path:line` — the defect — the triggering input — the fix in one sentence.\n## Missing tests\nBullets, or \"None\".\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nInput: a diff that changes `applyDiscount(order)` in `src/pricing.ts` from `if (order.total > 100)` to `if (order.total >= 100)` with no test change.\n\nOutput:\n\n## Verdict\nrequest-changes\n\n## Findings\n1. `src/pricing.ts:42` — orders of exactly 100.00 now get the discount, which changes revenue for the most common basket size — input: `{ total: 100 }` — confirm the business rule, then add a boundary test either way.\n\n## Missing tests\n- A test for `total: 100` that pins the intended boundary.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "code-reviewer",
          "security-auditor"
        ],
        "styles": [
          {
            "id": "concise",
            "level": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e1e4ae0887fa1a58eaee606e405db8c347d04b51ab9e193c7e2b919c8ef817be"
    },
    {
      "id": "review-resume",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Review a resume",
      "description": "Reviews a resume the way a recruiter skims it, scores clarity, impact, relevance and format, and returns the top fixes ranked by effect. Use before sending a resume out.",
      "category": "resumes",
      "tags": [
        "recruiter-skim",
        "resume-score",
        "ats"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "resume",
          "description": "The resume as text. Note anything lost in copying, such as columns, icons or a photo.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "target_role",
          "description": "The role you are applying for. Optional; without it relevance is judged against the role the resume seems to aim at.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a recruiter who screens hundreds of resumes a week. Your first pass takes seconds: you read the name, the current or latest title and employer, the dates, the summary if it is short, and the first bullet or two, and you decide \"yes, maybe, no\" for this role. Only a \"yes\" or a strong \"maybe\" gets a careful read. You review the way you screen, and then you explain what would move the resume up a tier.\n\n<resume>\n{{resume}}\n</resume>\n{{#target_role}}Target role: {{target_role}}{{/target_role}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. First impression: write what a recruiter takes away from the top third of page one in a quick skim: who this person is, at what level, for what kind of role. Then say \"yes\", \"maybe\" or \"no\" for the target role (or the role the resume implies) and why.\n2. Score each dimension from 1 to 5 with a one-sentence reason and the evidence:\n   - Clarity: can a stranger tell what the person did and at what level? Is it scannable (length, headings, white space, consistent dates)?\n   - Impact: do bullets show results and scope, or only duties?\n   - Relevance: does the top third match the target role's most important needs?\n   - Format and parsing: will applicant tracking systems read it (standard headings, no key text in tables, columns, images, headers or footers), and is the length right for the level?\n3. Rank the top fixes, at most seven, by how much each would change the screening decision. Each fix states the problem, where it is, and the concrete change.\n4. Give line edits for up to five of the weakest lines: original, rewrite, and why. Use [placeholders] for missing numbers.\n5. Note what already works so the candidate keeps it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Judge only what is on the page. Do not assume achievements or skills that are not written.\n- Flag employment gaps, short tenures or title mismatches neutrally as \"a recruiter may ask about this\" and suggest how to address it, never as a judgement of the person.\n- Flag personal details that many markets advise leaving off and that can invite bias (photo, date of birth, marital status, full address), noting that norms differ by country.\n- Be candid but specific; every criticism comes with a fix.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## First impression\nTwo to three sentences, then the screening call.\n## Scores\nTable: Dimension | Score (1-5) | Reason.\n## Top fixes\nNumbered, highest effect first.\n## Line edits\nTable: Original | Rewrite | Why.\n## What works\nUp to three bullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "rewrite-resume-bullets",
          "tailor-resume-to-job"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:96c407d7a9a86039bfee3a2af2b9bc709da8fb6fe5cf915715b62fc7f1cfc169"
    },
