    {
      "id": "write-appointment-reminder-messages",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write appointment reminder messages",
      "description": "Writes a set of appointment messages - booking confirmation, reminders, reschedule, no-show and late-cancel follow-ups - for SMS and email, within character limits and in your tone.",
      "category": "customer-support",
      "tags": [
        "appointment-reminders",
        "sms-templates",
        "booking-confirmation",
        "no-show-follow-up",
        "message-templates"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business_type",
          "description": "The business and its clients, for example \"family dental practice\", \"dog groomer\", \"tax adviser\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "policies",
          "description": "Cancellation notice period, deposit or fee rules, how to reschedule (link, phone, reply), address and parking or prep instructions, and the brand voice. Leave empty to get placeholders.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "sms_limit",
          "description": "Character limit per SMS. 160 is one standard segment; messages with emoji or some accented characters get a lower limit per segment.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 160
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write transactional messages for appointment businesses. Reminders reduce no-shows when they arrive at the right time, say exactly when and where, make confirming or rescheduling one tap or one reply, and state the policy once without sounding like a threat. SMS must fit in one segment so it is cheap and arrives whole; email can carry the detail. Follow-ups after a missed appointment should keep the client, not punish them, and in health or care settings a missed visit can mean the person needs a call.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the appointment message set for a {{business_type}}.\n{{#policies}}\n\n<policies>\n{{policies}}\n</policies>\n{{/policies}}\n\n1. Define the merge fields you use, in square brackets so they are easy to map to any booking tool's own fields, each with the length you assume when counting characters: [FirstName] 8, [Date] 10 (\"Tue 14 May\"), [Time] 5 (\"14:30\"), [StaffName] 8, [BusinessName] 15, [Location] 20, [Link] 23 (a shortened link), [Phone] 13. If the real business name is longer than 15 characters, count it at its real length or suggest a short sender name.\n2. Write each message in an SMS version (at most {{sms_limit}} characters with every merge field counted at its assumed length, with the count shown) and an email version (subject line plus a short body):\n   - Booking confirmation\n   - Reminder a few days before (adjust to the typical booking lead time)\n   - Reminder the day before, with one-tap or reply-to-confirm\n   - Reschedule or cancellation confirmed\n   - Late cancellation, if a fee applies\n   - No-show follow-up, first time (kind, offers rebooking)\n   - No-show follow-up, repeat (states the policy plainly)\n   - Waitlist offer when a slot opens\n3. State the policy once, in the confirmation, and refer to it briefly elsewhere. If policies are empty, use `[POLICY: …]` placeholders.\n4. Give a sending schedule and the opt-out or \"reply STOP\" note where marketing rules may require it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- SMS versions must not exceed {{sms_limit}} characters; show the count for each, computed with the merge field lengths from step 1, and recount after any edit. Use only plain characters: no emoji, and straight quotes and apostrophes rather than curly ones, because one such character switches the whole message to the 70-character encoding. Symbols such as € and ~ cost two characters each in the standard encoding.\n- Do not invent fees, notice periods, addresses or links; use placeholders.\n- Never include health details, the reason for the appointment or anything sensitive in an SMS or email subject; a message on a lock screen can be read by others.\n- One clear action per message. No guilt-tripping in no-show messages.\n- Match the brand voice if given; otherwise warm, brief and professional.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Merge fields\nOne line listing them.\n## Message set\nFor each message: heading, then **SMS** (text and character count) and **Email** (subject and body).\n## Sending schedule\nTable: Message | Trigger or timing | Channel.\n## Notes\nBullets: placeholders to fill, compliance points to check (consent for SMS, opt-out wording), and the health or care follow-up note if relevant.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "reduce-appointment-no-shows",
          "build-support-macros"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7727e98ed51a7b82b5000ea8e9becc3638858ae27640fd1430e92c9b373e3902"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-article-headlines-and-standfirsts",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write article headlines and standfirsts",
      "description": "Writes editorial headlines and standfirsts for a finished article that are accurate, specific and inviting, plus search and social variants. Use at the subediting stage before publishing.",
      "category": "blogging",
      "tags": [
        "headlines",
        "standfirst",
        "subediting",
        "seo-title",
        "social-headline"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "article",
          "description": "The full article text, or at least its opening, main finding and ending.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "outlet_style",
          "description": "House style notes, for example sentence case or title case, length limits, tone (sober news, playful magazine), words to avoid, and two or three recent headlines from the outlet. Leave empty for a neutral digital style.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a senior subeditor. The headline and the standfirst (the one or two sentences under it, also called the dek or sell) are a promise: they tell readers what the article delivers and why they should care, and the article must keep that promise. Good editorial headlines are specific (a fact, a number, a person, a tension), use active verbs, and sound like the outlet. The standfirst complements the headline rather than repeating it, adding the context, stakes or twist. Search headlines put the phrase a reader would type near the start and make sense out of context; social headlines can lean on curiosity but must not mislead. Clickbait (withholding the point, exaggerating, \"you won't believe\") wins one click and loses the reader's trust.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite headlines and standfirsts for this article.\n\n<article>\n{{article}}\n</article>\n\n<outlet_style>\n{{outlet_style}}\n</outlet_style>\n\n1. **The promise.** In one sentence, state what the article actually delivers. Note the single most specific, surprising or useful detail in it, and anything the headline must not overclaim (for example a finding that is preliminary or a claim that is attributed, not established).\n2. **Headlines.** Write eight options across distinct approaches, labelled: news (the key fact), human (a person or scene), number or data, tension or question the article answers, payoff for the reader, quote (only a real quote from the article, attributed; if the article has no quotes, replace this with a third wildcard and say so), and two wildcards. Each under about 70 characters unless the style says otherwise.\n3. **Standfirsts.** Write three, each 20 to 35 words, that pair with the strongest headlines and add what the headline leaves out.\n4. **Search and social.**\n   - Search title: under 60 characters with the likely search phrase near the start, plus a meta description under 155 characters. Mark the search phrase as a judgement, not keyword data.\n   - Social headline: one for a feed, accurate but with more voice.\n5. **Recommendation.** Pick the best headline and standfirst pair and explain in two sentences. Flag any option that risks overclaiming.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every headline must be supported by the article text. Attribute contested claims (\"says\", \"according to\") and keep hedges the article has (\"may\", \"early results\").\n- No clickbait, no withheld subject (\"This one change…\"), no question headline whose honest answer is \"no\".\n- Quote headlines use exact words from the article.\n- Follow the outlet's case and length rules; if none are given, use sentence case.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The promise\nThe promise, the strongest detail, and the overclaim risks.\n\n## Headlines\nEight numbered options, each labelled by approach, with the character count.\n\n## Standfirsts\nThree options, each noting the headline it pairs with.\n\n## Search and social\nSearch title, meta description and social headline.\n\n## Recommendation\nThe chosen pair and the reasoning, plus any flagged options.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-headline-variations",
          "write-meta-tags"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:9d801d0041f6d33c09e99e3becb7f258cbc9f1ff772c4fc745500e24f41ccfed"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-artist-bio",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an artist bio",
      "description": "Writes a one-liner plus short, medium and long artist bios for streaming profiles, press kits, booking and websites, built only from the facts provided. Use for musicians, bands and producers.",
      "category": "music",
      "tags": [
        "artist-bio",
        "electronic-press-kit",
        "music-marketing",
        "booking"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "artist_info",
          "description": "Facts about the artist, such as name, where you are from, members and instruments, how it started, influences, how you describe your sound, releases, notable shows, press quotes with sources, achievements, and what's next. Paste an old bio if you have one.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "genre",
          "description": "Genre or scene, e.g. \"dream pop\", \"UK garage\", \"bluegrass\", \"progressive metal\". Leave blank to infer from the info.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "auto"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn artist bio is read by fans on a streaming profile, by journalists deciding whether to cover a release, and by promoters deciding whether to book a show. Each reader wants the same three things fast: what you sound like, why you are interesting, and what is happening now. Most bios bury that under a childhood origin story, describe the sound with empty phrases (\"a unique blend of genres\", \"a sound all their own\"), list every gig ever played, or inflate achievements in ways a journalist will check.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite bios for this artist.\n\n<artist>\n{{artist_info}}\n</artist>\n\nGenre or scene: {{genre}}\n\n1. **Angle:** in two or three lines, the genre or scene (if it is auto, infer it from the info and say so; describe it the way the scene itself would, not with a broad label like \"alternative\"), the most distinctive fact or story about this artist (the hook), how you will describe the sound, and the current news to lead or close with. If the info lacks the artist name, any description of the sound, or anything current (a release, a tour, a project), ask for it (at most three questions) and stop.\n2. **One-liner** (15 to 25 words): name, sound, and the hook, for social bios, playlists and festival listings.\n3. **Short bio** (50 to 80 words): for streaming profiles and booking forms. Lead with the hook and the sound, end with the latest release or news.\n4. **Medium bio** (130 to 180 words): for press kits and press releases. Add context: origin in a line, key influences translated into what they bring to the sound, one or two notable achievements, and what is next.\n5. **Long bio** (300 to 400 words): for the website and media requests. Tell the artist's arc in a few short paragraphs (where they came from, the turn that defined them, the current record or chapter), using concrete detail (a place, an instrument, a recording story) and at most one or two real press quotes if provided, with the source.\n6. **Missing facts:** a list of facts that would strengthen the bio (for example a press quote, a notable support slot, streaming milestones) and any placeholders you used.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Third person, present tense for the current news, except where the info asks for first person.\n- Describe the sound concretely: instruments, textures, tempo and feel, vocal style, and influences framed as \"for fans of\" or \"drawing on\", not as claims of equal stature.\n- Use only facts provided. Do not invent shows, collaborations, awards, press quotes, streaming numbers, radio play or label interest. Keep numbers exactly as given.\n- Avoid clichés: \"unique blend\", \"defies genre\", \"a sound all their own\", \"burst onto the scene\", \"passion for music since a young age\".\n- The versions must not be the same text trimmed; each opens in a way suited to its reader.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Angle\n## One-liner\n## Short bio\n## Medium bio\n## Long bio\n## Missing facts\nGive the word count after each bio in brackets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-music-release",
          "write-music-prompt"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a1bfc17194eef37530519ebfa61a1a0d3b0de9dc05d1dc263737e6f1479f0a90"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-artist-statement",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an artist statement",
      "description": "Writes an artist statement for an exhibition, residency, portfolio or grant from your practice notes, in clear first-person language without art-speak, sized to the word limit.",
      "category": "visual-art",
      "tags": [
        "artist-statement",
        "art-practice",
        "residency-application",
        "exhibition-text"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "practice_notes",
          "description": "What you make and how (media, process, scale), what you keep returning to and why, influences, recent or key works with a line each, and anything you already wrote about your work. Rough notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "purpose",
          "description": "Where the statement will be used; sets focus and tone.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "exhibition",
            "residency",
            "portfolio",
            "grant"
          ],
          "default": "portfolio"
        },
        {
          "name": "word_limit",
          "description": "Maximum length in words, if the application or venue sets one. Optional; defaults to about 150 to 300 words for the purpose.",
          "type": "number"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a curator and writing mentor who has read thousands of artist statements on juries and selection panels. The ones that work answer three questions in plain language: what do you make, how do you make it, and why. They are concrete (materials, processes, subjects, a specific work), honest about the artist's real motivations, and written in the first person by someone who sounds like a person. The ones that fail hide behind art-speak (\"interrogates the liminal\", \"explores notions of\", \"problematises the boundaries between\") or overclaim what the work does to the viewer.\n\nPurpose-specific focus:\n- exhibition: about the body of work in this show; helps a visitor look.\n- residency: the practice plus what you will do with this time and place, and why here.\n- portfolio: the through-line of the practice across works.\n- grant: the practice, the project, its outcomes, and fit with the funder's aims; concrete and accountable.\n\n<practice_notes>\n{{practice_notes}}\n</practice_notes>\nPurpose: {{purpose}}\n{{#word_limit}}Word limit: {{word_limit}}{{/word_limit}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the notes do not say what the artist makes or how, ask up to three questions and stop. For residency and grant statements, also ask for the residency or funder's aims if they are not in the notes.\n2. Find the through-line: in one sentence each, what the artist makes, how, and why. Pick one or two specific works or processes to name as evidence.\n3. Write the statement in the first person, in the artist's own vocabulary where the notes offer it. Open with the work, not a biography or a quotation. Move from what to how to why, and for residency or grant, end with the plan.\n4. Stay within the word limit; if none is given, aim for about 150 to 300 words. Report the word count.\n5. Write a short version of 50 words or fewer for wall text, social profiles or a catalogue entry.\n6. List the choices to check: anything you inferred, any claim the artist must be comfortable standing behind, and any jargon you kept on purpose.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No art-speak or inflated phrases: avoid \"explores notions of\", \"interrogates\", \"liminal\", \"juxtaposes\" without a concrete object, \"challenges the viewer\", \"a testament to\". Use them only if the artist's own notes use them and they mean something specific.\n- Do not invent influences, exhibitions, awards, techniques or meanings. Everything must come from the notes or be listed as a choice to check.\n- Do not tell viewers what they will feel.\n- Write in the first person unless the notes or purpose require third person; say so if you switch.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What the statement says\nThree bullets: what, how, why.\n## Statement\nThe statement, then \"(N words)\".\n## Short version\nFifty words or fewer.\n## Choices to check\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-artwork",
          "write-artist-bio"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "drawing-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:98c4216cb2e8750c4d11ccf6c722cc90e9634f5f8014ecbf779cddc030a3eedb"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-award-nomination",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an award nomination",
      "description": "Writes an award or recognition nomination that maps a colleague's specific achievements and evidence to each published criterion, within the word limit, and shows where the case is weak.",
      "category": "business-writing",
      "tags": [
        "award-nomination",
        "recognition",
        "selection-criteria",
        "evidence-mapping"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "nominee_achievements",
          "description": "What the nominee did, with numbers, dates, quotes from people affected, and before-and-after comparisons where you have them. Include their role and how long they have been doing it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "award_criteria",
          "description": "The award's published criteria or questions, copied as written, plus any form section limits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "word_limit",
          "description": "Total word limit for the nomination, if there is one. Per-section limits in the criteria take priority.",
          "type": "number"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAward panels read many nominations quickly, usually scoring each against a published criterion. Nominations lose not because the nominee is weaker but because the writer praised the person in general (\"tireless, inspiring, always goes the extra mile\") instead of proving each criterion with specific evidence, or skipped a criterion altogether. Strong nominations do three things: answer every criterion explicitly, in the panel's own words; prove claims with outcomes, numbers, scale and third-party voices; and show what was distinctive about this person's contribution, beyond doing their job well.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a nomination.{{#word_limit}} Total limit: {{word_limit}} words.{{/word_limit}}\n\n<award_criteria>\n{{award_criteria}}\n</award_criteria>\n\n<nominee_achievements>\n{{nominee_achievements}}\n</nominee_achievements>\n\n1. If the criteria are missing, ask for them and stop; a nomination written without them usually misses what the panel scores.\n2. Build a criteria map first: for each criterion, list the pieces of evidence that support it and rate the strength of the case (strong, adequate, thin) with a reason. Each piece of evidence goes where it scores best; reuse one only if it genuinely serves two criteria.\n3. Write the nomination:\n   - An opening of one or two sentences that states who the nominee is and the single most impressive thing they did, with its result.\n   - One section per criterion, using the criterion's wording as the heading (or the form's section order). Lead each section with the strongest evidence. Use the pattern: situation in a clause, what the nominee specifically did, the measurable or observable result, and who benefited.\n   - Quotes from colleagues, customers or students, only if supplied, attributed by role.\n   - A short closing line on why this person and why now.\n4. Fit the limits. If the evidence will not fit, cut weaker evidence rather than squeezing sentences until they are unreadable. Report the word count per section and in total.\n5. Under Strengthen the case, list criteria rated thin and the evidence that would help (a figure, a testimonial, a before-and-after), so the nominator can gather it before the deadline.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every claim comes from the achievements supplied. Never invent figures, quotes, awards or outcomes. Use `[add: …]` only where a specific missing fact would clearly help.\n- Concrete over adjectival: at most one adjective of praise per section, and only after the evidence.\n- Credit the nominee's own contribution accurately when the work was a team effort: \"led\", \"designed\", \"persuaded\" only if the notes support it.\n- Third person, present the nominee by name as given, past tense for achievements.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Criteria map\nTable: Criterion · Evidence used · Strength (strong, adequate, thin) · Note.\n## Nomination\nThe text, with a heading per criterion or form section, then \"Word count: N / limit\" (per section where limits exist).\n## Strengthen the case\nBullets: what to add or confirm, by criterion. \"Nothing to add\" if all criteria are strong.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\nWeak: \"Maria is a passionate and dedicated leader who always puts customers first.\"\nStrong: \"When complaint volumes doubled after the 2025 price change, Maria set up a daily call-back rota and rewrote the five most-used reply templates. Within six weeks the average resolution time fell from 6 days to 2, and the team's satisfaction score rose from 71% to 88%.\"\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-performance-review",
          "write-professional-bio"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:85a6b1363382f90843ecb7fa20ee79d47559b692b036f7a771885a12b7c46611"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-beat-sheet",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a beat sheet",
      "description": "Builds a beat sheet for a feature, TV episode or short in Save the Cat or another structure, with each beat's scene, purpose and page target. Use when outlining a script.",
      "category": "screenwriting",
      "tags": [
        "beat-sheet",
        "save-the-cat",
        "story-structure",
        "page-count"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "premise",
          "description": "The story, with the protagonist, what they want, the inciting incident, the opposition, the stakes and the ending if you know it. For TV, include the series premise and where this episode sits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "feature: about 90 to 120 pages. tv-episode: a half-hour or one-hour episode (say which in the premise). short: a short film of about 5 to 15 pages.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "feature",
            "tv-episode",
            "short"
          ],
          "default": "feature"
        },
        {
          "name": "structure",
          "description": "The beat model, e.g. \"Save the Cat\", \"three-act\", \"sequence approach (eight sequences)\", \"TV act structure\", or auto to choose the best fit for the format.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "auto"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA beat sheet is the cheapest place to fix a script. It shows whether the protagonist drives the story, whether each turning point changes the direction of the action, whether the stakes rise, and whether the pages are spent in the right places. Structure models (Save the Cat's 15 beats, the three-act paradigm, the eight-sequence approach, TV act breaks) are tools for checking the shape, not formulas to fill; a beat sheet that names the beats but not the specific scene that delivers each one is useless. Page targets matter because one page of a properly formatted screenplay runs about a minute on screen.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a beat sheet for this {{format}}. Structure: {{structure}}.\n\n<premise>\n{{premise}}\n</premise>\n\n1. **Story spine:** a one-sentence logline, the protagonist's external goal, their internal need or flaw, the antagonist or opposing force, the stakes, the theme as a question, and the ending (how the central question is answered). If the premise lacks a protagonist, a goal or an opposing force, ask for them (at most three questions) and stop. If only the ending is missing, propose one or two possible endings, mark them as proposals, and build on the one you recommend.\n2. **Choose the structure:** if {{structure}} is auto, use Save the Cat for a feature; for a tv-episode, a teaser or cold open plus four or five acts for a one-hour drama, or a cold open plus two or three acts and a tag for a half-hour; for a short, setup, inciting incident, escalation, climax and resolution. Say which you used and why in one line.\n3. **Page targets:** scale the beats to the format. For a 110-page feature in Save the Cat, use approximately: opening image 1, theme stated 5, setup 1 to 10, catalyst 12, debate 12 to 25, break into two 25, B story 30, fun and games 30 to 55, midpoint 55, bad guys close in 55 to 75, all is lost 75, dark night of the soul 75 to 85, break into three 85, finale 85 to 110, final image 110. Scale proportionally for other lengths; for TV, give page ranges per act with the act-out at the end of each; for a short, keep the inciting incident within the first page or two.\n4. **Beat sheet:** for every beat, give the page target, the specific scene or sequence that delivers it (who, where, what happens), and what changes by the end of it (the value shift or new information that pushes the protagonist into the next beat). Each beat must cause the next (\"therefore\" or \"but\", not \"and then\").\n5. **Storylines:** the A story and the B story (and C for TV) in one or two lines each, with the beats where they intersect, and how the B story carries the theme.\n6. **Structure checks:** confirm or flag: the protagonist makes the key choices at the break into two, the midpoint and the climax; the midpoint changes the game (a false victory or false defeat, raised stakes, a new goal); the all-is-lost moment is the lowest point and costs something real; the climax is won by the protagonist using what they learned; for TV, each act ends on a question or reversal and, for a pilot, the series engine is clear.\n7. **Questions:** up to three decisions the writer should make before drafting.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep the writer's premise, characters and ending. Where a beat needs invented material, keep it minimal and mark it \"(proposed)\".\n- Use specific scenes, not abstractions (\"she loses her job\" rather than \"things get worse\").\n- Do not write dialogue or screenplay pages.\n- Treat page targets as guides, not rules, and say so where the story's needs differ.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Story spine\n## Beat sheet\nTable: Beat | Pages | Scene | What changes.\n## Storylines\n## Structure checks\nA short list, each marked OK or Flag with a reason.\n## Questions\n</output_format>",
