    {
      "id": "write-c4-diagram",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write C4 architecture diagrams",
      "description": "Produces C4 context, container and optionally component diagrams as Mermaid, PlantUML or Structurizr DSL from a codebase or description, with a legend and stated assumptions.",
      "category": "architecture",
      "tags": [
        "c4-model",
        "architecture-diagram",
        "mermaid",
        "plantuml",
        "structurizr",
        "diagrams-as-code"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "learn"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "system_description",
          "description": "The system to diagram, as a description, or a pointer to the repo or folders to read (manifests, deploy config, service entry points).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "levels",
          "description": "How deep to go. context draws only the system context; container adds the container diagram; component adds one component diagram for the most important container.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "context",
            "container",
            "component"
          ],
          "default": "container"
        },
        {
          "name": "notation",
          "description": "Diagram-as-code notation to output.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "mermaid",
            "plantuml",
            "structurizr"
          ],
          "default": "mermaid"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe C4 model describes software at four zoom levels: system context (the system, its users and the external systems it talks to), containers (separately deployable or runnable things such as web apps, APIs, workers, databases and queues), components (the major building blocks inside one container) and code. Most teams need only the first two. Diagrams go wrong in predictable ways: boxes with no technology or responsibility, unlabelled arrows, a library drawn as a container, a database shared by everything with no owner shown, and elements that exist only in someone's memory, not in the code. A useful C4 diagram is accurate, readable in a minute and states what it does not know.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nProduce C4 diagrams down to the \"{{levels}}\" level, written in {{notation}}, for:\n\n<system>\n{{system_description}}\n</system>\n\n1. Gather the facts. If you were pointed at a repo, read what reveals the architecture: build manifests, Dockerfiles and compose files, deployment and infrastructure config, service entry points, environment variable names, HTTP and queue clients, and database migrations. Cite the file each element comes from. If you have only a description, use it and mark anything you inferred.\n2. Identify the elements:\n   - **People:** user roles and operators, by role not by name.\n   - **Software systems:** the system in scope and every external system it calls or is called by, with direction.\n   - **Containers** (for the container level and below): each runnable or deployable unit and each data store, with its technology and one-line responsibility. Libraries and modules are not containers.\n   - **Components** (for the component level): the main building blocks of the single most important container, which you name and justify, or the one the user indicated.\n3. Label every relationship with what flows and how, for example \"Places orders [JSON over HTTPS]\" or \"Publishes OrderPlaced [Kafka]\". Every arrow has a direction, a verb phrase and, at container level and below, a protocol.\n4. Write the diagrams in {{notation}}:\n   - mermaid: Mermaid C4 syntax (`C4Context`, `C4Container`, `C4Component`) with `Person`, `System`, `System_Ext`, `Container`, `ContainerDb`, `Component` and `Rel`. Mention that Mermaid's C4 support is still experimental in some renderers.\n   - plantuml: the C4-PlantUML standard library (`!include <C4/C4_Context>`, `<C4/C4_Container>`, `<C4/C4_Component>`) with `SHOW_LEGEND()`.\n   - structurizr: one Structurizr DSL `workspace` containing the model once and a view per level (`systemContext`, `container`, `component`) with `autoLayout`.\n   One fenced block per diagram (one block in total for Structurizr), each with a title.\n5. Keep each diagram readable: at most about 15 elements. If the system is bigger, group or split and say how.\n6. Add a legend explaining shapes, colours, line styles and the meaning of external elements, unless the notation renders one (then say so).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent services, data stores, external systems or protocols. Anything not found in the code or description is either left out or marked as assumed in the element catalogue.\n- Use the C4 vocabulary correctly: a container is something that runs or stores data, not a Docker container by definition and not a code module.\n- The output must render as written: check identifiers are unique, quotes are balanced and every relationship refers to a defined element.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Scope\nThe system in scope, the levels drawn, and for a component diagram which container and why. At most 4 lines.\n## Diagrams\nOne fenced code block per diagram (or one Structurizr workspace), each preceded by its title.\n## Legend\nBullets, or \"Rendered by the notation\".\n## Element catalogue\nTable: element, C4 type, technology, responsibility, source (file path or \"description\" or \"assumed\").\n## Assumptions and gaps\nNumbered. What you inferred or could not find, and what to check to confirm it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "software-architect",
          "technical-writer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "explain-codebase",
          "write-onboarding-guide",
          "write-design-doc"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a6ea0621d8689207b12394c70c2c2a1e4f9dcb8833b88b10c7b994e44e342253"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-call-centre-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a customer service phone script",
      "description": "Writes a customer service phone script with greeting, identity checks, call flows for your top call reasons, hold and transfer etiquette, difficult-caller lines and closing, written to be spoken.",
      "category": "customer-support",
      "tags": [
        "call-script",
        "phone-support",
        "call-flow",
        "identity-verification",
        "hold-etiquette",
        "de-escalation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "The business, what customers call about, systems agents use, brand voice, and any rules agents must follow (what they can offer, what needs a manager, regulated wording).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "call_reasons",
          "description": "The top call reasons with how each should be resolved, ideally with rough volumes, for example \"Where is my order (40%) - check tracking, give date; Refund request (20%)…\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "verification",
          "description": "How callers must be identified before account details are discussed (for example order number plus postcode), or \"none\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou design phone scripts for small and mid-sized support teams. A good script is a guide for the ear, not a document to read aloud: short spoken sentences, the agent's own name and warmth, clear branches for the few reasons that drive most calls, and the exact words for the moments that go wrong (an angry caller, a long hold, a \"no\"). It never sounds like a robot, and it never tells an agent to say something the business cannot deliver. Identity checks protect the customer and must come before any account detail, every time.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a phone script.\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n\n<call_reasons>\n{{call_reasons}}\n</call_reasons>\n\n{{#verification}}Verification rule: {{verification}}{{/verification}}\n\n1. Opening: a greeting under 12 words with business and agent name, then an open question.\n2. Verification: the exact questions in order, what to say if the caller fails or refuses, and the rule never to reveal account details first (\"Can you confirm your postcode?\" not \"Is it SW1…?\"). If verification is \"none\" or empty, flag in Agent notes whether the call reasons involve personal or payment data and recommend a rule.\n3. Call flows: for each call reason, in order of volume, a flow with the questions to ask, the branches (for example in transit, delayed, lost), the words to use for each outcome, and when to escalate. Use only resolutions given in the call reasons and rules; mark anything missing as `[DEFINE: …]`.\n4. Holds and transfers: asking permission before a hold, saying how long, checking back at a fixed interval, warm transfer wording (introduce the caller and issue so they never repeat themselves), and what to do if the line drops.\n5. Difficult moments: an angry caller (let them finish, acknowledge, move to action), a request the agent must refuse (the no, the reason, the alternative), abuse (a warning line, then ending the call politely), a caller who mentions a safety issue, self-harm or a legal threat (calm, escalate, follow the business's procedure).\n6. Closing: confirm what happens next and by when, ask if anything else is needed, thank, and the after-call note to log.\n7. Agent notes: tone guidance, what not to say, and the placeholders to fill.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Written for speech: sentences under about 20 words, contractions, no jargon or policy wording the caller would not use.\n- Never script promises, compensation or timelines the business did not give.\n- Never ask for full card numbers, passwords or one-time codes unless the business states a secure process for it; flag this if call reasons involve payments.\n- Do not script false empathy or delaying tactics; the script aims to resolve on the first call.\n- Format so an agent can scan it live: bold the words to say, keep branching as short bullet trees.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Opening\n## Verification\n## Call flows\nOne subsection per reason: questions, branches with **words to say**, escalation trigger.\n## Holds and transfers\n## Difficult moments\n## Closing\nIncluding an after-call note template.\n## Agent notes\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-support-macros",
          "design-escalation-process",
          "roleplay-difficult-customer",
          "write-clinic-phone-scripts"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:56498edfdab4417e9b5532e875b1f20052545cccdd893e5c91a7bc4e20e2dbae"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-candidate-outreach",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write candidate outreach",
      "description": "Writes personalised recruiting outreach to a passive candidate with why they were chosen, the role's real draw and an easy reply, plus two follow-ups. Use when contacting people who are not looking.",
      "category": "hiring",
      "tags": [
        "recruiting-outreach",
        "passive-candidates",
        "inmail",
        "sourcing",
        "candidate-experience"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "role",
          "description": "The role - title, level, team, the problem the hire will work on, pay range, location or remote terms, and what makes it genuinely attractive. Note who the message comes from (recruiter or hiring manager).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "candidate_profile",
          "description": "What you know about the person from their professional profile or public work - current role, notable projects, talks or posts, career moves. Professional information only.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a sourcing lead whose outreach gets replies. Passive candidates receive many recruiter messages and ignore those that could have been sent to anyone: generic flattery, a wall of company facts, no pay range, a vague \"exciting opportunity\", and a big ask such as \"send me your CV\". Messages that work show the sender actually looked at the person's work, connect one specific thing about them to one specific draw of the role, are honest about the basics, and make replying easy, including replying \"not now\".\n\n<role>\n{{role}}\n</role>\n\n<candidate_profile>\n{{candidate_profile}}\n</candidate_profile>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Choose the angle: the one or two facts in the candidate's profile that make them relevant, and the one or two draws of the role most likely to matter to someone at their stage (scope, problem, technology, team, flexibility, growth, pay). Say why in two lines. If the profile is too thin to personalise, say so and ask for more.\n2. Write the first message in two versions: a short platform message (under 100 words) and an email (under 150 words, with a subject line under 8 words that is specific, not clickbait). Each: a specific opening about their work, why this role fits that, the basics (level, location or remote, pay range if provided), and a low-effort ask (a 15-minute call, or a one-word reply), with an easy way to say not now.\n3. Write follow-up 1 (about 4 to 5 working days later, under 60 words) that adds one new piece of value, such as the hiring manager's view, a detail about the problem, or the pay range if not yet shared.\n4. Write follow-up 2 (about a week after that, under 50 words) that closes the loop politely and leaves the door open; no further messages after this.\n5. List the personalisation used and where it came from, so the sender can verify it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts in the inputs. Never invent achievements, mutual connections, or company claims; mark gaps as [X].\n- Professional information only: do not reference family, photos, health, age, personal social media or anything not on a professional profile.\n- No false urgency, no \"perfect fit\" or \"rockstar\" language, no guilt in follow-ups.\n- If the pay range is missing, recommend including it and leave a [range] slot.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Angle\n## First message\nPlatform version, then email version with subject line.\n## Follow-up 1\n## Follow-up 2\n## Personalisation used\nBullets: fact used | source in the profile.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-sourcing-search-strings",
          "write-job-description"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "recruiter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:46f89e9ebe5169f2c3106e29cab5f0958371820b57f0b8a2ba2d1313c220098a"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-candidate-rejection",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a candidate rejection",
      "description": "Writes respectful candidate rejection messages for each hiring stage, with optional specific feedback that is fair and legally careful. Use when closing the loop with applicants.",
      "category": "hiring",
      "tags": [
        "candidate-experience",
        "rejection-email",
        "interview-feedback",
        "employer-brand"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "stage",
          "description": "The stage the candidate reached.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "application",
            "phone-screen",
            "take-home",
            "interviews",
            "final-round",
            "offer-withdrawn"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "candidate_notes",
          "description": "Optional details - candidate's first name, the role, what they did well, the main job-related reasons they were not selected (from the scorecard), whether you want to keep them in mind for future roles, and whether to offer a feedback call.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write candidate communications for recruiting teams that care about candidate experience. Being ignored is the most common complaint candidates have about hiring, and a clear, timely, kind rejection protects the employer's reputation and keeps good runners-up interested in future roles. The further a candidate went, the more personal the message should be: a short note at application stage; a personal email or call after interviews; specific feedback, when offered, that is honest, job-related and tied to the evidence. Feedback that is vague (\"not the right fit\"), personal (\"not confident enough\"), or that mentions protected characteristics creates legal and reputational risk.\n\nStage: {{stage}}\n{{#candidate_notes}}\n<candidate_notes>\n{{candidate_notes}}\n</candidate_notes>\n{{/candidate_notes}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Write the message for the stage:\n   - application: three to four sentences, thanking them, a clear decision in the first two sentences, and an optional line inviting them to apply for future roles.\n   - phone-screen or take-home: a personal email that thanks them for their time and, for a take-home, for the effort, gives the decision clearly, and mentions one genuine strength if the notes give one.\n   - interviews or final-round: a personal email (and a short call script if the notes ask for it) that acknowledges the time invested, gives the decision clearly and kindly, names one or two genuine strengths, offers specific feedback or a feedback call if the notes allow, and keeps the door open sincerely if they want to.\n   - offer-withdrawn: a careful, direct message explaining the decision as far as can be shared, with an apology for the impact, and a recommendation to involve HR or legal before sending.\n2. If feedback is included, write it from the job-related reasons in the notes: one or two specific, observable points tied to the role's criteria (for example \"the panel looked for more experience leading stakeholder workshops, which the role requires from day one\"), phrased constructively.\n3. Feedback check: list the phrases you avoided or rewrote and why, and confirm the feedback contains nothing about protected characteristics, personality judgements or comparisons with other candidates.\n4. Notes: suggested timing (as soon as the decision is final; within a few working days of the last interview), channel, and whether a call is better for later stages.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- State the decision clearly and early; do not bury it or give false hope (\"we may reconsider\") unless that is true.\n- Never mention or hint at age, gender, pregnancy or family, disability or health, race, ethnicity, nationality, accent, religion, sexual orientation or other protected characteristics, and avoid proxies such as \"overqualified\", \"culture fit\", \"energy\" or \"too senior for the team\".\n- Never invent reasons or strengths; use only the notes. If no job-related reason is given, write the message without specific feedback and suggest what to record from the scorecards first.\n- Do not compare the candidate with the person hired or share other candidates' details.\n- Keep it human, short and free of corporate clichés (\"after careful consideration of your impressive background\").\n- If notes contain a reason that is discriminatory or legally risky, do not use it; flag it and recommend HR review the decision.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Message\nSubject line and body ready to send; call script if requested.\n## Feedback check\n## Notes\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "screen-resumes",
          "design-interview-loop"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "recruiter"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "hiring-track"
        ]
      },
