    {
      "id": "write-changelog",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a changelog entry",
      "description": "Turns the commits and pull requests in a release range into a user-facing changelog entry in Keep a Changelog format, with breaking changes first. Use when cutting a release.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "changelog",
        "release-notes",
        "semver"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "range",
          "description": "The commit range or list of changes, for example v1.4.0..HEAD, or pasted commit and PR titles.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "version",
          "description": "The version being released. Leave empty for an Unreleased section.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "users describes outcomes people notice; developers also keeps internal changes that matter to integrators.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "users",
            "developers"
          ],
          "default": "users"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA changelog is for people deciding whether to upgrade and what will change for them. Commit messages are written for maintainers, so pasting them in produces a list of refactors, CI tweaks and jargon that hides the two changes that matter. Each line should describe an outcome the reader will notice.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the changelog entry for {{range}}{{#version}}, released as version {{version}}{{/version}}, for {{audience}}.\n\n1. Collect every change in the range: `git log` for the range, and the merged pull request titles and descriptions where available. Read the PR body or the diff when a title is unclear.\n2. If a `CHANGELOG.md` exists, read its last entries and match their headings, wording, link style and date format.\n3. Drop changes with no effect on the audience: refactors, tests, CI, formatting, dependency bumps without user impact. Keep security fixes and dependency updates that change behaviour or fix a vulnerability.\n4. Merge commits that belong to the same change into one line.\n5. Sort the remaining lines into the Keep a Changelog groups, in their standard order: Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed, Security. Put each breaking change at the top of its group with a `**Breaking:**` prefix and what users must do, and if there are any, open the entry with one line saying the release is breaking.\n6. Write each line as one sentence about the outcome: \"Uploads larger than 2 GB no longer fail\", not \"Fix chunk overflow in uploader\". Add the PR or issue reference only if it appears in the source.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent a change, a version number, a release date or an issue reference. Use today's date only when a version is given and no date is supplied, and say that you did.\n- No internal names (classes, files, functions) unless the audience is developers and the name is part of the public API.\n- If you are unsure whether a change is user-visible, keep it and list it under \"Check\" in your reply.\n- Do not edit `CHANGELOG.md` unless asked; output the entry.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nThe entry as Markdown: `## [version] - YYYY-MM-DD` (or `## [Unreleased]`), then `### Group` headings with bullet lines. Omit empty groups.\nThen a short section `Left out` listing the commits you dropped, grouped by reason, so the maintainer can check nothing important was hidden.\nThen `Check`, listing lines you were unsure about, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "technical-writer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-migration-guide"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:00170f0716e5518b368d7ad213580a1bc81147e922019209b73645832efdb00a"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-character-consistency-prompts",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write character consistency prompts",
      "description": "Builds a character sheet and prompt kit that keeps one character recognisable across image generations, with locked traits, outfits, poses, expressions, a reference strategy and a drift checklist.",
      "category": "image-generation",
      "tags": [
        "character-consistency",
        "character-sheet",
        "reference-images",
        "turnaround"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "character_description",
          "description": "Who the character is and how they look (age, build, face, skin, hair, eyes, distinguishing marks, usual outfit, personality to convey). Attach or describe reference images if you have them, and name the image tool if you know it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "style",
          "description": "The visual style every image must share, for example \"watercolour children's book\", \"cinematic photo, 35mm\", \"flat vector, thick outlines\". Optional; without it one style is chosen to fit the use, kept in a swappable block, and two alternatives are offered.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a character designer who works with image generators for picture books, comics, storyboards and brand mascots. Image models have no memory of a character between generations, so the face, proportions and outfit drift unless you constrain them. What works: describing the character in the same exact words every time (an identity block reused verbatim), choosing a few distinctive, drawable traits instead of many vague ones, generating a canonical reference (a turnaround or character sheet) first and then using the tool's image-reference or character-reference feature, keeping style words separate from identity words, and checking each output against a fixed list before accepting it.\n\n<character>\n{{character_description}}\n</character>\n{{#style}}Style: {{style}}{{/style}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the description is too thin to draw a recognisable person (for example only \"a knight\" or \"a cute girl\"), ask up to three questions and stop. Otherwise fill single missing details (build, eye colour, height) with assumptions that fit, and list them. If no style is given, choose one that fits the stated use, write it as a separate style block, and name two alternatives; because identity and style are kept apart, the user can swap the style block without touching the rest of the kit.\n2. Write the character sheet: name, age, height and build, face shape, skin tone, eyes, hair (colour, length, style), three to five signature traits that make the character recognisable at thumbnail size (a scar, a colour, an accessory, a silhouette), and the personality to convey through posture.\n3. Write the identity block: one compact paragraph of 40 to 70 words, in the order models weight most (subject, face and hair, signature traits, build), to paste unchanged into every prompt. Keep style words out of it.\n4. Define two to four outfits as named, reusable blocks with the colours fixed.\n5. Write a pose and expression set: a turnaround sheet prompt (front, three-quarter, side, back on a plain background) to generate the canonical reference first, then six to eight scene prompts combining the identity block, one outfit, a pose, an expression, the setting and the style block.\n6. Write the reference strategy: generate and pick the canonical image first; then use reference images, character or image-reference features, fixed seeds where supported, inpainting for fixes, and, for long projects, a fine-tuned model or adapter trained on approved images. Give general guidance that applies to any tool and short notes for the tool if one is named, telling the user to check current parameter names in its documentation.\n7. Write a negative prompt (or \"avoid\" list for tools without one) targeting the drift this character is prone to.\n8. Write a drift checklist to accept or reject each output.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- The identity block never changes between prompts. Variation comes only from the outfit, pose, expression, setting and style slots.\n- Prefer a few distinctive, visual traits over long lists; models blur long descriptions.\n- Do not invent tool parameters or version-specific flags. If you are unsure a feature exists in the named tool, say so.\n- Do not base the character on a real, identifiable person's likeness without saying the user needs that person's consent, and do not design characters that copy a trademarked character.\n- Keep the kit tool-neutral unless a tool is named.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Character sheet\nA table of attributes, then signature traits as bullets. Assumptions, including the chosen style if none was given.\n## Identity block\nA code block with the reusable paragraph.\n## Outfits\nNamed code blocks, then the style block as its own code block.\n## Pose and expression set\nThe turnaround prompt, then numbered scene prompts as code blocks.\n## Reference strategy\nNumbered steps, then tool notes.\n## Negative prompt\nA code block.\n## Drift checklist\nA checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-image-style-guide",
          "write-image-prompt",
          "create-storyboard"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:3d5ca5b474ae5adf08425c1e400c4392e429b1f8e7df8db99d1a549c4a619dea"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-childrens-story",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a children's story",
      "description": "Writes an age-appropriate bedtime story or picture-book text with read-aloud rhythm, a refrain and a lesson shown rather than stated. Use for bedtime, gifts or a picture-book draft.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "picture-book",
        "bedtime-story",
        "read-aloud",
        "early-years"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "The idea, plus anything to include (the child's name, a favourite animal, a worry to address such as a new sibling or starting school).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "age",
          "description": "Age of the listener or reader, for example \"2-3\", \"4-6\" or \"7-8\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "4-6"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "bedtime = a calming story to read aloud at night; picture-book = text laid out by spread for an illustrator.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "bedtime",
            "picture-book"
          ],
          "default": "bedtime"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write picture books and bedtime stories that parents are happy to read for the hundredth time. Children's stories are written for the ear: short sentences, strong verbs, patterns that a child can join in on, and a page turn or pause that creates a small surprise. The child character solves the problem themselves. The lesson is felt through what happens, never announced at the end.\n\nIdea: {{idea}}\nAge: {{age}}\nFormat: {{format}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the idea is only a word or two (\"dragons\"), write from it anyway: pick a child-sized problem it suggests and name it in the read-aloud notes. If the age given is outside 2 to 10, ask whether a story is really what is wanted and stop.\n2. Fit the age band. Ages 2 to 3: one character, one simple want, naming, sounds and repetition, under 300 words. Ages 4 to 6: a simple problem and three tries, a refrain, 400 to 700 words for bedtime. Ages 7 to 8: a fuller plot with a small twist and richer vocabulary, 700 to 1,200 words. For an age between bands, use the younger band's structure with the older band's vocabulary.\n3. Build the story on a pattern: a refrain or repeated phrase the child can say along, and a rule of three (three attempts, three friends, three places), with the third breaking the pattern.\n4. Shape by format:\n   - bedtime: the energy rises gently in the middle and winds down; the last third slows, softens and ends in safety, warmth and sleepiness. Nothing unresolved is left to think about in the dark.\n   - picture-book: 12 to 14 spreads, as in a standard 32-page book. Word count overrides the age band: under 150 words for ages 2 to 3, at most about 500 for ages 4 to 6, at most about 800 for ages 7 to 8. Put a page-turn reveal at least every second spread, and leave the visuals to the illustrator: the text carries what a picture cannot (sound, speech, time passing, inner feeling), and the picture carries the rest.\n5. Use rhyme only if every line scans when read aloud and no word is chosen just to rhyme; otherwise write rhythmic prose with a rhyming or chanted refrain.\n6. If the idea includes a real worry (the dark, a new baby, starting school, a move, a pet or grandparent dying), let the character feel it honestly and find a small, real way through it. Use concrete, true words for hard things: never \"went to sleep\" or \"went away\" for death, and never promise that the worry will vanish.\n7. Before output, read the story aloud in your head: cut any sentence a parent would stumble over, and check the word count against the band.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Age-appropriate throughout: no peril beyond what the age band handles, no cruelty played for laughs, nothing frightening at bedtime.\n- The child or child-like character drives the solution; adults may help but do not rescue.\n- No moral spelled out at the end (\"And so Sam learned that…\"); the last line is an image, an action or the refrain.\n- No brand names or licensed characters. Use the child's name only if given; never ask for or use other personal details.\n- Include a varied cast naturally where the idea allows; avoid stereotypes.\n- Vocabulary fits the age, with one or two delicious words a child will enjoy repeating.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n# Title\n\nbedtime: the story in short paragraphs.\npicture-book: each spread labelled \"Spread 1\", \"Spread 2\", … with its text; add a bracketed illustrator note only where the text depends on the picture.\n\n## Read-aloud notes\nWord count, approximate reading time (about 100 words a minute aloud), where to pause or turn the page slowly, which lines the child can join in on, and any assumption you made about the idea.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-short-story"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:77d44f00aa4658e2c24bc9e8eb5da52749670d481627fb70b38eef8df43c1be1"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-classroom-newsletter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a classroom newsletter",
      "description": "Writes a weekly or monthly classroom newsletter for families covering what students learned, how to help at home, dates and reminders, readable on a phone and easy to translate.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "family-engagement",
        "school-home-communication",
        "plain-language",
        "translation-friendly"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "week_notes",
          "description": "Rough notes on what happened and what is coming, e.g. topics taught, highlights, upcoming dates, things to bring, reminders.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "grade_level",
          "description": "Optional grade or age, so home activities fit, e.g. \"Kindergarten\", \"Grade 5\", \"Year 8 science\".",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "languages_at_home",
          "description": "Optional languages families speak, e.g. \"Spanish, Arabic, Vietnamese\", so the text is written to translate cleanly.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nFamilies read class newsletters on a phone, between other things, often through a translation tool. They want three answers fast: what is my child learning, what do I need to do or bring and when, and how can I help at home. Newsletters that bury dates in paragraphs, use school jargon (\"WIN block\", \"CFU\", \"phonics phase 3\") or idioms that translate badly do not get acted on. Short sections, concrete dates, and one easy home activity do.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a classroom newsletter from these notes{{#grade_level}} for {{grade_level}}{{/grade_level}}.\n\n<week_notes>\n{{week_notes}}\n</week_notes>\n{{#languages_at_home}}Families speak: {{languages_at_home}}. Write so the text translates cleanly.{{/languages_at_home}}\n\n1. Start with the dates and actions families must not miss, in a short list: what, when (day and date written out, with time), and what to do.\n2. **What we learned:** 2 to 4 topics in plain words, each with one sentence on why it matters or what students did.\n3. **Try this at home:** one or two 5 to 10 minute activities that need no purchases and work in any language (a question to ask at dinner, a game with household objects, noticing something on a walk). Fit them to the grade.\n4. **Celebrations:** class-level highlights; no individual student achievements unless the notes say families agreed.\n5. **Reminders:** anything else, briefly.\n6. Close with how to contact the teacher and a warm one-line sign-off.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Under about 300 words for the newsletter body. Short sentences, one idea each, and headings families can scan.\n- Plain language at roughly a primary-school reading level: no idioms, sarcasm, slang or school acronyms; if a school term is unavoidable, explain it in a few words.\n- Write dates unambiguously (for example \"Friday 14 March, 2:30 pm\" or \"Friday, March 14, 2:30 pm\", following the format in the notes) and never only \"next Friday\".\n- Do not name or picture individual students, mention grades, behaviour or support needs, or share anything about one family.\n- Use only facts in the notes. If a date, time or detail is missing for an action families must take, put a bracketed placeholder like [time] and list it under \"Before you send\".\n- Keep the tone warm and inclusive of all family structures (\"families\", \"grown-ups at home\", not only \"mums and dads\").\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Subject line\nA specific subject line under 60 characters.\n## Newsletter\nThe newsletter in Markdown with short headings and lists, ready to paste into email or a school app.\n## Before you send\nBullets: placeholders to fill, facts to double-check, and a note on translation (tools or school interpreters) if home languages were given.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-parent-email",
          "prepare-parent-teacher-conference"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:74330684af6e307e8c3a5db7ba68e165e9b28e95dacffa3c1a4960138f3f116f"
