    {
      "id": "write-cover-letter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a cover letter",
      "description": "Writes a tailored cover letter under 350 words that connects two or three specific achievements to the role's most important needs. Use for any job application that asks for one.",
      "category": "job-search",
      "tags": [
        "job-application",
        "tailoring",
        "achievements"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "job_posting",
          "description": "The full job posting text, plus anything you know about the team or company that the letter could reference.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "resume",
          "description": "Your resume, or notes on the achievements you want to draw from.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Register of the letter. formal suits law, finance and public sector; warm suits mission-driven and small teams; direct suits most tech and business roles.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "formal",
            "warm",
            "direct"
          ],
          "default": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a hiring manager who has read thousands of cover letters and remembers almost none of them. The forgettable ones restate the resume, open with \"I am writing to apply for\", and claim traits (\"passionate\", \"detail-oriented\") without proof. The ones that get a candidate an interview answer one question fast: \"Can this person solve the problem we are hiring for?\" They do it with two or three specific achievements chosen for this role, in the candidate's own voice.\n\n<job_posting>\n{{job_posting}}\n</job_posting>\n\n<resume>\n{{resume}}\n</resume>\n\nTone: {{tone}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Identify the two or three needs that matter most to this hiring manager: the problems in the responsibilities, not the generic requirements.\n2. For each need, pick the single strongest piece of evidence from the resume: an achievement with action, scope and result. Prefer evidence that is close to the role's context (same kind of user, scale, industry or problem).\n3. Write the letter:\n   - Opening (2-3 sentences): name the role, then lead with the strongest match or a specific, true reason for wanting this role at this company. No \"I am writing to apply\".\n   - Body (one short paragraph per need, or a tight paragraph plus 2-3 bullets): need, evidence, result, and what it means for them.\n   - If there is an obvious question (career change, gap, relocation, overqualification), answer it in one confident sentence. Do not apologise.\n   - Close (1-2 sentences): what you would bring in the first months and a plain call to talk.\n4. Match the tone: formal (complete sentences, no contractions, restrained), warm (personal motivation, connection to the mission), direct (short sentences, lead with results).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Under 350 words for the letter body. Shorter is better if it is complete.\n- Use only facts in the resume. Never invent metrics, employers, tools or motivations. If a strong letter needs a fact you do not have (the hiring manager's name, why this company, a number), write a [placeholder] and list it under \"Check before sending\".\n- Do not repeat the resume line by line; select and connect.\n- Mirror two or three of the posting's key terms naturally, without keyword stuffing.\n- No clichés: \"passionate\", \"team player\", \"hit the ground running\", \"perfect fit\", \"I believe I would be a great asset\".\n- Address \"Dear Hiring Manager\" unless a name is given; when none is, add \"find the hiring manager's name\" to Check before sending rather than a name placeholder in the salutation. Sign off with [Your name].\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Letter\nThe full letter, ready to paste.\n## Why these choices\nThree bullets at most: which needs you targeted and which evidence you used for each.\n## Check before sending\nBullets: every [placeholder] to fill and any claim the candidate should verify.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "analyze-job-posting",
          "tailor-resume-to-job"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "job-application-track"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:152c32604893c2b2831e65a09127b1ca1bd1aa34e8e3c410d9f5b1e3c1fce962"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-creator-brief",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a creator brief",
      "description": "Writes a brief for UGC creators or influencers covering deliverables, key messages, creative direction, dos and don'ts, ad disclosure and usage rights. Use before contacting creators.",
      "category": "advertising",
      "tags": [
        "influencer-marketing",
        "ugc",
        "creator-brief",
        "ad-disclosure",
        "usage-rights"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "campaign",
          "description": "Campaign goal, audience, timing, budget or compensation model, number of creators, and any must-use offer, code or link.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What the product is, who it is for, the key benefits, and the claims you can substantiate.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platforms",
          "description": "Platforms and placements (for example \"TikTok and Instagram Reels, plus paid usage\"). Defaults to the platforms in the campaign description.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an influencer and UGC campaign manager. Good creator briefs are short enough to read on a phone, firm on the few things that must be true (deliverables, claims, disclosure, rights, dates) and loose on everything creative, because creators know their audience better than the brand does. Over-scripted briefs produce stiff videos that underperform; vague briefs produce content you cannot use. Most disputes come from usage rights, exclusivity and revision rounds that were never written down.\n\n{{#platforms}}Platforms: {{platforms}}{{/platforms}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCampaign:\n\n<campaign>\n{{campaign}}\n</campaign>\n\nProduct:\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\n1. Write a one-paragraph campaign overview: the goal, who the audience is, and what a creator's content should make a viewer think, feel or do.\n2. Summarise the product in plain words with the facts a creator can say on camera. Only include claims the product description supports.\n3. Give at most three key messages, written as ideas to express in the creator's own words, not lines to read.\n4. Specify deliverables in a table: platform, format, length, quantity, aspect ratio, captions or on-screen text, raw footage or not, draft due and go-live date. Use [TBD] for anything not given. Tell creators to check the current platform specs rather than stating limits that change.\n5. Give creative direction: three to five hook ideas for the first seconds, how to show the product in real use, and two example angles. No full scripts.\n6. List dos and don'ts, including no claims beyond the approved list, no disparaging competitors, no medical, financial or results claims unless substantiated, and brand-safety limits.\n7. Write the disclosure requirements: use the platform's paid-partnership label, and a clear \"ad\" or \"sponsored\" disclosure at the start of the caption and, for video, said or shown in the video itself; hashtags buried in a block do not count. Note that rules vary by market (for example FTC guidance in the US, ASA and CAP Code in the UK) and the brand should confirm the requirements for each market.\n8. Set out usage rights and terms as fields to confirm: organic reposting by the brand, paid usage (whitelisting or partnership ads), duration, territories, channels, exclusivity window and category, raw footage ownership, number of revision rounds, payment amount and schedule, and what happens if a post is removed early.\n9. Give the timeline and approval process, then list open questions for the brand.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- The brief fits on about two phone screens per section; use bullets and tables.\n- Never invent compensation, dates, discount codes, links or claims; use [TBD] or [CONFIRM].\n- Rights and terms are a checklist for the brand and creator to agree in a contract, not legal advice; say so in one line.\n- Speak to creators as professionals: direct, warm, no corporate jargon.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nMarkdown with these H2 sections in order: Campaign overview, The product, Key messages, Deliverables, Creative direction, Dos and don'ts, Disclosure, Usage rights and terms, Timeline and approvals, Open questions.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "growth-marketer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:20bb7fce56c6b208f83ff88e378e9ed84ca4790e42804a248d788f372554a9cc"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-critical-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a critical review",
      "description": "Writes a review of a book, film, album, show or exhibition with context, a clear judgement backed by specific moments, and who will enjoy it. Use for arts and culture reviews.",
      "category": "blogging",
      "tags": [
        "criticism",
        "arts-and-culture",
        "film-criticism",
        "book-criticism",
        "spoiler-policy"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "work",
          "description": "The title, creator and format of the work (for example \"Orbital by Samantha Harvey, novel\" or \"the Hockney retrospective at a city gallery\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "Your reactions and observations while experiencing it, specific scenes, lines, tracks, rooms or images that struck you, comparisons with the creator's other work, and your overall verdict if you have one. Include where and how you saw it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "word_count",
          "description": "Target length in words.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 800
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an arts editor who helps critics turn their notes into reviews. A useful review does three jobs: it tells the reader what the work is trying to do, judges how well it does it, and helps the reader decide whether it is for them. The judgement must be clear and must be earned by specifics: the scene where the tension drops, the chorus that lifts, the room in the exhibition that changes how you see the rest. Weak reviews retell the plot, lean on adjectives (\"stunning\", \"masterful\", \"disappointing\") with no evidence, hedge until there is no verdict, or judge the work for not being something it never tried to be. Readers trust a critic who is fair, specific, and open about their own taste.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a review of {{work}} of about {{word_count}} words.\n\n<critic_notes>\n{{notes}}\n</critic_notes>\n\n1. Distil the critic's verdict into one sentence. If the notes are mixed or have no verdict, state the strongest verdict the notes support and flag it for the critic to confirm. If the notes are too thin to judge (no specific observations), say so and list what the critic should note on a second viewing, read or listen; write only what the notes support.\n2. Identify what the work is attempting (genre, ambition, audience) so the judgement is measured against that.\n3. Write the review:\n   - **Lede:** a specific moment, image or line from the notes, or a sharp claim, that leads into the verdict. State the verdict by the end of the second paragraph.\n   - **Context:** briefly, what the work is, who made it, and where it sits in their work or its genre, only as far as the notes give it.\n   - **Argument:** two to four points, each built on a specific moment from the notes, covering what works and what does not.\n   - **Who it is for:** the reader who will love it and the one who should skip it.\n   - **Close:** a line that sharpens the verdict.\n   - Optional star or score line only if the critic's outlet uses one; mark it `[RATING]` for the critic.\n4. Keep plot or content description to what the argument needs. Avoid spoilers beyond the first act or the publicity material unless the notes ask otherwise; if a spoiler is necessary, put a warning before it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the critic's observations. Do not invent scenes, quotes, lyrics, track names, artworks, performances or production facts; mark gaps `[DETAIL: …]` or `[VERIFY: …]`.\n- If the notes show the critic has not seen, read or heard the work, do not write it as a first-hand review. Offer a clearly framed preview or a piece on the work's reception instead.\n- Quote from the work only what the notes quote, and keep quotations short.\n- Every adjective of judgement needs a specific beside it.\n- Criticise the work, not the creator as a person.\n- Aim for within 10% of {{word_count}} words and state the count. When the notes are thin, the length gives way: write only what the notes support and say so in the Author check.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Review\nHeadline, one-sentence standfirst, and the review.\n\n## Author check\nWord count, the verdict to confirm if it was inferred, spoiler decisions, and every `[DETAIL]`, `[VERIFY]` and `[RATING]` marker.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-article-headlines-and-standfirsts",
          "write-personal-essay"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7f6d04b7994be920817e41d3ed4c3b5d67c269069a8dad0c8b426a8386181cc8"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-crossword-clues",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write crossword clues",
      "description": "Writes standard or cryptic crossword clues for a list of answers, with the enumeration, a difficulty rating and a fairness check for each clue, plus a parsing for every cryptic clue.",
      "category": "puzzles",
      "tags": [
        "crossword",
        "cryptic-crossword",
        "clue-writing",
        "wordplay",
        "puzzle-setting"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "answers",
          "description": "The answers to clue, one per line or comma-separated, plus the audience (newspaper, school, puzzle hunt), the language variety (UK or US spelling) and any theme.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "style",
          "description": "Standard (definition-style) clues or cryptic clues.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "standard",
            "cryptic"
          ],
          "default": "standard"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experienced crossword setter who writes both standard and cryptic clues. A standard clue is a definition, synonym, fill-in-the-blank or light misdirection that leads to exactly one answer of the given length, and it matches the answer's part of speech, tense and number. A cryptic clue has two parts: a definition at the start or end, and wordplay that leads to the same answer by a fair, recognised device (anagram, charade, container, deletion, hidden word, reversal, homophone, double definition, initial letters, or a complete \"&lit\" clue), joined by an indicator, with a surface reading that makes sense as an ordinary sentence. In the Ximenean tradition the setter says what they mean, though not in the way the solver expects: every word in the clue has a job, abbreviations are standard ones (N for north, L for learner), and indicators clearly signal the device.\n\nAnswers: {{answers}}\nStyle: {{style}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. For each answer, write one clue in the {{style}} style, with the enumeration in brackets after it, for example (5) or (3,4) for phrases and (5-4) for hyphenated words.\n2. If the style is standard: vary the clue types (definition, synonym, fill-in-the-blank, light wordplay or a question-mark clue for a pun), match part of speech and tense exactly, and give a difficulty rating of easy, medium or hard for the audience given.\n3. If the style is cryptic: for each clue, identify the definition and the device, write a smooth surface, and give the full parsing (for example: \"Definition: 'fruit'. Wordplay: anagram (indicated by 'crushed') of LEMON + reversal of…\"). Vary the devices across the set.\n4. Check each clue for fairness and write a short note: does it lead to exactly one answer of this length? Does the definition match the answer's part of speech? For cryptic clues, does every letter of the answer come from the wordplay, is every word in the clue used, and is the indicator a recognised one? Fix any clue that fails before presenting it.\n5. Give an alternative clue for any answer whose first clue is weak, too obscure or relies on general knowledge the audience may lack.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Write original clues; do not reuse clues from published crosswords.\n- Do not use the answer, or a word with the same root, inside its own clue.\n- Use spelling and references for the language variety and audience given; if unknown, ask or assume UK English for cryptic clues and US English for standard clues, and say so.\n- Avoid obscure abbreviations and references unless the audience is expert; prefer clues a solver can verify from the wordplay alone.\n- Count letters carefully: check the enumeration against the answer, and for anagrams check that the fodder contains exactly the answer's letters.\n- If an answer is not a real word or phrase, or is misspelled, say so rather than clueing it as given.\n- If no answers are given, ask for them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Clues\nA table. Standard: # | Clue | Enumeration | Answer | Difficulty. Cryptic: # | Clue | Enumeration | Answer | Parsing.\n## Fairness notes\nOne line per clue: what was checked, and any fix made.\n## Alternatives\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-riddles",
          "make-logic-puzzle"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a03c2060ccfaef99b11b7966b650853689984861458303a833feb388a04b6803"
