    {
      "id": "write-dbt-model",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a dbt model",
      "description": "Writes a dbt model from business logic, with declared sources, a stated grain, unique, not_null and relationships tests, column docs and a safe incremental strategy. Use when adding a dbt model.",
      "category": "data",
      "tags": [
        "analytics-engineering",
        "incremental-models",
        "data-tests",
        "grain"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business_logic",
          "description": "What the model should contain in business terms, including definitions such as what counts as an active customer or a completed order.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "source_tables",
          "description": "The source or upstream model names with their columns and types, plus the warehouse (Snowflake, BigQuery, Postgres, Databricks, Redshift…) if known.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "materialization",
          "description": "How dbt builds the model.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "view",
            "table",
            "incremental"
          ],
          "default": "table"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\ndbt models go wrong in quiet ways. A join fans out and nobody notices because no test pins the grain. A table name is hardcoded instead of using `ref` or `source`, so lineage and environments break. Business terms are implemented the way the author guessed. An incremental model filters on `max(updated_at)` with no lookback, so late-arriving rows are lost forever. Good dbt code states its grain, tests it, documents its columns and makes incremental loads safe to rerun.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a dbt model, materialised as {{materialization}}, for this logic:\n{{business_logic}}\n\nSources and upstream models:\n{{source_tables}}\n\n1. State the grain as \"one row per …\" and the key that enforces it. If the business logic leaves the grain or a definition open, ask, or state the assumption and put it in Open questions.\n2. Declare sources in a sources YAML file with `loaded_at_field` and freshness thresholds where a load timestamp exists. Reference upstream data only through `source()` and `ref()`.\n3. Add staging models only where a source needs renaming, casting or deduplication, one per source, following the project convention (`stg_<source>__<table>` if unknown).\n4. Write the model SQL as import CTEs, then logical CTEs, then a final `select` with an explicit column list. Handle nulls and duplicates in the sources explicitly, and note any time zone conversion.\n5. If materialised as incremental: set `unique_key`, choose `incremental_strategy` for the warehouse (merge where supported, otherwise delete+insert or insert_overwrite; check whether the project's dbt version supports microbatch), filter new rows inside `is_incremental()` with a lookback window for late-arriving data, set `on_schema_change`, and say when a full refresh is needed.\n6. Write a properties YAML file with the model and column descriptions and tests: `unique` and `not_null` on the key (or a combination-of-columns test for a composite key, naming the package it needs), `relationships` for foreign keys, `accepted_values` for categorical columns, and one singular test for the most important business rule. Use the `data_tests:` key on dbt 1.8 or later and `tests:` before that; if the project is on 1.8 or later and the rule is easier to show with fixed input rows, write a dbt unit test instead.\n7. Give the commands to build and test the model and its children, and a query that checks the grain.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only columns listed in the sources. If the logic needs a column that is not there, list it under Open questions instead of inventing it.\n- Keep SQL portable unless the warehouse is known. Flag any warehouse-specific function you use.\n- No `select *` in the final CTE. Keep Jinja to what the model needs.\n- Follow the project's naming and folder conventions if they are visible in the input.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions and grain\nThe grain statement, the key, and each assumption.\n\n## Files\nEach file in its own fenced block, preceded by its path (for example `models/marts/fct_orders.sql`, `models/marts/_marts__models.yml`, `models/staging/_sources.yml`).\n\n## Run and verify\nCommands, the grain-check query, and what a passing result looks like.\n\n## Open questions\nDefinitions or columns that need confirmation.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-star-schema",
          "write-data-quality-checks"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-engineer"
        ]
      },
