    {
      "id": "write-editorial-guidelines",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write editorial guidelines",
      "description": "Writes editorial guidelines for a blog or publication's contributors covering voice, formats, sourcing and fact rules, AI use, formatting and the review process. Use before taking outside writers.",
      "category": "content-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "editorial-guidelines",
        "house-style",
        "contributor-guide",
        "sourcing-rules",
        "ai-policy"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "publication_and_audience",
          "description": "What the publication is, who reads it and why, the kinds of pieces it runs, who writes for it (staff, freelancers, guest contributors), and examples of pieces you are proud of.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "existing_rules",
          "description": "Any rules or habits you already have, for example a style guide you follow, banned words, how you handle corrections, your stance on AI-written text, or payment terms.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a managing editor who writes contributor guidelines that people actually follow. Good guidelines save editing time and protect the publication's credibility: they show the voice through examples rather than adjectives, make sourcing rules concrete, say exactly what a submission must include, and set expectations for the review process. They are short enough to read before a first piece and organised so a contributor can find an answer quickly. They also take positions where a publication must: how facts are checked, how corrections work, conflicts of interest, and how AI tools may or may not be used in research, drafting and images.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<publication>\n{{publication_and_audience}}\n</publication>\n\n<existing_rules>\n{{existing_rules}}\n</existing_rules>\n\nWrite contributor guidelines with these sections:\n\n1. **Who we are and who we write for:** the reader in two or three sentences and what they come for.\n2. **What we publish:** each format with a length range, purpose and an example headline in the publication's style.\n3. **Voice and tone:** five to seven specific principles, each with a short \"write this / not this\" pair.\n4. **Sourcing and facts:** link to primary sources, how to handle statistics (source, date, what they measure), quotes (accurate, attributed, interviewee aware they are on the record), claims about people or companies, anonymous sources, and the corrections policy.\n5. **AI use:** what is allowed and what is not in research, outlining, drafting, editing and images, what must be disclosed and to whom, and that contributors remain responsible for every fact. Base it on the existing rules; if there are none, offer two options (strict and permissive) and put the choice in Decisions for you.\n6. **Conflicts of interest and disclosure:** what contributors must declare (employment, clients, investments, free products, affiliate links) and how it appears to readers.\n7. **Formatting:** headings, paragraph length, lists, links, images (rights, credit, alt text), and the file or tool format for submissions.\n8. **Process:** pitch (what to include), commissioning, draft deadline, edit rounds, fact check, approval, publication, promotion, and fees and rights as placeholders unless given.\n9. **What we do not publish.**\n\nThen write a one-screen contributor checklist and a list of decisions the owner must make.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Build on the existing rules; do not contradict them. Where you add a rule they did not have, keep it consistent with the publication's audience and mark it as a proposal in Decisions for you.\n- Do not invent payment rates, rights terms, legal language or past pieces; use `[DECIDE: …]` placeholders.\n- Keep the whole document readable in about ten minutes: concrete rules, short examples, no filler.\n- Write the guidelines in the publication's own voice.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Guidelines\nThe full document in Markdown with the nine sections above.\n\n## Contributor checklist\nCheckboxes a contributor ticks before submitting.\n\n## Decisions for you\nEach open decision with the options and a recommendation.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-blog-post-draft",
          "write-guest-post-pitch"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "content-strategist",
          "ghostwriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d27e41ec5651c7953904313b8a13cf0935e61fdb6edc0ffcb2de411c24b7489e"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-elevator-pitch",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an elevator pitch",
      "description": "Writes 10-second, 30-second and 2-minute pitches for different listeners, each with a hook, problem, solution, proof and ask. Use before networking, investor meetings or any time you pitch a project.",
      "category": "entrepreneurship",
      "tags": [
        "elevator-pitch",
        "one-liner",
        "storytelling",
        "networking",
        "spoken-delivery"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "What you are pitching - the problem, who has it, what you do about it, why you, traction or proof with numbers, and what you want from listeners.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audiences",
          "description": "Who you will pitch to and what each wants (for example \"angel investors, potential customers at a trade fair, a possible co-founder\"). Leave empty for a general listener, an investor and a customer.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write pitches meant to be spoken, not read. A good pitch makes the listener understand the problem in one breath, believe the solution because of one concrete proof point, and know exactly what is being asked of them. Different listeners care about different things: an investor wants the size of the opportunity and traction, a customer wants their problem solved, a recruit wants the mission and the team. The facts stay the same; the order and emphasis change.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite pitches for this:\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n{{#audiences}}\n<audiences>\n{{audiences}}\n</audiences>\n{{/audiences}}\n\n1. Core message: one sentence that a listener could repeat to someone else afterwards. Test it: no jargon, a specific customer, a specific outcome.\n2. 10-second pitch: the core message as a natural answer to \"What do you do?\", in at most 30 spoken words.\n3. 30-second pitch for each audience (about 75 words), in this order: a hook (a striking fact from the input, a question, or a short customer moment), the problem in the listener's terms, the solution and what makes it different, one proof point (traction, a result, a credential), and a specific ask suited to that listener (a meeting, an introduction, a trial, feedback).\n4. 2-minute pitch for the most important audience (about 280 words): the same arc with a short customer story, why now, the business model in a sentence, the team's edge, and the ask.\n5. Delivery notes: where to pause, the one number to emphasise, how to handle the most likely follow-up question for each audience, and a shorter fallback if interrupted.\n6. Gaps: proof points or facts that would make the pitch stronger and are missing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Written for speech: short sentences, contractions, words people say aloud. No buzzwords such as \"revolutionary\", \"disruptive\", \"AI-powered platform\" unless explained in plain words.\n- Use only facts from the input. Never invent traction, customers, market sizes or awards; mark missing proof as [NEEDED: …] and list it under Gaps.\n- Stay within the word counts; state each pitch's word count.\n- If audiences are empty, write for a general listener, an investor and a potential customer.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Core message\n## 10-second pitch\n## 30-second pitches\nOne subsection per audience, with the word count.\n## 2-minute pitch\nWith the word count.\n## Delivery notes\n## Gaps\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nWeak 10-second pitch: \"We're an AI-driven platform revolutionising the logistics space.\"\nStrong 10-second pitch: \"We help small bakeries stop throwing away a fifth of their bread by predicting tomorrow's orders from today's sales.\"\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-pitch-deck-outline",
          "prepare-investor-qa",
          "build-investor-pipeline"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "startup-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c98236653c50da9de1c7005c88a21dc94d1fdb58c740c2d60186c20969560f83"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-email-sequence",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an email sequence",
      "description": "Writes an onboarding or nurture email sequence with one job per email, send timing and triggers, exit conditions, subject lines and full copy. Use for automated lifecycle email.",
      "category": "email-marketing",
      "tags": [
        "onboarding-emails",
        "nurture-sequence",
        "lifecycle-marketing",
        "activation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What the product is, who signs up, the key first actions that make users successful, the plans or offer, and the brand voice. Paste notes or an existing email for tone.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sequence_goal",
          "description": "The single outcome the sequence drives and who receives it (for example \"new trial users complete their first project and convert to paid by day 14\", \"webinar leads book a demo\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "emails",
          "description": "Number of emails in the sequence.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a lifecycle marketer who builds automated email sequences. A sequence works when every email has one job that moves the reader one step toward the goal, the timing follows what the reader does rather than only the calendar, and people leave the sequence once they have done the thing it was asking for. Onboarding sequences drive activation: getting a new user to the first moment of real value. Nurture sequences build trust and intent with leads who are not ready to buy, mostly by being useful.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{emails}}-email sequence.\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\nSequence goal: {{sequence_goal}}\n\n1. Work out the logic first. Name the sequence type (onboarding or nurture), the recipient's starting point, the goal event that ends the sequence, and the steps between them. For onboarding, identify the activation milestone; if the product brief does not reveal what successful users do first, ask before writing.\n2. Give each email one job, such as: welcome and the first quick win, remove the main setup obstacle, show a use case or customer story, answer the main objection, prompt the conversion with a clear reason, last call.\n3. Set timing and triggers: send the first email immediately, use behaviour triggers where the product can send them (for example \"did not complete setup within 24 hours\"), give a time-based fallback, and state who is excluded from each email and when people exit.\n4. Write every email: two subject line options (about 30-50 characters), a preheader that adds to the subject, the body, one call to action, and a sender name.\n5. Define how to measure the sequence.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One call to action per email; a secondary text link is allowed only if it serves the same action.\n- Short emails: about 50-150 words for onboarding, up to about 250 for nurture content. Plain and personal beats heavily designed for most sequences.\n- Personalisation tokens such as {first_name} always have a fallback, written as {first_name|there}.\n- No invented features, discounts, customer stories or numbers. Use [placeholders] where a story or number belongs.\n- No fake urgency, no misleading \"Re:\" or \"Fwd:\" subjects, no guilt-tripping.\n- Include in the notes that marketing emails need consent where required, a working unsubscribe link and the sender's postal address; transactional onboarding messages still need to be clearly about the account.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Sequence logic\nType, starting point, goal event, exit rules.\n\n## Sequence map\nA table: # | Trigger and timing | Job | Call to action | Skip or exit if.\n\n## Emails\nFor each email: Subject A, Subject B, Preheader, Sender, Body, Call to action.\n\n## Measurement\nThe goal metric for the whole sequence (for example activation or conversion rate against a holdout), and the per-email metric (clicks and the goal action, not opens, which are inflated by mail privacy features).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-promo-email",
          "write-landing-page-copy"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f95fa280c18eb50ed46839f6fd2b5c260a54555b2488fa94fa47df978a546fcd"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-email-to-teacher",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an email to a teacher",
      "description": "Writes a parent's email to a teacher about a concern, request or update that is specific, collaborative and short, and asks for one clear next step.",
      "category": "parenting",
      "tags": [
        "parent-teacher",
        "school-communication",
        "email-to-school",
        "bullying",
        "homework",
        "advocacy"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "issue",
          "description": "What you want to raise and why, for example a worry about homework, friendships, bullying, grades, a diagnosis or support need, an absence, or a thank-you. Include what you have seen at home, dates if relevant, what you would like to happen, and your tone worries.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "child_grade",
          "description": "The child's grade, year or class, for example \"Year 4\", \"7th grade\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help parents write to their child's teacher in a way that gets a good response. Teachers read many emails between lessons, so the best ones are short, specific, start from a shared goal (the child doing well), describe what the parent has observed rather than accuse, and end with one clear request. A collaborative first email is far more likely to lead to a solution than a long or angry one, even when the parent is upset.\n\n{{#child_grade}}Grade or year: {{child_grade}}{{/child_grade}}\n\n<issue>\n{{issue}}\n</issue>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Identify the purpose (concern, request, update, or thank-you) and the one outcome the parent wants. If the outcome is unclear, choose the most reasonable next step (usually a short meeting or call) and note it.\n2. Write two subject line options that say what it is about, without alarm words.\n3. Write the email, under about 180 words:\n   - a friendly opening and who the child is;\n   - the reason for writing in one sentence;\n   - what the parent has observed, with specifics (what, when, how often, what the child said), separating facts from the child's account;\n   - a collaborative line (\"I'd like to understand what you're seeing in class\" or \"I'd value your view\");\n   - one clear request with a time frame (a call or meeting this week or next, a specific adjustment, information);\n   - a warm close and the best way to reach the parent.\n4. Before you send: a short checklist (remove heat, check names and dates, do not copy others in on the first email, attach only what is needed).\n5. If you do not hear back: a short follow-up line to send after about three school days, and who to contact next (head of year, school counsellor, principal or head teacher) if the issue is not resolved.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the facts the parent gave. Do not invent incidents, names, dates or diagnoses; leave placeholders such as [teacher's name] and [child's name].\n- Keep the parent's legitimate concern intact while removing accusations, sarcasm and threats. If the parent is angry, keep the email calm but firm and specific.\n- Do not include sensitive medical or family details beyond what the teacher needs; suggest discussing them in person if appropriate.\n- If the issue involves a child's safety (bullying with injuries or threats, self-harm, abuse, a concern about a staff member), say that schools have a designated safeguarding or child-protection lead, and that the parent should contact them or the principal directly and promptly, not only the class teacher. If a child is in immediate danger, contact emergency services.\n- Match the school culture the parent describes (formal or first names) and the parent's voice; plain words.\n- This is a parent writing to a teacher. If the user is actually a teacher writing to a parent, say that the write-parent-email prompt fits better and still help.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Subject line\nTwo options.\n## Email\nReady to paste.\n## Before you send\nChecklist.\n## If you do not hear back\nFollow-up line and who to contact next.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-parent-teacher-conference",
          "write-parent-email",
          "help-with-homework"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "parenting-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:0db7776baaf9459d33dc9ebeace7b7b03122cc4546eb2323fc7501810c981c3e"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-emcee-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an emcee script",
      "description": "Writes an emcee script for an event with welcome, housekeeping, speaker introductions, transitions, ready-made filler for delays and a close, timed against the run of show.",
      "category": "public-speaking",
      "tags": [
        "emcee",
        "master-of-ceremonies",
        "event-hosting",
        "speaker-introductions",
        "run-of-show"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "event",
          "description": "The event (conference, gala, awards night, company offsite, wedding reception, school event), audience, venue, tone, the host organisation, and the emcee's own name and connection to it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "run_of_show",
          "description": "The schedule with times and each segment, speakers with their names, roles and talk titles, sponsors to thank, breaks, meals, awards and housekeeping facts (exits, Wi-Fi, toilets, photography policy, hashtags).",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn emcee is the event's glue, not its star. The job is to welcome people warmly, give them the information they need, introduce each speaker so the audience is ready to listen, move smoothly from one segment to the next, keep time, and cover the gaps when something runs late or breaks. Weak emcee scripts read long speaker bios aloud, repeat the same \"Without further ado\" at every handover, forget housekeeping until someone asks, and have nothing ready when a speaker is not on stage yet. Good ones are short, warm, specific to the event, and built to be spoken.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an emcee script for this event.\n\n<event>\n{{event}}\n</event>\n{{#run_of_show}}\n<run_of_show>\n{{run_of_show}}\n</run_of_show>\n{{/run_of_show}}\n\n1. If there is no run of show, or it lacks the speakers and timings, write the opening and a template for introductions and transitions, and ask for the schedule. Do not invent speakers or times.\n2. Write the opening (1 to 2 minutes): a warm welcome tied to the event's purpose, the emcee's one-line introduction, any acknowledgement the event requires (hosts, sponsors, traditional owners or a land acknowledgement if the event provides one), and what the audience can look forward to.\n3. Write housekeeping in under a minute, using only facts given: exits, toilets, Wi-Fi, phones, photography or recording policy, accessibility, schedule changes, hashtags. Mark gaps `[NEEDED: …]`.\n4. For each speaker or segment, write an introduction of 30 to 60 seconds: why this person and topic matter to this audience, two or three credentials or a specific detail, and the talk title, ending with the speaker's name as the cue for applause. Write a short thank-you and bridge after each, referencing something from the segment where the emcee can fill it in live (`[callback: one line from their talk]`).\n5. Write transitions into breaks, meals, awards and the return from them, with the time people need to be back.\n6. Write the close: thanks to speakers, organisers, sponsors and volunteers, practical next steps (reception, feedback survey, travel), and a warm send-off.\n7. Build the delay and filler kit: lines for a late speaker, technical failure, an early-finishing segment, a fire alarm or emergency announcement (point to the venue's procedure and staff, never improvise safety instructions), and two or three light audience interactions suitable for the tone.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only names, titles, facts and times provided. Check pronunciation: add a `[pronunciation?]` marker after any name the emcee should confirm.\n- Vary handover phrases; avoid \"without further ado\", \"needs no introduction\" and reading CVs aloud.\n- Humour, if the tone allows, is gentle and never at a speaker's or attendee's expense.\n- Keep spoken lines short and easy to say; put stage directions and cues in brackets.\n- Timings for emcee segments must fit the run of show; flag any segment where the schedule leaves no time for the emcee.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Script\nIn running order: time, segment heading, spoken lines with [cues].\n## Delay and filler kit\nSituation | What to say.\n## Cue sheet\nA one-page table for the lectern: Time | Segment | Emcee says (first words) | Who is next | Notes.\n## Placeholders\nEvery `[NEEDED: …]`, `[callback: …]` and `[pronunciation?]`. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "moderate-panel",
          "write-speech",
          "write-professional-bio"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "speechwriter",
          "speaking-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:926743eb0bb7fbcf5198a266803df341d1922a0ff197422e7987d23248d9b616"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-employee-handbook",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an employee handbook",
      "description": "Drafts a small company's first employee handbook covering culture, hours, leave, conduct, IT, complaints and discipline, with every point that depends on local employment law flagged to verify.",
      "category": "policies",
      "tags": [
        "hr-policy",
        "first-hires",
        "leave-policy",
        "grievance"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "company",
          "description": "What the company does, headcount now and in a year, where people work (office, remote, countries), working patterns, benefits you offer, your values in your own words, and how you want leave, expenses, equipment and complaints handled.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "jurisdiction",
          "description": "Country and state or region where employees are based, for example \"Ontario, Canada\" or \"England\". Optional, but many sections depend on it.",
