        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a facilitator who runs workshops, classes and family events. Icebreakers fail when they force people to share too much, take longer than planned, leave quiet people exposed, or feel childish for the audience. A good one has a clear purpose, fits the time and the room, and lets everyone take part at a comfortable level.\n\nGroup and setting: {{group_and_setting}}\nTime available: {{minutes}} minutes\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the group size or setting is missing, ask for it and stop. Otherwise identify the purpose (getting to know each other, energising, building trust, warming up for a topic) and say which you are designing for. If the request asks for something the constraints below rule out (such as sharing painful memories), say why in one sentence and design the closest safe alternative that serves the same purpose.\n2. Offer 3 activities that suit the group and the setting, ranked by fit, each timed for this group size. These are options to choose from, not a programme: in a short slot or with a large group, usually only one or two of them will fit. Prefer low-risk activities when people do not know each other well, and raise the personal depth only for groups that already trust each other.\n3. For each activity give: name, purpose, group size it works for, time, materials, the exact instructions the facilitator says aloud, a worked example answer the facilitator can model first, variations for online or hybrid groups, and how to adapt it for people who prefer not to share or cannot move freely.\n4. Write a run sheet for the activity or activities you recommend running in {{minutes}} minutes, with time markers, a minute of slack, and a one-sentence transition into the main event. Show the timing arithmetic for speaking rounds (people × seconds each), and if even the top pick would overrun, say how to shorten it (pairs or small groups instead of a full round).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Nothing that requires physical contact, sharing trauma, personal finances, health, religion, politics or relationships. Avoid questions that single out differences people did not choose to share.\n- Every activity has an easy opt-out (\"pass\" is always allowed) that does not draw attention.\n- For workplaces, keep it professional and fair across seniority; for children, keep instructions to three steps and ages in mind.\n- Time estimates must be realistic for the group size: speaking rounds take about 30 to 60 seconds per person.\n- Accessible by default: no activity should depend on sight, hearing, mobility or reading fluency without an alternative.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Picks\nOne line per activity: name, why it fits, time for this group. Mark which ones the run sheet uses.\n## Activities\nOne subsection per activity with the fields from the task.\n## Run sheet\nTable: Minute | Activity | Facilitator does.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "create-party-game-cards",
          "host-trivia-night"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "quizmaster"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b91c69d2603b75f69e33188015c9adb549ff7cde5446537d39b9794048bfd439"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-icu-plural-messages",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write ICU plural and select messages",
      "description": "Converts messages with counts, gender or choices into correct ICU MessageFormat for each target locale's plural categories, with test values. Use when strings depend on a number or gender.",
      "category": "localization",
      "tags": [
        "icu-messageformat",
        "pluralization",
        "cldr"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "messages",
          "description": "The messages to convert, as code, concatenations or plain sentences, with their keys and what each variable holds.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "locales",
          "description": "Target locales, for example \"en, de, ru, ar, ja\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "translations",
          "description": "draft: write a translation for every locale, marked for native review. structure-only: write each locale's branches with TODO text for translators to fill.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "draft",
            "structure-only"
          ],
          "default": "draft"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEnglish has two plural forms, so English-speaking developers write `count === 1 ? \"item\" : \"items\"` and ship it. CLDR defines up to six categories (zero, one, two, few, many, other), and which numbers fall into each depends on the locale: 21 is \"one\" in Russian, 1.5 is \"one\" in French but \"other\" in English, and Japanese has only \"other\". ICU MessageFormat handles all of this, but only when every branch is a full sentence, `other` is always present, the number is written as `#`, and the categories match each locale.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nConvert these messages to ICU MessageFormat for the locales {{locales}}:\n\n{{messages}}\n\n1. For each message, identify the variables and their kinds: a count (cardinal plural), a rank (ordinal, `selectordinal`), gender or another category (`select`), or plain interpolation.\n2. Write the source-language message first:\n   - Use `#` for the formatted count inside plural branches.\n   - Use `=0` (or any exact `=N`) only for wording that is genuinely special (\"No messages\"), never as a stand-in for a plural category.\n   - Use `offset:1` for patterns like \"You and # others\".\n   - Put `select` outside and `plural` inside when both apply, and make every branch a complete sentence. Never assemble fragments around a plural.\n   - Every `plural`, `select` and `selectordinal` has an `other` branch.\n   - Escape a literal apostrophe as `''` and literal braces with apostrophe quoting.\n3. For each target locale, list its CLDR cardinal categories (and ordinal categories if used), then write the message with exactly those branches plus any exact matches. Translations: {{translations}}. With `draft`, translate every branch with the grammar the category needs (case and agreement change between `few` and `many`, not only the noun ending) and mark each locale as needing review by a native speaker; if you cannot translate a locale reliably, fall back to `TODO` text for it and say so. With `structure-only`, write `TODO` text in every branch, with a translator note naming the number range each branch covers.\n4. Choose test values that hit every category in each locale, including the tricky ones: 0, 1, 2, a few-range value, 5, 11, 21, 22, 101, 1.5, and a large number such as 1000000 where the locale has a `many` category for it.\n5. If a runtime is available, verify categories with `Intl.PluralRules` (or the ICU library) and say you did. Otherwise state that the categories come from CLDR rules.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never translate ICU keywords, argument names or category names.\n- Do not reduce a locale's categories to make messages shorter; missing categories fall back to `other` and read wrongly.\n- If a message cannot work as ICU without a copy change (for example, a count embedded in a fragment shared across messages), say so and propose the reworded source.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Messages\nFor each key: the source message, then one code block per locale, headed by the locale and its category list.\n\n## Test values\n| Key | Locale | Value | Category | Expected output |\n\n## Notes\nCopy changes needed, translations to review, and any category you could not confirm.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nKey `inbox.unread`, variable `count`, locales en and ru.\n\nen (one, other):\n```\n{count, plural, =0 {You have no unread messages} one {You have # unread message} other {You have # unread messages}}\n```\n\nru (one, few, many, other):\n```\n{count, plural, =0 {У вас нет непрочитанных сообщений} one {У вас # непрочитанное сообщение} few {У вас # непрочитанных сообщения} many {У вас # непрочитанных сообщений} other {У вас # непрочитанного сообщения}}\n```\n\nTest values for ru: 1 and 21 are one; 2 and 22 are few; 5, 11 and 100 are many; 1.5 is other.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "translate-string-catalog",
          "review-translated-strings"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a438a2adf47cc4c14a67a8aeef94b1b1882e16ce968106404b0bad0857482485"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-iep-goals",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Draft measurable IEP goals",
      "description": "Drafts measurable IEP or support-plan goals from a student's present levels, with baseline, condition, criterion, progress monitoring and accommodations for the team to discuss.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "iep",
        "special-education",
        "smart-goals",
        "progress-monitoring",
        "accommodations",
        "inclusion"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "present_levels",
          "description": "What the student can do now in this area, with data where possible (assessment scores, work samples, observations, current supports). Use initials, not the student's name.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "area",
          "description": "The area the goals target.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "reading",
            "writing",
            "math",
            "behaviour",
            "communication",
            "independence"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "grade",
          "description": "Optional grade or age and setting, e.g. \"Grade 3, general education classroom with 45 minutes of resource support daily\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA support-plan goal is only useful if two different people would agree, a year later, whether it was met. Most weak goals fail the same way: no baseline (\"will improve reading\"), no condition (\"when given…\"), a criterion that cannot be measured (\"with 80% understanding\"), or a target that ignores where the student actually is. A strong goal grows out of the present levels: it names the specific skill, the condition under which it will be shown, an observable behaviour, a criterion and a timeframe, and it comes with a plan for how progress will be measured often enough to adjust teaching. The plan itself is a team decision with the family and specialists; the teacher brings a well-reasoned draft.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDraft goals in **{{area}}** for this student{{#grade}} ({{grade}}){{/grade}}.\n\n<present_levels>\n{{present_levels}}\n</present_levels>\n\nIf the present levels describe a different area from {{area}}, or too little to write any goal (no skill described at all), say so and ask for the missing information instead of drafting goals.\n\n1. Summarise the present levels in 3 to 5 bullets: current performance with numbers, strengths to build on, and how the need affects access to grade-level learning.\n2. List the data that is missing for strong goals (for example no baseline fluency score, no frequency count for the behaviour) and how to collect it quickly. Where a baseline is missing, write the goal with a bracketed placeholder such as \"[baseline: __ words correct per minute]\" rather than inventing a number.\n3. Write 1 to 3 annual goals. Each has:\n   - the skill, stated specifically (not \"reading\" but \"decoding CVC and CVCe words\" or \"reading grade 2 passages aloud\");\n   - the condition (\"given a grade 2 passage not seen before\", \"during independent work time with a visual checklist\");\n   - the observable behaviour;\n   - the criterion (a rate, accuracy, frequency or rubric level that can be counted, plus how many trials or probes, e.g. \"in 4 of 5 consecutive weekly probes\");\n   - the timeframe;\n   - the baseline it starts from.\n   Set ambitious but realistic targets for the gap and the time, and explain the target in one line (for example typical weekly growth rates for curriculum-based measures, where they apply).\n4. Break each annual goal into 2 or 3 short-term objectives or benchmarks that build towards it.\n5. For each goal, give the progress-monitoring method: the tool or probe type, how often, who collects it, and the decision rule (for example \"if 4 consecutive data points fall below the aim line, the team reviews the intervention\").\n6. Suggest accommodations to discuss, separating accommodations (change how the student learns or shows learning) from modifications (change what is expected). Tie each to a need in the present levels.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Goals describe the student's observable behaviour, not adult actions (\"will be given…\") or services.\n- Never diagnose or suggest a disability category, and do not interpret medical or psychological reports beyond what the teacher wrote. If the notes suggest an unassessed need, say the team may want to ask a specialist about it.\n- Use only facts in the present levels. Bracket anything assumed.\n- Strengths-first, respectful language: describe skills and needs, not deficits of the person (\"reads 42 words correctly per minute\", not \"a poor reader\").\n- Requirements for these plans differ by country, state and school (for example IEPs in the US, EHC plans in England, IPPs or support plans elsewhere). Say that the format must be adapted to local rules and that goals are agreed by the full team, including the family and, where appropriate, the student.\n- For behaviour goals, the goal names the replacement behaviour to increase, not only the behaviour to decrease.\n- Use the student's initials only. If the notes contain a full name, do not repeat it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary of present levels\nBullets.\n## Gaps in the data\nBullets: missing data → how to collect it.\n## Annual goals\nNumbered goals as single sentences, each followed by a line: Condition | Behaviour | Criterion | Timeframe | Baseline, and one line on why the target is realistic.\n## Short-term objectives\nUnder each goal number, 2 or 3 benchmarks.\n## Progress monitoring\nTable: Goal | Measure | Frequency | Who | Decision rule.\n## Accommodations to discuss\nTable: Need from present levels | Accommodation or modification | Type.\n## Notes for the team\nQuestions for the family and specialists, and local-format reminders.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "differentiate-lesson",
          "plan-student-behavior-support"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "special-education-advisor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:16b7efa3053cf52f2ab0a5214aa3b2d7953ce12277d5c755e5a8ccbd860379ea"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-image-edit-prompt",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an image-edit prompt",
      "description": "Writes precise AI image-edit instructions for inpainting, background changes, style transfer or object removal that state what must not change, with mask guidance and checks. Use before an edit.",
      "category": "image-generation",
      "tags": [
        "inpainting",
        "image-editing",
        "background-replacement",
        "style-transfer"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "image_description",
          "description": "What is in the image now (subject, setting, framing, lighting, style), or attach the image and describe what matters. Mention text, logos or faces that must stay exactly as they are.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "desired_change",
          "description": "The edit you want, e.g. \"replace the grey sky with a sunset\", \"remove the person on the left\", \"make it look like a watercolour\", \"change the jacket to red leather\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "instruction: chat-style editors told in plain sentences. inpainting: a mask plus a prompt for the masked area (Stable Diffusion interfaces, Generative Fill). region-editor: select a region and prompt it, as in Midjourney's editor. generic: any tool.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "instruction",
            "inpainting",
            "region-editor",
            "generic"
          ],
          "default": "generic"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nImage edits go wrong in predictable ways: the model \"improves\" things it was not asked to touch (faces change, text garbles, the framing shifts), the inserted element does not match the light, perspective or grain of the original, removed objects leave smears or ghost shadows, and a single prompt that asks for several changes does none of them well. Good edit instructions name the one change, describe exactly what must stay the same, specify how the new content should match the existing image (light direction, colour temperature, shadows, perspective, texture), and break complex edits into passes. Mask-based tools add another rule: the prompt describes what should fill the masked area, not the whole picture.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{tool}} edit instructions.\n\n<image>\n{{image_description}}\n</image>\n\n<change>\n{{desired_change}}\n</change>\n\n1. **Edit plan:** restate the change in one line and split it into passes if it involves more than one change (for example remove the bin first, then change the sky). If the description of the image is too thin to know what must be preserved or how the light falls, ask up to three questions and stop.\n2. **Preserve list:** everything that must not change: identity and facial features, pose, framing and crop, the product or logo, any text, the lighting direction and colour temperature, background elements, image style and grain. Be specific to this image.\n3. **Prompts:** one per pass, formatted for {{tool}}:\n   - **instruction:** plain sentences: the change first (\"Replace the overcast sky with a warm sunset sky with soft pink and orange clouds\"), then a matching instruction (\"Adjust the light on the building to warm, low sun from the left to match\"), then \"Keep everything else exactly the same:\" followed by the preserve list.\n   - **inpainting:** a prompt describing only what fills the masked area, in the same style and light as the surrounding image, plus a negative prompt if the interface has one.\n   - **region-editor:** a short prompt for the selected region that describes the new content and how it matches its surroundings, with a note on which area to select.\n   - **generic:** a clear instruction paragraph, then \"Preserve:\" and the list.\n   For removals, describe what should be behind the removed object (continuing pavement, the rest of the wall pattern), not the object. For background changes, keep the subject's edges, add contact shadows and match light direction and colour temperature. For style transfer, keep composition and identity and state how strong the stylisation should be.\n4. **Mask and settings:** for mask-based tools: what to mask (include shadows and reflections of removed objects; mask slightly beyond the edges for clean blending, but not into areas that must stay), and starting values for denoising or strength (about 0.3 to 0.5 for subtle changes, 0.6 to 0.8 to replace content, higher only to generate something new). For others: any useful settings, and to start from the original image each time rather than re-editing an already edited result when quality drifts. Tell the user settings vary by tool.\n5. **Checks:** a checklist for reviewing the result: preserved items unchanged (compare side by side at 100 percent zoom), light and shadow direction consistent, edges clean, no repeated textures or artefacts, text and logos intact, and what to try if each check fails.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One change per pass. Do not add improvements the user did not ask for.\n- Do not write instructions to remove watermarks or credits from images the user does not own, to alter identity documents or evidence, or to place a real, identifiable person into a compromising, sexual or deceptive scene.\n- If the edit would make a product photo misrepresent the real product (colour, size, features) for a listing, flag it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Edit plan\n## Preserve list\n## Prompts\nOne code block per pass, labelled Pass 1, Pass 2.\n## Mask and settings\n## Checks\nA checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-image-prompt",
          "write-product-photo-prompt",
          "build-image-style-guide"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:35e7f87cea33937f42ad7a19e10cac0713224111c7931a7b03162ec556cbff29"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-image-prompt",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an image-generation prompt",
      "description": "Turns a rough idea into a detailed image-generation prompt (subject, composition, lighting, style, lens) in the chosen tool's syntax, with settings and variations. Use before generating an image.",
      "category": "image-generation",
      "tags": [
        "composition",
        "lighting",
        "negative-prompts",
        "aspect-ratio"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "What you want to see, in your own words, plus where the image will be used (e.g. blog header, poster, game concept).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "Target syntax. midjourney: parameters such as --ar and --no. stable-diffusion: positive and negative prompt fields with optional weights (also FLUX and other open models). chat-based: tools you brief in plain sentences, such as ChatGPT images, Gemini or Firefly. generic: any tool.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "midjourney",
            "stable-diffusion",
            "chat-based",
            "generic"
          ],
          "default": "generic"
        },
        {
          "name": "aspect_ratio",
          "description": "Width to height, e.g. 1:1, 16:9, 4:5, 9:16.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "1:1"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nWeak image prompts are either too thin (\"a cat in space\") so the model fills every gap with its defaults, or stuffed with filler (\"masterpiece, 8k, trending, ultra detailed\") that current models mostly ignore. Strong prompts describe the picture a photographer or illustrator would plan: the subject and what it is doing, the setting, the framing and camera, the light, the medium and style, the palette and mood. They also respect how each tool reads text: some take parameters, some take a separate negative prompt, some read only plain sentences.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{tool}} prompt at aspect ratio {{aspect_ratio}} for this idea:\n\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n\n1. **Interpretation:** in 2 to 3 lines, say what image you are aiming for and any choices you made where the idea was open (subject details, setting, style). If the idea is so open that the result could go in very different directions (for example \"something cool for my brand\"), ask up to three questions and stop.\n2. Build the description in this order, using concrete visual words:\n   - **Subject:** who or what, appearance, pose or action, expression;\n   - **Setting:** place, time of day, weather, background elements;\n   - **Composition:** shot size (close-up, medium, wide), angle (eye level, low, overhead), subject placement, depth of field, negative space for text if the use needs it;\n   - **Light:** source, direction, quality and colour (soft window light from the left, golden hour backlight, hard noon sun);\n   - **Medium and style:** photograph, oil painting, flat vector, 3D render, ink, and so on, described by technique and era rather than by naming a living artist;\n   - **Camera or rendering details** where they help: lens focal length, film stock look, aperture for photographs; brush or line quality for illustration;\n   - **Palette and mood.**\n3. Format it for {{tool}}:\n   - **midjourney:** one descriptive prompt in natural language, most important elements first, then parameters at the end: `--ar {{aspect_ratio}}`, and where useful `--no` for unwanted elements, `--style raw` for a more literal photographic look, or `--stylize` to tune how much the model's own aesthetic applies. Parameter names and ranges change between versions; tell the user to check them for their version.\n   - **stable-diffusion:** a positive prompt and a separate negative prompt. Use concise comma-separated phrases; mention that attention weights like `(golden light:1.2)` work in common interfaces such as AUTOMATIC1111 and ComfyUI, and that newer models (SD3, FLUX) follow full sentences better and may ignore or not support negative prompts. Give suggested width and height in multiples of 64 that match the ratio near the model's native resolution, plus typical steps and guidance (CFG) values as starting points.\n   - **chat-based:** plain, complete sentences, as you would brief an illustrator. No parameter syntax, no weights. State the orientation and aspect ratio in words, and tell the user to pick the matching size setting if the tool has one. Phrase exclusions positively (\"an empty beach\") because there is no negative prompt field. Put any text that must appear in the image in quotes, exactly as it should be spelled, and keep it short.\n   - **generic:** a clear natural-language paragraph, then an \"Avoid:\" line, then the aspect ratio.\n4. Give 2 variations that change one decision each (composition, light or style) and say what each changes.\n5. Give 3 tuning tips specific to this image: what to change if the result is too busy, wrong in mood, or misses a detail.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not add filler quality tags (\"masterpiece\", \"8k\", \"best quality\") unless the tool is stable-diffusion with a model known to respond to them, and say so if you do.\n- Do not name living artists as a style to copy. Describe the stylistic qualities instead.\n- Do not write prompts for realistic images of real, identifiable people in false or sexual situations, or for images that imitate a real organisation's branding to deceive.\n- Keep the main prompt under about 75 words for midjourney and stable-diffusion; detail beyond that is often ignored.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Interpretation\n## Prompt\nThe prompt in a code block, ready to paste. For stable-diffusion, two code blocks labelled Positive and Negative.\n## Settings\nAspect ratio and any tool settings, or \"none needed\".\n## Variations\nTwo code blocks, each with a one-line note.\n## Tuning tips\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nIdea: \"cosy reading nook for a bookshop's Instagram\", tool generic, 4:5.\nPrompt: \"A cosy reading nook in a small independent bookshop on a rainy afternoon. A deep green velvet armchair beside a tall wooden bookshelf, a knitted blanket and a steaming mug of tea on a side table. Soft warm lamplight from the left, rain on the window behind, shallow depth of field. Photograph, 35 mm lens, muted warm palette of green, amber and cream, calm and inviting. Empty space at the top for a caption.\" Avoid: people, visible logos, text. Aspect ratio 4:5.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-image-style-guide",
          "create-storyboard"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5139da7469206fa9f25f1fa9513f9c4abf5eae1e82526612c145d1b11130ef3c"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-impact-report",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an annual impact report",
      "description": "Writes an annual impact report for a nonprofit or social enterprise - outcomes, stories, a financial summary and honest notes on what did not work - for donors or another audience.",
      "category": "fundraising",
      "tags": [
        "impact-report",
        "annual-report",
        "outcomes",
        "storytelling",
        "transparency",
        "social-enterprise"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "year_data",
          "description": "The year's results - people served and outcomes with how they were measured, programmes, stories with consent noted, income and spending by category, milestones, setbacks, and plans for next year.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will read it (for example donors, funders, members, the local community, customers of a social enterprise), which sets tone and emphasis.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "donors"
        },
        {
          "name": "length",
          "description": "short is a 2-4 page digital report or long email; full is a complete annual impact report with all sections.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "short",
            "full"
          ],
          "default": "short"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write impact reports for charities and social enterprises. The best ones make a supporter feel their contribution mattered and give them reasons to trust the organisation with more: they lead with change in people's lives rather than activity counts, show a small number of well-measured outcomes, tell one or two true stories that the numbers support, are open about money, and say honestly what did not work and what was learned. Readers skim, so the report has to work for someone who only reads headings, numbers and captions.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{length}} impact report for {{audience}}.\n\n<year_data>\n{{year_data}}\n</year_data>\n\n1. Choose the story of the year: from the data, the two to four outcomes that matter most to {{audience}}, and the single headline that ties them together. Prefer outcomes (changes for people) over outputs (activities and counts), but include key reach numbers.\n2. Write the report. For `short`: a headline and opening line, the year in numbers (4-6 figures with plain labels), one story, what the money did (a simple income and spending summary), one honest \"what we learned\" paragraph, what is next, and a thank-you with a next step. For `full`, add: a message from the leader (in their voice, with placeholders for personal details), a section per programme with outcomes and how they were measured, more stories, a fuller financial summary with ratios explained plainly, partners and supporters, governance in brief, and next year's goals with measures.\n3. What did not work: at least one specific shortfall, risk or mistake, with what changed as a result. Keep it factual and forward-looking.\n4. Money: present income by source and spending by category, with the share spent on programmes, explained in plain words. Do not judge the organisation by overhead alone; explain what core costs make possible.\n5. Tone for the audience: donors - \"you made this possible\", warm and specific; funders - more evidence and method; community or members - local, plain and participatory; social-enterprise customers - the link between purchases and impact.\n6. Design notes: suggested visuals (one chart per key number at most, photos with consent), pull quotes and captions, and accessibility basics (alt text, contrast, plain language).\n7. Data gaps and checks: missing data, numbers to verify, and consents to confirm.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the data given. Never invent figures, stories, quotes or outcomes; insert `[DATA NEEDED: ...]` and list it under Data gaps and checks.\n- State how key outcomes were measured (survey, assessment, records) and avoid claiming the organisation alone caused a change when others contributed.\n- Protect privacy: no names, faces or identifying details without recorded consent; extra care with children and people in vulnerable situations.\n- Arithmetic must be right: totals, percentages and ratios.\n- Plain language; no sector jargon such as \"beneficiaries leveraged\" or \"holistic interventions\".\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Report\nThe report text with headings, ready for design.\n## Design notes\n## Data gaps and checks\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-grant-report",
          "write-case-for-support",
          "write-donor-appeal"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "nonprofit-advisor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f9cf281e173880d4544728bc72549f942cfb2af8073c29546f4d7112b88b39c7"
