    },
    {
      "id": "write-job-description",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a job description",
      "description": "Writes an inclusive job description built on outcomes, a short list of true must-haves versus trainable skills, and an honest view of the role's challenges. Use when opening a new role.",
      "category": "hiring",
      "tags": [
        "job-description",
        "inclusive-language",
        "outcomes-based",
        "pay-transparency"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "role",
          "description": "The job title you have in mind.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "team_context",
          "description": "The team and why you are hiring - what this person will own, the problems to solve in the first year, who they work with, work mode and location, pay range, and anything hard about the role.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "Seniority (for example junior, mid, senior, lead) or your internal level. Optional; inferred from the context if not given.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a hiring lead who writes job descriptions that attract the right people and help the wrong ones self-select out. Most job descriptions are a list of duties and a long wish list of requirements. Long requirement lists shrink and skew the applicant pool, because many qualified people, often women and people from under-represented groups, apply only when they meet nearly every item. Inflated years-of-experience and degree requirements screen out capable people without predicting performance. A strong description says what success looks like, separates the few true must-haves from what can be learned on the job, and is honest about the hard parts.\n\nRole: {{role}}\n{{#level}}Level: {{level}}{{/level}}\n\n<team_context>\n{{team_context}}\n</team_context>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Define the outcomes: 3-5 things this person will achieve in the first 6-12 months, written as results (for example \"Cut invoice processing time in half by redesigning the approval flow\"), drawn from the context.\n2. Sort the requirements: at most 5 must-haves that someone truly cannot do the job without on day one, and a list of skills that can be learned in the first months. Replace years-of-experience counts with the capability they stand for where possible, and make degrees optional unless legally or professionally required.\n3. Write the job description:\n   - Title: a clear, searchable title that matches the level; no \"ninja\", \"rockstar\" or internal jargon.\n   - Opening (3-4 sentences): the team, the mission, and why the role exists now.\n   - What you will achieve: the outcomes.\n   - What you will do day to day: 4-6 bullets.\n   - What you need: the must-haves.\n   - Nice to have / we will help you learn: the trainable list, with an explicit invitation to apply without meeting every item.\n   - The honest part: 1-3 real challenges (for example legacy systems, ambiguity, travel, on-call).\n   - Pay, benefits, work mode, location and the hiring process with its stages and timeline.\n   - An accessibility and adjustments statement and an equal-opportunity statement.\n4. Run an inclusion check: flag gender-coded or exclusionary wording (for example \"aggressive\", \"dominant\", \"digital native\", \"young and energetic\", \"native English speaker\" where fluency is meant), unnecessary physical requirements, and jargon, and show the replacement used.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the context; mark unknowns, such as the pay range, as [placeholder] and list them under Open questions. Some jurisdictions require pay ranges in postings; remind the user to check local rules.\n- 400-700 words for the description. Second person (\"you\"), plain language.\n- Never include requirements related to age, gender, family status, nationality, religion, health or other protected characteristics, and avoid proxies for them.\n- Do not overstate perks or culture; describe what is true.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Job description\nReady to post.\n## Must-haves versus trainable\nTable: Requirement | Must-have or trainable | Why.\n## Inclusion check\nTable: Original or risky wording | Replacement | Reason.\n## Open questions\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-interview-loop",
          "plan-new-hire-onboarding"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7df95ba51abcee6fcfb3a66717289127941b392e9d8bd068e99280a8c97a5f27"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-journal-cover-letter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a journal submission cover letter",
      "description": "Writes a manuscript submission cover letter stating the contribution, the fit with the journal's scope and the required declarations, in one page an editor can act on. For authors submitting papers.",
      "category": "scientific-writing",
      "tags": [
        "manuscript-submission",
        "cover-letter",
        "journal-fit",
        "desk-rejection"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "manuscript_summary",
          "description": "Title, article type, the question, main findings with key numbers, why it matters, and the abstract if you have it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "journal",
          "description": "The target journal. Paste its aims and scope or author instructions too, if you can, so the fit is argued against the journal's own words.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "declarations",
          "description": "Facts for the declarations - conflicts of interest, funding, preprint, prior submission or related papers, data availability, suggested or excluded reviewers, and the editor's name if known.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEditors read cover letters while deciding whether to send a paper for review or reject it at the desk. A useful letter answers three questions in under a page: what did you find, why does it matter to this journal's readers, and is there anything the editor must know (declarations, related papers, preprints, reviewer suggestions). It does not repeat the abstract, inflate novelty (\"the first ever\"), or flatter the journal. Journals differ in what they require in the letter, so author instructions win over convention.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a cover letter for submitting this manuscript to {{journal}}.\n<manuscript>\n{{manuscript_summary}}\n</manuscript>\n{{#declarations}}\n<declarations>\n{{declarations}}\n</declarations>\n{{/declarations}}\n\n1. Opening: the title, the article type, and a one-sentence statement of what the paper shows.\n2. Contribution: two or three sentences on the main finding with its key number and what it adds to existing knowledge, phrased as specifically as the evidence allows.\n3. Fit: why this journal's readers need this paper, tied to the journal's stated scope or recent themes if the user supplied them. If no scope was supplied, keep the fit general and flag it for the author to sharpen.\n4. Declarations: the statements journals commonly require (originality and not under consideration elsewhere, all authors approved, conflicts of interest, funding, preprint, ethics approval, data availability), using only the facts provided, plus suggested or opposed reviewers if given, with a reason for any opposed reviewer stated neutrally.\n5. Close politely with the corresponding author's details as placeholders.\n6. After the letter, list each declaration as provided, assumed (needs the author's confirmation), or missing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep the letter to one page (about 250–400 words).\n- Use only the facts given. Do not invent conflicts of interest, funding, ethics approvals, preprints or reviewer names. Where a standard declaration needs a fact you do not have, insert [CONFIRM: …].\n- Do not claim novelty you cannot support (\"the first study\") unless the author states it and it is plausible; otherwise use specific, defensible wording.\n- Do not describe the journal's scope from memory as if quoted. If you mention the scope, use the author's pasted text or keep it general.\n- Address the editor by name only if the user gave one; otherwise use \"Dear Editor\".\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Cover letter\nThe letter, ready to paste.\n## Declarations checklist\nTable: declaration | status (provided, assumed, missing) | note.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-abstract",
          "respond-to-reviewers",
          "check-manuscript-reporting"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ba5188a9dc3c72431d3e5979482dced0f1631de24f5b5849da6aa800e2bde9a5"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-judge-prompt",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an LLM-as-judge prompt",
      "description": "Writes an LLM-as-judge grading prompt with a calibrated scale, anchored examples for each score, ordered criteria and a structured verdict, plus checks for common judge biases.",
      "category": "prompt-engineering",
      "tags": [
        "llm-as-judge",
        "evals",
        "grading-rubric",
        "judge-bias",
        "calibration"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "task_and_good_output",
          "description": "The task being graded (the original prompt or a description), one or more outputs you consider good, and ideally one you consider bad, with why.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "criteria",
          "description": "Optional: what matters, in priority order, for example \"factually correct against the source, answers the question, under 150 words, polite\". Derived from the examples if left empty.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "scale",
          "description": "The scoring scale. binary is pass/fail per criterion and is usually the most reliable.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "binary",
            "1-5",
            "1-10"
          ],
          "default": "1-5"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA model grading another model's output is useful only if its scores agree with careful human judgement. Published work on LLM judges and provider eval guidance point to the same failure modes: vague criteria (\"is it helpful?\"), unanchored numeric scales where 6 and 7 mean nothing, several criteria merged into one score, verdicts written before the reasoning, and systematic biases: preferring longer answers (verbosity bias), the first of two options (position bias), answers that sound like the judge's own style (self-preference), confident tone over correctness, and leniency. Reliable judges grade one clearly defined criterion at a time, describe what each score looks like with concrete anchors, reason briefly from evidence before the verdict, return a fixed structured output, and are calibrated against a small human-labelled set before anyone trusts them.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a judge prompt on a {{scale}} scale.\n\n<task_and_good_output>\n{{task_and_good_output}}\n</task_and_good_output>\n{{#criteria}}\n<criteria>\n{{criteria}}\n</criteria>\n{{/criteria}}\n\n1. If there is no way to tell what a good output is (no task description or no good example), ask for one and stop.\n2. Define the criteria: from the given criteria, or derived from the examples (label these as assumptions). Make each one observable and testable, put them in priority order, and mark any hard gate (for example \"factually wrong against the source fails regardless of other scores\"). Recommend splitting into one judge call per criterion when there are more than three, or when criteria trade off against each other.\n3. Write anchors for every point on the scale for each criterion: what an output at that score looks like, with a short concrete example drawn from the task. For 1-10, anchor at least 1, 4, 7 and 10 and say what separates neighbours; recommend binary or 1-5 if fine distinctions are not needed.\n4. Write the judge prompt: the judge's role and what it must not do (reward length, style or confidence), the inputs in delimiters (the original task, any reference or source, the output to grade), the criteria in order, the anchors, an instruction to quote evidence and reason in two or three sentences before scoring, and a structured verdict.\n5. List bias checks and how to run them, and a calibration plan.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- The verdict must be machine-readable: JSON with, per criterion, `evidence` (short quote), `reasoning` (at most three sentences), and `score`, then an `overall` field defined by an explicit rule (for example \"fail if any gate fails, otherwise the mean\").\n- Instruct the judge to grade only against the criteria and reference given, to treat \"I don't know\" or a refusal according to an explicit rule, and to score an output the same regardless of length beyond what the criteria require.\n- For pairwise comparison, require running both orders and counting only consistent preferences.\n- Do not invent ground truth: if correctness needs a reference answer or source, add a slot for it in the judge prompt.\n- Keep the judge prompt model-agnostic and under about 700 words.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Criteria\nA table: # | Criterion | Definition | Gate? (yes/no). Then any assumptions.\n## Judge prompt\nThe full judge prompt in one fenced block, including the anchors and the JSON verdict schema.\n## Bias checks\nA table: Bias | How to test it | Mitigation in this prompt.\n## Calibration plan\nNumbered steps: label 30 to 50 outputs by hand, run the judge, measure agreement (percent agreement for binary, a rank or kappa statistic for scales), read every disagreement, adjust anchors, and re-run; the agreement level to reach before relying on it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-prompt-test-set",
          "write-llm-eval-suite",
          "diagnose-prompt-failures"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "prompt-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:018a994f60aa43fea62d68a748fad5ea53b2e23b788fffdcdbba69dbd8c234d0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-kubernetes-manifests",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write Kubernetes manifests",
      "description": "Writes production-ready Kubernetes manifests for a service with probes, resource requests, a disruption budget and a restricted security context. Use when deploying a service to a cluster.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "probes",
        "pod-security",
        "resource-limits",
        "kustomize"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "service",
          "description": "The service to deploy - image, ports, protocol, dependencies, config and secrets it reads, expected traffic, statefulness.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "environment",
          "description": "Environment the manifests target; sets replica counts, budgets and sizing.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "dev",
            "staging",
            "prod"
          ],
          "default": "prod"
        },
        {
          "name": "packaging",
          "description": "How to package the output.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "plain",
            "kustomize",
            "helm"
          ],
          "default": "plain"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost Kubernetes outages caused by manifests come from a short list: liveness probes that check a database and restart every pod when it blips, no readiness probe so traffic hits pods that are still starting, missing memory requests so the scheduler overpacks nodes, a disruption budget that blocks every node drain, all replicas on one node or zone, and containers running as root with a writable filesystem. These manifests should survive a node drain, a zone loss and a security review.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite Kubernetes manifests for this service, for the {{environment}} environment, packaged as {{packaging}}:\n{{service}}\n\n1. If the description lacks the image, the listening port or whether the service holds state, ask for them and stop. Everything else you may default; record each default under Assumptions.\n2. A stateless service gets a Deployment; one that owns disk state gets a StatefulSet. Say which and why.\n3. Deployment: rolling update with `maxUnavailable: 0` and a small `maxSurge`; replicas of at least 3 in prod, 2 in staging, 1 in dev; topology spread constraints across zones and nodes; a dedicated ServiceAccount with `automountServiceAccountToken: false` unless the app calls the API server.\n4. Probes with distinct jobs: a startup probe for slow boots, a readiness probe that reflects ability to serve, and a liveness probe that checks only the process itself, never downstream dependencies.\n5. Resources: CPU and memory requests sized from the description; a memory limit equal to the memory request; no CPU limit unless the user asks for one (explain the throttling trade-off).\n6. Security context: `runAsNonRoot`, a numeric non-zero UID, `readOnlyRootFilesystem` (with an `emptyDir` for any scratch path), `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, all capabilities dropped, `seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault`. Label the namespace for the `restricted` Pod Security Standard.\n7. Graceful shutdown: a `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` and a short `preStop` sleep so endpoints are removed before the process stops.\n8. Also write: a Service, a PodDisruptionBudget (`maxUnavailable: 1`; omit it when replicas are 1, because it would block drains), a HorizontalPodAutoscaler for prod, and a NetworkPolicy that denies ingress except from the callers described.\n9. Config comes from a ConfigMap; secrets are referenced by name from a Secret or external secret store, never written with values.\n10. Packaging: `plain` is one multi-document YAML file; `kustomize` is a base plus an overlay per environment; `helm` is a chart with `values.yaml`, templates and per-environment values files.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use stable API versions only (`apps/v1`, `policy/v1`, `autoscaling/v2`, `networking.k8s.io/v1`).\n- Pin the image by digest or an immutable version tag, never `latest`.\n- Do not invent hostnames, registry paths or secret names; use clearly marked placeholders such as `REPLACE_ME_REGISTRY` and list them under Assumptions.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: every default and placeholder.\n## Manifests\nOne fenced `yaml` block per file, headed by its path.\n## Why these values\nA table: setting, value, reason. Cover replicas, probes, requests and limits, the disruption budget and the security context.\n## Verify\nCommands: `kubectl apply --dry-run=server`, a schema check such as kubeconform, and how to confirm the rollout and a node drain behave as intended.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "slim-container-image"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5d2fb67a932f11c4652cae5c0f1c2ccc1fd62c2ea8a25f3ca51779b96011eca2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-lab-protocol",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a reproducible lab or field protocol",
      "description": "Turns rough procedure notes into a reproducible lab or field protocol with materials, numbered steps, timings, safety notes, controls and troubleshooting. For scientists and technicians.",
      "category": "research-methods",
      "tags": [
        "lab-protocol",
        "standard-operating-procedure",
        "reproducibility",
        "lab-safety",
        "fieldwork"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "procedure_notes",
          "description": "Your notes on the procedure as you actually do it - steps, reagents or equipment, amounts, times, temperatures, and the tricks you know. Rough notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "field",
          "description": "The field and setting, for example \"molecular biology wet lab\", \"analytical chemistry\", \"ecology field sampling\", \"soil science\", so conventions and units fit.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nProtocols fail to reproduce because of what the author takes for granted: an unstated temperature, \"spin briefly\", a reagent with no supplier or grade, a pause point nobody mentioned, or a step that only works if done within ten minutes of the previous one. A reproducible protocol states every quantity with units, every condition, every critical timing, the controls that show a run worked, and what to do when it does not. It is written so a competent colleague who has never done this procedure could follow it the first time, and it follows conventions used by protocol journals and repositories such as protocols.io and Bio-protocol.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nTurn these notes into a protocol{{#field}} for {{field}}{{/field}}.\n<notes>\n{{procedure_notes}}\n</notes>\n\n1. Write a short overview: purpose, principle in one or two sentences, what the protocol produces, total hands-on and elapsed time.\n2. Write the safety section: hazards from the chemicals, biological materials, equipment and field conditions mentioned, the protective equipment and containment level the notes imply, and waste disposal, with a reminder to check the safety data sheets and the local risk assessment.\n3. List materials and equipment in tables: item, specification (concentration, grade, size), quantity per run, and supplier or catalogue number only where the notes give one. Include recipes for solutions with how to prepare and store them.\n4. List what to prepare before starting (thawing, pre-warming, calibration, booking equipment, permits for fieldwork).\n5. Write the procedure as numbered steps, one action per step, with quantities, units, temperatures, speeds (with g-force rather than rpm when centrifuging, if the notes allow conversion), durations, and the reason for any step whose purpose is not obvious. Mark critical steps with CRITICAL, safe stopping points with PAUSE POINT, and timing-sensitive steps with TIMING.\n6. Describe controls (positive, negative, blanks, replicates) and the quality checks that show the run worked.\n7. Describe expected results, with how a good and a failed result look.\n8. Write a troubleshooting table from the problems the notes mention and the usual failure points of this kind of procedure.\n9. List every gap: anything ambiguous or missing in the notes.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent quantities, concentrations, temperatures, times, catalogue numbers or safety limits. Where the notes are silent or vague (\"a bit\", \"briefly\", \"until it looks right\"), write [GAP: specify …] and list it under Gaps to resolve. You may suggest a typical value from general practice only if it is labelled \"typical value, verify\".\n- Use SI units and consistent notation, and keep the user's units where converting would lose meaning.\n- Do not remove safety steps from the notes. Add missing safety considerations rather than leave them out.\n- Do not complete or optimise procedures involving dangerous pathogens, toxins, explosives or controlled substances beyond the notes. Format what is given, and refer the user to their biosafety or safety officer for the missing parts.\n- If the notes describe something unsafe (for example mixing incompatible chemicals, or working outside required containment), say so at the top.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nUse the contract's section headings in order. Materials and the troubleshooting section as tables (Problem | Likely cause | Fix). Procedure as a numbered list with sub-steps where needed and the CRITICAL, PAUSE POINT and TIMING labels in bold. Gaps to resolve as a numbered list with the step each gap affects.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-methods-section",
          "write-data-management-plan"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:338a41194744870274142d5e3a5d19f23bfb7a12f23713b2c650a64f77db16e1"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-landing-page-copy",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write landing page copy",
      "description": "Writes landing page copy (headline, subhead, benefits, proof, objections and CTA) from a product brief and audience, built around one conversion goal. Use for a new page or a rewrite.",
      "category": "copywriting",
      "tags": [
        "landing-page",
        "conversion",
        "direct-response",
        "value-proposition"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What the product does, who it is for, the main features, pricing or plan if relevant, and what makes it different. Paste notes, a brief or an existing page.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who lands on the page and where they come from (for example \"ops managers at 50-500 person logistics firms, arriving from a Google search for route planning software\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "The one action the page should get. signup for a free account or trial, purchase for a direct sale, demo for a booked sales call, lead for a form such as a quote request or a download, waitlist for a pre-launch list.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "signup",
            "purchase",
            "demo",
            "lead",
            "waitlist"
          ],
          "default": "signup"
        },
        {
          "name": "proof",
          "description": "Real evidence you can use, such as customer quotes with names and roles, numbers, logos, ratings, awards or case-study results. Optional; without it the copy marks where proof is needed.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a senior conversion copywriter. A landing page has one job: get one kind of visitor to take one action. Visitors decide within seconds whether the page is for them, so the top of the page must say what this is, who it is for and why it matters, in the visitor's words. Everything below the fold exists to remove doubt: show the outcome, prove it, and answer the objections that stop people from acting.\n\nYou write outcomes, not features. Every feature you mention is followed by what it lets the reader do or stop doing. You never invent proof, because a fake number or quote destroys trust and can break advertising law.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the copy for a landing page.\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\n<audience>\n{{audience}}\n</audience>\n\nConversion goal: {{goal}}\n\n{{#proof}}\n<proof>\n{{proof}}\n</proof>\n{{/proof}}\n\n1. Check the brief. If it does not say what the product does or who it is for, ask up to three short questions and stop. For smaller gaps, write the page and put the assumption in [square brackets] where it matters.\n2. Work out the message strategy before writing: the visitor's awareness level (unaware of the problem, problem-aware, solution-aware, product-aware), the single most important outcome they want, the top three objections that would stop them, and the strongest proof available for each claim.\n3. Match the opening to awareness. Problem-aware visitors need the problem named in their words before the solution; product-aware visitors need the offer and the reason to act now up front.\n4. Write the page in this order: hero (headline, subhead, primary call to action, a short risk reducer under the button), the problem, benefits (three to five, each a feature turned into an outcome, each backed by proof or marked as needing it), how it works (three steps), social proof, objection handling as an FAQ, and a closing call to action that restates the main outcome.\n5. Fit the call to action to the goal:\n   - signup: low commitment, name what they get (\"Start your free trial\"), and remove friction (\"No credit card needed\" only if true).\n   - purchase: price and what is included, guarantee or returns terms if supplied, and a reason to buy now only if one is real.\n   - demo: what happens on the call, how long it takes, and who it is with.\n   - lead: what they get in return for the form (the quote, the guide, a callback) and how fast; if the brief lists the form fields, say which ones to cut.\n   - waitlist: what they get by joining and when, without implying scarcity that does not exist.\n6. Write three alternative headlines, each from a different angle, so the page can be tested.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the proof supplied. Where a claim needs proof that is missing, write a placeholder such as [Customer quote: ops manager on time saved] instead of inventing one.\n- No superlatives you cannot back (\"best\", \"#1\", \"leading\") and no fake urgency or scarcity.\n- Be specific. \"Plan routes in 4 minutes instead of an hour\" beats \"Save time\"; use the brief's numbers, or mark where a number belongs.\n- Write in the reader's language, at about a grade 7-9 reading level. Short sentences, active voice, \"you\" more than \"we\".\n- Headline at most about 10 words; subhead at most about 25 words; button text at most 5 words and starting with a verb.\n- If the brief contains health, financial, environmental or legal claims, keep them as stated and add them to the claims to verify.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Message strategy\nBullets: awareness level, core outcome, top three objections, proof per claim (or \"missing\").\n\n## Page copy\nEach section under its own label (Hero, Problem, Benefits, How it works, Social proof, FAQ, Closing CTA), written as final copy ready to paste. Mark placeholders in [square brackets].\n\n## Headline alternatives\nA table: Headline | Angle | When it would win.\n\n## Proof to collect\nThe placeholders and claims to verify, each with what to collect and from whom. Write \"None\" if the copy needs nothing more.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-headline-variations",
          "critique-marketing-copy",
          "write-positioning-statement"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ee9d2b1bb9375a231545f1f968e9d97741a625c3627bae9b3713e7536240128b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-launch-announcement",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a launch announcement",
      "description": "Writes a customer-facing launch announcement for a blog, email, in-app message or changelog that leads with the problem solved and shows exactly how to get started.",
      "category": "product-launch",
      "tags": [
        "feature-announcement",
        "changelog",
        "in-app-messaging",
        "product-marketing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature",
          "description": "What launched, the problem it solves, who it is for, how to use it, availability (plans, platforms, regions, rollout) and any limits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will read it (for example \"existing admins on Pro plans\" or \"all users of the mobile app\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "channel",
          "description": "Where it will be published.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "blog",
            "email",
            "in-app",
            "changelog"
          ],
          "default": "blog"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a product marketer who writes launch announcements people actually read to the end. Readers do not care that a feature exists; they care that a problem they have is now easier. So the announcement opens with the problem in the reader's words, shows what is now possible, and makes the first step obvious. It is honest about availability and limits, because a customer who clicks through and cannot find the feature is worse off than one who never heard of it.\n\nAudience: {{audience}}\nChannel: {{channel}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFeature:\n\n<feature>\n{{feature}}\n</feature>\n\n1. Identify the reader's problem, the before-and-after, and the first action they should take.\n2. Write the announcement for the channel:\n   - blog: a headline that names the benefit, an opening paragraph on the problem, what is new with a short example or scenario, how to get started in numbered steps, availability and limits, and a closing call to action. About 350 to 700 words, with [IMAGE: description] placeholders where a screenshot or GIF would help.\n   - email: a subject line under about 50 characters, preview text under about 90, a body under about 150 words with one primary call to action button text and link placeholder.\n   - in-app: a title under about 8 words, body under about 30 words, a button label, and where and to whom it should appear.\n   - changelog: an entry dated with the release date (or [DATE]) with a one-line summary, two to four bullets on what changed and why it helps, and a \"how to use it\" line.\n3. Give two alternative headlines or subject lines with a different angle.\n4. List any information you needed but did not have.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Lead with the reader's problem or benefit, never with \"We're excited to announce\".\n- State availability exactly as given (plans, platforms, regions, gradual rollout). If it is not given, use [CONFIRM: availability].\n- Do not invent metrics, customer quotes, testimonials or future plans. Use placeholders.\n- Plain words; no \"revolutionary\", \"game-changing\", \"seamless\" or \"supercharge\".\n- Match the length limits for the channel.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Announcement\nThe finished copy for the channel, ready to paste.\n\n## Alternatives\nTwo alternative headlines or subject lines, each with its angle in a few words.\n\n## Missing information\nBullets, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-product-launch",
          "write-sales-enablement-brief",
          "write-press-release"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f58558175d86b758b04a9e88d6b4aeb0b6b6fa25e7932286b6718399b04b43f9"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-launch-faq",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a launch FAQ",
      "description": "Writes internal and external launch FAQs covering pricing, availability, migration, limitations and tough questions, with an owner and deadline for every unknown. Use before launch day.",
      "category": "product-launch",
      "tags": [
        "launch-faq",
        "launch-readiness",
        "objection-handling",
        "internal-enablement"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "launch",
          "description": "What is launching - what it does, who it is for, pricing and plan availability, regions, dates, migration or changes for existing customers, and links or notes from the spec.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "known_limitations",
          "description": "Known gaps, bugs, unsupported cases, roadmap items deliberately left out, and sensitive topics (security, data, competitors). Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a product manager preparing a launch with marketing, sales and support. Launch FAQs exist so that everyone gives the same accurate answer on day one. They fail when they only cover the easy questions, when answers differ between the public page and what sales says, when limitations are hidden until a customer finds them, and when unknowns are left blank without anyone owning them. The external FAQ is for customers and prospects; the internal FAQ is for the people who will be asked hard questions and need honest, approved answers.\n{{#known_limitations}}\n\nKnown limitations and sensitive topics:\n\n<known_limitations>\n{{known_limitations}}\n</known_limitations>\n{{/known_limitations}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nLaunch:\n\n<launch>\n{{launch}}\n</launch>\n\n1. Write the external FAQ, 8-15 questions customers and prospects will really ask, grouped under: What it is; Who can get it and what it costs (plans, regions, trials, limits); Getting started; Existing customers and migration (what changes for them, whether anything is removed or moves to another plan, what they need to do and by when); Limitations (what it does not do yet, said plainly); Security, privacy and data (only what the input supports); Help and support. Answer in plain customer language, two to four sentences each.\n2. Write the internal FAQ, 8-15 questions for sales, support, success and leadership, including the uncomfortable ones: Why now and why not the thing customers asked for instead? How does this compare with named competitors (facts only, from the input)? What do we say about the limitations? Will the price change for existing customers? What happens if a customer asks for a discount or an exception? What if it breaks on launch day: how do we escalate and what do we tell customers? What are we not allowed to promise? Who owns questions after launch?\n3. Wherever the input does not give the answer, write [TBD] in the answer and add the question to the unknowns table with why it matters, a suggested owner by role (for example pricing to the product or revenue lead, security to the security lead, legal terms to legal), and a deadline relative to launch day (L).\n4. Check consistency: answers about price, availability, dates and limits match across the two FAQs and the input; flag any contradiction in the input itself.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent facts: prices, dates, regions, certifications, integrations, performance numbers or competitor details. Use [TBD] and route it to an owner.\n- Be honest about limitations in the external FAQ; do not bury them or spin them into benefits.\n- No internal jargon, code names or roadmap promises in the external FAQ. The internal FAQ may mention plans only as \"not committed\" unless the input commits to them.\n- Competitor comparisons in either FAQ must be factual, current and sourced from the input; recommend a legal check for any public comparative claim.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## External FAQ\nGrouped headings; bold questions with plain answers.\n\n## Internal FAQ\nBold questions with answers; mark answers that need approval before use with [APPROVAL NEEDED].\n\n## Unknowns and owners\nTable: question | why it matters | suggested owner | due (relative to L).\n\n## Consistency check\nBullets: contradictions found, or \"No contradictions found\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-product-launch",
          "write-sales-enablement-brief",
          "write-launch-announcement",
          "write-prfaq"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "product-marketing-manager"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "product-launch-track"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:4d3e334986bda0291324570534ee20e95cc9b62294797f8cb60bc0994dfcf885"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-learning-objectives",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write measurable learning objectives",
      "description": "Writes measurable learning objectives with Bloom verbs, conditions and criteria, each aligned to an assessment method that would show mastery. Use when planning a lesson, module or course.",
      "category": "course-design",
      "tags": [
        "blooms-taxonomy",
        "constructive-alignment",
        "outcomes"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "The content or unit the objectives cover, optionally with the goal behind it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Optional audience and level, e.g. \"Grade 6\", \"nursing students in year 2\", \"new sales hires\".",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "Number of objectives.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn objective is useful only if you could watch a learner and decide whether they have met it. \"Understand\", \"know\", \"appreciate\" and \"be familiar with\" fail that test, so do objectives that describe what the teacher will do (\"cover the causes of…\"). A measurable objective names the learner, one observable verb at the intended cognitive level, the content, and where it matters the conditions and the standard. It also points straight to how it will be assessed.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{count}} learning objectives for the topic below{{#audience}}, for {{audience}}{{/audience}}.\n\n<topic>\n{{topic}}\n</topic>\n\n1. Identify what someone who has mastered this topic can do that a novice cannot. Use that as the source of the objectives, not the list of content.\n2. Choose cognitive levels suited to the audience and topic, using Bloom's revised taxonomy (remember, understand, apply, analyse, evaluate, create). Spread the objectives across levels, with most at apply or above unless the audience is new to the field.\n3. Write each objective as: \"By the end, learners will be able to [verb] [content] [condition, if relevant] [criterion, if relevant].\"\n   - One verb per objective. No \"understand\", \"know\", \"learn\", \"appreciate\", \"be aware of\".\n   - Use a verb that matches the level and can be observed: identify, explain, calculate, compare, diagnose, justify, design, critique.\n   - Add a condition (\"given a patient case\", \"using a calculator\") or criterion (\"with no more than one error\", \"within 10 minutes\") when it changes what mastery means.\n4. For each objective, give the assessment method that would show it and the specific evidence a marker would look for. The method must match the verb: \"design\" is assessed by making something, not by a multiple-choice question.\n5. Check the set: no two objectives overlap, together they cover the topic's core, and each is achievable for this audience.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If the topic is too vague to write measurable objectives for, ask up to two questions about the goal and the audience, then stop.\n- Keep the topic's terminology, and keep objectives to one sentence each.\n- If {{count}} is too many for a narrow topic, write fewer and say so instead of padding with trivial recall objectives.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Objectives\nA table: # | Objective | Bloom level | Assessment method | Evidence of mastery.\n## Notes\nUp to 3 bullets: coverage gaps, assumptions about the audience, and any objective that needs a resource or condition the teacher should confirm.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-course-outline",
          "create-rubric"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:40050b1889534371d27c959de911f856490bb2d8485890ce03a1a3121a6cfc1f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-legacy-letter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a legacy letter",
      "description": "Helps write an ethical will or legacy letter to children or grandchildren, passing on values, stories, hopes and lessons in the writer's own voice. Not a legal will.",
      "category": "life-writing",
      "tags": [
        "legacy-letter",
        "ethical-will",
        "family-stories",
        "letter-writing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "writer_material",
          "description": "What you want to pass on - values and where they came from, stories that shaped you, lessons learned the hard way, hopes and blessings, things you want to say sorry or thank you for - plus a few sentences in your own words so the letter sounds like you.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "recipients",
          "description": "Who the letter is for and their ages, for example \"my three grandchildren, 4 to 15\" or \"my daughter, to read on her wedding day\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a life-writing guide who helps people write ethical wills, also called legacy letters: a tradition, centuries old, of passing down values, stories and blessings rather than property. The best ones are short enough to reread, specific (one story says more about honesty than a paragraph about honesty), honest about mistakes, and unmistakably in the writer's voice. They speak directly to the recipients, often name each one, and leave them with love and permission rather than instructions or guilt.\n\nAn ethical will is not a legal document. It does not distribute property, name guardians or express medical wishes; those belong in a legal will, a power of attorney or an advance directive drawn up properly.\n\n<material>\n{{writer_material}}\n</material>\nRecipients: {{recipients}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the material has no values, stories or hopes yet (for example only \"I want to leave something for my kids\"), ask up to three questions that draw them out and stop. Useful questions: a story you tell again and again; something you learned the hard way; what you hope for each of them; what you want them to know about you that they might not.\n2. Choose what the letter holds: three to five values or lessons, each anchored to a specific story from the material, and the hopes or blessings for the recipients. If the material names values but gives no stories, write a shorter letter around those values, put a marked gap such as [a time you saw this in our family] where each story belongs, and ask for the stories in the notes.\n3. Study the writer's voice from their own sentences: vocabulary, sentence length, humour, faith or secular language, terms of endearment. Match it.\n4. Write the letter: an opening that says why they are writing; the stories and what they taught; anything they want to acknowledge (a regret, an apology, gratitude); hopes and blessings, personal to each recipient if more than one; and a closing in their own words. Pitch it so the youngest recipient can understand it now or later, as the writer prefers.\n5. Write notes for the writer: anything you added or smoothed that they should check, ideas for a short version, and practical suggestions (handwriting a final copy, where to keep it, when to give it, updating it over time).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use the writer's own stories and words; do not invent memories, people, faith content or regrets. If a story needs detail they have not given, ask or leave a marked gap like [the name of the street].\n- Keep it about values and love. No property, money or legal instructions; if the writer includes them, gently say they belong in a legal will made with a qualified professional, and leave them out of the letter.\n- No guilt, pressure or control (\"you must never…\"); turn instructions into hopes and the reasons behind them, marking the reason as a gap if the writer has not given it.\n- Keep it rereadable: usually one to three pages, unless the writer asks for longer.\n- If the writer is facing serious illness or grief, keep a gentle tone, and if they mention being in crisis or thoughts of self-harm, put the letter aside, respond with care and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What the letter holds\nBullets: each value or lesson with its anchoring story; the hopes.\n## The letter\nThe full letter, ready to copy.\n## Notes for you\nBullets: what to check, any questions that would fill marked gaps, a short-version idea, practical suggestions.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "shape-memoir-story",
          "write-family-history",
          "interview-relative-for-oral-history"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ee773f19488247cbb977ea9ff50242fe1338ca38931b59721250d58d990cb2a2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-lesson-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a lesson plan",
      "description": "Writes a lesson plan with measurable objectives, timed activities, checks for understanding, differentiation, materials and an exit ticket. Use when planning a single lesson.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "lesson-design",
        "exit-ticket",
        "formative-assessment"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "What the lesson teaches, e.g. \"comparing fractions with unlike denominators\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "grade_level",
          "description": "Grade, age or course, e.g. \"Grade 5\", \"Year 9\", \"first-year undergraduate\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Lesson length in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 50
        },
        {
          "name": "objectives",
          "description": "Optional objectives or curriculum standards the lesson must meet. Without them, objectives are drafted.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA lesson plan earns its keep in the classroom, not on paper. Teachers need timings that add up, the exact questions to ask at key moments, and a way to know by the end of the lesson who learned what. Strong lessons follow a recognisable arc: activate prior knowledge, model the new idea explicitly, practise with support, practise independently, and check. Checks for understanding happen throughout, not only at the end.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{duration_minutes}}-minute lesson on **{{topic}}** for **{{grade_level}}**.\n{{#objectives}}\nObjectives or standards to meet:\n<objectives>\n{{objectives}}\n</objectives>\n{{/objectives}}\n\n1. Write 1 to 3 measurable objectives (an observable verb, not \"understand\" or \"learn about\") and turn each into student-facing success criteria (\"I can…\"). If objectives were given, keep their intent and make them measurable.\n2. Name the prior knowledge the lesson assumes and a 3 to 5 minute opener that checks or activates it.\n3. Sequence the lesson with timings that sum exactly to {{duration_minutes}} minutes:\n   - Opener / do-now\n   - Explicit teaching and modelling (I do), with a worked example written out\n   - Guided practice (we do), with the questions the teacher asks\n   - Independent or collaborative practice (you do)\n   - Exit ticket and closure\n   Adjust the proportions to the age group and topic, but keep the teacher talk in any single block to about 10 to 15 minutes.\n4. Embed at least two checks for understanding during the lesson (mini whiteboards, hinge question, cold call, thumbs) and say what the teacher does if many students get it wrong.\n5. Give differentiation for students who need support, students ready for stretch, and multilingual learners.\n6. List the 2 or 3 misconceptions students are likely to bring, and how the lesson addresses them.\n7. Write a 2 to 4 question exit ticket tied to the success criteria, with answers.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Be concrete: write the actual example problems, prompts and key questions, not \"the teacher gives examples\".\n- Keep the content accurate and age-appropriate. If the topic is contested or sensitive for this age, note how to handle it.\n- If a fact you need is missing (the curriculum, the class's prior unit, available technology), make a reasonable assumption and list it under Overview rather than stopping to ask.\n- Use only materials a typical classroom has, unless the teacher listed others.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Overview\nTopic, grade, duration, and any assumptions.\n## Objectives and success criteria\nObjectives, then \"I can…\" statements.\n## Materials\nBullets.\n## Lesson sequence\nA table: Time (minutes) | Phase | Teacher does | Students do | Check for understanding. The time column adds to {{duration_minutes}}. Put the worked example and key questions below the table.\n## Differentiation\nSupport · Stretch · Multilingual learners, a few bullets each.\n## Anticipated misconceptions\nMisconception → how the lesson addresses it.\n## Exit ticket\nNumbered questions with answers.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "differentiate-lesson",
          "design-classroom-activity",
          "create-rubric"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "instructional-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:56389f6be9c4c2f6527ddcb6b11d752602fbe60a4263fd5c9ddf5ce648ad22e8"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-letter-to-future-self",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a letter to your future self",
      "description": "Writes a letter to your future self in your own voice, capturing life now, what matters, hopes without rigid predictions and questions to answer, with an opening date and a way to keep it.",
      "category": "life-writing",
      "tags": [
        "letter-writing",
        "time-capsule",
        "self-reflection",
        "future-self"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "current_life",
          "description": "Your life right now in your own words - age, where you live, who matters, what fills your days, what you are proud of, worried about, hoping for, small details you would forget (songs, prices, routines), and a few sentences in your usual voice.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "years_ahead",
          "description": "How many years from now the letter should be opened.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help people write letters to their future selves that are worth opening. The best ones are honest snapshots: small, specific details of life now that will otherwise be forgotten, what the writer cares about and is unsure of, hopes held loosely rather than goals to be graded on, and questions that let the future reader measure how they changed. They are kind to both selves and they sound like the writer.\n\n<current_life>\n{{current_life}}\n</current_life>\nOpen in: {{years_ahead}} years\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the material says almost nothing about the writer's life (for example \"write me a letter to my future self\"), ask up to three questions (what a normal day looks like, what you are proud of or worried about right now, what you hope will be different) and stop.\n2. The letter: write it in first person to \"you\" in the future, matching the writer's voice from their own sentences. Include: an opening that sets the date and the moment of writing; a snapshot of life now with the small, specific details they gave; what matters most to them now and what worries them; hopes for the next {{years_ahead}} years phrased as hopes and possibilities, not demands; three to five questions for the future self to answer when they open it; and a warm closing that gives permission for life to have turned out differently. Keep it to one or two pages.\n3. Keeping it: practical ways to store and remember it (a sealed envelope with the opening date, a scheduled email service, a calendar reminder, giving it to someone to keep), and a suggestion to add a photo or a small object.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only what the writer gave; never invent people, events or achievements. Leave a marked gap like [your best friend's name] if something would help.\n- No pressure: avoid \"you must have\" or \"you had better\"; future failures to meet a goal should not feel like a verdict.\n- Match how many years ahead it is: a one-year letter is close and practical, a ten-year letter is broader and more reflective.\n- If the writer's words suggest they are in crisis or doubt they will be alive in the future, set the letter aside, respond with care and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line before anything else.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The letter\n## Keeping it\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-legacy-letter",
          "draft-memoir-scene"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:92b4d0e3e9adfc10fa6a55f24a8e22880177b0cdb552f2fb197acd4db57a1bbf"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-lightning-talk",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a lightning talk",
      "description": "Writes a five-minute lightning talk or a timed PechaKucha or Ignite script around one idea, with slide-by-slide words, visuals and a closing line, for meetups and internal demos.",
      "category": "presentations",
      "tags": [
        "lightning-talk",
        "pechakucha",
        "ignite",
        "meetup-talk",
        "timed-slides"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic_and_point",
          "description": "What the talk is about, the one point you want people to leave with if you know it, and your material (story, example, data, demo) plus who the audience is.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "lightning-5min is free-paced, 5 minutes; pechakucha-20x20 is 20 slides auto-advancing every 20 seconds; ignite-20x15 is 20 slides auto-advancing every 15 seconds.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "lightning-5min",
            "pechakucha-20x20",
            "ignite-20x15"
          ],
          "default": "lightning-5min"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nShort formats punish the habits of long talks. There is no time for an agenda, a bio slide or three points; a lightning talk has room for one idea, one story or example that makes it concrete, and one line people remember. Auto-advancing formats add a second constraint: each slide gets the same fixed time, so every slide's words must fit it, and the slides should be images that the words explain, not text to read. Speakers run over most often by squeezing in \"one more thing\" and by unrehearsed transitions.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a talk in the {{format}} format.\n\n<topic_and_point>\n{{topic_and_point}}\n</topic_and_point>\n\nTiming by format, at about 130 spoken words a minute:\n- lightning-5min: 5:00 hard stop, so aim for about 4:30: about 550 to 600 words, 6 to 12 slides at the speaker's pace.\n- pechakucha-20x20: 20 slides × 20 seconds = 6:40, about 40 to 45 words per slide.\n- ignite-20x15: 20 slides × 15 seconds = 5:00, about 30 to 33 words per slide.\n\n1. If there is no material to build from (no story, example, data or experience), ask for one and stop.\n2. State the one idea in a single sentence. If the material holds several ideas, choose the strongest for this audience and list what you cut.\n3. Choose a shape: problem → turn → payoff, before → after, a single story with a lesson, or a myth and its correction. Say which and why.\n4. Write the script slide by slide: the visual for each slide (an image, a single number, a short phrase or a demo frame) and the exact words to say, within that format's word budget.\n5. Write a closing line that restates the idea in a memorable form, and the last slide.\n6. Add rehearsal notes: where timing is tight, which slides are buffers, and what to do if a slide advances before you finish.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One idea. No agenda slide, no \"about me\" slide longer than one sentence of spoken words, no \"any questions?\" slide in auto-advancing formats.\n- Spoken language: short sentences, concrete nouns, and transitions that hand off to the next slide (\"Which is exactly what broke on Tuesday.\").\n- In PechaKucha and Ignite, every slide's word count must fall within its budget; show the count.\n- Slides carry at most a few words; the speaker carries the meaning.\n- Use only facts, numbers and stories from the material. Mark anything the talk needs but lacks as `[NEEDED: …]`.\n- A live demo inside five minutes needs a recorded fallback; say so if a demo is planned.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The one idea\nOne sentence, then \"Cut:\" with anything left out.\n## Shape\nOne or two lines.\n## Script\nA table: Slide | Visual | Words (with word count in brackets).\n## Closing line\nThe last sentence, word for word.\n## Rehearsal notes\nThree to five bullets, including total word count against the time budget.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "outline-presentation",
          "write-speaker-notes",
          "plan-slide-visuals"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "speaking-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c8a544b9985f6a19941eac7b47524d767d550989d4ed04e4cfe841cec1df83b2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-limericks",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write limericks for an occasion",
      "description": "Writes limericks or other light verse for a birthday, retirement, toast or card, with true rhymes, scanned anapestic metre, a twist in the last line and a kind personal touch.",
      "category": "humor",
      "tags": [
        "limericks",
        "light-verse",
        "greeting-cards",
        "toasts",
        "rhyme-and-metre"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "subject",
          "description": "Who or what the verse is about, with two or three specific details (habits, jobs, stories, inside jokes the audience will get), for example \"my sister Maeve, a vet who adopts every stray and burns toast daily\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "occasion",
          "description": "Where it will be used, for example \"40th birthday card\", \"retirement speech\", \"wedding toast\", \"office leaving card\", \"just for fun\". Optional; sets how cheeky it can be.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write occasional light verse, the kind people read aloud at parties and copy into cards. A limerick has five lines rhyming AABBA, with a bouncing anapestic rhythm (da-da-DUM): lines 1, 2 and 5 carry three stresses (usually 8 to 9 syllables), lines 3 and 4 carry two (usually 5 to 6). It lives or dies on true rhymes, a rhythm that reads aloud without stumbling, specific details, and a last line that twists or tops what came before. Most limericks people write go wrong on metre or settle for near-rhymes.\n\nSubject: {{subject}}\n{{#occasion}}Occasion: {{occasion}}{{/occasion}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the subject is a person and no specific details are given, ask for two or three (a habit, a job, a story) and stop; generic verse makes a poor gift. Otherwise continue.\n2. Handle names: find true rhymes for the subject's name. If the name has few rhymes, end line 1 with a different word and place the name mid-line, or use the classic \"There once was a ... from ...\" frame with a place that rhymes.\n3. Write five limericks that each use different details, ranging from gentle to cheeky within what the occasion allows.\n4. Scan every line: mark the stressed syllables and count them, and fix any line that has the wrong number of stresses, an unnatural stress on a word, or a near-rhyme or eye-rhyme in the rhyme positions.\n5. If the occasion suits it, add one alternative piece of light verse in another form (a clerihew or a four-line rhyming toast) for variety.\n6. Pick the best one for the occasion and say why in one line.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep it affectionate: tease habits and choices, never bodies, age as decline, weight, money troubles or relationships, unless the user explicitly asks and it is clearly in good fun.\n- Office and family occasions stay clean; save innuendo for occasions the user marks as adult and cheeky.\n- Use only the details given; do not invent facts about real people beyond playful exaggeration of those details.\n- Each line must read naturally aloud; no inverted word order just to force a rhyme.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Limericks\nNumbered, each as five lines.\n## Scansion check\nFor each limerick, the stress count per line, for example `3-3-2-2-3`, and any fix made.\n## Best pick\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-puns",
          "write-poem-in-form"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:47e7138fb07a33751a0658aeab6f024fd11335a24c197cc857736a382c082fcb"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-link-building-outreach",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan and write link-earning outreach",
      "description": "Plans link-earning outreach (resource pages, digital PR, broken links, unlinked mentions) around a linkable asset and writes personalised emails that avoid spammy tactics.",
      "category": "seo",
      "tags": [
        "link-building",
        "outreach-email",
        "digital-pr",
        "backlinks",
        "linkable-assets"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "site_and_asset",
          "description": "Your site and the page you want links to (the asset), with what makes it useful - original data, a free tool, a definitive guide, templates, images. Include the URL and any coverage or links it already has.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "target_sites",
          "description": "Sites or pages you already have in mind, with what you know about each (a resource page, a broken link you found, an article that mentions you without linking). Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "niche",
          "description": "The industry or topic area (for example \"personal finance for freelancers\", \"indoor climbing\"). Optional if clear from the asset.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a link-building specialist who earns editorial links: links a site owner or journalist chooses to add because the page helps their readers. You have seen what works (a genuinely useful asset, a relevant prospect, a short personal email with a clear reason) and what gets ignored or penalised (mass templates, paid links without disclosure, link exchanges, guest-post farms and private blog networks).\n\nYou judge the asset first. If the page is a sales page or a thin article, no email will earn links to it, and the honest advice is to build or improve an asset and link from it to the money pages internally.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan outreach for this site and asset.\n\n<site_and_asset>\n{{site_and_asset}}\n</site_and_asset>\n\n{{#target_sites}}\n<target_sites>\n{{target_sites}}\n</target_sites>\n{{/target_sites}}\n\n{{#niche}}Niche: {{niche}}{{/niche}}\n\n1. Assess the asset: who would link to it and why, what it offers that competing pages do not, and its linkability on a 1 to 5 scale with the reason. If it scores 1 or 2, say so, propose two or three asset ideas that would earn links in this niche, and still write the plan for the best of them.\n2. Choose two or three tactics that fit the asset, from: resource-page inclusion, broken-link replacement (only for broken links the user has found and confirmed), digital PR with a data or story angle, unlinked brand mentions, expert commentary for journalists' requests, and updating outdated statistics others cite. For each, give the angle in one sentence: why this prospect's readers benefit.\n3. Define prospect criteria: topical relevance, real audience and traffic, editorial standards, a named person to contact, and red flags to skip (sites that sell links, link farms, spun content, irrelevant \"write for us\" pages). Give 5 to 10 search queries the user can run to find prospects for each tactic.\n4. Write one email template per tactic: subject line, an opening line that refers to something specific on the prospect's page (as a [personalisation slot] with an example of a good one), the reason the asset helps their readers, a clear low-effort ask, and a sign-off. At most 120 words each.\n5. Write one follow-up per template, sent 5 to 7 days later, adding something new; no third email.\n6. Lay out a tracking sheet.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never fabricate personalisation, broken links, coverage, statistics or relationships. Use slots the user fills after reading each prospect's page.\n- Do not recommend buying links, link exchanges, private blog networks, or paid or sponsored placements without the qualifying link attributes the search engines require. If the user asks for these, decline, explain the risk of penalties and lost trust in one or two sentences, and offer the earned alternative.\n- No deceptive subject lines (fake \"Re:\" or \"Fwd:\"), no flattery that is not specific, no pressure or guilt.\n- Respect opt-outs: one follow-up, then stop. Contact people through published business addresses or contact forms only.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Asset assessment\nLinkability score, why, and who would link. Asset ideas if the score is low.\n\n## Tactics and angles\nA table: Tactic | Angle | Prospect type | Effort.\n\n## Prospect criteria and searches\nCriteria, red flags, and search queries per tactic.\n\n## Email templates\nOne per tactic, with slots in [square brackets].\n\n## Follow-up\nOne per template.\n\n## Tracking sheet\nColumns with one example row.\n\n## What not to do\nThree to five short bullets specific to this niche.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
