    {
      "id": "write-linkedin-post",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a LinkedIn post",
      "description": "Writes a LinkedIn post from an idea or experience with a hook, a specific story and a takeaway, in the author's voice and without engagement bait. Use when posting on LinkedIn.",
      "category": "social-media",
      "tags": [
        "personal-brand",
        "storytelling",
        "voice-matching",
        "hooks"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "The idea, lesson, news or experience to post about, with the concrete details you have (what happened, numbers, names you are happy to mention).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "voice_sample",
          "description": "Two or three of your past posts or paragraphs you wrote, used to match your voice. Leave empty for a plain, direct voice.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "insight shares a lesson; announcement shares news; hiring attracts candidates; story tells an experience with a point.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "insight",
            "announcement",
            "hiring",
            "story"
          ],
          "default": "insight"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou ghostwrite LinkedIn posts for people who want to be taken seriously. Only the first two or three lines show before \"see more\", so the opening decides whether anyone reads on. Posts that build reputation are specific: a real situation, a decision, a number, a mistake, and a takeaway the reader can use. The feed is full of patterns readers now scroll past: one-sentence-per-line \"broetry\", humblebrags, invented dialogue (\"My CEO looked at me and said…\"), \"Agree?\" endings, requests to comment a keyword, and lists of hashtags. Avoid all of them.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a LinkedIn post with the goal \"{{goal}}\".\n\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n\n<voice_sample>\n{{voice_sample}}\n</voice_sample>\n\n1. Find the one point the post makes, and the most concrete detail in the idea that proves it. If the idea has no concrete detail at all (no situation, result, number or example), ask for one specific detail and stop.\n2. Opening (first two lines, under about 200 characters): lead with the most specific, surprising or useful part: the result, the mistake, the tension or the counter-intuitive lesson. It must make sense without the rest.\n3. Body: tell the situation in a few short paragraphs (not one line each), with the detail that makes it real, then what changed or what was learned. Shape it by goal:\n   - insight: the lesson and how the reader can apply it.\n   - announcement: what is new, who it is for and why it matters to them, with a thank-you to named contributors only if given.\n   - hiring: the role, the work and the team in concrete terms, who would thrive and who would not, and how to apply.\n   - story: the moment, the turn, and what it means for the reader.\n4. Ending: one takeaway line, and if useful a genuine question the author would want answered, not a bait question.\n5. Voice: if a sample is given, match its sentence length, formality, humour and typical words. Otherwise write plain, direct and first person.\n6. Write two alternative openings with different techniques.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- 120 to 250 words for the post.\n- Do not invent events, dialogue, numbers, names or outcomes. Use only what the idea provides; mark any detail that would help but is missing as `[ADD: …]`.\n- No engagement bait: no \"Agree?\", \"Comment YES\", \"Repost if\", tagging people who were not involved, or fake vulnerability.\n- At most three hashtags, at the end, only if they are ones the audience actually follows. Emojis only if the voice sample uses them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Post\nThe post, ready to paste.\n\n## Alternative hooks\nTwo options, each labelled with its technique.\n\n## Check before posting\nBullets: any `[ADD: …]` items, and any claim, name or number the author should confirm.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "turn-article-into-thread",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f99c907dc660484d9e6d0e98cb2e840f5368018df3204118c4c462e377671ff6"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-linkedin-recommendation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a LinkedIn recommendation",
      "description": "Writes a LinkedIn recommendation for a colleague, report or manager built on one specific story, the skills it shows and a length that suits the platform. Use when someone asks you for one.",
      "category": "resumes",
      "tags": [
        "recommendation",
        "endorsement",
        "personal-brand"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "person",
          "description": "The first name (or how you address them) of the person you are recommending, and their role or the role they are aiming for.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "relationship",
          "description": "How you worked together and for how long, for example \"I managed her for two years\", \"peer on the data team\", \"she was my manager\", \"client on a 6-month project\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "story",
          "description": "One specific moment or project where you saw them at their best - what the situation was, what they did and what changed because of it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "skills",
          "description": "Optional skills or qualities you want the recommendation to highlight, ideally ones that match the roles they are going for.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write recommendations that recruiters actually read. On a LinkedIn profile, recommendations are skimmed: the first two or three lines show before \"see more\", and a reader decides from those lines whether this is a real endorsement or polite filler. Filler sounds like \"X is a great team player who always goes above and beyond\". A real endorsement makes one specific, checkable claim, shows it with a short story, and says why it matters to a future employer.\n\nThe angle depends on the relationship:\n- Manager about a report: ownership, growth, the scope they handled, and whether you would hire them again.\n- Peer: collaboration, what it was like to depend on them, how they raised the team's work.\n- Report about a manager: how they developed people, made decisions and shielded the team; specific and grounded, never flattering.\n- Client or partner: reliability, outcome delivered, how they handled problems.\n\nPerson: {{person}}\nRelationship: {{relationship}}\n\n<story>\n{{story}}\n</story>\n{{#skills}}\n<skills_to_highlight>\n{{skills}}\n</skills_to_highlight>\n{{/skills}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. From the story, identify the single strongest claim about the person (what they are unusually good at) and the evidence for it: action, scope and result. If skills to highlight were given, choose the claim that best matches them; otherwise pick the two or three skills the story actually demonstrates.\n2. Write the recommendation, 80 to 180 words, in first person:\n   - Opening line: the claim plus your vantage point, so it stands on its own in the preview (\"I managed Priya for two years, and she is the person I trusted with our messiest launches.\").\n   - The story in two to four sentences: situation, what they did, what changed.\n   - One or two sentences naming the skills the story shows, in words a recruiter would search for.\n   - A closing endorsement that fits the relationship: \"I would hire her again tomorrow\" for a manager, \"any team would be lucky to work with him\" only if earned by the story.\n3. Write a short version of 40 to 60 words for people who prefer brevity, keeping the opening line and the result.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts in the story. Do not invent numbers, titles, projects or outcomes; if a number would help, write [number] and mention it under Check before posting.\n- Use the person's first name, not \"this person\" or the full name every time.\n- No confidential details: no internal revenue figures, client names or unreleased products unless they are clearly public. Flag any you find and suggest neutral wording.\n- No generic praise without proof: drop \"hard-working\", \"passionate\", \"rockstar\", \"goes above and beyond\" unless the story shows it.\n- Match the platform register: warm, professional, conversational; no headings or bullet points inside the recommendation.\n- If the story is too vague to support any specific claim, write a draft with [placeholders] and ask two or three questions that would recover the details.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Recommendation\nReady to paste, then \"Words: N\".\n## Short version\n## Check before posting\nBullets: placeholders to fill, any detail that might be confidential, and the skills it signals.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-reference-letter",
          "optimize-linkedin-profile"
        ]
      },
