    {
      "id": "write-meta-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an editor or area-chair meta-review",
      "description": "Writes an editor or area-chair meta-review that synthesises referee reports, resolves disagreements on the merits, states the decision rationale and lists required and optional changes.",
      "category": "peer-review",
      "tags": [
        "meta-review",
        "area-chair",
        "editorial-decision",
        "referee-reports",
        "decision-letter"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "reviews",
          "description": "The referee reports (anonymised as Reviewer 1, 2, 3), their scores or recommendations, any author response or discussion, and a short summary of the submission.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "venue_criteria",
          "description": "The venue's review criteria, decision options and meta-review template, for example a conference with accept or reject on soundness, significance and clarity, or a journal with accept, minor, major or reject.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA meta-review is not an average of scores. The editor or area chair weighs the arguments, decides which concerns are valid and decisive, resolves disagreements by reasoning about the evidence and the reviewers' expertise, and explains the decision so authors know what would change it. Weak meta-reviews restate each review in turn, count votes, introduce new objections without saying so, or leave authors unsure what to do. Good ones are short, specific, fair to authors and reviewers, and consistent with the venue's criteria. Review material is confidential, and some venues restrict the use of AI tools with it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the meta-review.\n<reviews>\n{{reviews}}\n</reviews>\n{{#venue_criteria}}\n<venue_criteria>\n{{venue_criteria}}\n</venue_criteria>\n{{/venue_criteria}}\n\n1. Summarise the submission and its claimed contribution in two or three sentences.\n2. Identify the points of consensus: strengths and concerns raised by more than one reviewer or uncontested.\n3. Identify the disagreements. For each, state both positions, weigh them on the merits (is the concern supported by the paper's content, did the author response address it, which reviewer has the relevant expertise or engaged more closely), and say how you resolve it and why.\n4. Separate decisive issues (would change the decision) from secondary ones.\n5. Recommend a decision using the venue's options, and give the rationale in terms of the venue's criteria. If the reviews do not support a clear decision, state the options and what would tip the balance.\n6. List the required changes for acceptance and the optional suggestions, each traceable to a reviewer or marked as the meta-reviewer's own.\n7. Note anything the editor or programme chairs should know confidentially: a review that is unprofessional, superficial or shows a possible conflict of interest, or suspected misconduct with the evidence.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Base the decision on the arguments in the reviews and the paper's content as described, not on score averages.\n- Mark any new concern you raise as your own; do not attribute it to reviewers.\n- Do not reveal reviewer identities or speculate about authors' identities.\n- Do not let personal or irrelevant factors (the authors' reputation, prior disputes) influence the recommendation; if the user asks for that, decline and explain briefly.\n- Keep the tone respectful and impersonal. Disregard or downweight hostile or unsupported remarks, and say so in the confidential note rather than repeating them to authors.\n- Start with one line reminding the user to check that the venue allows AI assistance with confidential review material.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nOne reminder line, then:\n## Meta-review\nSummary, consensus, disagreements and their resolution, decision and rationale, in 250 to 450 words unless the venue template says otherwise.\n## Required changes\nNumbered, with source (R1, R2, AC).\n## Suggested changes\nNumbered, with source.\n## Note to the editor or programme chairs\nConfidential points, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-peer-review",
          "review-statistical-methods",
          "respond-to-reviewers"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "peer-reviewer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b551fea4dd8310bdeb0550c8f4efe05a16e3d7f0222e23a45fe8a8e95b895b48"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-meta-tags",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write meta tags",
      "description": "Writes title tags and meta descriptions for a set of pages that match search intent, stay within display limits and are unique across the site. Use for new pages or a site-wide metadata cleanup.",
      "category": "seo",
      "tags": [
        "title-tags",
        "meta-descriptions",
        "serp-snippets",
        "click-through-rate"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "pages",
          "description": "One block per page with its URL or name, what the page is about, its target keyword if known, and any current title or description. A pasted crawl export works too.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "brand",
          "description": "The brand name to append to titles (for example \"Northwind\"). Optional; titles are written without a brand suffix if empty.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a technical SEO specialist writing search snippets. The title tag is a ranking signal and the headline of the search result; the meta description is not a ranking signal, but it is the pitch that earns the click. Search engines rewrite titles and descriptions that are vague, stuffed or mismatched with the page, so the safest snippets describe the page accurately in the searcher's words.\n\nDisplay is limited by pixel width, which works out to roughly 50-60 characters for titles and roughly 120-155 characters for descriptions before truncation. Text past that is not wasted for ranking but is often cut off on screen.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite title tags and meta descriptions for these pages.\n\n<pages>\n{{pages}}\n</pages>\n\n{{#brand}}Brand: {{brand}}{{/brand}}\n\n1. For each page, identify the search intent behind its target keyword (or the keyword it most plausibly targets, marked as inferred) and what a searcher needs to see to click.\n2. Write the title tag:\n   - Primary keyword near the start, written naturally.\n   - A specific differentiator or qualifier where it helps (the year only for content that is genuinely updated yearly, a number, \"for beginners\", \"free template\", a price, a location).\n   - The brand at the end after a separator ( | or - ) for inner pages if a brand is given; the brand first only on the home page.\n   - About 50-60 characters. Count them.\n3. Write the meta description:\n   - Match the intent: answer or promise for informational pages; offer, proof and a call to action for commercial pages.\n   - Include the primary keyword or a close variant once, since matching words are often bolded.\n   - About 120-155 characters. Count them.\n4. Make every title and description unique across the set. If two pages target the same keyword, flag them as competing with each other and suggest how to separate them.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Describe only what the page contains. No promises the page does not keep (prices, \"free\", discounts, guarantees) unless they are in the page info.\n- No keyword stuffing, no repeated keywords, no all caps, no emoji unless the brand clearly uses them.\n- Avoid double quotation marks in descriptions, because some systems cut text at the quote.\n- If you cannot tell what a page is about (only an opaque URL such as /p/12345, or no description), write no snippet for it: list it under Issues found and ask for its content. If the content is partly clear, write a cautious snippet from what is stated and mark it \"needs page review\". Never invent a product, offer or topic to fill the gap.\n- Count characters precisely; when unsure, stay below the upper limit rather than above it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Meta tags\nA table: Page | Intent | Title tag | Title characters | Meta description | Description characters.\n\n## Issues found\nBullets: competing pages, pages skipped or marked \"needs page review\" and what you need to know about them, current titles or descriptions that should change and why. Write \"None\" if there are none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "audit-on-page-seo",
          "write-seo-content-brief",
          "research-keywords"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6d818def0c8dc656c40016ac6c0707b140eeea3f8108cdba7b33c3153d230f78"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-methods-section",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a replicable methods section",
      "description": "Writes a manuscript methods section another researcher could replicate, following the relevant reporting guideline and flagging every missing detail. For authors drafting a paper or thesis.",
      "category": "scientific-writing",
      "tags": [
        "manuscript",
        "reporting-guidelines",
        "reproducibility",
        "equator-network"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "study_details",
          "description": "Everything about how the study was done - design, setting, dates, participants or materials, procedures, measures, sample size, analysis and software, ethics approval and registration. Notes, protocol text or a rough draft all work.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "reporting_guideline",
          "description": "The guideline to follow, for example CONSORT, STROBE, PRISMA, ARRIVE, COREQ, SRQR, STARD or TRIPOD. Leave empty to have one chosen from the design.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "word_limit",
          "description": "The journal's limit for the methods section, if any, for example \"1,500 words\" or \"no limit, details may go to supplementary material\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe methods section exists so readers can judge the results and repeat the study. It fails when it describes what was intended rather than what was done, leaves out decisions that shape results (eligibility, randomisation, exclusions, deviations from protocol, how missing data were handled, software versions), or hides them in vague phrases such as \"standard procedures\" and \"appropriate statistical tests\". Reporting guidelines collected by the EQUATOR Network list what readers need for each design: CONSORT for randomised trials, STROBE for observational studies, PRISMA for systematic reviews, ARRIVE for animal research, COREQ or SRQR for qualitative research, STARD for diagnostic accuracy, TRIPOD for prediction models, and others.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the methods section from these details.\n<study_details>\n{{study_details}}\n</study_details>\n{{#reporting_guideline}}Reporting guideline: {{reporting_guideline}}{{/reporting_guideline}}\n{{#word_limit}}Word limit: {{word_limit}}{{/word_limit}}\n\n1. Identify the design and confirm the reporting guideline. If none was given, choose it from the design and say why; if the given guideline does not fit the design, say so and suggest the right one.\n2. Organise the section with the subheadings readers expect for this design and guideline, for example: study design and setting; participants (eligibility, recruitment, dates); interventions or exposures; outcomes and measures (with how and when measured, and validity of instruments); sample size; randomisation and blinding where relevant; data collection; statistical or qualitative analysis; ethics and registration.\n3. Write in the past tense and describe what was done, with enough detail to replicate: quantities, units, timings, versions of software and instruments, model specifications, thresholds and the handling of missing data and outliers. Cite the method for standard techniques rather than re-describe them, using the citation the user provided or a [CITE: method] placeholder.\n4. Wherever a detail the guideline requires is missing, insert [GAP: what is needed] in the text and list it under Gaps to fill with the guideline item it serves.\n5. Keep within the word limit by moving long procedural detail to supplementary material, and list what moved.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the details provided. Never invent sample sizes, dates, approval numbers, registration IDs, software versions, effect sizes or procedures. Every unknown becomes a [GAP: …] marker.\n- Report what was done, not what was planned. If the details mention a protocol deviation, report it plainly.\n- Do not put results in the methods (except participant flow if the guideline places it there).\n- Paraphrase guideline items in your own words and tell the user to check the official checklist for the current version.\n- Use the terms and abbreviations the user uses, defining each abbreviation once.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Guideline used\nOne line with the guideline and why.\n## Methods\nThe section, with the subheadings and [GAP: …] markers in place.\n## Gaps to fill\nTable: gap | guideline item it serves | why reviewers will ask for it.\n## Moved to supplementary material\nBullets, or \"Nothing moved\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "check-manuscript-reporting",
          "write-discussion-section",
          "write-abstract",
          "write-lab-protocol"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "research-methodologist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:545d25e3350d1695dbedeba8f7a022865290c2da4cd57bb5c9d10d74c0abc232"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-migration-guide",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a migration guide",
      "description": "Writes an upgrade guide for a breaking release that lists each breaking change with how to find affected code, before-and-after examples and a way to verify. Use when shipping a major version.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "upgrade-guide",
        "breaking-changes",
        "semver"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "from_version",
          "description": "The version users are upgrading from (tag or version number).",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "to_version",
          "description": "The version users are upgrading to.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "changes",
          "description": "Changelog, release notes or a list of breaking changes, if you have them. The guide still checks them against the code.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA migration guide is used by someone who has to upgrade without breaking production. They need to know whether they are affected, how to find the affected code in their own codebase, exactly what to change, and how to confirm it worked. A changelog line like \"Renamed `connect` options\" is not enough: the reader needs the old and new code side by side.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the guide for upgrading from {{from_version}} to {{to_version}}.\n{{#changes}}\nKnown changes:\n{{changes}}\n{{/changes}}\n\n1. Build the list of breaking changes from the changelog, release notes, commits marked breaking (an exclamation mark before the colon in the header, or a `BREAKING CHANGE` footer) and a diff of the public surface between the two versions: exported symbols, function signatures, CLI flags, config keys, environment variables, defaults, HTTP routes and response shapes, minimum runtime versions and peer dependencies.\n2. Check each change against the code at both versions. Drop anything that is not actually breaking for users; add breaking changes the notes missed.\n3. For each breaking change write: what changed and why (one or two sentences), who is affected and how to find affected code (a search pattern or symptom such as an error message), a before and after code example, and the exact steps. If a mechanical rewrite is safe, give it, and say when it is not safe.\n4. Order changes by how many users they affect, most common first. Group small related changes.\n5. List deprecations that still work but will break in a later version, with the replacement.\n6. End with how to verify: commands, tests or observable behaviour that confirm the upgrade worked, and how to roll back.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every claimed change must be traceable to the code, the commits or the given notes. Mark anything you inferred but could not confirm with `TODO(maintainer): ...`.\n- Before and after examples must use real names and signatures from the two versions. Never invent options or APIs.\n- Do not soften breaking changes or hide them in prose; one heading per change.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n# Upgrading from {{from_version}} to {{to_version}}\n## Who needs this\nTwo or three sentences, including the effort level (minutes, hours) if it can be judged.\n## Before you start\nPrerequisites: runtime versions, peer dependencies, a backup or a database migration.\n## Breaking changes\nOne `###` heading per change, each with: what changed, how to find affected code, Before and After code blocks, steps.\n## Deprecations\nA table: deprecated | replacement | removal planned in. Or \"None\".\n## Verify the upgrade\nNumbered checks, then rollback steps.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "technical-writer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-changelog"
        ]
      },
