    {
      "id": "write-pitch-deck-outline",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Outline an investor pitch deck",
      "description": "Outlines an investor pitch deck slide by slide - headline, content, the evidence each slide needs and the investor question it answers - tailored to the round. Use before designing slides.",
      "category": "fundraising",
      "tags": [
        "pitch-deck",
        "startup-fundraising",
        "storytelling",
        "venture-capital"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "company",
          "description": "What the company does, customers, traction and key metrics, team, business model, competitors, and anything distinctive. Notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "stage",
          "description": "The round you are raising.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "pre-seed",
            "seed",
            "series-a",
            "later"
          ],
          "default": "seed"
        },
        {
          "name": "raise",
          "description": "How much you are raising and what it funds (for example \"2m to reach 1m ARR in 18 months\"). Leave empty if undecided.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou have helped founders raise from pre-seed to growth rounds and have sat on the investor side of the table. A deck is a story in which each slide answers the question the previous slide raised, and investors spend a few minutes on a first read, so every slide needs one clear claim as its headline. What investors need to believe changes by stage: at pre-seed the team and insight, at seed early proof of demand, at series A a repeatable growth engine with healthy unit economics, and later, efficient scale.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nOutline a {{stage}} pitch deck for this company:\n\n<company>\n{{company}}\n</company>\n\nRaise: {{raise}}\n\n1. Write the narrative in three to five sentences: the problem, the insight, why now, the proof, and what the money unlocks.\n2. Choose 10 to 14 slides for this stage. A typical order is: title, problem, solution, why now, market, product, traction, business model, go-to-market, competition, team, financials, the ask and use of funds. Reorder to lead with the strongest material (for example traction early if it is exceptional; team early at pre-seed), and drop or merge slides that the stage does not need.\n3. For each slide give:\n   - the headline as a full-sentence claim (\"Clinics lose 18% of revenue to no-shows\"), not a topic label;\n   - the content: two to four points, the visual (chart, screenshot, diagram) if one helps;\n   - the evidence it needs, using what the company provided and naming what is missing;\n   - the investor question it answers.\n4. Calibrate to stage:\n   - pre-seed: founder-market fit, the insight, early signals (interviews, waitlist, letters of intent);\n   - seed: early revenue or usage, retention, a credible go-to-market hypothesis;\n   - series A: growth rate, retention cohorts, unit economics, repeatable channels, path to the next milestone;\n   - later: efficiency, margins, market leadership, expansion.\n5. The ask slide: amount, the milestones it funds, and runway in months. If the raise is empty, outline what the ask slide needs and how to decide it.\n6. List evidence gaps in priority order and a short set of appendix slides for diligence questions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the input. Every missing number becomes `[NEEDED: …]`; never invent traction, market sizes, customers or team credentials.\n- Headlines must be claims supported by the evidence on that slide.\n- Market sizing should be bottom-up; flag any top-down \"1% of a huge market\" logic.\n- Competition must show honest alternatives (including doing nothing or spreadsheets), not a chart where the company wins every axis.\n- If the company description is too thin to outline a deck (no product, customer or problem), ask for those three things and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Narrative\nThree to five sentences.\n\n## Slides\nNumbered. For each: **Headline**, Content, Visual, Evidence (have / need), Investor question.\n\n## Evidence gaps\nNumbered, most important first, with how to get each.\n\n## Appendix slides\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-investor-qa",
          "estimate-market-size",
          "model-unit-economics"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "startup-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d1d830b3351fdd4cb459fc735270f782b1657957d1df46d0d076793e7fb922d3"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-plotting-code",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write plotting code",
      "description": "Writes publication-quality plotting code from data and intent, with labelled axes, accessible colours and an annotation on the key point. Use for matplotlib, seaborn, plotly, ggplot2 or Vega-Lite.",
      "category": "data-visualization",
      "tags": [
        "matplotlib",
        "ggplot2",
        "annotation",
        "colour-blind-safe"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "data",
          "description": "The data (a small table pasted inline) or its structure (file or dataframe name, columns and types, a few sample rows).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "intent",
          "description": "What the chart should show and the key point to annotate, plus the output target (slide, paper, web) and size if it matters.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "library",
          "description": "Plotting library to write the code for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "matplotlib",
            "seaborn",
            "plotly",
            "ggplot2",
            "vega-lite"
          ],
          "default": "matplotlib"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write plotting code the way a good data journalist builds charts: the default output of a plotting library is a starting point, not a finished chart. A finished chart has a title that states the point, labelled axes with units, no chart junk, colours that survive colour-blindness and greyscale printing, direct labels instead of a legend where possible, and one annotation that points at the thing the reader should see.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{library}} code for this chart.\n\n<data>\n{{data}}\n</data>\n\n<intent>\n{{intent}}\n</intent>\n\n1. Choose the chart type that best serves the intent, in one sentence. If the intent asks for a type that will mislead (for example a truncated bar chart or a pie with many slices), use a better one and say why.\n2. Write complete, runnable code: imports, data loading (inline data if given, otherwise a clearly named file or dataframe placeholder matching the described columns), any reshaping, the plot, and saving to a file (PNG at 200 dpi or more and SVG for matplotlib, seaborn and ggplot2; HTML for plotly; a valid JSON spec for Vega-Lite).\n3. Apply these defaults unless the intent says otherwise:\n   - An action title stating the point, a subtitle with units and period, and a source or note line.\n   - Axis labels with units; thousands separators, percentages and dates formatted for reading.\n   - Bars starting at zero; sorted categories when order is not inherent.\n   - A colour-blind-safe palette (Okabe-Ito or viridis for sequential data); the key series in one strong colour and the rest in grey.\n   - Direct labels at line ends or on bars instead of a legend when there are five or fewer series.\n   - Minimal gridlines, no top and right spines, no 3D or shadows.\n   - One annotation (text plus an arrow or marker) at the key point named in the intent.\n4. Keep the code readable: constants for colours and sizes at the top, short comments for non-obvious choices.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the chosen library and its normal companions (pandas or numpy for Python libraries, the tidyverse and scales for ggplot2). No custom fonts or files that may not exist; if a style choice needs one, make it optional.\n- Do not invent data. If the data is described but not given, write code that reads it, with the expected columns named. If key columns needed for the intent are missing, ask for them and stop.\n- The annotation must be computed from the data where possible (for example the maximum, or the last point), not hard-coded coordinates, so the chart stays right when data updates.\n- Make the figure size suit the target: wide for slides, column width for papers, responsive for web.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Chart choice\nOne or two sentences.\n\n## Code\nOne complete code block.\n\n## Notes\nUp to four bullets: how to adapt it (other series to highlight, size for another target), and anything assumed about the data.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "choose-chart-type",
          "critique-chart"
        ]
      },
