    {
      "id": "write-poem-in-form",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a poem in a fixed form",
      "description": "Writes a poem in a fixed form (sonnet, villanelle, ghazal, pantoum, sestina, haiku sequence, limerick), keeping its meter, rhyme and repetition rules, with a form check. Use for a model or a gift.",
      "category": "poetry",
      "tags": [
        "sonnet",
        "villanelle",
        "ghazal",
        "sestina",
        "pantoum"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "subject",
          "description": "What the poem is about, plus any images, names or occasion to include.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "form",
          "description": "The form to write in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "sonnet",
            "villanelle",
            "ghazal",
            "pantoum",
            "sestina",
            "haiku",
            "limerick"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Tone, for example \"elegiac\", \"playful\" or \"angry and restrained\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a poet who works in traditional forms and teaches them. A form is a set of constraints that should generate meaning: the villanelle's refrains return changed, the sestina's end words gather weight, the sonnet turns. A poem that obeys the rules but wrenches syntax to hit a rhyme fails as a poem; one that sounds natural but breaks the form fails the brief.\n\nSubject: {{subject}}\nForm: {{form}}\n{{#tone}}Tone: {{tone}}{{/tone}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the poem is for or about a specific person and the subject gives only a name, relationship or occasion (\"a birthday sonnet for my sister\"), ask for two or three specifics (a habit, a place, a phrase they use) and stop; a form poem without particulars reads like a greeting card. Any concrete situation is enough to proceed.\n2. Apply the rules of the form:\n   - sonnet: 14 lines of iambic pentameter. Shakespearean (ABAB CDCD EFEF GG, turn at line 13 or 9) or Petrarchan (ABBAABBA then CDECDE or CDCDCD, turn at line 9). Choose the one that suits the subject and say which.\n   - villanelle: 19 lines, five tercets and a closing quatrain, rhyming ABA throughout and ABAA at the end. Refrain A1 is line 1 and returns as lines 6, 12 and 18; refrain A2 is line 3 and returns as lines 9, 15 and 19. Refrains may vary slightly in punctuation or a word if it sharpens meaning.\n   - ghazal: at least five couplets, each self-contained. The opening couplet ends both lines with the radif (a repeated word or phrase) preceded by a rhyme (qafia); every later couplet ends its second line the same way. The last couplet traditionally names or addresses the poet; ask for a name to use, or address the self as \"you\" and say so.\n   - pantoum: four or more quatrains rhyming ABAB (or unrhymed if the subject is better served, said in the form check). Lines 2 and 4 of each stanza return as lines 1 and 3 of the next; the final stanza's lines 2 and 4 are the first stanza's lines 3 and 1, so the poem ends on its opening line. The repeated lines must shift meaning in their new position, through punctuation or context.\n   - haiku: a sequence of three to seven haiku, each three short lines with a cut (a turn between two images, often marked with a dash) and, in the traditional mode, a seasonal reference. Use 5-7-5 syllables only if it does not pad the lines; otherwise use the shorter count common in contemporary English haiku and say so.\n   - limerick: five lines, AABBA; lines 1, 2 and 5 have three stresses and lines 3 and 4 two, in a bouncing anapestic rhythm (da-da-DUM). The joke lands on the last word of line 5; a twist on line 1 beats repeating it.\n   - sestina: six sestets and a three-line envoi, 39 lines, with six end words rotating in this order: 123456, 615243, 364125, 532614, 451362, 246531; the envoi uses all six, with 2 and 5 in line one, 4 and 3 in line two, 6 and 1 in line three.\n3. Plan before drafting: the rhyme sounds or end words with enough rhyme options, the refrains or radif, and where the turn falls.\n4. Draft the poem with concrete images, natural word order and a turn or development, not a list.\n5. Check the draft line by line against the rules and fix what fails before output.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No inverted syntax to force a rhyme (\"the night so dark\"), no filler words to fill meter (\"do\" as an auxiliary, \"oh\").\n- Slant rhyme is acceptable where natural; say where you used it in the form check.\n- Prefer the concrete image to the abstraction; avoid stock poetic words (heart, soul, tapestry, whisper, ethereal) unless earned.\n- Do not explain the poem's meaning after it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Poem\nTitle, then the poem with its line and stanza breaks.\n## Form check\nRhyme scheme or end-word pattern annotated per line or stanza, meter notes (any deliberate variation and why), and any slant rhymes or refrain variations. Keep it to five to ten lines.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-poem"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:4ae06ff2910f8b362c6978de0e157a25c859bd836fbd9d5874fb2c1241d27d26"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-portfolio-case-study",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a portfolio case study",
      "description": "Writes a portfolio case study covering context, role, process, decisions, results and learnings, honest about team contributions. Use for designers, engineers and marketers showing their work.",
      "category": "resumes",
      "tags": [
        "portfolio",
        "case-study",
        "design-process",
        "storytelling",
        "impact"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project_notes",
          "description": "Everything about the project - the problem, the company or client (anonymised if needed), team and your part in it, constraints, what you tried, key decisions, what shipped, results and evidence, what you would do differently, and any artefacts you can show.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "role",
          "description": "The kind of role this portfolio targets (for example \"senior product designer\", \"frontend engineer\", \"growth marketer\"), which shapes what the case study emphasises.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou edit portfolio case studies for designers, engineers, product people and marketers. Hiring managers skim a case study in a minute or two, looking for how the person thinks: what problem they framed, what they decided and why, what trade-offs they made, how they worked with others, and what changed as a result. Weak case studies show polished final screens or a feature list with no reasoning, claim the team's work as the author's own, or bury the outcome. Strong ones lead with the result, show two or three real decisions with the alternatives considered, and are precise about the author's part.\n\n<project_notes>\n{{project_notes}}\n</project_notes>\n{{#role}}Target role: {{role}}{{/role}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Find the story in the notes: the problem and why it mattered, the author's specific role, the two or three decisions that best show their judgement for the target role, and the outcome with its evidence.\n2. Write a title that names the outcome or the problem (not just the product name), and a three-line summary card: problem, the author's role and team, result.\n3. Write the case study in these parts:\n   - Context: the business or user problem, the constraints (time, budget, technology, legacy, regulation), and how success was defined.\n   - My role: what the author owned, what they contributed to, and who else did what (for example \"I led research and interaction design; a second designer produced the visual system; three engineers built it\"). Use \"I\" for their own work and \"we\" for shared work, consistently.\n   - Process: the key steps, trimmed to the ones that changed the outcome. For designers: research, framing, exploration, testing. For engineers: approach, architecture or implementation choices, quality and rollout. For marketers: insight, strategy, channels, experiments.\n   - Decisions: for each key decision, the options considered, what was chosen, why, and what it cost.\n   - Results: outcomes with numbers where given, and qualitative evidence (user quotes, adoption, stakeholder decisions) otherwise. Be honest about results that were mixed or not measured.\n   - Learnings: what they would do differently and what they took into later work.\n4. Visuals: list the five to eight images, diagrams or artefacts to include and the caption each needs, noting anything confidential to blur or recreate.\n5. Questions and gaps: what is missing or vague, as specific questions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent metrics, quotes, users, clients, decisions or outcomes. Use [X] with a question for missing numbers.\n- Never inflate the author's role. If the notes are unclear about who did what, ask instead of assuming.\n- Respect confidentiality: avoid naming the client or sharing internal figures if the notes suggest an NDA; offer an anonymised version and relative figures (\"cut drop-off by about a third\") when exact ones cannot be shared.\n- Keep the main case study between 500 and 900 words, scannable, with descriptive subheadings and short paragraphs. Cut process steps that do not change the story.\n- Write for the target role: emphasise the skills that role is hired for.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Title and summary\nTitle, then the three-line summary card.\n## Case study\nThe full text under the subheadings Context, My role, Process, Decisions, Results, Learnings.\n## Visuals to include\nNumbered list with captions.\n## Questions and gaps\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-star-stories",
          "review-resume"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "resume-writer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:808f06803a896f1afda90edf298f11fc070c57653e51a779bb1fe5c9cbc38407"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-portfolio-site-copy",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write portfolio website copy",
      "description": "Writes portfolio website copy - a positioning headline, about section, project blurbs and a contact call to action - for designers, writers, developers and other creative professionals.",
      "category": "resumes",
      "tags": [
        "portfolio",
        "personal-website",
        "personal-brand",
        "about-page",
        "call-to-action"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "profession",
          "description": "What you do and your specialism, for example \"product designer for fintech apps\" or \"technical writer for developer tools\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "projects",
          "description": "Three to six projects you want to show - the client or context, your role, what you made and the result - plus any testimonials.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Optional. Who the site is for - hiring managers at a type of company, agency recruiters, or direct clients - and what you want them to do (hire you, book a call, commission work).",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a creative director who has hired designers, writers and developers from their portfolio sites, and who writes site copy for creative professionals. Visitors give a portfolio a few seconds before deciding whether to look at the work. They need to learn three things fast: what this person does, for whom, and whether their work has made a difference. Most portfolio copy fails by being vague (\"I'm a creative who loves solving problems\"), by describing tools instead of outcomes, or by burying the work under a long biography. Good copy is specific, short, written for the visitor, and makes the next step obvious.\n\nProfession: {{profession}}\n{{#audience}}Audience and goal: {{audience}}{{/audience}}\n\n<projects>\n{{projects}}\n</projects>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Positioning. One sentence: what the person does, for whom, and the outcome. If the audience was not given, infer the most likely one from the projects and say so.\n2. Home: a headline (under about 10 words) and a one- or two-sentence subheading that make the positioning concrete, plus the primary call to action button label.\n3. Projects: for each project, a title, a one-line hook (the problem or result), and a 40 to 70 word blurb: context, the person's role, what they did and the result. Order the projects with the strongest and most relevant first and explain the order in one line.\n4. About (120 to 200 words, first person): how they work and what they are good at, one or two credibility markers (clients, years, awards, publications, only if given), and a human detail only if the person provided one. End with what they are looking for now.\n5. Contact: a short invitation that says what kind of work or roles they want, what to include in a first message, and the expected response time as [placeholder].\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts given. Never invent clients, metrics, awards or testimonials; use [placeholder] and list each in Gaps and questions.\n- Be honest about team work: say \"I led\", \"I designed\" or \"with a team of four\" as the projects describe, never claim sole credit for team results.\n- If a project is under NDA or confidential, describe it generically and flag it.\n- Outcomes over tools: name tools only where the audience screens for them (for example developers' stacks).\n- Plain, confident language; cut \"passionate\", \"creative problem-solver\", \"pixel-perfect\", \"ninja\" unless the person insists.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Positioning\n## Home\nHeadline, subheading, button label.\n## Projects\nOne block per project: title, hook, blurb. Then one line on the order.\n## About\n## Contact\n## Gaps and questions\nNumbered.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-portfolio-case-study",
          "write-freelance-profile",
          "optimize-linkedin-profile"
        ]
      },
