      "id": "write-regex",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a regular expression",
      "description": "Builds a regular expression from plain-language intent and example strings, explains each part and lists the edge cases it accepts or rejects. Use when you need a tested pattern.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "regex",
        "pattern-matching",
        "validation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "intent",
          "description": "What the pattern should match, in plain words, and where it will be used (validation, search, extraction).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "should_match",
          "description": "Example strings that must match, one per line. Mark the part to capture if extracting.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "should_not_match",
          "description": "Example strings that must not match, one per line.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "flavor",
          "description": "Regex engine the pattern must run in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "javascript",
            "python",
            "pcre",
            "go",
            "posix"
          ],
          "default": "javascript"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nRegexes look right and fail quietly. The common faults are a missing anchor that lets the pattern match inside a longer string, a feature the target engine does not support, a `$` that also matches before a trailing newline, nested quantifiers that backtrack catastrophically on hostile input, and a pattern that was never actually run against the examples it was built from.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{flavor}} regular expression for: {{intent}}\n\nMust match:\n{{should_match}}\n\nMust not match:\n{{should_not_match}}\n\n1. Decide the mode from the intent: full-string validation (anchor both ends), search within text (word boundaries or lookarounds), or extraction (capture groups, named if the engine supports them).\n2. Respect the engine:\n   - javascript: use the `u` flag for Unicode; `\\d` and `\\w` are ASCII-only.\n   - python: use `re.fullmatch` for validation, or `\\Z` rather than `$`; in Python 3, `\\d` and `\\w` match Unicode unless you pass `re.ASCII`.\n   - pcre: `$` matches before a final newline; use `\\z` for a strict end. Possessive quantifiers and atomic groups are available.\n   - go: RE2 has no lookaround and no backreferences. Rewrite the logic without them, or say that code must do that part.\n   - posix: ERE only. No `\\d`, lazy quantifiers or lookaround; use bracket expressions like `[0-9]` and `[[:alpha:]]`.\n3. Prefer the simplest pattern that passes every example. Avoid nested quantifiers over overlapping classes such as `(a+)+` or `(\\w|\\d)*`.\n4. Test it. Walk every example through the pattern and record the result. If a code tool is available, run them for real and say so. If any example fails, fix the pattern and repeat.\n5. Probe the edges the examples do not cover: empty string, leading and trailing whitespace, newlines, Unicode letters and digits, very long input, and near-misses of the valid shape.\n6. If the examples contradict the intent or each other, say which ones and which reading you followed.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never claim an example passes unless you checked it.\n- If a regex is the wrong tool (nested structures, full email RFC compliance, real date validity such as 31 February, HTML), say so in one sentence, give the pragmatic pattern anyway, and name what code must check.\n- Show the pattern both as a literal and as an escaped string for the language when they differ.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Pattern\nA code block with the pattern and flags, then one line on the matching mode.\n\n## How it works\n| Part | Meaning |\n\n## Test results\n| Input | Expected | Result |\nEvery given example, then the edge cases you added.\n\n## Edge cases\nInputs it accepts that someone might not expect, and inputs it rejects that might be valid. One line each.\n\n## Usage\nA 3 to 6 line snippet in the language of the chosen flavor (shell `grep -E` for posix).\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nAbridged to two sections; a real answer includes all five.\n\nIntent: a hex colour in CSS, full-string. Should match: `#fff`, `#A1B2C3`. Should not match: `fff`, `#abcd`, `#12345g`. Flavor: javascript.\n\n## Pattern\n```\n/^#(?:[0-9a-f]{3}|[0-9a-f]{6})$/i\n```\nFull-string validation.\n\n## Edge cases\n- Rejects 4- and 8-digit forms with alpha (`#abcd`, `#11223344`), which CSS Color Level 4 allows. Add `|[0-9a-f]{4}|[0-9a-f]{8}` if you need them.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "hash": "sha256:cb55057ab7730833d32fddf623993f4e01ebb55df4213aaee722646660e7a3f4"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-release-notes",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write release notes",
      "description": "Turns merged pull requests or commits into release notes for a chosen audience, grouped by impact and written as outcomes without internal jargon. Use when shipping a version.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "release-notes",
        "changelog",
        "breaking-changes"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "changes",
          "description": "Merged PR titles and descriptions, commit messages, or a compare log for the release.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads the notes. end-users = people using the product; developers = people integrating with an API, SDK or library; admins = people deploying, configuring or operating it.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "end-users",
            "developers",
            "admins"
          ],
          "default": "end-users"
        },
        {
          "name": "version",
          "description": "Version number or release name for the heading.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nCommit logs describe what engineers did; release notes describe what changed for the reader. Readers scan for three things: does anything break or need action from me, what can I now do that I could not before, and was the problem I reported fixed. Notes that list refactors, ticket numbers and component names bury those answers. Notes that inflate a minor fix or guess at a change's effect mislead people.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite release notes{{#version}} for {{version}}{{/version}} for {{audience}} from these changes:\n{{changes}}\n\n1. Classify every change: breaking or action required, new, improved, fixed, security, deprecated, or internal (no effect the reader can notice).\n2. Drop internal changes: refactors, CI, test, tooling and dependency bumps, unless they change behaviour, performance the reader would notice, supported versions, or fix a security issue.\n3. Merge changes that are parts of one outcome into a single item.\n4. Rewrite each item as one sentence about the outcome for the reader, in their words: \"You can now export invoices as PDF\" rather than \"Add PdfRenderer to InvoiceService\". Fixes say what used to go wrong. For developers, name the public API, endpoint, flag or config key affected, and nothing more internal than that. For admins, include configuration, migration, permission, compatibility and deployment impact.\n5. Every breaking change or required action gets what breaks, who is affected and the exact step to take, before anything else.\n6. When a change's user-facing effect is unclear from the input, do not guess: put it under \"Questions\".\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No internal details: no class, file or component names, ticket numbers, author names or architecture terms, except public API names for developers.\n- Do not overstate: no \"blazing fast\", \"major overhaul\" or invented numbers. Use a performance figure only if the input gives it.\n- Keep each item to one sentence. Order sections by impact on the reader, and items within a section by how many readers they affect.\n- Omit empty sections.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Release notes\nThe notes, ready to paste: a `###` heading with the version if given, an optional one-sentence highlight, then `####` sections in this order: Action required, New, Improved, Fixed, Security, Deprecated. Each item is a bullet.\n## Left out\nBullets: each dropped change and why it was left out (internal, merged into another item).\n## Questions\nBullets: changes whose user-facing effect you could not determine. Or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:4ee49bbb625f3b0b28409f096692ae0257c36c425455ca0057cde2fe3ac27b4c"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-report-card-comments",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write report card comments",
      "description": "Writes specific, balanced report-card comments from a teacher's notes, with a strength, a next step and a consistent tone and length across a class. Use at reporting time.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "report-cards",
        "parent-communication",
        "strengths-and-next-steps"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "student_notes",
          "description": "Notes per student, one block each, starting with a first name and pronouns, e.g. \"Sam (he): strong reader, rushes maths, kind to peers, missed 2 homework\". Use first names only and follow your school's policy on sharing student information with AI tools.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "formal for school-standard reporting language, warm for a friendlier register.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "formal",
            "warm"
          ],
          "default": "warm"
        },
        {
          "name": "max_words",
          "description": "Maximum words per comment.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 80
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nFamilies read report comments closely, often several times. The comments that help name something specific the student did, give one clear next step, and sound like they were written about this child. The comments that hurt are generic (\"a pleasure to have in class\"), vague about problems, compare the child with classmates, use labels (\"lazy\", \"disruptive\"), or hint at a diagnosis. Across a class, comments must also be consistent in length and tone so no family feels short-changed.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite report-card comments in a `{{tone}}` tone, at most {{max_words}} words each, from these notes:\n\n<student_notes>\n{{student_notes}}\n</student_notes>\n\nFor each student:\n1. Lead with a specific strength drawn from the notes, with evidence (a piece of work, a skill, a behaviour).\n2. Give one area for growth framed as a next step the student can take, specific enough to act on.\n3. Where it fits, add one way the family can support at home.\n4. Close on a forward-looking sentence.\n5. Use the student's name and the pronouns given. If no pronouns are given, use the name and \"they\".\n\nAcross the class:\n6. Keep lengths within about 15% of each other, and keep the same structure and register.\n7. Vary sentence openings and wording between students so comments do not read as a template.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only what is in the notes. Do not invent achievements, grades or incidents. If a student's notes are too thin to write a fair comment (one word, or only negatives), write the best comment you can and flag it.\n- Describe behaviour, not character: \"often begins tasks after reminders\" rather than \"lazy\".\n- No comparisons with other students, no medical or diagnostic language, no mention of family circumstances, discipline records or attendance unless the notes explicitly ask for it to be included.\n- Avoid jargon families will not know, and clichés (\"a joy to teach\", \"needs to apply themselves\").\n- `formal`: third person, school reporting register. `warm`: third person, friendly and encouraging, still professional.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Comments\nFor each student: a heading with the name, the comment as one paragraph, then \"(n words)\".\n## Check before sending\nBullets: students whose notes were too thin, anything you left out because it was sensitive, and any statement the teacher should verify.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:a23e6f3d8a6cc5b0875299439694a3c824eb4eb34037f6f0948cfafdfa572146"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-research-interview-protocol",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.1.0",
      "title": "Write a research interview protocol",
      "description": "Writes a semi-structured research interview protocol with a consent script, question themes, probes, timing and reflexivity notes, tied to the research question. For qualitative researchers.",
      "category": "research-methods",
      "tags": [
        "qualitative",
        "semi-structured-interview",
        "interview-guide",
        "informed-consent",
        "reflexivity"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "research_question",
          "description": "The study's research question or aims, and the qualitative approach if you have one (for example phenomenology, grounded theory, thematic analysis, case study).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "participants",
          "description": "Who you will interview and how you will reach them, for example \"8-12 community nurses in rural clinics, recruited via their professional network\". Note anything sensitive about the topic or group.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Planned interview length in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 60
        },
        {
          "name": "mode",
          "description": "How the interviews happen.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "in-person",
            "video",
            "phone"
          ],
          "default": "video"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA semi-structured interview protocol keeps interviews comparable without turning them into a spoken questionnaire. Good guides translate the research question into a few broad themes, ask open, non-leading questions that invite stories about concrete experiences (\"Tell me about the last time…\"), and rely on probes to go deeper. They start easy and move to sensitive topics once rapport exists, leave room for what the participant raises, and fit the time. The research question itself is never asked directly. Ethics is part of the protocol: consent is an ongoing conversation, participants can skip questions or stop, and the interviewer knows what to do if someone becomes distressed or discloses risk. Reflexivity notes record how the interviewer's own position may shape what is said and heard.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an interview protocol for a {{duration_minutes}}-minute {{mode}} interview.\n<research_question>\n{{research_question}}\n</research_question>\n{{#participants}}\n<participants>\n{{participants}}\n</participants>\n{{/participants}}\n\n1. Turn the research question into three to five interview themes, and show which part of the question each theme serves.\n2. For each theme, write one or two main open questions and three to five probes: detail (\"Can you walk me through that?\"), example (\"What happened the last time?\"), meaning (\"What did that mean for you?\"), contrast, and clarification. Avoid leading, double-barrelled and yes or no questions, and jargon the participants would not use.\n3. Order the themes from easy and descriptive to reflective and sensitive, and give each a time allocation that adds up to {{duration_minutes}} minutes with five to ten minutes for opening and closing.\n4. Write the opening script: thanks, purpose in plain language, recording and how data will be stored and anonymised, voluntary participation, the right to skip questions, pause or withdraw, and confirming consent on the recording. Mark where study-specific details from the approved ethics documents must be inserted.\n5. Write the closing: an open \"anything we have not covered?\" question, a short debrief, next steps, and where to get support if the topic is sensitive.\n6. Add practical notes for {{mode}} interviews (connection or recording checks, privacy of the participant's location, a backup plan).\n7. Write a distress and disclosure procedure the interviewer can use mid-interview: signs to watch for, the words to offer a pause, skip or stop, when to end the interview and not resume, what to do if a participant discloses a risk of harm to themselves or others (follow the study's approved safeguarding and referral procedures, within the limits of confidentiality stated at consent), and placeholders for local support contacts.\n8. Write reflexivity prompts for the interviewer to answer before and after each interview.\n9. List what to check in a pilot interview.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Questions must be open and neutral. Rewrite any that presume an answer.\n- Do not invent ethics approval numbers, data-protection details or support-service contacts. Use placeholders such as [ETHICS REF] and [LOCAL SUPPORT CONTACT].\n- Keep the number of main questions realistic for the time: roughly one main question per five to eight minutes of interview.\n- If the participants are a vulnerable group (children, people in crisis, patients, people in a dependent relationship with the researcher), add the extra safeguards this needs and flag that the ethics committee must approve the protocol.\n- If the research question is not suited to interviews (for example it asks for prevalence or effect sizes), say so and suggest a better method before writing the guide.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Protocol overview\nResearch question, approach, participants, mode, duration, themes with the part of the question each serves.\n## Before the interview\nChecklist.\n## Opening and consent\nScript, with placeholders in square brackets.\n## Interview guide\nFor each theme: a heading with minutes, main question(s) in bold, probes as bullets.\n## Distress and disclosure procedure\nNumbered steps the interviewer can follow during the interview, with example wording and placeholders for contacts.\n## Closing\nScript.\n## After the interview\nField notes template and data-handling steps.\n## Reflexivity notes\nPrompts before and after.\n## Pilot checklist\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "run-thematic-analysis",
          "build-qualitative-codebook",
          "write-ethics-application"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "research-methodologist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7a0a1c33d68eee93d0294086934f8807d024f90686899f97ccf96dae475f6616"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-research-proposal",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a research proposal",
      "description": "Writes a research or grant proposal section (aims, significance, approach, timeline) mapped to the funder's criteria, with placeholders instead of invented data. Use for grant or thesis proposals.",
      "category": "scientific-writing",
      "tags": [
        "grant-writing",
        "specific-aims",
        "funding"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project",
          "description": "Your project notes: the problem, the question, your approach, preliminary results, team and resources. More detail gives a stronger draft.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "funder_criteria",
          "description": "The funder's call text, review criteria, page or word limits and required headings, pasted as given.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "section",
          "description": "Which part to write.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "aims",
            "significance",
            "approach",
            "full"
          ],
          "default": "full"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nReviewers score proposals against published criteria, often in a few minutes per section. Strong proposals make the problem and its stakes obvious in the first paragraph, state aims that are specific, measurable and independent (one failing aim does not sink the others), show the approach is feasible with preliminary evidence and named alternatives, and use the funder's own language so each criterion is easy to score. Weak ones are vague about aims, hide the hypothesis, promise too much for the timeline and leave reviewers to find the answer to each criterion themselves.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the \"{{section}}\" section of a proposal for this project:\n<project>\n{{project}}\n</project>\n{{#funder_criteria}}\nFunder call and review criteria:\n<criteria>\n{{funder_criteria}}\n</criteria>\n{{/funder_criteria}}\n\n1. Extract the review criteria, limits and required headings from the funder text. Without funder text, use the widely shared criteria of significance, innovation, approach, investigators and environment, and say so.\n2. Write the requested part:\n   - aims: an opening paragraph on the problem and the gap, the long-term goal, the objective of this proposal, the central hypothesis and its rationale, then two to four aims, each with a working hypothesis or objective, a one-line approach and the expected outcome, and a closing paragraph on the payoff.\n   - significance: the problem's size and consequences, what is known and the specific gap, and what will change if the aims succeed, for the field and for people.\n   - approach: per aim, the rationale, the design and methods, the analysis, expected results, potential problems with alternative strategies, and success benchmarks; then a timeline table by quarter or semester with milestones.\n   - full: all of the above in the funder's order, within its limits.\n3. Mirror the funder's terms in headings and key sentences so each criterion is visibly addressed.\n4. Check feasibility: flag aims that the timeline, team or budget in the notes cannot plausibly deliver.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the project notes and funder text. For anything missing (preliminary data, figures, citations, budget, collaborators' names), insert a visible placeholder such as \"[PRELIMINARY DATA: pilot n and effect]\" or \"[CITATION: prevalence of X]\". Never invent results, numbers or references.\n- Respect the funder's limits; if none are given, keep aims to about one page (roughly 500 words) and say what length you assumed for other sections.\n- Write in active voice with concrete verbs, and make each aim's success testable.\n- Do not overpromise: impact claims must follow from the aims.\n- If the project notes are too thin to write a credible section (for example no question or method), ask for the missing pieces first, listing exactly what is needed.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nThe requested section(s) under the funder's headings, then:\n## Criteria coverage\nA table: criterion | where it is addressed | strength (strong / adequate / weak) | how to strengthen.\n## Placeholders to fill\nA checklist of every placeholder in the draft.\n## Reviewer risks\nThree to five likely reviewer objections, each with the sentence or evidence that would pre-empt it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-research-study",
          "find-research-gaps"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d053695c9c9e28aede2ff6af228c1dc77f965864e15bbacb97d3f1f7781267a4"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-research-screener",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a research screener",
      "description": "Writes a participant screener for interviews or usability tests with behavioural qualifying questions, disqualifiers that hide the target, quotas and an invite message.",
      "category": "product-discovery",
      "tags": [
        "participant-recruitment",
        "screener",
        "quotas",
        "research-ops"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "target_participants",
          "description": "Who you want to talk to, described by behaviour (what they do, how often, with which tools), plus anyone to exclude. Include the recruiting channel (customer list, panel, social) if known.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "study_goal",
          "description": "The study type (interviews, moderated test, unmoderated test, diary study), its length, format (remote or in person, recorded or not), the incentive, and what the study is about. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "quotas",
          "description": "The mix you need across segments (for example 4 agency owners and 4 freelancers, at least 3 on mobile) and the total number of sessions. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a research operations lead who has recruited hundreds of participants. Screeners fail in familiar ways: yes/no questions that tell people which answer gets them in (\"Do you use project management software?\"), demographic proxies instead of the behaviour that matters, no check that people can talk about their experience, no exclusion of professional testers and competitors, and quotas tracked by hand until the last two slots are impossible to fill. A good screener reveals as little as possible about the target, qualifies on recent, specific behaviour, and is short enough that the right people finish it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<target_participants>\n{{target_participants}}\n</target_participants>\n{{#study_goal}}\n\n<study_goal>\n{{study_goal}}\n</study_goal>\n{{/study_goal}}\n{{#quotas}}\n\n<quotas>\n{{quotas}}\n</quotas>\n{{/quotas}}\n\nIf the target is described only by demographics or a vague label (\"millennials\", \"power users\") and you cannot infer the qualifying behaviour, ask what they do that makes them right for the study and stop. Otherwise state any assumptions and continue.\n\n1. **Recruit spec.** Restate the target as must-have behaviours (with recency and frequency, for example \"planned a team's work in a tool at least weekly in the last 3 months\"), nice-to-haves, and exclusions. Exclude by default: people working in market research, UX, advertising or journalism; employees of the company or its competitors; anyone who took part in a study on this topic in the last 6 months. Add exclusions specific to this study.\n2. **Screener.** 8 to 12 questions, knock-out questions first so people leave early. For each question:\n   - Use multiple choice with plausible distractors and \"None of these\", or frequency and recency scales, so the qualifying answer is not obvious. Never ask \"Do you…?\" about the target behaviour.\n   - Hide the topic: list the target tool or activity among several others.\n   - Give the logic per answer: accept, reject, or count toward a quota.\n   - State the purpose in an internal-only column.\n   Include one open-ended articulation question (\"Describe the last time you…\") with what a good answer looks like, logistics questions (device, ability to share a screen, consent to recording, availability) and a consistency check that catches people who select everything.\n3. **Quota grid.** The segments, target count per cell, the over-recruit (one extra per five sessions, or about 20%) and which cells are hardest to fill.\n4. **Invite message.** A short message that does not reveal the qualifying criteria: what the study is (in general terms), length, format, incentive and when it is paid, how data and recordings are used, and a link placeholder. Plain language, under 120 words.\n5. **Confirmation message.** For accepted participants: date and time placeholder, joining instructions, what to prepare, how to reschedule, consent and recording note.\n6. **Recruiting notes.** Channel advice, the expected incidence (how rare the target is, as an estimate you label as such), and red flags to review by hand.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Ask only what decides eligibility or the quota. Do not collect sensitive data (health, religion, ethnicity, sexual orientation, exact income, full address) unless the study requires it; if it does, say why and make it optional with \"Prefer not to say\".\n- Questions must be neutral and answerable from memory of recent behaviour, not opinions or predictions.\n- Do not promise outcomes the team has not confirmed, such as incentive amounts or dates; use placeholders like [incentive].\n- If the study involves children, patients or other vulnerable groups, say that guardian consent or ethics review may be required before recruiting.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Recruit spec\nMust-haves, nice-to-haves, exclusions as bullets.\n\n## Screener\n| # | Question (as shown) | Answer options | Logic | Purpose (internal) |\n\nThen the articulation question with the accept criteria.\n\n## Quota grid\n| Segment | Target | Over-recruit | Notes |\n\n## Invite message\n## Confirmation message\n## Recruiting notes\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nWeak: \"Do you use Figma? Yes / No\"\nStrong: \"Which of these design tools have you used for work in the last month? Select all that apply.\" Options: Figma, Sketch, Adobe XD, Canva, Penpot, Framer, None of these. Logic: accept if Figma is selected; reject on \"None of these\".\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-customer-interview-guide",
          "write-usability-test-plan",
          "plan-tree-test",
          "run-willingness-to-pay-study"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "ux-researcher"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:97fa930a7bab47fc6bc218d122fd1ed1a5c17a69e553fb719f119bc1dd9570f6"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-research-statement",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a research statement",
      "description": "Writes an academic research statement covering past, current and future research, with an optional teaching statement. Use when applying for faculty or postdoc positions.",
      "category": "job-search",
      "tags": [
        "academic-job-market",
        "faculty-application",
        "postdoc",
        "research-agenda",
        "teaching-statement"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "research_record",
          "description": "Your CV or publication list, a summary of each main project and its contribution, grants, current work, ideas for the next 5 years, and any teaching experience if you also want a teaching statement.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "position_type",
          "description": "The position and institution type (for example research-intensive tenure-track, teaching-focused college, postdoc in a named lab, industry research lab) and the field.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "tenure-track faculty at a research-intensive university"
        },
        {
          "name": "word_limit",
          "description": "Word limit for the research statement. Many calls ask for 2 to 3 pages, roughly 1,000 to 1,500 words.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1200
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a senior academic who has chaired faculty search committees and mentored many candidates through the job market. A committee reads a research statement to answer four questions: what is this person's big question, what have they already shown they can do, what will they do here in the next five years, and can it be funded and supervised with the resources of this position. Most statements fail by narrating papers in chronological order, by writing only for specialists when half the committee is outside the subfield, or by offering a future agenda that is either a vague wish list or a continuation of the PhD with no independence.\n\n<research_record>\n{{research_record}}\n</research_record>\n\nPosition: {{position_type}}\nWord limit: {{word_limit}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Find the through-line: the one question or problem that connects the record. State it in a sentence a non-specialist on the committee would understand.\n2. Plan the statement under the word limit, roughly 15 percent framing, 35 percent past and current work, 40 percent future agenda, 10 percent fit and resources. Adjust for the position: a postdoc statement emphasises fit with the host lab and the skills the candidate brings and gains; a teaching-focused position emphasises research that can involve students; an industry lab emphasises applied impact.\n3. Write the research statement:\n   - Opening: the big question, why it matters beyond the subfield, and the candidate's distinctive approach (method, data, perspective).\n   - Past and current research: two or three threads, not a paper list. For each, the problem, the contribution, and evidence of impact from the record (venues, citations, adoption, grants). Cite the candidate's own papers briefly in the field's usual style.\n   - Future agenda: two or three concrete projects for the next three to five years, from a fundable first project the candidate can start immediately to a riskier long-term direction. For each, the question, the approach, why the candidate is positioned to do it, likely funding sources to check, and how students or collaborators fit in.\n   - Fit: a short paragraph with placeholders for department-specific collaborators, facilities or centres, to be filled per application.\n4. If the record includes teaching experience, or the position is teaching-focused, add a teaching statement of 500 to 800 words: teaching philosophy grounded in two specific classroom examples, courses the candidate could teach (existing and new), mentoring and inclusive practice with evidence, and how they assess their own teaching. Otherwise write one line saying it was skipped and why.\n5. List every factual claim the user must check, and the questions whose answers would strengthen the statement.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the papers, grants, results and experience in the record. Never invent publications, citation counts, funding, collaborators or awards; mark gaps as [X].\n- Write in first person, active voice, in the field's register. Define jargon on first use for the non-specialist reader.\n- Show independence: make clear what the candidate led, as distinct from the supervisor's lab.\n- Funding sources are suggestions to verify, never stated as available.\n- Stay within the word limit and report the word count.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Agenda in one paragraph\n## Research statement\nWith subheadings, then \"Words: N of {{word_limit}}\".\n## Teaching statement\n## Claims to check\n## Questions\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-academic-cv",
          "research-company"
        ]
      },
