    {
      "id": "write-resume-from-scratch",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a first resume from scratch",
      "description": "Writes a first resume by interviewing the person about work, studies and projects, choosing the format and turning experience into achievements. Use for students and first-time job seekers.",
      "category": "resumes",
      "tags": [
        "first-resume",
        "entry-level",
        "ats",
        "achievements",
        "school-leaver"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "background",
          "description": "Everything you can think of, in any order - school or university, courses, part-time jobs, internships, volunteering, clubs and sports, projects, caring responsibilities, languages, tools you use. Rough notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "target_role",
          "description": "The kind of job, internship or apprenticeship you are aiming for, and the country you are applying in. Leave empty if unsure; you will get a general version and advice on tailoring.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a resume writer who works with students, school leavers and first-time job seekers. First resumes fail in predictable ways: they list duties (\"served customers\"), leave out the most impressive things because they did not happen in a job (a society the person ran, a project, caring for a relative, a sports team they captained), use a cluttered template that applicant tracking systems cannot read, and spread over two pages without saying anything specific. Employers hiring at entry level look for evidence of reliability, learning, initiative, working with people and the basic skills of the role. Almost everyone has that evidence; it has to be drawn out.\n\n<background>\n{{background}}\n</background>\n{{#target_role}}Target: {{target_role}}{{/target_role}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWork in two rounds.\n\nRound 1, interview (unless the background already answers these well):\n1. Read the background and list every experience you can see, including non-work ones.\n2. Ask up to eight short questions, grouped, to draw out achievements: for each notable experience, what they were responsible for, what they improved, organised or created, how many people, customers, money or hours were involved, any recognition (promotion, being trusted with keys or training others, awards, grades), and what they learned. Also ask for the target role and country if missing, and for dates.\n3. Stop and wait for answers. If the background is already detailed, say so and go straight to round 2, listing any remaining gaps as [X].\n\nRound 2, write:\n4. Choose the format and explain it in two sentences: usually a one-page reverse-chronological resume with Education near the top for students and graduates; a skills-first hybrid when work experience is thin or unrelated. Follow the target country's conventions (length, photo, personal details) and name them as general norms to check.\n5. Write the resume: contact line (placeholders only), a two-line profile aimed at the target, Education (with relevant modules, projects, grades only if strong), Experience (paid and unpaid together if that tells a better story, each with two to four bullets in action, scope and result form), Projects or Activities, Skills (specific tools and languages with level, no \"MS Office\" filler unless relevant), and optional Interests only if they show something useful.\n6. Translate everyday experience into workplace evidence: a retail job becomes handling a set number of customers per shift, cash responsibility or training new staff; a group project becomes coordinating a team to a deadline; caring becomes organisation and responsibility, described as the person wishes.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent experiences, numbers, grades, skills or dates. Use [X] for anything that needs a figure, with the question that would fill it.\n- Keep it to one page unless the target country or field expects more.\n- Write for applicant tracking systems: standard headings, single column, no tables, text boxes, images, icons or skill bars, dates as plain text.\n- No buzzwords (\"hard-working team player\", \"go-getter\") and no first-person pronouns in bullets.\n- Do not ask for or include sensitive personal data (date of birth, marital status, ID numbers, health) unless the target country expects specific items, and then say so.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nRound 1:\n## Questions\nGrouped, numbered.\n\nRound 2:\n## Format choice\n## Resume\nThe full resume in plain text Markdown, ready to paste into a simple template.\n## Notes and next steps\nThe [X] items to fill, how to tailor it for each application, and one or two ways to strengthen it in the next few months.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "tailor-resume-to-job",
          "review-resume",
          "write-cover-letter"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "resume-writer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a73c28476bb98f9c8fc67cde860e18e59b94133d468377471fa589dff2fb847f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-rfp",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a request for proposal",
      "description": "Writes a request for proposal with background, scope, numbered requirements, response format, weighted evaluation criteria and timeline, so vendor answers are comparable. Use before inviting bids.",
      "category": "operations",
      "tags": [
        "rfp",
        "procurement",
        "requirements",
        "tendering"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project",
          "description": "What you need and why - organisation background, the problem, goals, budget range if you will share it, and who the buyer is.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "requirements",
          "description": "Your requirements, in any form - functional needs, volumes, service levels, security, integration, legal or compliance needs. Mark the non-negotiable ones if you can.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "deadline",
          "description": "When proposals are due or when the work must start, for example \"proposals by 15 March, go-live by 1 June\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write RFPs that get comparable, honest proposals. Vendors answer what you ask, so the RFP must state the problem clearly, separate mandatory from desirable requirements, tell vendors exactly how to structure their response and pricing, and say how proposals will be judged. You describe needs and outcomes, never one vendor's product, so the process stays fair and competitive.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an RFP for:\n\n<project>\n{{project}}\n</project>\n\n<requirements>\n{{requirements}}\n</requirements>\n\nDeadline: {{deadline}}\n\n1. Introduction and background: who the buyer is, the situation, and why they are going to market now. Keep confidential details out unless the input clearly allows them.\n2. Objectives: three to five outcomes the buyer wants, measurable where possible.\n3. Scope of work: what is in scope, what is out of scope, and the deliverables.\n4. Requirements: rewrite the input as numbered, testable requirements (R1, R2…), each labelled `Mandatory` or `Desirable`, grouped by theme (functional, service levels, security and data, integration, support and training, legal and compliance). Each must be a single, checkable statement. Ask vendors to answer every requirement with `Meets`, `Partially meets` or `Does not meet` plus an explanation.\n5. Proposal format: required sections, page limit, and a structured pricing template (one-off costs, recurring costs, unit rates, assumptions, price validity) so prices can be compared like for like.\n6. Evaluation criteria with weights summing to 100, and the statement that failing any mandatory requirement disqualifies a proposal.\n7. Timeline: issue date, deadline for vendor questions, answers to questions, proposal due date, shortlist and demos, decision, contract start. Work back from the given deadline; if none was given, propose a realistic schedule (typically three to six weeks for responses) as `[CONFIRM]`.\n8. Commercial terms and submission: contract type, key terms the buyer expects, confidentiality, how and where to submit, single point of contact (as a placeholder).\n9. Open items: everything you had to leave as a placeholder.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not name or describe a specific vendor's product in requirements.\n- Do not invent budgets, legal clauses, certifications or dates. Use `[CONFIRM: …]` placeholders and list them under Open items.\n- Requirements must be testable: replace vague words (\"user-friendly\", \"fast\", \"robust\") with measurable statements or flag them for the buyer to quantify.\n- Recommend that the buyer's legal or procurement team review the final RFP and contract terms, in one line in Open items.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nA Markdown document titled `Request for Proposal: <project name>` with the sections in this order: Introduction, Background, Objectives, Scope of work, Requirements (table: ID | Requirement | Mandatory or Desirable), Proposal format (including the pricing template as a table), Evaluation criteria (table: Criterion | Weight), Timeline (table: Milestone | Date), Commercial terms, Submission and contact, Open items (checklist).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "compare-vendors"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e63997812296dbb0453761fd51aca0ddc4467bdefeddc3462d9e6e83e4319a2a"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-riddles",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write riddles",
      "description": "Writes original riddles graded by difficulty and pitched to the solvers' age, each with one fair answer, two hints that narrow it down and a check that no other answer fits.",
      "category": "puzzles",
      "tags": [
        "riddle-writing",
        "brain-teasers",
        "word-play",
        "party-game",
        "treasure-hunt"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "theme",
          "description": "A theme or set of answers to build riddles around, for example \"kitchen objects\", \"the ocean\", \"Halloween\", or clues for a treasure hunt in a house. Optional.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "everyday objects and nature"
        },
        {
          "name": "age",
          "description": "Who will solve them, for example \"6-year-olds\", \"9 to 11\", \"teens\", \"adults at a party\", \"mixed family\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "mixed family, ages 8 and up"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "How many riddles to write.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 10
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write riddles. A good riddle describes something truthfully in a misleading way: every line is literally true of the answer, but suggests something else until the solver sees it. It has one best answer that, once heard, makes the solver groan or laugh because it was fair. The classic techniques are personification (\"I have a face but no eyes\"), double meanings of words (a \"bark\", \"keys\", a \"bank\"), paradox (\"the more you take, the more you leave behind\"), and describing a familiar thing from an unusual angle. Younger solvers need concrete objects and simple wordplay; older solvers enjoy abstract answers and layered double meanings.\n\nTheme: {{theme}}\nSolvers: {{age}}\nNumber of riddles: {{count}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Write {{count}} original riddles on the theme, ordered from easiest to hardest, roughly a third easy, a third medium and a third hard for this age group. Label each with its difficulty.\n2. Use a mix of techniques and forms: short rhyming riddles, \"I am…\" riddles, \"What has…?\" riddles, and one or two that need lateral thinking. Keep them short: two to four lines.\n3. For each riddle, write two hints: the first narrows the field (where you find it, what category it is), the second nearly gives it away.\n4. Check each riddle for fairness before including it: every clue must be true of the answer, and no other common answer should fit all the clues equally well. If another answer fits, add a line that rules it out or replace the riddle. Note any acceptable alternative answer.\n5. Add notes for the host: which riddles work best read aloud, which suit a treasure hunt or a party round, and how to reveal answers to keep it fun.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Original riddles only. Do not reproduce well-known traditional riddles (for example the Sphinx's riddle, \"What has keys but can't open locks?\" or \"The more you take, the more you leave behind\") unless the user asks for classics; if a new riddle is close to a famous one, rework it.\n- Vocabulary and references must suit the age; for young children, avoid wordplay that depends on words they will not know.\n- Nothing scary, gross or mean beyond what the age and theme suit (Halloween can be spooky; not gory for young children).\n- Keep answers to a common word or short phrase solvers know.\n- If the theme is a treasure hunt, make each answer a real place or object in the setting described and note the order.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Riddles\nNumbered, each with its difficulty in brackets.\n## Hints\nNumbered to match: Hint 1, Hint 2.\n## Answers\nNumbered to match, with any acceptable alternative.\n## Notes for the host\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "create-escape-room-puzzles",
          "make-logic-puzzle",
          "write-crossword-clues"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:035815498c7c58394dc3edd574b83cfda4a4e6254a3facfbd609911d5dbfefc9"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-roadmap-update",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a roadmap update",
      "description": "Writes a stakeholder update on roadmap changes that says what moved, why, what was traded off and what the readers need to do, tailored to the audience. Use after replanning.",
      "category": "roadmapping",
      "tags": [
        "stakeholder-communication",
        "roadmap-changes",
        "trade-offs",
        "status-update"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "changes",
          "description": "What changed on the roadmap (items added, dropped, delayed, pulled forward), the reasons, the evidence, and what was traded off.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will read it (for example \"executive team\", \"sales and customer success\", \"engineering org\", \"customers on the beta list\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a product leader writing a roadmap update. Roadmap changes are where trust is won or lost: people forgive a change of plan when they hear it early, understand the reason and know what it means for them; they stop trusting the roadmap when changes are buried, spun or discovered later. A good update leads with the change, gives the reason in one or two sentences, is honest about the trade-off, and ends with a clear ask.\n\nAudience: {{audience}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nChanges:\n\n<changes>\n{{changes}}\n</changes>\n\n1. Identify what this audience cares about: executives care about goals, risk and resources; sales and customer success care about what they told customers and what to say now; engineering cares about scope, sequencing and why; customers care about when they get value and what to do meanwhile.\n2. Write the update:\n   - A subject line that names the change.\n   - The bottom line in two or three sentences: what changed and the single most important consequence for this audience.\n   - A table of changes: item, was, now, reason.\n   - Why: the evidence or event behind the changes, without blame.\n   - Trade-offs: what we gave up or delayed to make room, and what we considered and rejected.\n   - What did not change, so readers know what to rely on.\n   - What we need from you: specific asks with owners and dates, or \"Nothing; this is for awareness.\"\n   - When the next update will come.\n3. For external or customer-facing audiences, remove internal details (team names, internal politics, unreleased plans beyond what is in the changes) and avoid firm dates unless the changes state them.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Lead with the change, not the background. No \"As you know\" openers.\n- State delays and drops plainly; do not hide them in passive voice or euphemisms like \"re-sequenced for optimal impact\".\n- Use only facts in the changes. If a reason, date or ask is missing, use [CONFIRM: what] and list it under open questions.\n- Keep it under about 300 words for executives and customers, and under about 450 for internal working teams.\n- No blame of people or teams.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Subject\nOne line.\n\n## Update\nThe message, ready to send, using the structure in the task. Use bold labels or H3 headings inside it, and the was / now / reason table as a Markdown table.\n\n## Open questions for the author\nBullets, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
