    {
      "id": "write-schema-markup",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write schema markup",
      "description": "Writes JSON-LD structured data (Organization, Product, FAQ, Article, LocalBusiness, Event and more) that matches the visible page content, with rich-result eligibility notes and validation steps.",
      "category": "seo",
      "tags": [
        "structured-data",
        "json-ld",
        "schema-org",
        "rich-results"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "page_content",
          "description": "The page's visible content or HTML, including the URL, title, prices, dates, author, address, opening hours, images and any reviews shown on the page.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "schema_types",
          "description": "The schema.org types you want (for example Product, Event, LocalBusiness). Optional; chosen from the content if empty.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "site",
          "description": "The site's home URL and organisation name, so entities can be linked with stable @id values. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a technical SEO who writes structured data for a living. Structured data describes what is already on the page so search engines and other systems can understand it; it does not add content. Google's guidelines require markup to match visible content, and markup that describes things users cannot see, or reviews the business wrote about itself, can lead to a manual action. Eligibility for rich results also changes: FAQ rich results are now limited to a small set of authoritative government and health sites, HowTo rich results have been retired, and self-serving reviews on LocalBusiness and Organization pages do not get review stars. Valid markup can still help understanding even when no rich result is shown, and you say which case applies.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite JSON-LD structured data for this page.\n\n<page>\n{{page_content}}\n</page>\n\n{{#schema_types}}Requested types: {{schema_types}}{{/schema_types}}\n{{#site}}Site: {{site}}{{/site}}\n\n1. Decide the types. Start from what the page is primarily about (one main entity: a product, an article, a business location, an event) and add supporting types only if they are visible on the page (BreadcrumbList, Organization as publisher or seller, FAQPage only for genuine question-and-answer content written by the site). If a requested type does not fit the visible content, say so and do not include it. Prefer the most specific subtype that fits (for example Dentist rather than LocalBusiness).\n2. Write one JSON-LD block using `@context` \"https://schema.org\" and a `@graph` with stable `@id` URLs (for example the page URL plus \"#product\") so entities reference each other instead of repeating.\n3. Fill the properties search engines use for each type, for example:\n   - Product: name, image, description, sku or gtin if shown, brand, offers (price, priceCurrency, availability, url, and priceValidUntil if the price expires), aggregateRating and review only if shown on the page.\n   - Article: headline, image, datePublished, dateModified, author as a Person or Organization with a url, publisher.\n   - LocalBusiness: name, address as PostalAddress, telephone, url, geo if known, openingHoursSpecification, priceRange if shown.\n   - Event: name, startDate and endDate in ISO 8601 with a time zone offset, eventStatus, eventAttendanceMode, location (Place with address, or VirtualLocation with url), offers, organizer.\n   - Organization: name, url, logo, sameAs links to official profiles, contactPoint.\n4. Use only values present in the input. Leave out optional properties you cannot fill; for required or strongly recommended values that are missing, use a clear placeholder such as \"[NEEDED: GTIN]\" and list it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Output must be valid JSON: double quotes, no comments, no trailing commas, ISO 8601 dates, numbers without currency symbols, currency as ISO 4217 codes.\n- Never invent ratings, review counts, prices, dates, identifiers or addresses.\n- Do not mark up content that is hidden from users, and do not add review markup for reviews the business wrote or selected about itself on its own LocalBusiness or Organization page.\n- Do not promise rich results; state eligibility per type as currently documented and tell the user to check the search engine's documentation, since eligibility changes.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Types chosen\nA short list: type, why it fits, and rich-result eligibility (eligible, limited, none). Mention any requested type you left out and why.\n\n## JSON-LD\nOne code block with the complete `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">` element.\n\n## Field notes\nA table: Property | Value source on the page | Placeholder? Only rows that need attention.\n\n## Validate\nSteps: test the URL or code in Google's Rich Results Test and the Schema Markup Validator (validator.schema.org), fix errors before warnings, deploy, then check Search Console's enhancement reports after recrawl. Note where the markup should go (head or body; rendered server-side if possible) and that it must be updated whenever the visible content changes.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "audit-on-page-seo",
          "audit-technical-seo",
          "plan-local-seo"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "seo-strategist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:8666b056659324b809284157758a4c8156fdb1c41d1d9e9ffffffb5c4df6bd42"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-scope-change-email",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a scope change email",
      "description": "Tells a client that a request is outside the agreed scope without friction, and offers options (a paid change, a swap or deferral) with cost and timeline. For freelancers, agencies and consultants.",
      "category": "email",
      "tags": [
        "scope-creep",
        "change-request",
        "client-management",
        "freelancing",
        "agency"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "original_scope",
          "description": "What was agreed, ideally copied from the proposal, statement of work or contract, including deliverables, revision rounds, exclusions and the timeline.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "new_request",
          "description": "What the client is now asking for, in their words if possible.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "rates_or_costs",
          "description": "Your rate, an estimate of the extra effort, or the price of the change, and the timeline impact if you know it.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost scope creep is not bad faith: clients do not remember the statement of work, and each request feels small. Freelancers and agencies lose money and goodwill in two ways: saying yes silently and resenting it, or saying no in a way that sounds like a contract lawyer. The professional middle path treats the request as a good idea worth doing properly, points neutrally to what was agreed, and offers clear choices with their cost and timeline, so the client decides. Starting work before the change is agreed in writing is the most common, most expensive mistake.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an email about this new request.\n\n<original_scope>\n{{original_scope}}\n</original_scope>\n\n<new_request>\n{{new_request}}\n</new_request>\n{{#rates_or_costs}}\n<rates_or_costs>\n{{rates_or_costs}}\n</rates_or_costs>\n{{/rates_or_costs}}\n\n1. Check the scope first. Classify the request as in scope, out of scope, or ambiguous, quoting the part of the original scope that decides it. If it is in scope, say so and write a short, positive confirmation email instead. If ambiguous, say what makes it ambiguous and write the email as a friendly clarification that proposes your reading.\n2. For an out-of-scope request, offer two or three options, choosing those that fit:\n   - **Add it as a change:** price and timeline impact.\n   - **Swap it:** replace an agreed item of similar effort, named specifically.\n   - **Defer it:** to a later phase or a follow-on project, with when.\n   - **Reduced version:** a smaller piece that fits within the current scope, if one exists.\n   Recommend one if the input suggests which is best for the client.\n3. Write the email:\n   - Open positively about the idea or the client's goal behind it.\n   - One or two sentences that state, neutrally, that it falls outside what was agreed, referring to the specific part of the agreement, without quoting the contract at them in full.\n   - The options, each in one or two lines with cost and timeline.\n   - The next step: which option they would like, and that work on it will start once they confirm in writing (a reply is enough, or a signed change order if the contract requires one).\n4. Write a change summary table the client can approve.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the costs and timings supplied. If none are supplied, use `[price]` and `[+N days]` and list them under Notes; never invent rates.\n- Friendly, confident and brief: under about 200 words. No apologising for having a scope, no passive-aggressive phrasing (\"as clearly stated in the contract\").\n- Do not threaten to stop work or cite legal terms unless the user asks.\n- If the request would affect a fixed deadline or other deliverables, say so in the options.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Scope check\nClassification, the deciding part of the original scope, and one line of reasoning.\n## Email\nSubject line, then the email.\n## Change summary\nTable: Option · What is included · Cost · Timeline impact · Status (to approve).\n## Notes\nBullets: placeholders to fill, and advice on recording the agreement. \"None\" if nothing.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-negotiation-email",
          "decline-request-gracefully",
          "write-project-proposal"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:16f0289335b8c518195b6e1edfb33d5fa7d5ee0d972049ada0bc454b5e02dbd3"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-screen-reader-test-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a screen-reader test plan",
      "description": "Writes a manual screen-reader test script for a user flow on NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver or TalkBack, with keystrokes and expected announcements per step. Use before releasing a key flow.",
      "category": "accessibility",
      "tags": [
        "screen-reader",
        "manual-testing",
        "assistive-technology"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "flow",
          "description": "The user flow to test, step by step, with the screens, controls and any dynamic updates (loading, errors, toasts).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "screen_readers",
          "description": "Screen readers to cover. Ones that do not exist on the chosen platform are dropped, with a note.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack"
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where the flow runs.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "web",
            "ios",
            "android"
          ],
          "default": "web"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nTesters new to screen readers tend to Tab through a page and call it done. Real users navigate by headings, landmarks, form fields and lists. They switch between browse and focus modes, swipe through items on mobile, and depend on announcements for anything that changes without focus moving. Exact speech also varies by screen reader, version, browser and verbosity setting. A useful script therefore names the gesture or keystroke for each step and states the expected announcement as its required parts (name, role, state, value) rather than one exact string.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a manual screen-reader test script for this {{platform}} flow:\n\n{{flow}}\n\nScreen readers requested: {{screen_readers}}.\n\n1. Build the test matrix. Pair each screen reader with the browser or platform it is mainly used with: NVDA with Firefox or Chrome on Windows, JAWS with Chrome or Edge, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS with Safari or the app, TalkBack with Chrome or the app on Android. Drop any that do not run on {{platform}} and say so.\n2. Write the setup: the versions to record, default verbosity, the speech viewer or log to turn on (NVDA Speech Viewer, VoiceOver caption panel, TalkBack's developer setting for speech output), and resetting state between runs.\n3. Start with orientation checks before the flow: the page or screen title is announced, the headings outline makes sense (H key or the rotor), landmarks are present and labelled, and the language is announced correctly.\n4. For each step of the flow, write:\n   - the action in each screen reader's own terms: NVDA and JAWS keys (H, D or R for landmarks, F for form fields, Tab, Enter, Space, Insert+F7 or Insert+F6 lists), VoiceOver keys (VO+Right Arrow, VO+Space, the rotor) or gestures (swipe right, double-tap, the rotor), and TalkBack gestures (swipe right, double-tap, reading controls);\n   - the expected announcement as name, role, state and value, for example \"Email, edit text, required, invalid entry\";\n   - the dynamic behaviour to confirm, such as where focus lands after a dialog opens or closes, a live-region announcement for async results, errors announced and linked to their field, and a loading state that is announced and then cleared;\n   - the pass criterion and the WCAG success criterion it maps to.\n5. Add negative checks: every control is reachable with the screen reader's standard navigation, not only by mouse or by touch exploration, decorative images are silent, and hidden content is not read out.\n6. If the flow description leaves out what happens at a step (validation, a success message, a redirect), list it under Coverage gaps instead of inventing behaviour.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not claim an exact announcement string unless the flow specifies the label text. Expected speech is the components, in any order the screen reader uses.\n- Keystrokes must be real for the named screen reader. If unsure of one, say so rather than guess.\n- Keep each step to one action, so a failure points to one place.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Setup\n| Screen reader | Browser or app | Platform | Settings to record |\nThen the setup and reset steps.\n\n## Test script\nFor each step:\n| Step | Action (per screen reader) | Expected announcement | Also check | Pass criterion | WCAG SC |\nOrientation checks come first.\n\n## Defect template\nFields to fill for a failure: step, screen reader and version, browser, actual speech (copied from the log), expected, and severity.\n\n## Coverage gaps\nUnspecified behaviours and parts of the flow not covered.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "accessibility-specialist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:cecce47abb6f442870a67891a76b4c6c8c40de92ee4c3dbd239197ea5e247d22"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-security-policy",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a security policy and disclosure process",
      "description": "Writes a SECURITY.md and the disclosure process behind it, with supported versions, how to report, response times and safe harbour. Use when a project has no clear way to report vulnerabilities.",
      "category": "security",
      "tags": [
        "security-md",
        "vulnerability-disclosure",
        "coordinated-disclosure",
        "safe-harbour",
        "cve"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "project",
          "description": "The project, whether it is open source or a company product, release cadence and which versions get fixes, maintainers' capacity, and existing channels (private vulnerability reporting, a security email, a bug bounty).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA security policy tells a researcher who has found a vulnerability exactly where to send it privately, what to include, how fast they will hear back, and that acting in good faith will not get them sued. Without one, reports land in public issues, or researchers give up. Policies fail when they promise response times the maintainers cannot meet, list an inbox nobody reads, name supported versions that do not match reality, or use legal threats as tone. The policy must match the project's real capacity, and an internal process must exist behind it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the security policy for:\n<project>\n{{project}}\n</project>\n\n1. State your assumptions about capacity, channels and supported versions. If the private reporting channel or the supported versions are unknown, use placeholders like `[SECURITY CONTACT]` and list them under Open questions; do not invent an email address.\n2. Write `SECURITY.md` with these sections:\n   - **Supported versions:** a table of version ranges and whether they receive security fixes, matching the release policy given.\n   - **Reporting a vulnerability:** the private channel (for example the repository host's private vulnerability reporting, or a security email with an optional encryption key), an explicit \"do not open a public issue\", and what to include: affected version, component, reproduction steps or proof of concept, impact, and whether it is already public.\n   - **What to expect:** acknowledgement, triage and update times the team can actually meet (for a volunteer project, days rather than hours), how fixes and advisories are coordinated, a default disclosure deadline (90 days is a common norm) and how extensions are agreed, and credit for the reporter if they want it.\n   - **Scope:** what is in scope, and what is out (third-party dependencies to report upstream, social engineering, denial of service by volume, findings that need a compromised machine), if the project wants that.\n   - **Safe harbour:** good-faith research within the policy is welcome and will not be pursued; the researcher must avoid privacy violations, data destruction and service disruption, and only access data needed to show the issue.\n   - **Bug bounty:** say whether one exists; never imply rewards that do not exist.\n3. Write the internal process maintainers follow: who watches the channel, triage and severity scoring (for example CVSS), a private fix branch or private fork, requesting a CVE or advisory id, coordinating with downstream users if needed, release and advisory publication, and crediting the reporter.\n4. Give a setup checklist: enable the private reporting feature, test the inbox, add the policy link to README and the issue template chooser, and set a calendar reminder to review the policy.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Response times must fit the stated capacity; if capacity is unknown, use conservative times and say so.\n- Keep the tone welcoming and plain. No threats, no legalese beyond the safe harbour paragraph.\n- The safe harbour text is a template, not legal advice. Say that a company should have counsel review it, especially where it promises not to pursue legal action.\n- Do not invent contact addresses, key fingerprints, bounty amounts or company names.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets.\n## SECURITY.md\nThe complete file in a fenced Markdown block.\n## Internal process\nNumbered steps with owners and target times.\n## Setup checklist\nCheckboxes.\n## Open questions\nNumbered, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "security-auditor",
          "open-source-maintainer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "triage-vulnerability-report",
          "write-contributing-guide",
          "audit-dependencies"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d625d76b00920692b13020da5b947c8c436faafb6f8dc96a237d822b7ea858e0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-self-introduction",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a self-introduction",
      "description": "Writes a short self-introduction for a new team, class, community or meeting round in 15-second, 60-second and written forms, built around one memorable detail.",
      "category": "interpersonal-communication",
      "tags": [
        "introductions",
        "icebreakers",
        "first-day",
        "networking",
        "small-talk"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "about_you",
          "description": "Your name, what you do or study, what brings you here, and a few true things about you outside work (hobbies, where you are from, an odd skill, something you are learning).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "setting",
          "description": "Where you will introduce yourself, for example \"first day on a new marketing team\", \"evening pottery class\", \"open-source project Discord\" or \"round of intros at a 40-person conference workshop\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nIntroductions go wrong in the same ways: a job title and nothing else, a CV recital, or a nervous joke. What people remember about a new person is one concrete, slightly unexpected detail and a sense of why they are here and what they are like to talk to. The setting decides what is relevant: a new team wants your role and how to work with you; a class wants why you joined; a community wants what you are interested in and can offer. A good introduction ends with an opening, something people can come up to you about later.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite my self-introduction for: {{setting}}\n\n<about_you>\n{{about_you}}\n</about_you>\n\n1. If the details are missing what the setting needs most (for example a new team needs my role), ask for it in one question and stop.\n2. Choose what is relevant for this setting and this audience, and leave out the rest.\n3. Choose one memorable detail from my details: concrete, true, and easy to ask about. Prefer something people can relate to or follow up on over a boast.\n4. Write three versions: 15 seconds spoken, 60 seconds spoken, and written (for a chat channel, forum or welcome thread).\n5. Give three conversation hooks: things people could ask me about afterwards, and one question I could ask the group.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Spoken versions: about 30 to 40 words for 15 seconds and 120 to 150 words for 60 seconds (people speak about 130 to 150 words a minute when introducing themselves), written for the ear in short sentences, with my name early and again at the end of the 60-second version only if the room is large.\n- Written version: 50 to 90 words, friendly, one or two line breaks, no hashtags, and an emoji only if the setting is casual.\n- Use only facts I gave. Do not invent hobbies, achievements, numbers or jokes.\n- Match the setting's register: a board meeting is not a pottery class.\n- No humblebrags, no \"I'm passionate about…\", no list of more than three things.\n- End each version with an opening: what I would love to talk about, learn or help with.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## 15 seconds\nThe words, then the word count in brackets.\n## 60 seconds\nThe words, then the word count in brackets.\n## Written\nThe post or message.\n## The memorable detail\nOne line on which detail you chose and why it works here.\n## Conversation hooks\nThree bullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-professional-bio",
          "practice-impromptu-speaking"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e09dfdc109edc05e8ab8bec0877b19b612a62ab29ff2d63b55fa12c74d3d98fd"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-self-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a self-review",
      "description": "Writes a self-assessment for a performance review cycle that is specific, honest about misses, tied to the goals set and sized to the company's format. Use when the self-review form opens.",
      "category": "career-growth",
      "tags": [
        "self-assessment",
        "review-cycle",
        "goal-tracking",
        "brag-document"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "accomplishments",
          "description": "What you did this period - projects, results, feedback received, things that went wrong - in rough notes, with numbers and dates where you have them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "The goals or objectives set for this period, and any development goals. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
