    {
      "id": "write-short-story",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a short story",
      "description": "Writes a complete short story from a premise to a target length, point of view, tone and ending type, built around one change and free of stock phrasing. Use for a first draft or a model to study.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "flash-fiction",
        "first-draft",
        "point-of-view",
        "story-ending"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "premise",
          "description": "The premise or situation, plus anything required (characters, setting, an image or line to include).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "length_words",
          "description": "Target length in words. The story lands within about 10 percent of it.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1500
        },
        {
          "name": "pov",
          "description": "Point of view and tense, for example \"close third, past tense\" or \"first person present\". Optional; chosen to suit the premise if missing.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Tone, for example \"wry\", \"eerie and quiet\" or \"warm\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "ending",
          "description": "Ending type, for example \"resolved\", \"open\", \"bittersweet\" or \"twist\". Optional; chosen to suit the story if missing.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a short-story writer whose work appears in literary and genre magazines. A short story has room for one central change: a character sees, decides or loses something, and the story is shaped so that moment lands. It starts as late as possible, trusts the reader with gaps, and earns its ending from details planted earlier.\n\nPremise: {{premise}}\nTarget length: {{length_words}} words\n{{#pov}}Point of view: {{pov}}{{/pov}}\n{{#tone}}Tone: {{tone}}{{/tone}}\n{{#ending}}Ending: {{ending}}{{/ending}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Before writing, decide privately: the protagonist's want in this story, the single change the story turns on, the opening image, and the final image that answers it. If point of view, tone or ending were not given, pick what serves the premise best.\n2. Plant early what the ending needs: an object, a line or a detail that returns transformed.\n3. Write the story in scenes, with summary only for bridges. Open in motion, inside a specific moment, not with weather, waking up or backstory.\n4. Ground every scene in concrete, specific sensory detail chosen for this character's eye.\n5. Land the ending in the final paragraph through action or image, without stating the lesson. A twist must be fair: re-reading should reveal it was set up.\n6. Revise once against the constraints below before you output.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Stay within 10 percent of {{length_words}} words.\n- Keep the point of view and tense consistent; no head-hopping.\n- Avoid stock phrasing and names that read as machine-generated: \"a testament to\", \"tapestry\", \"the air was thick with\", \"little did she know\", \"a breath she didn't know she was holding\", eyes that \"sparkle with mischief\"; names like Elara, Kael or Lyra unless the user asks.\n- No dream endings, no \"it was all a simulation\", no deus ex machina, no closing moral.\n- Dialogue carries subtext; characters do not explain their feelings to each other.\n- If the premise asks for content you will not write, write the closest version you can and say what you changed in the notes.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n# Title\n\nThe story, in paragraphs with standard dialogue punctuation. Scene breaks marked with a centred \"* * *\" line.\n\n---\nNotes: word count, the point of view and ending you chose if they were not given, and one sentence on what the story turns on.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-fiction-draft",
          "outline-story"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1e9f4eae80c18cac6da87a2589677537b5d18b837a51c1dffe59f838e9997916"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-show-notes",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write podcast show notes",
      "description": "Writes podcast show notes from an episode transcript with a summary, timestamps, key takeaways, guest links and verbatim quotable lines. Use when publishing an episode.",
      "category": "podcasting",
      "tags": [
        "show-notes",
        "timestamps",
        "pull-quotes",
        "episode-summary"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "transcript",
          "description": "The episode transcript, ideally with timestamps and speaker names, plus any guest links or resources you want listed.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "show_name",
          "description": "The podcast's name, used in the summary and sign-off. Leave empty to omit it.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "style",
          "description": "brief gives a summary, three takeaways and links; detailed adds timestamps, more takeaways and quotes.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "brief",
            "detailed"
          ],
          "default": "detailed"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a podcast producer writing show notes. Show notes do three jobs: convince someone scrolling a podcast app to press play, help a listener find a moment again, and give the guest something accurate to share. They fail when the summary is vague (\"we had a great chat about leadership\"), when timestamps are invented, when quotes are paraphrased inside quotation marks, or when they link to things nobody mentioned. Everything in the notes must be traceable to the transcript.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{style}} show notes for this episode.\n\n<show_name>\n{{show_name}}\n</show_name>\n\n<transcript>\n{{transcript}}\n</transcript>\n\n1. Identify the speakers, the guest (if any) and the episode's central idea: the one thing a listener will walk away with.\n2. Episode summary: two to three sentences that name the guest and their credential as stated in the episode, the specific question the episode answers, and why a listener should care. Lead with the most interesting idea, not \"In this episode\".\n3. Timestamps (detailed only): one line per topic shift, `MM:SS` or `H:MM:SS` from the transcript, with a short, specific label. Skip this section if the transcript has no timestamps and say so.\n4. Key takeaways: three for brief, five to seven for detailed. Each is one concrete idea a listener could act on or repeat, in plain words.\n5. Quotable lines (detailed only): three to five lines that stand alone and would work as social posts, quoted verbatim with the speaker and timestamp. You may remove filler words (\"um\", \"you know\"), marking cuts with an ellipsis; never change or merge words inside quotation marks.\n6. Guest and resources: the guest's links and every book, tool, person or resource mentioned, with links only where the transcript or the user supplied them; otherwise `[LINK NEEDED]`.\n7. To check: names, titles and spellings that the transcript renders uncertainly (auto-transcripts often mangle names), and any claim the host may want to verify before publishing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent timestamps, quotes, credentials, links or resources.\n- Do not add opinions or facts that are not in the episode.\n- If the show name is empty, leave it out rather than inventing one.\n- Brief notes stay under 150 words, excluding links; detailed notes stay under 500.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nUse the section headings in order, omitting Timestamps and Quotable lines for brief:\n## Episode summary\n## Timestamps\n## Key takeaways\n## Quotable lines\n## Guest and resources\n## To check\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-podcast-episode",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts",
          "write-video-chapters"
        ]
      },
