    },
    {
      "id": "write-talk-openings-and-closings",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write talk openings and closings",
      "description": "Writes alternative openings (story, question, surprising fact, demo) and closings (call to action, callback, challenge) for a talk, each labelled with when it works best.",
      "category": "presentations",
      "tags": [
        "opening-line",
        "hook",
        "call-to-action",
        "callback",
        "storytelling"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "talk_summary",
          "description": "What the talk is about, its main message, and any stories, data, demos or examples you have that could be used.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will be listening, what they know and care about, and the setting (for example \"200 product managers at a conference, after lunch\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What you want the audience to think, feel or do after the talk.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a speechwriter and speaking coach. The first 30 seconds decide whether an audience leans in, and the last 30 seconds decide what they remember and do. Most speakers waste both: they open with thanks, an agenda or \"a bit about me\", and close with \"so, that's it, any questions?\". Strong openings create a question in the listener's mind that the talk then answers; strong closings return to that question, state the message once more and tell people exactly what to do. The right choice depends on the audience, the room and the speaker's comfort: a risky joke or a live demo can fail, a quiet story can work anywhere.\n\n<talk_summary>\n{{talk_summary}}\n</talk_summary>\n\nAudience and setting: {{audience}}\nGoal: {{goal}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Write the line the whole talk must land: the one sentence the audience should repeat afterwards. If the summary is too vague to find one (no message, no material), ask for the main point and one story or fact in one message and stop.\n2. Write four openings, one of each type. Each is the actual words to say, 40 to 90 words, ready to rehearse:\n   - **Story:** a specific moment with a person, place and tension, taken from the material supplied.\n   - **Question:** a question the audience genuinely wonders about, or one they can answer silently or by a show of hands.\n   - **Surprising fact:** a figure or finding from the material that challenges what this audience assumes.\n   - **Demo or show:** something they see or do in the first minute (an object, a live result, a before-and-after).\n3. Write three closings, the actual words, 40 to 90 words each:\n   - **Call to action:** one specific, doable next step tied to the goal, with when and how.\n   - **Callback:** returns to an image, question or story from an opening and resolves it.\n   - **Challenge:** asks the audience to think or act differently, framed as an invitation.\n4. Label each option with: when it works best (audience, setting, speaker style), the risk, and which opening it pairs with.\n5. Recommend one opening and one closing for this audience and goal, in two or three sentences, and give the transition sentence from the opening into the first point of the talk.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts, figures, stories and demos that appear in the summary. If an opening type needs material that was not supplied, write it with a clearly marked placeholder (`[YOUR STORY: a time a customer…]`, `[FIGURE NEEDED]`) and say what to find.\n- No opening that starts with thanks, an agenda, an apology or \"Today I'm going to talk about…\". Those can come after the hook.\n- No jokes at the audience's or anyone else's expense; humour only if it fits the setting and is low risk.\n- Write for the ear: short sentences, concrete words, one idea per sentence, natural pauses marked with a line break.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The line to land\nOne sentence.\n\n## Openings\nFor each of the four: a heading with the type, the script, then \"Works best when:\", \"Risk:\" and \"Pairs with:\".\n\n## Closings\nFor each of the three: the same format.\n\n## Recommended pair\nThe choice and why, plus the transition sentence into the body.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "outline-presentation",
          "write-speech",
          "craft-personal-story"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "speaking-coach",
          "speechwriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:72d88b7ec21121b52ae06b3d44396293c0ad61ba258cad0702e11ceed2e39f1f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-task-prompt",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a reusable task prompt",
      "description": "Writes a reusable prompt from a plain description of a task, with context, typed variables and defaults, constraints, an output format, an example and a rule to ask for missing inputs.",
      "category": "prompt-engineering",
      "tags": [
        "prompt-design",
        "prompt-templates",
        "variables",
        "reusable-prompts"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "task_description",
          "description": "What the prompt should do, in plain words - the input it gets, what it should produce, who uses the result, and anything that usually goes wrong when you ask an AI for this.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "example_input_output",
          "description": "Optional: one real input and the output you would call good. This is the strongest signal of what you want.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "target_user",
          "description": "Optional: who will run the prompt, for example \"me, weekly\", \"our support team, non-technical\" or \"an automation, no human in the loop\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "the person who described the task"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA reusable prompt is a small program: it is run many times, by people who did not write it, on inputs the author did not foresee. Current guidance from the major model providers converges on the same structure: give the context and purpose (who it is for and why), state the task as an explicit deliverable, separate variable inputs from instructions with clear delimiters, phrase constraints as what to do and why, define the output format exactly, add an example when the format or tone is hard to describe, and tell the model what to do when information is missing instead of letting it guess. A role line helps only when it carries real expertise or a stance; \"You are a helpful assistant\" adds nothing.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a reusable prompt for this task, to be run by {{target_user}}.\n\n<task_description>\n{{task_description}}\n</task_description>\n{{#example_input_output}}\n<example_input_output>\n{{example_input_output}}\n</example_input_output>\n{{/example_input_output}}\n\n1. Restate the job in one sentence: input, deliverable, audience, and what \"good\" means. If the description leaves the deliverable or its audience unclear in a way that would change the prompt, ask up to three questions and stop.\n2. Identify the variables: everything that changes between runs. For each, choose a name (snake_case), a type (string, text, enum, number or boolean), whether it is required, and a sensible default for optional ones. Keep the list short; fold rarely changed settings into the prompt.\n3. Write the prompt in this order:\n   - context: purpose, audience and the domain knowledge the model needs, including what usually goes wrong;\n   - the task, with each variable as a placeholder (the variable name in double curly braces) inside its own delimiters or XML-style tag;\n   - numbered steps only where order matters;\n   - constraints, each phrased positively with its reason when not obvious;\n   - a rule for missing or ambiguous input: ask, or proceed with stated assumptions, whichever suits the target user;\n   - the exact output format (sections, length, structure);\n   - one example if the format or tone is subtle, based on the example given or clearly marked as illustrative.\n4. Add design notes explaining the non-obvious choices, and three test inputs, including an edge case and an input that should trigger the missing-information rule.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Model-agnostic: plain Markdown and tags any assistant understands; no vendor-specific syntax or model names unless the description requires a specific tool.\n- No filler roles, flattery or shouting (ALL CAPS, \"CRITICAL\", \"NEVER EVER\"); they cause over-application rather than compliance.\n- Keep the prompt as short as complete allows, usually under 600 words.\n- Do not add features, steps or outputs the task did not ask for; put optional ideas in the design notes.\n- If the target user is an automation, make the output strictly parseable (for example a fixed JSON shape) and replace \"ask\" with a defined fallback value.\n- If the task involves medical, legal, financial or mental-health advice, include a line in the prompt that states its limits and points to a qualified professional when stakes are high.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Prompt\nThe complete prompt in one fenced block, ready to paste.\n## Variables\nA table: Name | Type | Required | Default | Description.\n## Design notes\nThree to six bullets.\n## Try it with\nThree test inputs and what a good output should do for each.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "improve-prompt",
          "turn-chat-into-prompt",
          "build-prompt-test-set",
          "create-few-shot-examples"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "prompt-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e07800b078a9a8da7ba1832cbe72da9ded7a319f4de7a8e38c99e3bbb048ba89"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-team-charter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a team charter",
      "description": "Writes a team charter with purpose, scope, roles, working agreements, decision rights, communication norms and conflict handling, plus a session to agree it. Use when forming or resetting a team.",
      "category": "people-management",
      "tags": [
        "team-charter",
        "working-agreements",
        "decision-rights",
        "raci",
        "team-norms"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "team_context",
          "description": "The team (members' roles, locations and time zones), why it exists, who its customers and stakeholders are, what it owns and what it does not, how it works today, and known friction (slow decisions, unclear ownership, meeting overload).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an organisational effectiveness consultant who helps teams set up how they work. A charter is useful when it settles the questions that otherwise cause friction: what the team is for, what it owns, who decides what, how work flows in and out, how people communicate, and what happens when they disagree. It fails when it is a list of values nobody can act on, when the manager writes it alone and announces it, or when it is never revisited. The best charters are short, specific, written in the team's language, and agreed in a session where the contentious points are actually decided.\n\n<team_context>\n{{team_context}}\n</team_context>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Draft the charter, at most two pages:\n   - Purpose: one or two sentences on why the team exists and who benefits.\n   - Scope: what the team owns, what it explicitly does not own, and interfaces with other teams.\n   - Goals and measures: two to four outcomes the team will be judged on, with how they are measured.\n   - Roles and responsibilities: each role's main accountabilities, avoiding overlap and gaps.\n   - Decision rights: a table of recurring decision types (priorities, technical or design choices, hiring, budget, process changes) with who decides, who is consulted and who is informed, and the default decision method (decider after consultation, consensus, or vote) and when to escalate.\n   - Working agreements: six to ten specific, testable norms (core hours across time zones, response-time expectations by channel, meeting-free time, how work is requested and prioritised, definition of done, how feedback is given).\n   - Communication: which channel for what, meeting cadence and purpose for each, where decisions are recorded.\n   - Conflict: steps from direct conversation, to a facilitated conversation, to escalation, with expected timeframes, and a commitment to disagree on ideas without attacking people.\n   - Review: when the charter is revisited.\n2. Mark every item the team must decide together, rather than the manager alone, with [team to decide] and give two options for each.\n3. Design a 90-minute charter session to agree it: pre-reading, agenda with timings, how to surface disagreement (silent writing, then discussion), how to decide each open item, and how to capture the final version.\n4. Keeping it alive: how the charter is used in onboarding, retrospectives and when friction appears, and the signals it needs updating.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the input; mark gaps as [X].\n- Every working agreement must be specific enough that a team member could tell whether it was kept (\"reply to direct messages within one working day\", not \"communicate openly\").\n- Address the friction named in the context directly in the decision rights or working agreements.\n- Plain language; no buzzwords or value statements that cannot be acted on.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Team charter\nThe charter with the headings above; decision rights as a table: Decision | Decides | Consulted | Informed | Method.\n## Decisions for the team\nTable: Item | Option A | Option B.\n## Charter session\n## Keeping it alive\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-transition-to-manager",
          "plan-one-on-one"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "leadership-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:970ee33e5156ea9da6b227ad5bab8e5bdb52f761b613fe924c8b707830fa24bf"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-team-newsletter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an internal team newsletter",
      "description": "Writes an internal team or company newsletter from updates, wins and dates, with a lead story chosen for readers, short sections and recognition that names specific contributions.",
      "category": "business-writing",
      "tags": [
        "internal-comms",
        "company-update",
        "recognition",
        "shout-outs"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "updates",
          "description": "Raw material for this issue, such as project news, wins, people news, upcoming dates, asks and anything people should know. Paste notes, Slack messages or bullets.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who receives it, for example \"the 12-person design team\" or \"the whole company across three offices\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "whole company"
        },
        {
          "name": "length",
          "description": "Short is a two-minute read (up to about 350 words); standard is up to about 700 words.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "short",
            "standard"
          ],
          "default": "short"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nInternal newsletters compete with every other message in the inbox and usually lose. The ones people read lead with the item that matters most to the reader (not to the most senior person), keep each item short with a link for more, and make recognition specific enough that the person recognised feels seen and others learn what good looks like. \"Thanks to the ops team for their hard work\" is noise; \"Priya rebuilt the returns process so refunds now take two days instead of nine\" is news. Newsletters also go wrong by leaking things not yet announced, praising only the visible roles, or burying the one action people need to take.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{length}} newsletter for {{audience}} from these updates:\n\n<updates>\n{{updates}}\n</updates>\n\n1. If the updates are too thin to fill even a short issue (fewer than two real items), say what is missing and suggest the kinds of items to gather, then stop.\n2. Choose the lead story by impact on the readers: what changes their work, what they will be asked about, or a win that affects many of them. Explain the choice in one line under Check before sending.\n3. Write the lead in three to five sentences: what happened, why it matters to the reader, and what happens next or where to learn more.\n4. Group the rest into short sections, using only those that have content: Updates · Wins and shout-outs · People news · Coming up (dates in date order) · One ask (the single action readers should take, if any).\n5. For each shout-out, name who did what and the effect, using only details in the updates. If the updates thank someone without saying what they did, write `[add: what they did and the effect]` rather than generic praise.\n6. Write three subject line options: specific, under 60 characters, no clickbait.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts in the updates; never invent numbers, names, quotes or dates.\n- Short: up to about 350 words for short, about 700 for standard. Each item two to four sentences.\n- Warm and plain, like a well-written note from a colleague. No \"exciting times ahead\", no exclamation marks in every line.\n- Flag anything that looks confidential or not yet announced (unreleased financials, unannounced departures or hires, customer names under NDA, personal health or family details) instead of publishing it.\n- If recognition clusters on one team or role, note the imbalance under Check before sending.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Subject lines\nThree numbered options.\n## Newsletter\nThe issue with a short title, the lead story, then the sections in order.\n## Check before sending\nBullets: why this lead, items flagged as possibly confidential, `[add: …]` placeholders to fill, recognition balance, and links to add. \"Nothing to check\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-internal-announcement",
          "write-status-report"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:74b70e9b6484448f4158878ca8d0ae9cba5a578820dab02a7a156b4a1582cc2d"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-tech-blog-post",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a technical blog post",
      "description": "Turns engineering notes, code and results into a technical blog post with one clear takeaway, real numbers and working code, and no hype. Use for engineering blogs and write-ups of a project.",
      "category": "writing",
      "tags": [
        "engineering-blog"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "What the post is about, in one line.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "The source material, such as notes, code, measurements, incident timelines or a PR description. The post uses only facts from here.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads the blog and what they already know.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "working software engineers who do not know this codebase"
        },
        {
          "name": "words",
          "description": "Target length in words.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1200
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEngineers read technical posts to learn something they can use: a technique, a trade-off, a mistake to avoid. They leave at the first sign of marketing or vagueness, and they distrust numbers without a method. The best posts follow one concrete problem from symptom to solution, show the dead ends honestly and end with a takeaway the reader can apply elsewhere.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a post about: {{topic}}\nFor: {{audience}}. Target length: about {{words}} words.\n\n<notes>\n{{notes}}\n</notes>\n\n1. Decide the one takeaway a reader should leave with, in one sentence. Every section must serve it; cut material that does not.\n2. Open with the concrete problem or surprising result in the first two sentences: a symptom, a number, a failure. No scene-setting about the industry.\n3. Give only the context needed to follow along.\n4. Walk through what was tried, in order, including what did not work and why. Show code or config where it carries the explanation, trimmed to the lines that matter.\n5. Present the result with the numbers from the notes and how they were measured.\n6. Name the trade-offs and when this approach is the wrong choice.\n7. Close with the takeaway, phrased so it applies beyond this codebase.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts, numbers, quotes and code from the notes. If a claim needs a figure that is not there, write `[needs number: ...]` instead of estimating.\n- Code must be consistent with the notes and minimal; do not invent APIs or library features.\n- No hype or filler: avoid \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"game-changer\", \"seamless\", \"unlock\", \"delve\", \"robust\" and rhetorical questions as openers.\n- Use \"we\" for the team's work and \"you\" for the reader. Short paragraphs; descriptive subheadings.\n- Do not name customers, colleagues or internal systems unless the notes say they can be named.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Titles\nThree title options: one plain and descriptive, one leading with the result, one leading with the problem. No clickbait.\n## Post\nThe full post in Markdown with subheadings.\n## Facts to verify\nEvery number, quote and factual claim in the post, each with where it came from in the notes, plus any `[needs number]` gaps.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "rewrite-for-clarity"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c94111e732085e053001d190aec85cb3ab64772a69bbc58c0230275444bcd1f5"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-tech-debt-proposal",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a tech debt proposal",
      "description": "Turns a piece of technical debt into a business case with evidence, cost of delay, options, the smallest valuable paydown and success measures. Use when you need product or leadership buy-in.",
      "category": "planning",
      "tags": [
        "tech-debt",
        "business-case",
        "cost-of-delay",
        "refactoring-budget",
        "stakeholder-buy-in"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "debt",
          "description": "The debt in plain words, where it lives and how it got there, for example \"orders module has no tests and three copies of the pricing logic\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "evidence",
          "description": "Concrete evidence of its cost, such as incidents, bug counts, lead times, on-call pages, estimates that slipped, support tickets or developer survey results.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who must say yes. product weighs roadmap trade-offs, leadership weighs risk and money, team weighs effort and sequencing.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "product",
            "leadership",
            "team"
          ],
          "default": "product"
        },
        {
          "name": "capacity",
          "description": "Capacity you could realistically ask for, for example \"one engineer for a sprint\" or \"20% of the team for a quarter\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nTech debt proposals usually fail for the same reasons: they describe the code instead of the consequences, ask for a big rewrite with no end date, rely on adjectives (\"fragile\", \"a mess\") instead of numbers, and leave the decision-maker unable to compare the request with feature work. A proposal that wins treats debt like any other investment: what it costs us now, what it will cost if we wait, the smallest piece of work that pays back first, and how everyone will know it worked.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a proposal to pay down this debt, aimed at a {{audience}} audience.\n\n<debt>\n{{debt}}\n</debt>\n\n<evidence>\n{{evidence}}\n</evidence>\n\n{{#capacity}}\nCapacity available to ask for: {{capacity}}\n{{/capacity}}\n\n1. Translate the debt into consequences the audience already cares about: slower delivery of named roadmap items, incidents and their customer impact, security or compliance exposure, on-call load and attrition risk, or infrastructure cost. Keep only consequences the evidence supports.\n2. Quantify with the evidence given. Show the arithmetic (for example \"6 incidents in 2 quarters × about 4 engineer-hours each\"). Where a number is an estimate, say so and give a range. If the evidence is too thin to make the case, say what to measure first and how, and draft the proposal with clearly marked placeholders.\n3. Explain the cost of delay: what gets worse each month the debt stays (a growing workaround, an end-of-life date, a hiring plan that doubles the people touching this code) and any deadline that makes now cheaper than later.\n4. Give two to four options, always including \"do nothing\" and an incremental option. For each: scope, effort as a range, what it unlocks, risk and reversibility.\n5. Recommend the smallest valuable paydown: a first slice that fits the available capacity, delivers a measurable benefit on its own and can stop cleanly. Prefer tying it to an upcoming feature that touches the same code over a standalone project.\n6. Define success measures with a baseline, a target and a review date, using measures the audience trusts (lead time for changes in this area, change failure rate, incident count, time to onboard, cloud cost).\n7. Tune for the audience: product wants the roadmap trade-off and the date impact; leadership wants risk, money and a one-paragraph decision; the team wants scope, sequencing, ownership and how the work coexists with feature work.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent incidents, metrics, costs or quotes. Every figure comes from the evidence, is shown as arithmetic on it, or is marked as an estimate or placeholder.\n- No jargon the audience would not use. Explain any technical term in a few words the first time.\n- Do not ask for an open-ended rewrite. Every option has a defined end and a way to stop early.\n- Keep the whole proposal readable in five minutes: about 600 words, plus tables.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The ask\nTwo or three sentences: what you want approved, how much capacity, for how long, and the decision date.\n## Problem\nThe consequences in the audience's terms.\n## Evidence\nBullets, each with its source.\n## Cost of delay\n## Options\nTable: option, scope, effort range, benefit, risk, reversible.\n## Recommended first step\nWhat, who, how long, what it unlocks, and the stop point.\n## How we will measure success\nTable: measure, baseline, target, review date.\n## Risks and open questions\nNumbered.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "staff-engineer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "explain-tech-to-executives",
          "plan-large-refactor",
          "compare-design-options"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f249532993f743ef659b5982654da97a887314b350f1df37dc23fb1034f1ad98"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-technical-roadmap",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a technical roadmap",
      "description": "Writes an engineering roadmap from goals and known tech debt, with themes, sequencing, dependencies, capacity assumptions and what is deliberately left out. Use for quarterly or half-year planning.",
      "category": "planning",
      "tags": [
        "engineering-roadmap",
        "tech-debt",
        "sequencing",
        "capacity-planning",
        "trade-offs"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "Business and product goals for the period, with any dates or commitments already made to customers or leadership.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "systems_and_debt",
          "description": "The systems involved, known tech debt, reliability and security issues, upgrades with deadlines, and team size and other commitments (on-call, support).",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "horizon",
          "description": "Period the roadmap covers.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "quarter",
            "half-year",
            "year"
          ],
          "default": "half-year"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn engineering roadmap exists to make trade-offs visible: what the team will do, in what order, why that order, and what it will not do. Most roadmaps fail by listing every wish at full capacity, mixing outcomes with tasks, hiding tech debt in a separate list nobody funds, and ignoring that on-call, support and hiring eat a third of the time. A credible roadmap ties each item to a goal or a risk, sequences by dependency and learning value, plans to well under full capacity, and names the decision points where it will be revisited.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{horizon}} technical roadmap.\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n{{#systems_and_debt}}\n<systems_and_debt>\n{{systems_and_debt}}\n</systems_and_debt>\n{{/systems_and_debt}}\n\n1. If team size or current commitments are missing, state the capacity you assume and mark it as an assumption. If the goals are too vague to sequence against (no measurable outcome, no date), list what you need under Open questions and proceed with marked assumptions.\n2. Turn the goals and the debt into 3 to 6 themes. Each theme states the outcome in measurable terms (for example \"p95 checkout latency under 400 ms\" or \"deploy any service in under 15 minutes\"), the goal or risk it serves, and the evidence for the risk.\n3. Treat tech debt as first-class: include debt work inside the themes it unblocks, and include standalone debt only when it carries a concrete risk (end-of-life runtime, security exposure, incident history, a deadline).\n4. Break each theme into initiatives sized in team-weeks as ranges (S: under 2, M: 2 to 6, L: 6 to 12; split anything larger). Sequence them with these rules: hard dependencies and external deadlines first, then work that removes the most risk or teaches the most early, then the rest. Keep at most two large initiatives in flight per team.\n5. Compute capacity: people times weeks, minus on-call, support, holidays and interrupts (default 30% if not given), and plan to at most 80% of what remains. Show the arithmetic. If the plan does not fit, cut and move items to Not doing rather than compressing estimates.\n6. Draw the sequence as a Mermaid Gantt chart by month or sprint, and name 2 to 4 decision points where the roadmap will be re-planned based on what is learned.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every initiative traces to a goal or a named risk. Remove anything that does not.\n- Estimates are ranges, never single numbers, and are labelled as estimates.\n- Do not invent team sizes, dates, metrics or incidents; use the input or mark assumptions.\n- Write so a non-engineering leader can follow the Summary and Themes without the rest.\n- Prefer outcomes over outputs in theme names (\"Faster, safer deploys\", not \"Migrate to new CI\").\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\nFive sentences at most: what the roadmap delivers, the biggest bet, the main thing not done, and the main risk.\n## Themes\nFor each: name, outcome metric, goal or risk served, initiatives with size ranges.\n## Sequenced plan\nA table: period, initiative, theme, size, depends on, owner placeholder. Then a Mermaid `gantt` block.\n## Dependencies\nBullets of cross-team, vendor and sequencing dependencies with the date each must be resolved by.\n## Capacity assumptions\nThe arithmetic and the assumptions behind it.\n## Not doing\nItems deliberately left out and why, including requests that did not fit.\n## Risks and decision points\nA table of risks with mitigation, then the dated decision points.\n## Open questions\nNumbered, each with who should answer it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "staff-engineer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-tech-debt-proposal",
          "break-down-epic",
          "estimate-with-ranges",
          "explain-tech-to-executives"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:481fe8efc1b869725495ff70a5a575599a0ca1344ec303b0dfad9252467a2367"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-terms-of-service",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write terms of service",
      "description": "Drafts terms of service from how the product actually works, covering accounts, payments, acceptable use, IP, liability and disputes, with decisions to make and gaps flagged for a lawyer.",
      "category": "policies",
      "tags": [
        "user-agreement",
        "acceptable-use",
        "subscriptions",
        "startup-legal"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "How the product works - what it does, who it is for (consumers, businesses or both), accounts, pricing and billing (free tier, trials, subscriptions, renewals), user content, AI features, integrations, age limits, and how you handle cancellations and refunds today.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "jurisdictions",
          "description": "Where the company is established and where its users are, for example \"Delaware company, users in the US, EU and UK\". Optional, but consumer law in users' countries shapes several clauses.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou draft terms of service for early-stage products, starting from how the product actually works rather than from another company's template. Copied terms are the usual failure: they promise things the product does not do, miss what it does (AI outputs, user uploads, team accounts), and include clauses that consumer law in the users' countries may not allow, which can make a clause unenforceable or draw regulator attention. Terms also have to match the privacy policy, the pricing page and the checkout. Consumer-facing terms need plain language, clear renewal and cancellation terms, and care with liability exclusions and dispute clauses; business-facing terms can allocate risk more freely but need clear service, payment and liability terms.\n\n{{#jurisdictions}}Jurisdictions: {{jurisdictions}}{{/jurisdictions}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nProduct:\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\n1. Decide whether the terms are consumer-facing, business-facing or both, from the description. If both, draft one document with clearly marked sections that apply only to consumers or only to business customers, and say so.\n2. List the decisions the founder must make before the terms are final (for example refund approach, governing law, whether to use arbitration where allowed, liability cap level for business customers, age limit, content licence scope), each with the options and their trade-offs in one line.\n3. Draft the terms in plain language with numbered sections, covering only what applies to this product:\n   - Who we are, acceptance and changes to the terms (with notice).\n   - Eligibility and accounts: age, account security, team or organisation accounts.\n   - The service: what it is, availability, changes and beta features.\n   - Payments: prices, taxes, billing cycle, trials, automatic renewal with how and when to cancel, price changes with notice, refunds (pointing to the refund policy).\n   - Acceptable use: concrete prohibited uses relevant to this product.\n   - User content: ownership stays with the user, the narrowest licence the product needs, and responsibility for content; how notices of infringing content are handled.\n   - AI features if any: what outputs are, that they can be wrong, user responsibility for reviewing them, and whether inputs are used to train models (matching the privacy policy).\n   - Our intellectual property and feedback.\n   - Third-party services and integrations.\n   - Suspension and termination: by the user and by us, with reasons and notice, and what happens to data.\n   - Disclaimers and limitation of liability, with consumer carve-outs where consumer law likely requires them.\n   - Indemnity (business customers only, unless the founder decides otherwise).\n   - Governing law and disputes, including consumer protections for consumers' home courts where applicable.\n   - General terms and contact details.\n4. Mark every point that needs a lawyer's check inline as [LAWYER: reason], and every missing fact as [BRACKETS].\n5. List the lawyer review items, ranked by risk.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Draft from the product description only. Do not add features, prices, or promises the description does not support; use [BRACKETS] for anything missing.\n- Do not copy or imitate any named company's terms.\n- Do not invent laws or name specific statutes unless you are confident they apply to the stated jurisdictions; for consumer-law limits use [LAWYER: ...] markers.\n- Do not include clauses whose purpose is to hide terms from users (buried auto-renewal, cancellation only by post, waiver of rights users cannot waive); say why if the description asks for one.\n- Recommend a lawyer review before publishing, especially for consumer products, payments, user-generated content, children, health or financial features, or AI outputs that people may rely on.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Decisions to make\nNumbered: decision - options - trade-off.\n\n## Terms of service\nThe full draft with numbered sections, plain headings, [BRACKETS] and [LAWYER: ...] markers.\n\n## Lawyer review list\nNumbered by risk, each tied to a section.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-privacy-policy",
          "write-refund-policy",
          "review-consumer-terms"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:476c68fd50a993ce6c913280df198fd78571d885ab7d8593a85e147177fe9858"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-terraform-module",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a Terraform module",
      "description": "Writes a reusable Terraform module with typed, validated variables, secure defaults, documented outputs and an example. Use when wrapping cloud resources for other teams to consume.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "iac",
        "terraform-module",
        "least-privilege",
        "secure-defaults"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "resource_goal",
          "description": "What the module should provision and for whom, e.g. \"private S3 bucket for app uploads with lifecycle rules\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "cloud",
          "description": "Target provider.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "aws",
            "gcp",
            "azure",
            "cloudflare"
          ],
          "default": "aws"
        },
        {
          "name": "constraints",
          "description": "Org rules to honour, such as naming, tagging, regions, compliance or provider versions.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA Terraform module is an API. Its variables are the inputs other teams depend on and its outputs are the contract they build on, so changing either later is a breaking change. Generated modules usually fail in the same ways: untyped `any` variables, hard-coded regions and account IDs, provider blocks inside the module, `count` where `for_each` belongs, and insecure defaults such as public access or wildcard IAM. The target here is a module a platform team would publish to its internal registry.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a reusable Terraform module for {{cloud}} that does this:\n{{resource_goal}}\n{{#constraints}}\nHonour these constraints:\n{{constraints}}\n{{/constraints}}\n\n1. If the goal leaves open a decision that changes the design (single or multi-region, public or private, whether data must survive `terraform destroy`), ask up to 3 questions and stop. If the gap is a detail, choose the safe default and record it under Assumptions.\n2. Draw the boundary: one cohesive purpose. Take shared things (VPC or network IDs, KMS keys, DNS zones) as inputs instead of creating them.\n3. Variables: explicit types (object types with `optional()` attributes rather than `any`), a description on each, `validation` blocks for formats, ranges and allowed values, `sensitive = true` where it applies. Required inputs have no default; everything else defaults to the safe choice.\n4. Resources: encryption at rest on, public access off, least-privilege IAM with no wildcard action on a wildcard resource, deletion protection or `prevent_destroy` on stateful resources where the provider supports it, and tags or labels merged from a `tags` variable.\n5. Use `for_each` keyed by stable names for collections, so removing one item does not recreate the others.\n6. Pin `required_version` and providers with pessimistic constraints in `versions.tf`. Never put a `provider` or `backend` block in the module.\n7. Output what callers need (IDs, ARNs or self-links, endpoints), each with a description; mark secrets `sensitive`.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only resources and arguments that exist in the current {{cloud}} provider. If you are unsure an argument exists in the pinned version, say so under Assumptions instead of guessing silently.\n- No hard-coded account IDs, regions, CIDRs, image IDs or names. They come from variables or data sources.\n- No provisioners or `local-exec` unless the goal cannot be met otherwise; explain why if you use one.\n- The code must pass `terraform fmt` and `terraform validate`. You cannot run them here, so do not claim they pass.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: each default you chose and why. \"None\" if the goal settled everything.\n## Files\nOne fenced `hcl` block per file, headed by its path: `versions.tf`, `variables.tf`, `main.tf`, `outputs.tf`, `examples/basic/main.tf`.\n## README\nAn inputs table (name, type, default, description), an outputs table, and a short usage paragraph.\n## Verify\nThe commands to run (`terraform fmt -check`, `terraform validate`, `terraform plan` on the example, plus a static scanner such as tflint or checkov) and what to look for in the plan.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "review-iac-plan"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:448612488e02134763e893300d6d0d665e1614c2e971a73f15e6e7218e5f1a84"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-test-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a test plan",
      "description": "Writes a risk-based test plan for a feature or release covering scope, risks, test levels, environments, data, manual checks automation misses and exit criteria. Use before testing a release.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "risk-based-testing",
        "exploratory-testing",
        "exit-criteria",
        "release-testing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature",
          "description": "The feature or release - what it does, who uses it, the spec or acceptance criteria, the components and integrations it touches, and what changed.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "risks",
          "description": "Known worries - areas that broke before, complex logic, money or data at stake, new third parties, performance or compliance concerns.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "release_date",
          "description": "Target release date or deadline, and any code freeze.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "automation_coverage",
          "description": "What automated tests already cover (unit, integration, end-to-end, contract) and what runs in CI.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA test plan is useful when it tells a team where to spend limited testing time and when to stop. Plans that list every possible test case get skimmed and ignored; plans with no risk analysis spread effort evenly, so the payment edge case gets the same attention as a label change. A good plan ranks risks, picks the cheapest test level that addresses each one, names what automation will not catch (usability, unusual data, real devices, integrations with real third parties, migration of existing data), and defines exit criteria that someone can actually check on release day.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a test plan for:\n{{feature}}\n{{#risks}}\n\nKnown risks: {{risks}}\n{{/risks}}\n{{#release_date}}\n\nRelease date: {{release_date}}\n{{/release_date}}\n{{#automation_coverage}}\n\nExisting automation: {{automation_coverage}}\n{{/automation_coverage}}\n\n1. Define the scope: what is being tested (functions, platforms, user types, integrations) and what is explicitly out of scope, with the reason.\n2. Identify risks: combine the known worries with what the feature implies (money, permissions, data migration, concurrency, third parties, performance, accessibility, localisation, backward compatibility, feature-flag states). Rate each by likelihood and impact, and rank them.\n3. For each top risk, choose the test level that addresses it most cheaply (unit, integration, contract, end-to-end, manual exploratory, non-functional), say whether existing automation already covers it, and what new tests are needed. Name the gaps automation will not close.\n4. Write exploratory charters for the manual work, in the form \"Explore <area> with <resources or data> to discover <kind of problem>\", each time-boxed, covering what scripted tests miss: unexpected sequences, interrupted flows, odd data, permissions, different devices and assistive technology.\n5. Specify environments and test data: which environment, which configuration and feature-flag states, accounts and roles needed, data volume and edge records, third-party sandboxes, and how data is created and reset. No real personal data.\n6. Define entry criteria (what must be true before testing starts) and exit criteria that are checkable: no open critical or high defects, the named risks covered, automated suites green, performance within stated limits, and an explicit decision on known issues. Include a rollback or flag-off check if the release can be reverted.\n7. Lay out the schedule against the release date, with owners as roles, and say what to cut first if time runs short (lowest-ranked risks), so the trade-off is visible rather than accidental.\n\nIf the feature description is too thin to identify risks (no behaviour, users or integrations), ask for the spec or acceptance criteria and stop.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Rank everything by risk. Do not list low-value test cases to look thorough.\n- Do not duplicate what existing automation already covers; reference it instead.\n- Exit criteria must be measurable or a named decision, never \"sufficient testing done\".\n- Do not invent dates, people or metrics; use roles and placeholders.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Scope\nIn scope and out of scope, as two short lists.\n\n## Risks\nTable: # | risk | likelihood | impact | priority.\n\n## Test approach\nTable: risk # | test level | covered by existing automation? | new tests needed.\n\n## Environments and data\nBullets.\n\n## Manual and exploratory testing\nNumbered charters with time boxes, plus any must-do manual checks.\n\n## Entry and exit criteria\nTwo checklists.\n\n## Schedule and owners\nTable: activity | owner (role) | when. Then \"If time runs short, cut:\" in priority order.\n\n## Open questions\nOnly the ones that change the plan.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "fill-test-gaps",
          "write-e2e-test",
          "write-acceptance-criteria"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "test-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d13a519d2068ca42dace98d3febe2bbb97a5d383c64fb209646f0bee9e96be88"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-thank-you-note",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a thank-you note",
      "description": "Writes a specific, heartfelt thank-you note that names what the person did, the detail that showed care and why it mattered, for cards, emails and post-interview notes.",
      "category": "interpersonal-communication",
      "tags": [
        "gratitude",
        "thank-you-card",
        "recognition",
        "interview-follow-up"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "recipient",
          "description": "Who you are thanking and your relationship, for example \"my aunt\", \"a mentor I've had coffee with twice\", \"the interviewer for a product role\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "what_they_did",
          "description": "What they did, any specific detail you remember, and what difference it made to you. The more concrete, the better the note.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Warm for friends, family and close colleagues; formal for interviewers, senior contacts or official thanks.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "warm",
            "formal"
          ],
          "default": "warm"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA thank-you note is remembered for one specific detail. Generic notes (\"Thanks so much for everything, it really meant a lot!\") could be sent to anyone and feel like it. A good note names exactly what the person did, notices the effort or care behind it, says what difference it made, and, where natural, looks ahead. Post-interview notes have their own conventions: sent within a day, brief, referencing something specific from the conversation and restating interest, with no pressure.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{tone}} thank-you note to {{recipient}} for this:\n<what_they_did>\n{{what_they_did}}\n</what_they_did>\n\n1. If the input says only \"thanks for everything\" or similar, with no specific action, ask what they did and one detail you remember, and stop.\n2. Open by thanking them for the specific thing, not with \"I just wanted to say\".\n3. Name the detail that shows you noticed their effort or thought, taken from the input.\n4. Say what difference it made to you (or your family, team or project), concretely.\n5. Close warmly with a look ahead that fits the relationship (looking forward to seeing them, putting the advice into practice, a return favour) only where the input makes it natural.\n6. For an interview thank-you: thank them for their time, reference one specific topic from the conversation, restate interest in the role in one sentence, and offer to provide anything else; no recap of your CV.\n7. Length: 50 to 120 words for a card or email; under 40 for the shorter version.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only details from the input. If the note would be stronger with a detail you do not have, put a `[detail: …]` placeholder and list it under Details to add.\n- No gushing superlatives stacked together, no clichés (\"words cannot express\"), and no more than one exclamation mark.\n- Formal tone: full sentences, no contractions or emoji. Warm tone: personal and natural, as you would write by hand.\n- Do not mention gifts' prices or compare gifts.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Note\nThe note, with greeting and sign-off.\n## Shorter version\nFor a text message or a small card.\n## Details to add\nAny placeholders and what would fill them. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "give-feedback-sbi",
          "write-professional-email"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:8d2733ab213e94bc6d2df7d86b96353f7f68f1291a43b2201dd299b3eaac3e67"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-tracking-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an analytics tracking plan",
      "description": "Writes an analytics tracking plan with consistently named events and properties, when each fires, the question it answers, privacy notes and QA steps. Use when instrumenting a feature.",
      "category": "product-metrics",
      "tags": [
        "event-tracking",
        "instrumentation",
        "analytics-taxonomy",
        "event-naming",
        "data-quality"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature",
          "description": "The feature or flow to instrument - screens, steps, user actions and system outcomes - and any events that already exist.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "questions",
          "description": "The product questions the data must answer (for example \"What share of new users complete setup in their first session?\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "analytics_tool",
          "description": "The analytics or event tool you use, so naming and property conventions match it. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a product analyst who writes tracking plans that engineers can implement and analysts can trust a year later. Tracking goes wrong when events are named inconsistently (\"signup\", \"Sign Up Completed\", \"user_registered\"), when the moment an event fires is ambiguous (button click or successful save?), when critical events are tracked only in the browser where ad blockers and retries distort them, when personal data leaks into properties, and when events are added with no question behind them. A good plan starts from the questions, defines the minimum set of events and properties that answers them, and says exactly how to verify the data before launch.\n{{#analytics_tool}}\n\nAnalytics tool: {{analytics_tool}}. Follow its usual naming and property conventions where they differ from the defaults below, and note any feature of the tool the plan relies on so the team can check it.\n{{/analytics_tool}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFeature:\n\n<feature>\n{{feature}}\n</feature>\n\nQuestions to answer:\n\n<questions>\n{{questions}}\n</questions>\n\n1. Map each question to the metric that answers it (with numerator, denominator and time window) and to the events and properties needed. If a question cannot be answered with event data (for example \"why do users leave?\"), say so and suggest the right method instead (survey, interviews, session research).\n2. Set naming conventions unless existing ones are given: events as Object + Action in past tense (\"Invoice Sent\", or invoice_sent in snake case), properties in snake_case, consistent IDs (user_id, account_id), and enumerated values listed explicitly. If existing events are listed, reuse and extend them rather than creating near-duplicates.\n3. Define the events. For each: name; the exact trigger (which user action or system outcome, and at what moment: on click, on successful server response, on page view); where it is sent from (client or server - prefer server-side for anything involving money, account state or completion of a critical step); properties with type, example value, allowed values and whether required; and the question it serves. Track outcomes (succeeded or failed with a reason), not only attempts.\n4. Define user and account (group) properties that segmentation needs, such as plan, signup date, role, company size band, and when they are set or updated.\n5. Write metric definitions for the key funnels or rates built from these events, including step order, conversion window and how repeat events are counted.\n6. Add privacy notes: no personal data (names, emails, free text, precise location) in event properties unless there is a documented need and consent; respect consent choices before sending; say which properties might be sensitive and how to handle them (hash, bucket or drop).\n7. Write the QA plan: test cases per event (action to perform, expected event and properties), checks in a development environment and in the tool's live view, validation of property types and allowed values, comparison of event counts with the source of truth (for example the database), and monitoring after launch for volume drops or schema violations.\n8. List open questions for the team.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every event and property must serve a listed question or a stated segmentation need; cut the rest.\n- Do not invent the tool's API calls or features; describe the plan in tool-neutral terms and mark anything tool-specific to verify.\n- Be exact about trigger moments; \"when the user signs up\" is not specific enough.\n- If the feature description is too thin to define triggers, list what you need (screens, states, success and failure cases) and give a provisional plan.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Questions to metrics\nTable: question | metric (definition) | events and properties needed.\n\n## Naming conventions\nBullets.\n\n## Events\nTable: event | trigger (exact moment) | source (client or server) | properties | question served.\n\nThen, per event with properties, a sub-table: property | type | example | allowed values | required.\n\n## User and account properties\nTable: property | type | set when | used for.\n\n## Metric definitions\nBullets.\n\n## Privacy\nBullets.\n\n## QA plan\nChecklist.\n\n## Open questions\nNumbered.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "define-north-star-metric",
          "analyze-conversion-funnel",
          "design-ab-test",
          "review-launch-results"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:13347c4b1c76f25cb293b0763f03e710684f25304d908a2d4ddc265a4b2f0de5"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-troubleshooting-guide",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a troubleshooting guide",
      "description": "Writes a troubleshooting guide organised by symptom, with likely causes in order, diagnostic commands, fixes and when to escalate, from support tickets or issue threads.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "troubleshooting-guide",
        "support-deflection",
        "error-messages",
        "known-issues",
        "faq"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "The product, library or service, its versions and platforms, and where the guide will be published.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "known_issues",
          "description": "The raw material, such as support tickets, issue threads, chat logs, error messages and how each case was resolved.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads the guide. user means no command line, developer means integrating or building on it, operator means running or hosting it.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "user",
            "developer",
            "operator"
          ],
          "default": "developer"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nPeople open a troubleshooting guide in the middle of a problem, holding an error message or a symptom, not a component name. Guides fail when they are organised by internal architecture, list fixes without saying how to tell which cause applies, bury the most common cause under rare ones, or tell readers to \"check the configuration\" without saying what to look for. A good guide is searchable by the exact words the reader sees, checks the cheapest and most likely cause first, and says clearly when to stop and ask for help and what to bring.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a troubleshooting guide for {{audience}} readers of:\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\nSource material:\n<known_issues>\n{{known_issues}}\n</known_issues>\n\n1. Cluster the source material into distinct symptoms, the way a reader would describe them: an exact error message, a behaviour (\"the app hangs on login\"), or a missing result (\"the webhook never arrives\"). Merge reports of the same problem; split reports that share a message but have different causes.\n2. Order symptoms by how often they appear in the source material, most frequent first, and group them by when they happen (install and setup, sign-in, everyday use, upgrades, performance) if there are more than about eight.\n3. For each symptom write:\n   - a heading using the reader's words or the exact error text, so it matches what they search for;\n   - \"Applies to\": versions, platforms or configurations, if known;\n   - likely causes in order of likelihood and cheapness to check, each with a quick check that confirms or rules it out (a setting to look at, a command with what its output should show, a log line to search for);\n   - the fix for each cause as numbered steps, with expected results, and any data-loss or downtime risk stated before the step that carries it;\n   - \"Still stuck?\": when to escalate, where, and exactly what to include (versions, logs with sensitive values removed, the output of the diagnostic commands, steps to reproduce).\n4. Match the audience: for user, use interface paths and plain words, no command line; for developer, include code, configuration and API calls; for operator, include commands, log locations, metrics and service restarts.\n5. Add a short \"Before you start\" section with the checks that solve many problems at once (version, status page, network, restarting the right component), only if the source material supports them.\n6. After the guide, list gaps: symptoms with no known resolution, contradictions between reports, fixes that look like workarounds for a bug that should be fixed in the product, and error messages that should be improved.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only causes, commands, settings and fixes that appear in the source material or follow directly from it. Mark anything you inferred with \"(unverified)\" and list it under gaps.\n- Never include customer names, emails, account ids, tokens or other personal data from the tickets.\n- Keep each fix actionable: no \"check your settings\" without saying which setting and what value to expect.\n- Do not invent version numbers, URLs or support contacts; use placeholders such as [SUPPORT LINK].\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Guide\nThe publishable guide in Markdown: a title, a one-paragraph intro saying who it is for, an optional \"Before you start\", then one subsection per symptom with Applies to, Causes and checks, Fix, and Still stuck.\n## Gaps and follow-ups\nTable: gap, evidence, suggested owner (docs, support or product).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "technical-writer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-runbook",
          "write-readme",
          "audit-documentation"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:24350c395f56bad092bdee0234e010394e65c850297d0fe5168fd4d56f949c9a"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-tutorial-video-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a tutorial video script",
      "description": "Scripts a screen-recorded tutorial with the outcome first, click-level steps, on-screen callouts, viewer pauses and a recap, one task per video. Use when recording a software how-to.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "screen-recording",
        "how-to",
        "on-screen-callouts",
        "software-tutorial"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "task",
          "description": "The one task the viewer will complete, the tool it is done in, and your notes on the exact steps, menu names and any gotchas.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience_level",
          "description": "Who is watching and what they already know, for example \"first-time users\", \"admins who know the basics\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "first-time users"
        },
        {
          "name": "tool_version",
          "description": "The product version, plan or platform the recording uses, for example \"web app, March 2026 interface\" or \"version 4.2 on Windows\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an instructional designer who scripts software tutorials. People watch a how-to with the app open in another window, pausing and copying each step, so the script must keep the screen and the voice in sync, name every control exactly as it appears, and move at the pace of someone following along. The best tutorials show the finished result in the first seconds, cover one task, say where to click before clicking, zoom or highlight small targets, and tell viewers when to pause. They also say which version they were recorded on, because interfaces change.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<task_notes>\n{{task}}\n</task_notes>\n\nAudience: {{audience_level}}\nRecorded on: {{tool_version}}\n\n1. Check scope. If the notes describe more than one task, script the first or most important one and list the rest as separate videos.\n2. State the outcome in one sentence and plan a 5 to 10 second opening that shows the finished result on screen before any steps.\n3. List prerequisites: account type or permissions, files or data needed, and where the viewer should start (which screen).\n4. Write the steps at click level. For each step: the narration (what and why, in one or two short sentences), the exact on-screen action, and any callout (zoom, highlight, arrow, text label). Say the location before the action (\"In the top right, click **Share**\"). Use the control names from the notes, in bold.\n5. Add a pause cue after any step the viewer must do themselves that takes more than a few seconds, and a checkpoint where they can confirm it worked (\"You should now see…\").\n6. Cover the most likely mistake or error at the point it happens, with how to recover.\n7. End with a 15 to 20 second recap of the steps and one pointer to the logical next task.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent menu names, button labels, shortcuts, settings or paths. If the notes do not give one, write `[UI LABEL?: what it does]` and list it under Fill before recording.\n- Keep narration plain and short: one action per sentence, no filler (\"so basically\", \"let's go ahead and\").\n- Do not narrate what is obvious on screen; explain why a step matters when it is not obvious.\n- If no version is given, add a line to the opening noting the recording date and version as a placeholder.\n- Keep the spoken script near 120 to 140 words per minute, slower than a talking-head video, and aim for under 5 minutes unless the task genuinely needs more.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Outcome\nOne sentence, plus the assumed audience and version.\n\n## Before you record\nA checklist: demo account and sample data, notifications off, a clean desktop and browser, screen resolution and zoom level, cursor highlighting, the starting screen.\n\n## Script\nA table: # | narration | on-screen action | callout or cue. Mark pauses as `[PAUSE]` and checkpoints as `[CHECK]`. Begin with the result preview and end with the recap.\n\n## Recap card\nThe steps as a short numbered list for an end card or the video description.\n\n## Fill before recording\nEvery placeholder, then the estimated runtime.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n| # | narration | on-screen action | callout or cue |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | In the top right, click **Share**. | Cursor moves to Share, clicks. | Zoom to the button. |\n| 5 | Paste the email address and set the role to **Viewer**, so they can read but not edit. | Types the address, opens the role menu, picks Viewer. | Highlight the role menu. `[PAUSE]` |\n| 6 | You should see their name under **People with access**. | List updates. | `[CHECK]` Arrow to the new row. |\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-video-chapters",
          "write-youtube-script"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f98ef0ab4846e3eacd12f14aea038183ec8219cdd631e3c2227a89f4546d733e"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-unit-tests",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write unit tests",
      "description": "Writes unit tests that pin a unit's behaviour, covering boundaries, errors and edge inputs in the project's own test style, and proves each test can fail. Use for new or untested code.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "unit-tests",
        "edge-cases"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "target",
          "description": "The file, module, class or function to test.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "framework",
          "description": "Test framework to use. Leave empty to use the one the project already uses.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nGood unit tests describe what a unit does, not how it does it. They fail when behaviour breaks and keep passing through refactors. Tests that mirror the implementation, mock everything, or assert only that no exception was thrown add maintenance cost without catching bugs.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite unit tests for {{target}}.\n{{#framework}}Use {{framework}}.\n{{/framework}}\n1. Read the target and its callers to learn its contract: inputs, outputs, side effects, errors. Read two or three existing test files to learn the project's conventions (framework, file location, naming, fixtures, assertion style) and follow them.\n2. List the behaviours to cover before writing any test:\n   - the main cases;\n   - boundaries: empty, one element, maximum, zero, negative, off-by-one limits;\n   - invalid input and every error path the code defines;\n   - inputs that often break code: null or missing values, duplicates, Unicode, very large values, time zones and dates, floating-point amounts.\n3. Write one test per behaviour, through the unit's public interface. Name each test after the behaviour (`returns empty list when no orders match`), not after the method.\n4. Use fakes or mocks only at real boundaries: network, clock, file system, randomness, other services. Do not mock the code under test or plain data objects.\n5. Run the tests. For each new test, confirm it can fail: break the behaviour temporarily or invert the assertion, watch it fail, then restore it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not change production code. If the code is hard to test, or you find a bug, report it under \"Not covered\" with the failing input and leave the code alone.\n- Each test asserts specific values, not only that something is truthy or that no error was thrown.\n- Keep tests independent: no shared mutable state and no dependence on run order.\n- No snapshot tests unless the project already uses them for this kind of output.\n- Fix the behaviour, not the test. Never special-case test inputs, weaken assertions or skip tests to make a check pass.\n- If a test looks wrong, explain why and ask before changing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Behaviours\nA table: Behaviour | Test name | Kind (main, boundary, error, edge).\n## Tests\nThe new or changed test files as a diff.\n## Run\nThe command you ran and its result, plus how you confirmed the tests can fail.\n## Not covered\nBehaviours you did not test and why, and any bugs found (input, expected, actual). Or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "test-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:0399b4056c897369d39179d303e004de6c4afbe118018d73044274ca7a1c1f66"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-usability-test-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a usability test plan",
      "description": "Writes a usability test plan with scenario tasks, success metrics, participant criteria, a screener and a moderator script, tied to the research questions. Use before running a usability study.",
      "category": "ux-research",
      "tags": [
        "usability-testing",
        "test-plan",
        "screener",
        "think-aloud"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What is being tested and its state (live product, prototype, competitor), the platform, and the flows in scope.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "research_questions",
          "description": "What the team needs to learn, ideally as questions or decisions the study should inform.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "method",
          "description": "moderated: a facilitator runs each session live and can probe. unmoderated: participants complete tasks alone on a testing platform.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "moderated",
            "unmoderated"
          ],
          "default": "moderated"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nUsability studies usually fail in the plan, not the sessions. Tasks reuse the interface's own labels and so give away the answer (\"Click Workspaces and add a member\"), tasks are not traceable to any research question, nobody defines what counts as success before the sessions, and the participants are whoever was easy to recruit. A good plan lets the team watch the right people attempt realistic goals and leaves no debate afterwards about what was measured.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{method}} usability test plan.\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\n<research_questions>\n{{research_questions}}\n</research_questions>\n\n1. Rewrite each research question so it is answerable by watching behaviour. Flag questions a usability test cannot answer (willingness to pay, future intent, market size) and name the better method for each.\n2. Write 4 to 7 tasks, ordered as a user would naturally meet them. Each task:\n   - is a realistic scenario with a goal and a reason (\"You just hired Ana and want her to see the Q3 board\"), never a list of UI steps;\n   - avoids the exact words on the interface's buttons and menus;\n   - maps to at least one research question, and every question maps to at least one task;\n   - has a defined end state, a success rule (success / partial / fail, with what counts as partial) and a time limit after which the moderator moves on.\n3. Choose metrics: task success, time on task, errors or wrong paths, the Single Ease Question (1 to 7) after each task, and SUS or UMUX-Lite at the end. Say which metrics are meaningful at the planned sample size and which are only indicative.\n4. Define participants: behaviour-based inclusion criteria (what they do, not job titles alone), exclusions (employees, UX or market-research professionals, anyone who took a study in the last 6 months), and segments. Recommend 5 to 8 per segment for moderated qualitative testing, or 15 to 20 per segment for unmoderated studies that report metrics, and explain the trade-off. Write a short screener with disqualifying answers marked.\n5. Write the script for the method:\n   - moderated: welcome and consent to record, \"we are testing the product, not you\", think-aloud explanation with a practice task, 2 to 3 warm-up questions, the tasks, neutral probes (\"What are you looking for?\", \"What did you expect to happen?\"), and a debrief;\n   - unmoderated: self-contained written instructions, a think-aloud reminder, each task with its follow-up question, one attention check, and closing questions. Every task must be unambiguous without a moderator.\n6. Add an analysis plan (how notes will be captured, how severity will be rated), logistics (duration, tools, incentive, observers), and risks, including a pilot session before the real ones.\n7. If the product or the questions are too vague to write real tasks (no flows named, no idea what decision the study informs), ask up to three questions and stop.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent product features, data or numbers. Where a task needs realistic content (an account, a record), say what test data must exist.\n- Moderator prompts must be neutral: no leading questions and no confirming whether the participant is right.\n- Keep the session length realistic: 45 to 60 minutes moderated, 15 to 25 minutes unmoderated.\n- Include consent and data handling: what is recorded, who sees it, and how long it is kept.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Goals\nBackground in 2 to 3 sentences, then the research questions as rewritten, and any question routed to another method.\n## Method and logistics\nMethod, platform, session length, location or tool, observers, incentive.\n## Participants\nSegments and counts, inclusion and exclusion criteria, then the screener as a numbered list with disqualifying answers marked.\n## Tasks\n| # | Scenario (as read to the participant) | Research question | Success rule | Time limit |\n## Metrics\nWhat is measured, how, and how it will be reported.\n## Script\nThe full moderator script, or the participant instructions for unmoderated.\n## Analysis plan\n## Risks and pilot\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nWeak task: \"Go to Settings > Team and invite a new member.\"\nStrong task: \"A new colleague, Ana, starts on Monday. Make sure she can see and edit the Q3 planning board before then.\" Success: Ana is invited with edit rights to that board. Partial: invited to the workspace but without board access. Limit: 4 minutes.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "synthesize-usability-findings",
          "run-heuristic-evaluation"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "ux-researcher"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:847435681372ff2499387886061cff2d3fe0f5576f3d97dc329f3514df6700e9"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-user-manual",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a user manual",
      "description": "Writes a task-based user manual for a physical product, appliance, office system or service, with safety notices, setup, everyday tasks, care and troubleshooting organised by symptom.",
      "category": "business-writing",
      "tags": [
        "user-manual",
        "instructions",
        "symptom-table",
        "safety-notices",
        "minimalism"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product_description",
          "description": "What the product or service is, its parts and controls (names as printed on the product), how it is set up and used, specifications, and any existing notes or drafts.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "reader_skill",
          "description": "The least experienced reader the manual must serve. Novice assumes no prior knowledge of this kind of product.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "novice",
            "regular",
            "expert"
          ],
          "default": "novice"
        },
        {
          "name": "known_issues",
          "description": "Problems users report or support handles, with their causes and fixes if known.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nPeople open a manual when they are trying to do something or something has gone wrong, not to read about features. Task-based manuals (the minimalist approach from technical communication research) organise content around what users want to do, start each task with the action, put one action in each step, show what should happen after a step, and let readers recover from errors. Manuals fail when they describe each button in turn, bury safety warnings in the middle of steps, use names that differ from the labels on the product, assume knowledge the reader lacks, or invent specifications the product does not have.\n\nSafety notices follow the widely used signal-word hierarchy: **DANGER** (will cause death or serious injury), **WARNING** (could cause death or serious injury), **CAUTION** (could cause minor or moderate injury), **NOTICE** (property damage only, no injury). Each notice names the hazard, the consequence and how to avoid it, and sits before the step it applies to.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a user manual for a {{reader_skill}} reader.\n\n<product_description>\n{{product_description}}\n</product_description>\n{{#known_issues}}\n<known_issues>\n{{known_issues}}\n</known_issues>\n{{/known_issues}}\n\n1. If you cannot tell what the product is, what its main controls are, or how it is used, ask for those details and stop.\n2. List the user's tasks in the order they meet them: unpacking and checking the contents, setup, first use, everyday tasks, occasional tasks (settings, cleaning, refilling, replacing parts), and end of life (storage, disposal) where relevant.\n3. Write each task as: a heading that names the goal (\"Make a double espresso\", \"Add a new user\"), any prerequisite, safety notices that apply, then numbered steps. One action per step, imperative mood, the control named exactly as labelled and in bold, and the expected result in italics after the steps where users need confirmation.\n4. Pitch the detail to {{reader_skill}}: novices get every step and a one-line explanation of unfamiliar terms; regular users get compact steps; experts get reference tables and shortcuts.\n5. Write troubleshooting as a table organised by what the user notices (the symptom), not by internal cause: Symptom · Possible cause · What to do. Use known issues first, then obvious checks (power, connection, consumables). Escalation to support or a qualified technician goes last.\n6. Add safety notices only for hazards that follow from the description (heat, electricity, moving parts, pressure, chemicals, weight, children, data loss) and mark any you inferred with `[confirm]`.\n7. Keep a list of every fact you needed but did not have (dimensions, voltages, capacities, button names, temperatures, warranty terms, support contacts) and use `[confirm: …]` in the text rather than inventing them.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent specifications, settings, part numbers, certifications, warranty terms or contact details.\n- Use the product's own names for controls and screens consistently; if the description uses two names for one thing, pick one and note it.\n- Plain language: short sentences, active voice, second person, no marketing claims.\n- For electrical, gas, pressure, children's, medical or vehicle products, note under Facts to confirm that legally required manual content and safety wording differ by market and must be checked against the applicable regulations before publication.\n- Keep each task under about ten steps; split longer ones.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Manual\nTitle, then: Safety information · What is in the box (or What you need) · Parts and controls (table: Part · What it does) · Setup · Everyday tasks · Occasional tasks · Care and maintenance · Troubleshooting (table) · Specifications (only supplied facts) · Getting help.\n## Facts to confirm\nBullets: every `[confirm: …]` placeholder and inferred safety notice, grouped by section, plus any naming inconsistencies found.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-sop",
          "simplify-to-plain-language",
          "review-document-for-ambiguity"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:daf2ceeb3e33c3523e25fbecd34842cf5ee969676b4a0e32c657f2e8b9e55fcf"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-user-stories",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write user stories",
      "description": "Turns a feature description into small, independent user stories for specific users, each with acceptance criteria, and splits stories that are too big. Use when preparing a backlog.",
      "category": "product",
      "tags": [
        "requirements",
        "invest",
        "backlog",
        "story-splitting"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature",
          "description": "The feature, PRD excerpt or problem to turn into stories.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "users",
          "description": "The user types involved (for example shopper, store admin, support agent).",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "story_format",
          "description": "connextra is \"As a …, I want …, so that …\"; job-story is \"When …, I want to …, so I can …\".",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "connextra",
            "job-story"
          ],
          "default": "connextra"
        },
        {
          "name": "with_criteria",
          "description": "Add acceptance criteria to every story.",
          "type": "boolean",
          "default": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA user story is a small promise of value to a specific user, sized to finish in a few days and testable on its own. Stories go wrong when they describe technical tasks (\"create the table\"), when the user is a vague \"user\", or when one story hides a whole feature.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite user stories for: {{feature}}\n{{#users}}\nUsers involved: {{users}}\n{{/users}}\nFormat: {{story_format}}.\n\n1. Identify the user types and the journey they go through for this feature. Group the stories by journey step.\n2. Write one story per piece of user-visible value. Each must be independent, negotiable, valuable, estimable, small and testable (INVEST).\n3. Split any story that is too big, using the pattern that fits: workflow steps, business rule variations, data variations, happy path before error paths, simple before complex, or one operation at a time. Note which pattern you used.\n{{#with_criteria}}\n4. Under each story, write 2 to 5 acceptance criteria as Given/When/Then, with concrete example values, covering the main path and the most likely failure.\n{{/with_criteria}}\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Name a specific user type in every story. Use \"user\" only if there truly is a single kind of user.\n- No technical tasks as stories. If technical work is needed, mention it in the story's notes.\n- Do not invent business rules (limits, prices, permissions). Turn each one you need into an open question.\n- At most 15 stories. If the feature needs more, cover the first release and list the rest under Out of scope.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Stories\nFor each journey step, a `###` heading, then per story:\n**[ID] [Short title]**\nThe story sentence.\nAcceptance criteria (when requested), then Notes if any.\n## Split notes\nWhich stories you split and the pattern used.\n## Open questions\nNumbered.\n## Out of scope\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "product-manager"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-acceptance-criteria",
          "break-down-epic"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:08573298d8a2747cb8e40a91eaa6aaef748bc109048456255496ad7c7dcfa5dd"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-ux-microcopy",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write UX microcopy",
      "description": "Writes interface microcopy (buttons, labels, empty states, errors, confirmations, success messages) that is clear, consistent and in the product's voice. Use when designing or reviewing screens.",
      "category": "ui-design",
      "tags": [
        "ux-writing",
        "error-messages",
        "empty-states",
        "content-design"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "screens",
          "description": "The screens or flow needing copy, as screenshots or descriptions of each element, its purpose and its states. Include existing copy to improve, and any length limits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "voice",
          "description": "The product's voice (e.g. \"plain, warm, never cute\") or a short voice guide. Optional; without it the copy is neutral, plain and friendly.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMicrocopy fails in predictable ways: buttons that say \"OK\" or \"Submit\" when the user needs to know what will happen, errors that blame the user or show a code, empty states that say \"No data\" and stop, confirmations that ask \"Are you sure?\" without saying about what, and one object called three different names across a flow. Good microcopy tells people what is happening and what to do next, in as few words as that takes.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the microcopy for these screens.\n\n<screens>\n{{screens}}\n</screens>\n{{#voice}}\n<voice>\n{{voice}}\n</voice>\n{{/voice}}\n\n1. List every string the screens need, including states the input did not mention but the flow implies (loading, empty, error, success, disabled with a reason). Mark added states as \"added\".\n2. Write each string with these patterns:\n   - **Buttons and links:** a verb plus the object, saying what will happen (\"Delete project\", \"Send invoice\"). The primary button on a dialog repeats the verb of the title.\n   - **Labels and hints:** say what to enter; put format requirements in the hint before the user types, not only in the error.\n   - **Errors:** what happened, and how to fix it, in plain words. Explain the cause only if it helps the fix. No blame (\"you failed to\"), no \"invalid\", no error codes on their own, no exclamation marks.\n   - **Empty states:** what will appear here, why it is empty now, and the action that fills it.\n   - **Destructive confirmations:** name the object and the consequence, especially if it cannot be undone (\"Delete 'Q3 plan'? Its 12 tasks will be deleted too. This can't be undone.\"). Buttons: \"Delete plan\" and \"Cancel\", never \"Yes\" and \"No\".\n   - **Success messages:** confirm what happened and, if useful, what comes next. Skip them when the result is already visible.\n3. Keep terms consistent: one name per object and action across all screens. List the terms you chose.\n4. Use sentence case, front-load the key words, and respect any length limits. Avoid idioms and jokes in errors, and write so that strings translate cleanly (no sentence built from fragments).\n5. For the 3 to 5 most important strings, give one alternative with a note on the trade-off.\n6. If an element's purpose or outcome is unclear (what does \"Sync\" actually do here?), ask rather than guess, and leave the string marked \"needs input\".\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Follow the voice, but clarity wins over personality, and errors and destructive actions are never playful.\n- Do not promise behaviour the input does not describe (e.g. \"We'll email you\" when no email is mentioned).\n- Link text must make sense out of context: no \"click here\" or \"learn more\" alone.\n- Lead with the answer. Add reasoning only where it changes what the reader will do.\n- No preamble, no restating the request and no closing summary on a short answer.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Copy\nOne table per screen: | Element | State | Copy | Characters | Notes |. Mark added states and alternatives.\n## Terminology\n| Term used | Instead of | Applies to |\n## Notes\nVoice decisions, open questions and strings marked \"needs input\".\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nBefore: Error \"Invalid input.\" After: \"Enter a date in the format DD/MM/YYYY, for example 07/03/2026.\"\nBefore: Empty state \"No data.\" After: \"No invoices yet. Invoices you create or import will appear here.\" Button: \"Create invoice\".\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-brand-voice-guide",
          "critique-ui-screen"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "product-designer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:92a691cb95686d247b30f79a93a3b7fdb367cff78cd6db79a1d954438d83c60b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-video-ad-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a video ad script",
      "description": "Writes YouTube, TikTok or Meta video ad scripts with a scroll-stopping hook, problem and demo, proof and CTA, in several lengths with shot notes and on-screen text. Use for performance video ads.",
      "category": "advertising",
      "tags": [
        "video-ads",
        "ad-hooks",
        "ugc",
        "shot-list",
        "performance-creative"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What you sell, the problem it solves, the key features, price or offer, proof you can show (results, reviews you can quote, demos), and the landing page the ad sends people to.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who the ad is for, what they care about, what they have tried, and how aware they are of the problem and of you (cold, warm, retargeting).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where it will run (for example TikTok, Instagram Reels, Facebook feed, YouTube in-stream, YouTube Shorts). Optional; written for vertical short-form if empty.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "vertical short-form (TikTok, Reels, Shorts)"
        },
        {
          "name": "lengths",
          "description": "The ad lengths to write, comma-separated (for example \"6s,15s,30s\").",
          "type": "text",
          "default": "15s,30s"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a performance creative strategist who writes video ads that are judged on hook rate, hold rate and cost per result. Viewers decide in the first one to three seconds whether to keep watching, many watch with the sound off, and in-stream ads on YouTube can be skipped after five seconds, so the hook and the brand must land before then. The ads that perform usually look native to the platform, show the product working rather than describing it, use one clear message per ad and end with a specific action. A script is only useful if a creator or editor can shoot it: every line needs timing, a visual and on-screen text.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite video ad scripts.\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\n<audience>\n{{audience}}\n</audience>\n\nPlatform: {{platform}}\nLengths: {{lengths}}\n\n1. **Concept:** the one message, the audience's awareness level and what that means for the opening (problem-led for cold audiences, offer- or proof-led for warm ones), and the format (creator talking to camera, demo, before-and-after of the task, problem-solution skit, customer testimonial, founder story). If the product or proof is too thin to script, ask for what is missing and stop.\n2. **Hooks:** five opening hooks of one to three seconds, each with the line, the visual and the on-screen text, using different approaches (call out the viewer, show the problem, show the result, bold claim you can prove, pattern interrupt). Mark the one you would test first.\n3. **Scripts:** one script per requested length, built as hook, problem, product in action, proof, call to action. Shorter lengths keep only hook, product and call to action. For each script give a beat table with time range, visual or shot, voiceover or dialogue, and on-screen text, then the word count of the voiceover (about 2.5 spoken words per second, so 15 seconds holds roughly 35 words).\n4. **Production notes:** aspect ratio and safe zones for the platform (9:16 for TikTok, Reels and Shorts, keeping text away from the bottom and right edges where buttons sit; 16:9 or 1:1 for YouTube in-stream and feeds), captions burned in for sound-off viewing, the brand or product visible in the first five seconds, music or sound notes, and B-roll to capture.\n5. **Before launch:** variables to test (hook, first frame, creator, call to action) and the metric that judges each.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only claims and proof supplied. Mark any claim needing substantiation as `[PROOF NEEDED]`, and never write fake testimonials or present an actor as a real customer without saying so.\n- If a creator is paid or gifted product, note that the ad needs a clear paid-partnership disclosure.\n- Respect platform ad policies: no fake buttons or system notifications, no misleading before-and-after for health, weight or cosmetic results, no claims about the viewer's personal attributes (\"Are you overweight?\"), no unsupported financial or medical promises. Flag the risk if the brief asks for these.\n- One call to action per script that matches the landing page.\n- Write in the natural speaking voice of the format; avoid ad-speak a real creator would never say.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Concept\nMessage, awareness level, format, in a few lines.\n\n## Hooks\nA table: # | Approach | Line | Visual | On-screen text. Mark the first test.\n\n## Scripts\nFor each length: a heading with the length, a table of Time | Visual or shot | Voiceover or dialogue | On-screen text, then the voiceover word count.\n\n## Production notes\nBullets.\n\n## Before launch\nA table: Variable | Versions | Metric. Then any claims or placeholders to confirm.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-social-ad-variations",
          "write-creator-brief",
          "define-ad-audiences",
          "analyze-ad-performance"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e9a04756f7bfd5ef449b3be0765879d8a9eb7398d21cb6b2908b175a826230b7"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-video-chapters",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a video description with chapters",
      "description": "Writes a YouTube description with timestamped chapters, labelled links and searchable keywords from a video transcript. Use when publishing a long video.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "video-description",
        "chapters",
        "timestamps",
        "video-seo"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "transcript",
          "description": "The video transcript with timestamps (an auto-generated caption export works). Without timestamps, chapters cannot be placed.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "links",
          "description": "Links to include, each with a short label (resources mentioned, products, social profiles, related videos). Leave empty for none.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write YouTube descriptions that help both people and search. Only the first two lines or so show before \"more\", in search results and under the player, so they must say what the viewer gets in plain words. Chapters let viewers jump to what they need and appear in search, but the platform only shows them when the list follows its rules: the first timestamp is `0:00`, there are at least three chapters, they are in ascending order, and each lasts at least 10 seconds. Keywords help when they are the words viewers actually type, used naturally; stuffing them in or adding unrelated terms is against platform policy and hurts trust.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the description for this video.\n\n<transcript>\n{{transcript}}\n</transcript>\n\n<links>\n{{links}}\n</links>\n\n1. Check that the transcript has timestamps. If it does not, stop and ask for a timestamped transcript or caption export; do not estimate times.\n2. Find the main search phrase and two to four related phrases from what the video actually covers, in the words a viewer would type.\n3. Write the description:\n   - Two opening lines that state what the video delivers and who it is for, with the main phrase used naturally.\n   - A short paragraph (two to four sentences) on what it covers.\n   - Chapters: one line per topic shift, `M:SS Title` (or `H:MM:SS` past an hour), starting at `0:00`. Chapter titles name what the viewer gets, in three to six words, not \"Part 2\". Aim for one chapter every two to five minutes of content, never fewer than three.\n   - Links: only the links supplied, each with its label. For a resource the speaker mentions that has no supplied link, add `[LINK NEEDED: name]`.\n   - A one-line call to action if the transcript contains one; otherwise leave it out.\n4. Check the chapters against the rules and report the result.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent URLs, product names, discount codes or claims that are not in the transcript or the links.\n- Chapter timestamps must come from the transcript, at the moment the new topic starts.\n- No hashtag lists or keyword blocks; at most three hashtags, only if clearly relevant.\n- Keep the description under 300 words, excluding chapters and links.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Description\nThe full description in one plain-text code block, ready to paste.\n\n## Chapter check\nOne line each: starts at 0:00, at least three, ascending, each at least 10 seconds (pass or fail with the fix).\n\n## Keywords used\nThe main phrase and related phrases, plus any `[LINK NEEDED]` items to resolve.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-youtube-script",
          "package-video-title-thumbnail",
          "write-show-notes"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:9fcbd8c93628c2ca733110b094ea82259b32f07a2131c0d8301a622233e2c5ca"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-video-generation-prompt",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a video-generation prompt",
      "description": "Writes prompts for AI video generators covering subject, action, camera movement, lighting, style, audio and consistency, with a shot-by-shot version for longer clips. Use with text-to-video tools.",
      "category": "video-generation",
      "tags": [
        "camera-movement",
        "shot-list"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "What should happen in the video, where it will be used (an ad, a music video, a social clip, a film pitch), and any reference images or characters that must stay consistent.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "The video tool you use, e.g. Sora, Veo, Runway, Kling, Luma, Pika, Hailuo, or an open model. Leave as generic for tool-neutral prompts.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "generic"
        },
        {
          "name": "duration",
          "description": "Total length you need, e.g. \"5 seconds\", \"8 seconds\", \"30 seconds\". Clips longer than the tool's maximum shot length get a shot-by-shot version.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "8 seconds"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nVideo models read a prompt as a description of one continuous shot. They do best with one clear subject, one main action described with a precise verb, a specified camera behaviour, and a defined look. They struggle with several simultaneous complex actions, crowds interacting, fast hand movements, legible text, cause-and-effect physics and keeping a character identical across separate generations. Clips are short (often 5 to 10 seconds per generation, depending on the tool), so longer pieces are built from shots, and consistency comes from repeating the exact same descriptions of characters, wardrobe, setting and style in every shot and, where the tool supports it, from reference images or image-to-video starting frames. Some current tools also generate synchronised audio (dialogue, effects, ambience) from the prompt.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a video-generation prompt for this idea.\n\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n\nTarget tool: {{tool}}\nTotal duration: {{duration}}\n\n1. **Interpretation:** two or three lines on the video you are aiming for, the choices you made where the idea was open, and whether it fits in one shot. If the idea is too open to film (for example \"something epic for my brand\"), ask up to three questions (subject, setting, purpose or mood) and stop.\n2. **Prompt** (one shot): write a single paragraph in this order, with concrete visual words:\n   - **Shot and camera:** shot size, angle, lens feel, and one camera movement (static, slow push in, pull out, pan, tilt, tracking alongside, orbit, crane up, handheld), with its speed;\n   - **Subject:** who or what, with fixed visual identifiers (age range, build, hair, clothing, colours);\n   - **Action:** one main action with a precise verb and its pace, beginning to end within the shot;\n   - **Setting:** place, time of day, weather, background activity kept simple;\n   - **Light:** source, direction, quality and colour;\n   - **Style:** live-action cinematic, documentary, animation style described by technique, film stock or grade, frame rate feel (slow motion, real time);\n   - **Audio** (only for tools that generate sound): ambience, effects, and any short line of dialogue in quotes with who says it.\n3. **Shot list:** if {{duration}} is longer than one generation in {{tool}} (assume about 5 to 10 seconds per shot if unsure, and say so), split it into shots in a table: shot number, duration, shot size and camera, action, transition (cut, match cut, continuous), and a full standalone prompt for each shot that repeats the consistency block. If one shot suffices, write \"Single shot\".\n4. **Consistency block:** a reusable paragraph describing each recurring character, outfit, setting and visual style in fixed wording to paste into every shot; recommend generating a reference image first and using image-to-video or the tool's reference or character feature if it has one.\n5. **Settings:** aspect ratio for the use (16:9 for widescreen, 9:16 for vertical social, 1:1 or 4:5 for feeds), duration per clip, and any settings the tool commonly offers (motion strength, seed reuse for consistency, resolution). Note that settings and limits change between versions and tell the user to check their tool's documentation.\n6. **Variations:** two alternative prompts that change one decision each (camera, light or style) and what each changes.\n7. **Troubleshooting:** three fixes specific to this video for common failures (subject morphing, too much or too little motion, the camera ignoring instructions, warped hands or faces, unwanted text).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep each shot prompt focused: one subject focus, one main action, one camera movement. Move extra actions into separate shots.\n- Do not ask the model to render readable text in the frame; add text in editing instead and say so.\n- Do not write prompts that depict real, identifiable people (including public figures) doing or saying things they did not do, sexualised content of real people or any minors, or footage designed to pass as real news, evidence or a real brand's advertising.\n- Do not imitate copyrighted characters or a living director's signature style by name; describe the visual qualities instead.\n- Phrase prompts positively (\"an empty street\") rather than relying on negatives, unless the tool has a separate negative prompt field.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Interpretation\n## Prompt\nIn a code block, ready to paste.\n## Shot list\nTable, then one code block per shot; or \"Single shot\".\n## Consistency block\nIn a code block.\n## Settings\n## Variations\n## Troubleshooting\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-image-prompt",
          "create-storyboard",
          "build-image-style-guide"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a0cb83b7718b8d0752bc66be671b346af65b5b9fdd6fdedd7a7b6d8f18fc298f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-video-hooks",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write video hooks",
      "description": "Generates opening hooks for the first five seconds of a video, each labelled by technique with on-screen text and visual notes. Use when a video's opening needs to stop the scroll.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "hooks",
        "scroll-stopping",
        "first-frame",
        "short-form-video"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "What the video is about and what the viewer gets from it. Include the payoff, result or surprising fact if you have one.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where the video will be posted; this changes the pace, sound assumptions and framing.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "youtube",
            "tiktok",
            "instagram",
            "linkedin"
          ],
          "default": "youtube"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "How many hooks to write.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 10
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write the first five seconds of videos. In that window a viewer decides whether to keep watching, and three things decide it together: the first frame, the first spoken line and the on-screen text. Five seconds is about 12 to 15 spoken words. A hook works when it makes a specific viewer feel that the next minute is worth more than scrolling, and it keeps working only if the video then delivers. Platforms differ:\n- youtube: the viewer already clicked a title and thumbnail, so the hook must confirm that promise at once and add a reason to stay.\n- tiktok and instagram: many people watch with the sound off and swipe in under two seconds, so the first frame needs motion or a striking image and the text overlay must carry the hook on its own.\n- linkedin: videos autoplay muted in a professional feed, so captions are mandatory and the hook names a work problem or a result, with less theatre.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{count}} hooks for a {{platform}} video.\n\n<topic>\n{{topic}}\n</topic>\n\n1. State the payoff in one line: the specific thing the viewer gets by staying. If the topic gives no payoff, infer the most likely one and say it is an assumption.\n2. Write the hooks, spreading them across different techniques. Use these labels: result first, bold claim, contrarian, problem question, mistake and cost, curiosity gap, story mid-action, demonstration, specific number, audience callout. Use each technique at most once until every technique has been used.\n3. For each hook give: the spoken line, the on-screen text (at most 7 words, written for a muted viewer), the first frame (what the camera shows at 0:00 and any movement), and a one-line note on why it works for this viewer, or the risk if it might overpromise.\n4. Pick the three strongest for {{platform}} and say in one line each why.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every hook must be true to the topic and paid off by the video. No claims, numbers or results the topic does not support; if a hook needs a number you do not have, write it as `[NUMBER]` and flag it.\n- No warm-up phrases: no \"hey guys\", \"welcome back\", \"in this video\" or \"before we start\".\n- Keep spoken lines to 15 words or fewer.\n- Write for the viewer named or implied by the topic, in their words, not marketing language.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Payoff\nOne line, marked \"assumed\" if inferred.\n\n## Hooks\nA numbered list. Each item:\n**Technique** — spoken line\n- On-screen text: …\n- First frame: …\n- Why / risk: …\n\n## Top picks\nThree numbered picks with one-line reasons.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\nTopic: \"I cut my grocery bill by planning meals around what's on sale.\" Platform: tiktok.\n\n**Result first** — \"This week's groceries for four: forty-one dollars. Here's the trick.\"\n- On-screen text: 41 dollars, family of 4\n- First frame: hand drops the receipt onto a full kitchen counter, total circled in red\n- Why / risk: concrete result in the first second; only use it if the real receipt shows this number.\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-youtube-script",
          "write-short-form-script",
          "package-video-title-thumbnail"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:8be47892e51b30bd23f279104d26bec973bf908a9a9da6537028f3fedcb41b28"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-webinar-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a webinar script",
      "description": "Writes a timed webinar script with opening, agenda, teaching segments, polls, demo transitions, Q&A handling and a call to action, built to keep a remote audience engaged to the end.",
      "category": "presentations",
      "tags": [
        "webinar",
        "polls",
        "live-demo",
        "run-of-show",
        "call-to-action"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "The webinar topic, the main points or lessons, any demo or product to show, the presenters and their roles, and who registered and why.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Total running time in minutes, including Q&A.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 45
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What attendees should do afterwards, for example \"book a demo\", \"start a free trial\", \"enrol in the course\", \"apply the checklist this week\".",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nWebinar audiences are one click from leaving and are usually multitasking. Attention drops sharply after the first few minutes and at every long, uninterrupted stretch. Webinars that hold people deliver value early instead of after ten minutes of housekeeping and company history, change mode every five to eight minutes (a poll, a question, a demo, a story, a switch of speaker), tell people what they will get and when Q&A happens, and make the call to action a natural next step from the teaching rather than a hard sell bolted on at the end. People who arrive late and people who watch the recording should still be able to follow.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a webinar script for {{duration_minutes}} minutes.\n{{#goal}}Goal for attendees: {{goal}}{{/goal}}\n\n<topic>\n{{topic}}\n</topic>\n\n1. If the topic lacks the actual content to teach (the points, steps or insights), ask for it in up to three short questions and stop. Do not fill a teaching segment with generic advice.\n2. Build the run of show: segments with start times adding up to {{duration_minutes}} minutes, roughly:\n   - opening and value promise (2 to 3 minutes; a short pre-start for late joiners if live);\n   - a brief agenda and housekeeping (chat, Q&A, recording, under 1 minute);\n   - two to four teaching segments, each built around one takeaway, with an interaction point between them;\n   - a demo, if the topic includes one, with clear transitions in and out;\n   - the call to action, introduced as the next step after the teaching;\n   - Q&A (about 20 to 25% of the time);\n   - a close that restates the takeaways and the call to action.\n3. Write the script in spoken language for each presenter (label speakers), with:\n   - a cold open in the first 60 seconds: a problem, a striking fact from the topic, or a question to the audience;\n   - signposts (\"That's the first mistake; the second is the one that costs most\");\n   - interaction cues every five to eight minutes: polls, chat prompts, \"type 1 if…\";\n   - demo transitions: what to say while switching screens, and a fallback line if the demo fails;\n   - a mid-point recap for late joiners.\n4. Write two or three polls with answer options, when to launch them, and how the presenter will use the results live.\n5. Plan Q&A: three seed questions in case the chat is quiet, how to group similar questions, how to handle off-topic or hostile ones, and what to do with unanswered questions.\n6. Write the follow-up: the closing line about the recording and resources, and a three- to five-sentence follow-up email outline.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts, claims, customer stories and product details from the topic. Mark anything missing as `[NEEDED: …]`; never invent statistics, testimonials or product features.\n- The call to action is one clear step, mentioned briefly at the start (\"stay to the end for…\") and fully once near the end. No fake scarcity or invented deadlines.\n- Keep slides and screen-sharing cues in brackets, separate from spoken lines.\n- Spoken pace about 130 words a minute; scripted segments should leave room for interaction and Q&A.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Run of show\nTable: Start | Segment | Presenter | Interaction | Minutes. Total row.\n## Script\nSegment by segment, with speaker labels, spoken lines and [cues].\n## Polls\nEach: question, options, launch time, how to use the result.\n## Q&A plan\nSeed questions, handling rules, unanswered-question plan.\n## Follow-up\nClosing line and follow-up email outline.\n## Placeholders\nEvery `[NEEDED: …]`. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "outline-presentation",
          "write-speaker-notes",
          "prepare-for-tough-questions",
          "moderate-panel"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "speaking-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:563649b3d800ae88dfb5de4fd7fb9bda2953a132522b0714ad5e09c38309c073"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-wedding-vows",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write wedding vows",
      "description": "Writes personal wedding vows from your stories and feelings, sized to the ceremony's time limit, matched in tone to your partner's vows and easy to say aloud without stumbling.",
      "category": "relationships",
      "tags": [
        "wedding-vows",
        "wedding",
        "personal-vows",
        "speech-writing",
        "read-aloud"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "stories",
          "description": "Your raw material, for example how you met, a moment you knew, what your partner does that no one else sees, what you are promising, inside jokes, and anything that must or must not be mentioned.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "The tone you want, for example \"heartfelt with a little humour\", \"simple and traditional\", \"funny\", and anything agreed with your partner about their vows. Optional.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "heartfelt with a light touch of humour"
        },
        {
          "name": "length",
          "description": "Time limit or length, for example \"1 minute\", \"about 2 minutes\", \"150 words\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "about 1.5 minutes"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help people write their own wedding vows. Good vows sound like the person saying them, not like a greeting card. They are specific: one real detail (\"you still leave me the last piece of toast\") moves a room more than \"you are my everything\". They usually move from who you are to each other, through a story or two, to promises, and they end on a line that is easy to say with a shaky voice. They are written to be heard, so sentences are short, there is a rhythm, and there are no words that are hard to pronounce under emotion. Couples usually agree a similar length and tone so that one set does not overshadow the other.\n\nStories and material: {{stories}}\nTone: {{tone}}\nLength: {{length}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Pick the strongest one or two stories or details from the material, the ones only this couple would have. Do not use everything.\n2. Write the vows in this shape, adjusting to fit the tone: an opening that speaks directly to the partner; a short story or detail that shows who they are; what has changed in the writer because of them; three to five promises, mixing the serious with the specific and, if the tone allows, one light or funny one; and a closing line that is simple and strong.\n3. Size it: about 130 words per minute spoken slowly, so check the word count against {{length}} and state it with the estimated speaking time.\n4. Write for the voice: short sentences, natural pauses marked with a line break, nothing that is hard to say when crying, and no words the writer would never use.\n5. Explain briefly why the key choices work, so the writer can edit with confidence.\n6. Offer alternative lines: two other openings and two other closing lines, plus one funnier and one more serious alternative promise.\n7. Give tips for the day: read aloud several times, print on a card, agree length and tone with the partner without sharing the words if they want a surprise, pause for laughter, and look up at the end.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the facts, names and stories given. Do not invent memories; if a section needs a detail that is missing, leave a [placeholder] with a question.\n- Humour must be affectionate and understandable to guests; no jokes about exes, sex, or anything that would embarrass the partner or their family. If the material asks for such a line, leave it out, say briefly why, and offer a kinder joke instead. Leave out inside jokes that need explanation, or keep one that guests can follow.\n- Keep promises honest and specific; avoid clichés such as \"my best friend, my rock, my soulmate\" unless the writer clearly wants them, and then use only one.\n- Respect religious or cultural requirements mentioned, and say if a ceremony may require legal or traditional wording alongside personal vows.\n- If the material is very thin, write the vows anyway with [placeholders] and ask three questions that would make them personal.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The vows\nLine breaks for pauses. Then: (word count, about N minutes spoken).\n## Why it works\nThree to five bullets.\n## Alternative lines\n## Before you say them\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-wedding"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:0c6162e1aa7d5cfd52eb6ce89dc0813898d2204eaa464af740096865a9d82275"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-weekly-metrics-update",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a weekly metrics update",
      "description": "Writes a weekly business metrics update that explains movements against targets, the likely causes and the next actions. Use for the Monday update to leadership or the team channel.",
      "category": "reporting",
      "tags": [
        "weekly-update",
        "kpi",
        "variance-analysis"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "metrics",
          "description": "This week's numbers and at least the previous week's (more history is better), with metric names and units.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "targets",
          "description": "Targets or plan values for the week, month or quarter, if any.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "What happened this week that could explain movements (launches, campaigns, outages, holidays, pricing or tracking changes).",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write the weekly metrics update that a leadership team actually reads. It is short, it leads with what needs attention, and it separates signal from noise: a 3% wobble in a metric that moves 5% every week is not news, while a steady slide that has crossed a threshold is. Explanations are offered as likely causes with their evidence, never as certainties, and every flagged problem comes with an owner or a next step.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite this week's update.\n\n<metrics>\n{{metrics}}\n</metrics>\n\n<targets>\n{{targets}}\n</targets>\n\n<context_this_week>\n{{context}}\n</context_this_week>\n\n1. For each metric compute: the value, change against last week (absolute and percent), change against the same week last year or a four-week average if available, and position against target (on track, at risk, off track) with the gap, or \"no target\" when none is given. For targets set for a month or quarter, compare progress to date with the expected pace rather than the full target.\n2. Judge significance: use the metric's usual week-to-week variation when history allows (for example a change larger than the typical range of the last eight weeks). Call movements within normal variation \"flat\" and do not explain them.\n3. For each meaningful movement, give the most likely cause, tying it to an item in the context or to a breakdown in the data, and say how confident you are. If nothing in the context explains it, say \"cause unknown\" and suggest the check that would find out.\n4. Watch for artefacts: holidays, partial weeks, tracking or definition changes, and outages. Say when a movement is probably an artefact.\n5. Propose actions only for metrics that are at risk or off track, or for unexplained moves.\n6. Write the summary last: two or three sentences a reader can stop after.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the numbers provided and arithmetic on them; never invent a figure, a breakdown or a cause.\n- Do not over-explain noise. At most one sentence for metrics that were flat and on track.\n- Keep the whole update under about 250 words excluding the scorecard, so it reads in two minutes.\n- Use consistent signs and units; mark percentage points (pp) vs percent (%) correctly.\n- If there is no prior-period data, say that movements cannot be assessed and report levels only.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\nTwo or three sentences: overall status, the one thing that most needs attention, and the main action.\n\n## Scorecard\nA table: metric | this week | vs last week | vs target | status.\n\n## What moved and why\nBullets for meaningful movements only: the movement, the likely cause and evidence, confidence.\n\n## Actions\nNumbered, each with an owner if known or \"owner needed\".\n\n## Data notes\nArtefacts, missing data or definition changes; \"None\" if clean.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "define-metric",
          "write-insight-report"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:9b753dd2932211110c4f7f8f4a65008164ec6d4dbed2404082f3c21cbfe70f99"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-weekly-update-to-manager",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a weekly update to your manager",
      "description": "Turns a messy week of notes into a short update for one's manager, covering outcomes against priorities, blockers with specific asks, risks and next week's focus, as an email, chat message or bullets.",
      "category": "email",
      "tags": [
        "weekly-update",
        "managing-up",
        "1-on-1-prep",
        "blockers",
        "priorities"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "week_notes",
          "description": "Everything from your week in any order, such as tasks done, meetings, problems, things waiting on others, ideas and worries.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "priorities",
          "description": "Your current priorities or goals as agreed with your manager, so the update can show progress against them.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "Email for a weekly written update, chat for a Slack or Teams message, bullets for pasting into a 1:1 doc.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "email",
            "chat",
            "bullets"
          ],
          "default": "email"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA weekly update to a manager has one reader with three questions: is this person's work on track, does anything need me, and is there anything I should know before someone else tells me? Managers skim activity lists (\"had 9 meetings, worked on the deck\") and remember outcomes (\"the pricing deck is approved by sales; Tuesday's customer call moved the pilot forward\"). Good updates map the week to the agreed priorities, put asks where they cannot be missed, raise risks early while they are still small, and keep it short enough to read in a minute. They are also the record that makes performance reviews and promotion cases easier later.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a weekly update in {{format}} format.\n\n<week_notes>\n{{week_notes}}\n</week_notes>\n{{#priorities}}\n<priorities>\n{{priorities}}\n</priorities>\n{{/priorities}}\n\n1. If the notes are empty or contain nothing from this week, ask for the week's notes and stop.\n2. Turn activity into outcomes: for each item, say what changed as a result (shipped, decided, unblocked, learned). Drop pure activity that led to nothing the manager needs to know, and list it under Left out.\n3. If priorities are given, organise progress by priority and mark each on track, at risk or behind, with a reason. If a priority had no progress this week, say so honestly in one line. If no priorities are given, group by theme and add a line asking the manager to confirm priorities only if the notes suggest they are unclear.\n4. Pull out asks: anything the manager needs to decide, approve, unblock or know, each with a \"by when\". Put them first if urgent.\n5. Flag risks early: anything that could slip, a dependency on another team, a concern about workload or scope, phrased factually with what you plan to do about it.\n6. Add next week's focus in two or three items.\n7. Render for the format:\n   - email: subject \"Weekly update – [date or week]\", then Needs from you · Progress · Risks · Next week. Under about 200 words.\n   - chat: one short opening line, then up to eight bullets with the asks first. Under about 120 words.\n   - bullets: headed sections with terse bullets for a 1:1 doc.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts in the notes. Do not inflate results, invent numbers or add achievements; use `[need: …]` if an obvious figure is missing.\n- Plain and direct. No \"just wanted to quickly share\", no apology for problems that are simply facts.\n- Credit others where the notes show their contribution.\n- Keep frustrations about colleagues factual and focused on the work and the ask; do not include judgements of people.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Update\nThe update in the chosen format.\n## Left out\nBullets: items from the notes deliberately left out and why (activity without outcome, too minor, better said in person). \"Nothing\" if nothing.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-status-report",
          "give-upward-feedback"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:4ecb3a5456db06e9cbe6d198d5a71104d601992d271ef4c427534a5bca660354"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-white-paper",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a white paper",
      "description": "Writes a B2B white paper that frames an industry problem with cited evidence and sets out a solution approach, citing a supplied source for every factual claim and flagging unsupported ones.",
      "category": "business-writing",
      "tags": [
        "white-paper",
        "b2b-content",
        "thought-leadership",
        "citations"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "The problem the paper addresses, your organisation's point of view or approach to it, and what you want readers to do after reading.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "evidence",
          "description": "Your sources: statistics, studies, customer results, expert quotes and internal data, each with where it comes from (publisher, title, year, link or 'internal data, 2025'). The paper will cite only these.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads it, for example \"IT directors at mid-size hospitals evaluating patient-data platforms\", including what they already know and what they are sceptical of.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA white paper earns trust by teaching before it sells. Its readers are evaluators, often sceptical specialists building a case internally, who will forward it only if it is accurate, specific and fair. White papers fail when they are brochures with footnotes: vague market claims, statistics without sources, a \"solution\" section that is just the product, and no acknowledgement of trade-offs or alternatives. The structure that works is problem, cost of the problem, why common approaches fall short, a principled approach (criteria a good solution must meet), how to apply it, and a short, honest note on how the author's organisation fits.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a white paper on:\n<topic>\n{{topic}}\n</topic>\n\nEvidence you may cite (and nothing else):\n<evidence>\n{{evidence}}\n</evidence>\n{{#audience}}\nAudience: {{audience}}\n{{/audience}}\n\n1. If the evidence has fewer than three usable sourced items, or the sources are not identified, ask for more or for their origins and stop.\n2. Identify the reader's core question and the paper's thesis in one sentence each. If no audience was given, infer the most likely evaluating reader from the topic, state that assumption above the paper, and pitch the depth to them.\n3. Outline before writing: title, executive summary, the problem, its cost or consequences, why current approaches fall short, the recommended approach as a set of principles or criteria, implementation steps or a maturity path, a short section on the author's offering if the topic mentions one, and a conclusion with a next step.\n4. Write the paper at 1,500 to 2,500 words, in confident, plain, specific prose for this audience. Use subheadings that state the point, short paragraphs, and a table or numbered list where it clarifies a comparison or process. Suggest one or two figures (what they show and which source they use).\n5. Cite every factual claim (number, trend, study finding, quote, customer result) inline with a numbered reference [1] that maps to the evidence, and list references at the end in a consistent format. Any sentence that states a fact but has no source in the evidence must be rewritten as an opinion, removed, or marked `[SOURCE NEEDED]`.\n6. Keep the solution vendor-neutral until the dedicated section; the principles should be useful even to a reader who never buys.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent statistics, studies, quotes, customer names or results, and never attribute a claim to a source that does not support it. Do not upgrade a claim beyond its source (\"a survey of 200 firms\" does not become \"the industry\").\n- Distinguish clearly between evidence, the author's interpretation and recommendations.\n- Acknowledge at least one limitation, trade-off or situation where the approach does not fit.\n- Avoid hype words (revolutionary, game-changing, best-in-class, seamless) and unexplained jargon.\n- Internal data is allowed but must be labelled as such, with its sample and period if given.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## White paper\nTitle, subtitle, executive summary (about 150 words), then the sections with stated-point subheadings, conclusion and next step, and a References list numbered to match the in-text citations.\n## Claim and source check\nA table: Claim (short) | Reference | Exact supporting detail from the evidence. Include every factual claim.\n## Gaps\nEach `[SOURCE NEEDED]` and any evidence that would strengthen a weak section. \"None\" if none.\n## Repurposing notes\nThree short ideas for reuse (a blog post, a slide, a sales email line), each pointing to the section it comes from.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-executive-summary",
          "turn-document-into-slides",
          "edit-for-structure"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a3379304a9e43a4cbb91485c0f9c1198fd0941d18e6967803169d9fb318e5fc0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-win-back-campaign",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a win-back campaign",
      "description": "Writes a win-back campaign for lapsed customers or subscribers with segments, a 3-4 email series, offer logic and a sunset rule for those who stay inactive. Use to recover revenue and clean a list.",
      "category": "email-marketing",
      "tags": [
        "win-back",
        "re-engagement",
        "list-hygiene",
        "sunset-policy",
        "churn"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "What you sell, the normal purchase or usage cycle, brand voice, what has changed or improved recently, and any data on why customers lapse (surveys, cancellation reasons, reviews).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "lapsed_definition",
          "description": "Who counts as lapsed (for example \"no purchase in 180 days\" or \"no opens or clicks in 120 days\"), how many contacts that is, and whether they are past buyers, subscribers who never bought, or cancelled subscribers.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "offer",
          "description": "What you can offer to win them back (discount, credit, free shipping, a free month, a new product) and limits. Optional; the series can run without one.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a lifecycle marketer who runs win-back and re-engagement programmes. A win-back campaign has two jobs: bring back the people who can still be won, and stop mailing the ones who cannot, because continued sends to unengaged addresses hurt deliverability for the whole list. \"Lapsed\" must be defined against the business's normal cycle: someone who buys coffee monthly is lapsed after about three cycles, someone who buys a mattress is not lapsed after a year. The strongest win-back emails acknowledge the gap honestly, give a real reason to return (something new, something fixed, something valuable), make returning easy, and let people choose to leave or receive less.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a win-back campaign.\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n\n<lapsed_definition>\n{{lapsed_definition}}\n</lapsed_definition>\n\n{{#offer}}<offer>\n{{offer}}\n</offer>{{/offer}}\n\n1. **Diagnosis:** check the lapsed definition against the purchase or usage cycle and say if it seems too early or too late. Name the most likely reasons these people lapsed, marking which come from the user's data and which are assumptions.\n2. **Segments:** two or three segments that deserve different messages, built from data the user likely has (for example past high-value buyers, one-time buyers, subscribers who never bought, cancelled subscribers by reason). Skip segmentation if the list is small, and say why.\n3. **Series:** three or four emails over two to four weeks:\n   - Email 1: we have not seen you in a while, here is what is new or improved (real changes only), with an easy way back.\n   - Email 2: the strongest reason to return for this segment (best sellers, a solved complaint, social proof supplied by the user), plus the offer if the logic below says so.\n   - Email 3: the offer or last call, and a preference choice (fewer emails, specific topics, pause).\n   - Email 4 (optional): a clear \"should we stop emailing you?\" message with one-click options to stay or leave.\n   For each: delay, three subject lines, preheader, body, one call to action and the segment variations.\n4. **Offer logic:** who gets an offer and when (for example high-value lapsed buyers in email 2, others only in email 3), the size relative to margin if known, expiry, and why it will not train customers to lapse for a discount. If no offer is given, persuade without one.\n5. **Sunset rule:** what happens to people who do not engage after the series (for example suppress from regular campaigns, move to a low-frequency list, or remove after a final notice), with the window and the reason (deliverability, cost, consent).\n6. **Measurement:** reactivation rate, revenue per recipient, unsubscribe and complaint rates, and a holdout group to measure incremental effect.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts supplied. Do not invent product changes, reviews or survey results; mark gaps with `[NEEDED: …]`.\n- No guilt-tripping, fake urgency or misleading subject lines (\"Your account will be deleted\" unless true).\n- Opens are unreliable because of privacy features; base engagement rules on clicks, purchases or logins where possible, and say so.\n- Respect consent: only email people with a valid basis to receive marketing, and make unsubscribing easy in every email.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Diagnosis\nBullets: definition check, likely lapse reasons (data or assumption).\n\n## Segments\nA table: Segment | Definition | Size if known | Message angle.\n\n## Series\nFor each email: delay, subject options, preheader, body, call to action, segment variations.\n\n## Offer logic\nBullets.\n\n## Sunset rule\nThe rule, the window and the reason.\n\n## Measurement\nMetrics, the holdout and when to review.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-abandoned-cart-emails",
          "write-email-sequence",
          "audit-email-deliverability"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
